/* Bizzing Bee — the ADVANCED concept curriculum (Advanced Pack only).
   43 expert chapters beyond the 121-chapter general course. These are the
   national-level decision procedures, orthographic patterns with real library inventory,
   the origin groups the general loanword set never covered, and word-formation machinery.
   Built from ADVANCED-CONCEPTS.md; drill words are looked up from words-data.js /
   words-full.js by the build script, so every word here is one the speller can practise.
   SB_ADV_CSCRIPT holds the narration scripts, one entry per chapter index. */
window.SB_ADV_CONCEPTS={"version":2,"chapters":[{"title":"Schwa Rescue — find the relative that stresses the vowel","category":"Championship Procedure","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"The schwa is the largest single source of misspelling because it carries no spelling information: any vowel, and the letter y, can reduce to it. Listening harder cannot help. The procedure is to find a related word in which that syllable takes the stress, which restores the vowel to its true identity.","method":"<div class='trick'><b>DEFINITE</b></div>\n<b>Problem.</b> DEF-uh-nit — the second vowel is a schwa, so it could be a, e, i, o or u\n<b>Find a relative.</b> definition\n<b>Stress it.</b> def-i-NI-tion — the syllable is now stressed\n<b>Read the vowel.</b> i\n<b>Return.</b> def-i-nite\n<b>Check.</b> the root is Latin finis = boundary, which also carries i","cards":[{"title":"Why the schwa is different","body":"Every other sound in English narrows your options. Hear /f/ and you are choosing between f, ph and gh. Hear a schwa and you are choosing between every vowel in the alphabet plus y. It is the only sound that gives you nothing, which is why it accounts for the majority of championship misspellings."},{"title":"The relative test","body":"English keeps a vowel's spelling constant across a word family even when the sound changes. So a relative where the syllable is stressed shows you the letter directly. custody is unclear; cus-TO-dian is not. major is unclear; ma-JOR-ity is not. local is unclear; lo-CAL-ity is not."},{"title":"Building the reflex","body":"The reflex to train is: hear a schwa, immediately reach for the -ity, -ic, -ian, -ation or -ify form of the same word. Those four suffixes pull stress toward themselves, which is exactly what you need. If no relative exists, fall through to the root: the Latin or Greek source usually carries the vowel you want."},{"title":"When there is no relative","body":"Some words are morphological orphans and have no stressed relative. For those, the root is your only route, and if the root is unknown, ask for the language of origin and spell to that language's default. Guessing a is not a strategy; guessing the origin default is."}],"words":[{"w":"definite","def":"precise; explicit and clearly defined","ex":"I want a definite answer.","say":"DEH-fuh-nuht","hook":"Definite ends in -ite not -ate — remember 'finite' hides inside definITE.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"competence","def":"the quality of being adequately or well qualified physically and intellectually","ex":"Her competence in both math and science made her a top candidate for the engineering scholarship.","say":"KAH-mpuh-tihns","hook":"comPETENCE ends in ENCE not ANCE — think 'excellent competENCE shows you can.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"separate","def":"a separately printed article that originally appeared in a larger publication","ex":"Our teacher passed around a separate, a single article reprinted from the big journal.","say":"SEH-per-ayt","hook":"There's 'a rat' in sep-A-RAT-e — the rat separates the two e's.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"benefit","def":"financial assistance in time of need","ex":"For the benefit of all.","say":"BEH-nuh-fiht","hook":"BENEfit: think BENE (well) + FIT — a benefit makes you financially FIT, and it's spelled BENE not BENI.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"category","def":"a collection of things sharing a common attribute","ex":"Which category does a dolphin belong to, since it swims yet breathes air like us?","say":"KA-tuh-gaw-ree","hook":"CATEGORY: a CAT in a GORE-y story belongs in its own — spell it catEGORY, hiding GORE in the middle.","o":"French"},{"w":"comparative","def":"the form of an adjective or adverb that shows more of a quality, like 'faster'","ex":"`faster' is the comparative of the adjective `fast'.","say":"kuh-MPEH-ruh-tihv","hook":"COMPAR-ATIVE: the A after COMPAR is the KEY—think 'A for Assessment of similarities.'","o":"Latin"},{"w":"grammar","def":"the study of the way sentences are constructed","ex":"Her English teacher insisted that mastering grammar was the foundation of all good writing.","say":"g-r-A-m-ur","hook":"GRAMMAR ends in '-ar' not '-er'—a grAmmAr has two A's, like two rules to remember.","o":"English"},{"w":"similar","def":"marked by correspondence or resemblance","ex":"Similar food at similar prices.","say":"SIH-muh-ler","hook":"","o":"French"},{"w":"familiar","def":"a person attached to the household of a high official (as a pope or bishop) who renders service in return for support","ex":"The melody sounded so familiar that Jonah hummed along without realizing it, only later remembering it was the song his grandmother used to sing every evening.","say":"fuh-MIH-lyer","hook":"FAMILiar: spot FAMILY minus the Y, then add -ar—it's a word you should already know well!","o":"Latin"},{"w":"peculiar","def":"beyond or deviating from the usual or expected","ex":"The old clock made a peculiar ticking sound that no one could quite explain.","say":"puh-KYOO-lyer","hook":"PECULIAR ends in -IAR not -IER — something peculiar is IARly odd; remember the I before A.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"academy","def":"a secondary school (usually private)","ex":"She earned a scholarship to attend the prestigious academy known for its rigorous science and mathematics programs.","say":"uh-KA-duh-mee","hook":"ACADEMY has one 'c' and one 'a' at the start — Plato's grove had ONE entrance, not two.","o":"French"},{"w":"monastery","def":"a house occupied by usually monks","ex":"The ancient monastery perched on the cliff had been home to monks for over five hundred years.","say":"m-AH-n-uh-s-t-eh-r-ee","hook":"A MONASTERY is where monks live alone—remember it ends in -ery like a bakERY, a place of work.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"allegory","def":"a short moral story (often with animal characters)","ex":"The teacher explained that the novel was an allegory about freedom, with each animal representing a different social class.","say":"A-luh-gaw-ree","hook":"An ALLEGORY has a hidden STORY — ALLEGO-RY, find the hidden meaning like the hidden RY at the end.","o":"French"},{"w":"custodian","def":"one having charge of buildings or grounds or animals","ex":"The school custodian arrived early every morning to make sure the hallways were clean before students arrived.","say":"kuh-STOH-dee-uhn","hook":"The custodIAN is the person who guards — remember, a person ends in -IAN.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"majority","def":"the property resulting from being or relating to the greater in number of two parts; the main part","ex":"The majority of his customers prefer it.","say":"muh-JAW-ruh-tee","hook":"MAJORITY ends in -ORITY like authority — the MAJORITY has authority over the minority.","o":"French"},{"w":"locality","def":"a surrounding or nearby region","ex":"Many rare butterflies live only in this sunny locality near the river.","say":"loh-KA-luh-tee","hook":"Local + ITY = locality; the 'ity' is where you find your identity of place.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"preference","def":"a strong liking","ex":"My own preference is for good literature.","say":"PREH-fer-uhns","hook":"PREFERENCE ends in -ENCE not -ANCE — you preFER something, and you need confid-ENCE to say so.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"reference","def":"a book or a passage in a work to which a reader is directed","ex":"The teacher told us to use the encyclopedia as a reference for our reports.","say":"r-EH-f-ur-uh-n-s","hook":"REFERENCE has three E's carrying the word — re-FER-ENCE: think 'I REFER to this, hence the -ENCE, not -ANCE.'","o":"Latin"},{"w":"occurrence","def":"an event that happens","ex":"A disease of frequent occurrence.","say":"uh-KUR-uhns","hook":"An OCCURRence has double 'c' AND double 'r' — four consonants for one big event.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"maintenance","def":"the act of keeping things in good order","ex":"Regular maintenance keeps the school bus running safely all year long.","say":"m-AY-n-t-uh-n-uh-n-s","hook":"MAINTENANCE has no second A — main-TEN-ance, ten fingers holding things in shape.","o":"French"},{"w":"resistance","def":"the action of opposing something that you disapprove or disagree with","ex":"He encountered a general feeling of resistance from many citizens.","say":"rih-ZIH-stuhns","hook":"RESISTANCE ends in -ANCE not -ENCE — remember 'a stANCE against' something.","o":"French"},{"w":"existence","def":"the state or fact of existing","ex":"Scientists search distant planets for any sign of the existence of tiny living creatures.","say":"eh-GZIH-stuhns","hook":"ExistENCE — remember ENCE not ANCE: existence is INTENSE, and intense people use E-N-C-E.","o":"French"},{"w":"independent","def":"a neutral or uncommitted person (especially in politics)","ex":"Because she was neither Republican nor Democrat, the candidate ran as an independent, promising voters that she would make decisions based on facts and community needs rather than loyalty to any political party.","say":"ih-ndih-PEH-nduhnt","hook":"An inDEPENDent person doesn't dePEND on anyone — end with '-ent,' like the person standing upright.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"descendant","def":"a person considered as descended from some ancestor or race","ex":"Maya discovered through a genealogy website that she was a direct descendant of a celebrated West African queen whose story had been passed down only through oral tradition for centuries.","say":"dih-SEH-nduhnt","hook":"A DESCENDANT of an ancestor — remember ANT: a tiny ANT crawls DOWN from its ancestors.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"relevant","def":"having a bearing on or connection with the subject at issue","ex":"The scientist corresponds with colleagues in order to learn about matters relevant to her own research.","say":"REH-luh-vuhnt","hook":"RELEVANT ends in -ANT: an ANT that LIFTS things above the rest is always relevant.","o":"French"},{"w":"hesitant","def":"lacking decisiveness of character; unable to act or decide quickly or firmly","ex":"Elena was hesitant to dive into the cold water, standing at the edge of the pool for several minutes.","say":"HEH-zih-tuhnt","hook":"A hesitANt ant pauses at every crumb — remember the 'a' in -ant, not -ent.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"dominant","def":"(music) the fifth note of the diatonic scale","ex":"Television plays a dominant role in molding public opinion.","say":"DAH-muh-nuhnt","hook":"The DOMINANT lord has an ANT working for him — D-O-M-I-N-A-N-T.","o":"French"},{"w":"prominent","def":"having a quality that thrusts itself into attention","ex":"A prominent ridge of mountains divided the two valleys, visible from every corner of the tiny farming town.","say":"PRAH-muh-nuhnt","hook":"A PROMINENT feature sticks out like a PROMINENT NOSE — end with -ENT not -ANT","o":"Latin"}]},{"title":"Stress Shift — predicting which vowel will fail","category":"Championship Procedure","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"Stress moves as suffixes attach, and whichever syllable loses the stress reduces to a schwa. Knowing where the stress will land lets you predict which vowel is about to become unreliable before you have to spell it, and which relative will rescue it.","method":"<div class='trick'><b>PHOTOGRAPH → PHOTOGRAPHY → PHOTOGRAPHIC</b></div>\n<b>Base.</b> PHO-to-graph — stress on 1\n<b>Add -y.</b> pho-TOG-ra-phy — stress moves to 2\n<b>Add -ic.</b> pho-to-GRAPH-ic — stress moves to 3\n<b>Consequence.</b> the o in the second syllable is clear in photography, mumbled in photograph\n<b>Use it.</b> to spell photograph, borrow the vowel from photography","cards":[{"title":"Suffixes that pull stress","body":"-ity, -ic, -ian, -ify, -ation and -ial move the stress toward themselves, usually onto the syllable immediately before them. This is why they are so useful for schwa rescue: they relocate the stress onto exactly the syllable you could not hear."},{"title":"Suffixes that leave stress alone","body":"-ness, -less, -ment, -ful, -ly and -er attach without moving the stress. They are the Germanic layer of English and they behave politely. They are no help for rescue, because the syllable you cannot hear stays unstressed."},{"title":"Reading a family as a set","body":"Strong competitors do not learn words, they learn families, because one family shares one set of vowels across three or four stress patterns. monarch, monarchy, monarchic. lithograph, lithography, lithographic. patriarch, patriarchy, patriarchal. Learn the family and every member becomes spellable."},{"title":"The trap of the base form","body":"The base form is often the hardest member of a family to spell, because base forms carry the most unstressed syllables. Derived forms are easier. So when a base form is called, reach for the derivative in your head first, then work back."}],"words":[{"w":"photograph","def":"a representation of a person or scene in the form of a print or transparent slide; recorded by a camera on light-sensitive material","ex":"I photographed the scene of the accident.","say":"FOH-tuh-graf","hook":"A PHOTOGRAPh writes (GRAPH) with light — always end in -graph, not -graf.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"photography","def":"the act of taking and printing photographs","ex":"Her passion for photography led her to document every corner of her city, from busy street markets to quiet rooftop gardens.","say":"fuh-TAH-gruh-fee","hook":"PHOTOGRAPHy is the craft of writing (GRAPH) with light — the A in -graphy, not -i-.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"photographic","def":"relating to photography or obtained by using photography","ex":"His photographic memory let him recall every detail of the map after a single glance.","say":"foh-tuh-GRA-fihk","hook":"PHOTOGRAPHic memory holds the PH in GRAPH — never drop the H before -ic.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"comparable","def":"able to be compared or worthy of comparison","ex":"The two puppies were comparable in size, though one had much longer, floppier ears.","say":"KAH-mper-uh-buhl","hook":"COMPAR-ABLE: drop the E from compare before adding ABLE—two things are equal (PAR).","o":"Latin"},{"w":"preparatory","def":"preceding and preparing for something","ex":"The athletes completed a rigorous preparatory training camp every August, conditioning their bodies and reviewing strategy before the official season games began in September.","say":"prih-PEH-ruh-taw-ree","hook":"preparATORY — it's preparatory, not preparitory; think 'prepare' + -atory like 'laboratory.'","o":"Latin"},{"w":"laboratory","def":"a workplace for the conduct of scientific research","ex":"In the science laboratory, we mixed baking soda and vinegar to make a fizzy volcano.","say":"LA-bruh-taw-ree","hook":"LABORATORY contains the word LABOR — scientists LABOR in a LABOR-atory; keep that A: lab-OR-a-tory.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"applicable","def":"capable of being applied; having relevance","ex":"Gave applicable examples to support her argument.","say":"A-pluh-kuh-buhl","hook":"If it's APPLIC-ABLE, you are ABLE to APPLY it — always end with -ABLE, not -IBLE.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"admirable","def":"deserving of the highest esteem or admiration","ex":"His admirable honesty impressed the teacher when he returned the extra change.","say":"A-dmer-uh-buhl","hook":"Something admirable makes you say 'Ahh, MIRA-ble!' — no extra i, just admire minus the e, plus able.","o":"French"},{"w":"reparable","def":"is capable of being repaired","ex":"Reparable damage to the car.","say":"REP-er-uh-bul","hook":"If it's reparable, you're ABLE to repair it — find ABLE at the end as your clue!","o":"English"},{"w":"despicable","def":"morally reprehensible","ex":"The villain's despicable plan to steal from orphans shocked everyone in the courtroom.","say":"dih-SPIH-kuh-buhl","hook":"Despicable deeds deserve to be looked DOWN upon (de- means down).","o":"Latin"},{"w":"hospitable","def":"favorable to life and growth","ex":"Soil sufficiently hospitable for forest growth.","say":"hah-SPIH-tuh-buhl","hook":"A HOSPITABLE host is comfortABLE making guests feel welcome — HOSPIT+ABLE opens the door!","o":"Latin"},{"w":"formidable","def":"extremely impressive in strength or excellence","ex":"The chess champion was a formidable opponent, but our shy classmate bravely challenged her anyway.","say":"FAW-rmuh-duh-buhl","hook":"A FORMIDABLE foe makes you FORM an ID of the ABLE enemy — spell it with ABLE at the end.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"conservatory","def":"the faculty and students of a school specializing in one of the fine arts","ex":"She practiced violin eight hours a day to earn her place at the prestigious conservatory.","say":"kuh-NSUR-vuh-taw-ree","hook":"A conservaTORY is a place where music is kept — think of a music dORmitORY where TORY rhymes with glory.","o":"French"},{"w":"exemplary","def":"worthy of imitation","ex":"Maya received the citizenship award for her exemplary conduct throughout the school year, always helping classmates, volunteering in the community, and treating every person she met with genuine kindness and deep respect.","say":"ih-GZEH-mpler-ee","hook":"EXEMPLARY behavior is worth an A — and there IS an A near the end: -ARY, not -ory.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"capillary","def":"a very thin tube that pulls liquid upward through narrow spaces","ex":"During the biology lab, students used a tiny capillary tube to observe how water crept upward against gravity — a process called capillary action.","say":"KA-puh-leh-ree","hook":"A capillary is as thin as a HAIR — the root capill means HAIR in Latin.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"corollary","def":"a practical consequence that follows naturally","ex":"A corollary of studying harder each night was that her test scores improved dramatically by the end of the semester.","say":"KAW-ruh-leh-ree","hook":"A COROLLARy follows like a smaller crown (corolla) after the main proof","o":"Latin"},{"w":"itinerary","def":"an established line of travel or access","ex":"Before the trip, Mom printed out a detailed itinerary so we would know exactly which cities to visit each day.","say":"eye-TIH-ner-eh-ree","hook":"Your ITINERARY maps your ITER — the Latin road — keep both I's in place.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"secretary","def":"a person who is head of an administrative department of government","ex":"The secretary of state traveled overseas to negotiate an important peace agreement between two nations.","say":"SEH-kruh-teh-ree","hook":"A SECRETary keeps your SECRETs—the word SECRET is hiding right at the start.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"monetary","def":"relating to or involving money","ex":"The government introduced a new monetary policy designed to control inflation and keep the cost of everyday goods like bread, fuel, and clothing affordable for ordinary working families.","say":"MAH-nuh-teh-ree","hook":"MONETARy: think of a MONET painting — it's worth lots of MONEY.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"arbitrary","def":"based on or subject to individual discretion or preference or sometimes impulse or caprice","ex":"The students complained that the teacher's grading system seemed arbitrary, since similar essays received wildly different scores.","say":"AH-rbuh-treh-ree","hook":"ARBITRary — an ARBITRATOR who rules by whim, not law","o":"Latin"},{"w":"momentary","def":"lasting for a markedly brief time","ex":"The momentary flash of lightning lit up the entire night sky so brilliantly that everyone standing on the porch gasped in surprise before darkness returned.","say":"MOH-muh-nteh-ree","hook":"A MOMENTARY thing relates to a MOMENT — keep the -ARY ending like 'temporary.'","o":"Latin"},{"w":"preferable","def":"more desirable than another","ex":"Coffee is preferable to tea.","say":"PREH-fer-uh-buhl","hook":"PREFERABLE has only ONE R — you PREFER one option, so keep just one R in the middle.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"transferable","def":"capable of being moved or conveyed from one place to another","ex":"The career counselor explained that the communication skills students develop in debate class are highly transferable and will serve them well in almost any profession they choose.","say":"tra-NSFUR-uh-buhl","hook":"TransferABLE has one r — this is the preferred spelling; you ABLE-y carry it with just one r.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"referable","def":"capable of being assigned or credited to","ex":"The scientist concluded that the unusual symptoms were referable to a rare mineral deficiency.","say":"REF-ur-uh-bul","hook":"REFERable has only one R after REFER — it's ABLE to be referred, no need to double up yet.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"commendable","def":"worthy of high praise","ex":"Her commendable honesty impressed the judges as much as her clever project did.","say":"kuh-MEH-nduh-buhl","hook":"It's commendABLE — you're ABLE to recommend it, so end with -able.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"remediable","def":"Able to be fixed, cured, or made right","ex":"The coach said my slow start was remediable with a few extra drills.","say":"rih-MEE-dee-uh-buhl","hook":"REMEDIable holds REMEDY — if there's a remedy, it can be fixed.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"irreparable","def":"impossible to repair, rectify, or amend","ex":"When the ancient manuscript was dropped into the flooded river, scholars feared that the damage to its delicate pages would be irreparable, erasing centuries of recorded history forever.","say":"ih-REH-per-uh-buhl","hook":"There's no 'air' in irreparable — the damage is done and you can't breathe life back into it.","o":"Latin"}]},{"title":"The Origin Tree — one sound, ranked spellings","category":"Championship Procedure","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"Knowing eight loanword groups is not the same as being able to use them. The usable form of that knowledge is the inverse lookup: given a sound you have just heard, which spellings are candidates, and in what order should you try them given the origin. This is why language of origin is the most valuable question at the microphone.","method":"<div class='trick'><b>HEARD /sh/</b></div>\n<b>English.</b> sh — shell, shawl\n<b>French.</b> ch — chef, chandelier, machine\n<b>German.</b> sch — schadenfreude, schnitzel\n<b>Italian.</b> sci — prosciutto, sciatica\n<b>Latin.</b> ti / ci — nation, precious\n<b>Ask.</b> the origin, and the list collapses to one","cards":[{"title":"Sounds that fan out","body":"A handful of sounds carry almost all the risk. /f/ splits into f, ph and gh. /k/ before a front vowel splits into k, c, ch and qu. /sh/ splits five ways. Long /ee/ at word end splits into i, y, ie, is and ee. Learn these five fans cold and you have covered most of what a hard word can throw at you."},{"title":"Origin defaults","body":"Each origin has a default that is right far more often than not. Greek: ph for /f/, y for short /i/, ch for hard /k/, rh at the start. French: silent final consonant, ou for /oo/, eau for /oh/. Italian: every vowel sounded, doubled consonants, final vowel. German: sch, and compounding rather than suffixing."},{"title":"Asking in the right order","body":"If the word sounds Greek, ask the origin first and the root second, because the root will tell you the internal vowels. If it sounds French, ask the origin then the part of speech, because that settles the ending. If it sounds like it could be a homophone, ask for the definition before anything else."},{"title":"When the tree gives two answers","body":"Sometimes two branches survive. That is when alternate pronunciations earn their keep: a second pronunciation often stresses a different syllable and exposes a vowel the first one hid. Ask for it. It is the least used of the permitted questions and one of the most informative."}],"words":[{"w":"chef","def":"a professional cook","ex":"The talented chef created a magnificent five-course dinner that left every guest at the table speechless.","say":"SHEHF","hook":"A CHEF wears a tall hat on his HEAD — the French word for head is CHEF, spelled C-H-E-F, not SH.","o":"French"},{"w":"chorus","def":"any utterance produced simultaneously by a group","ex":"When the teacher announced that school would be cancelled due to the snowstorm, a joyful chorus of cheers erupted from every classroom simultaneously, echoing all the way down the long hallway.","say":"KAW-ruhs","hook":"","o":"Latin"},{"w":"chaos","def":"a state of extreme confusion and disorder","ex":"When the fire alarm went off during lunch, the cafeteria erupted into complete chaos as students scrambled for the exits.","say":"KAY-ahs","hook":"","o":"Greek"},{"w":"schnitzel","def":"deep-fried breaded veal cutlets","ex":"During our class's cultural food day, Maya brought a platter of golden schnitzel that disappeared within minutes.","say":"SHNIT-sul","hook":"","o":"German"},{"w":"prosciutto","def":"Italian salt-cured ham usually sliced paper thin","ex":"The chef draped thin slices of prosciutto over the ripe melon for a classic Italian appetizer.","say":"pruh-SHOO-toh","hook":"","o":"Italian"},{"w":"machine","def":"any mechanical or electrical device that transmits or modifies energy to perform or assist in the performance of human tasks","ex":"The boxer was a magnificent fighting machine.","say":"muh-SHEEN","hook":"","o":"French"},{"w":"charade","def":"a composition that imitates or misrepresents somebody's style, usually in a humorous way","ex":"Everyone could see the politician's apology was just a charade meant to distract voters from the real issue.","say":"sher-AYD","hook":"","o":"French"},{"w":"chameleon","def":"a lizard that can as protection change color","ex":"We watched in amazement as the chameleon shifted from bright green to sandy brown the moment it moved onto the rock.","say":"ch-uh-m-EH-l-ee-uh-n","hook":"The CHAMELEON hides its 'h' just like it hides its color—spell it with 'ch' not just 'c'.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"chiaroscuro","def":"the artistic use of strong contrast between light and dark","ex":"Standing before the Caravaggio painting, Lena finally understood why her art teacher kept talking about chiaroscuro.","say":"kee-ahr-uh-SKYOOR-oh","hook":"CHIARO means light and OSCURO means dark — the two words share the O where they meet","o":"Italian"},{"w":"cortege","def":"a funeral procession","ex":"The solemn cortege moved slowly through the village streets as townspeople bowed their heads in respect.","say":"kaw-RTEHZH","hook":"","o":"French"},{"w":"bourgeois","def":"a capitalist who engages in industrial commercial enterprise","ex":"The novel described a comfortable bourgeois family who owned a busy little shop in town.","say":"b-uh-r-zh-w-AH","hook":"BOURGEOIS has 'bourg' like 'borough'—a person from the borough or town","o":"English"},{"w":"silhouette","def":"a two-dimensional representation of the outline of an object","ex":"As the sun set behind the mountains, the silhouette of the ancient castle appeared dramatically against the glowing orange sky.","say":"s-ih-l-uh-w-EH-t","hook":"silHOUette — the HOUSE makes a silhouette against the sky; remember -ETTE at the end (double T, final E).","o":"French"},{"w":"rendezvous","def":"a meeting planned at a certain time and place","ex":"He was waiting for them at the rendezvous.","say":"RAH-ndih-voo","hook":"RENDEZVOUS ends in -vous — you (vous) must show up at the meeting!","o":"French"},{"w":"connoisseur","def":"a person competent to pass critical judgment","ex":"The art museum hired a connoisseur of Renaissance paintings to authenticate the newly discovered canvas.","say":"k-ah-n-uh-s-UR","hook":"A connoisseur KNOWS their stuff — double n, double s","o":"French"},{"w":"entrepreneur","def":"a person who organizes or manages an enterprise","ex":"At just fourteen, the young entrepreneur launched her own custom jewelry business and sold her designs online.","say":"ah-n-t-r-uh-p-r-uh-n-UR","hook":"Remember the unusual 'eur' ending — the French entrepreneur ends like 'connoisseur'","o":"French"},{"w":"liaison","def":"the contact maintained between groups to insure concerted action","ex":"He provided a liaison with the guerrillas.","say":"l-ee-AY-z-ah-n","hook":"A LIAISON keeps two sides bound — picture the AI in the middle holding them together.","o":"French"},{"w":"camaraderie","def":"is good fellowship","ex":"After months of training together, the team had developed a genuine camaraderie that made them nearly unbeatable.","say":"k-ah-m-ur-AH-d-ur-ee","hook":"CAMARADerie — spot CAMARAD inside: good fellowship starts with a CAMERA snapping friends together.","o":"English"},{"w":"chauffeur","def":"a person employed to drive an automobile","ex":"The chauffeur held the door open as the ambassador stepped out of the sleek black limousine.","say":"sh-oh-f-UR","hook":"A CHAUFFEUR has a silent H and ends in -EUR — think 'chauf' like 'show' plus fancy French -EUR.","o":"French"},{"w":"denouement","def":"the final outcome or resolution of a complex sequence of events, especially in a story","ex":"In the thrilling denouement of the mystery novel, the detective revealed that the butler had hidden the stolen jewels in the clock.","say":"d-ay-n-oo-m-AH-n","hook":"The dénouement UNKNOTs the story — dé(undo)+noue(knot)","o":"Latin"},{"w":"pirouette","def":"a whirling about on one foot as in ballet dancing","ex":"The ballerina completed a flawless pirouette at center stage, spinning so fast she seemed to blur.","say":"p-ih-r-oo-EH","hook":"PIROUETTE has FOUR vowels in a row (ouet) — the dancer spins four times before sticking the landing.","o":"French"},{"w":"gnocchi","def":"(Italian) a small dumpling made of potato or flour or semolina that is boiled or baked and is usually served with a sauce or with grated cheese","ex":"The chef prepared a rich bowl of gnocchi topped with a creamy tomato basil sauce.","say":"NOH-kee","hook":"","o":"Italian"},{"w":"zucchini","def":"a variety of summer squash","ex":"She sliced fresh zucchini from her garden and added it to the stir-fry for extra color and crunch.","say":"z-oo-k-EE-n-ee","hook":"ZUC-CHINI: double C and double I — it's an Italian word so it dresses up with extra letters!","o":"Italian"},{"w":"fettuccine","def":"pasta in flat strips wider than linguine","ex":"She ordered the fettuccine Alfredo, twirling the wide, silky noodles around her fork.","say":"feh-tuh-CHEE-nee","hook":"","o":"Italian"},{"w":"maraschino","def":"distilled from fermented juice of bitter wild marasca cherries","ex":"She topped her ice cream sundae with a bright red maraschino cherry.","say":"ma-ruh-SKEE-noh","hook":"","o":"Italian"},{"w":"pizzicato","def":"playing a stringed instrument by plucking the strings instead of using a bow","ex":"The violinists switched to pizzicato, plucking their strings to create a light, bouncy effect.","say":"pit-sih-KAH-toh","hook":"","o":"Italian"},{"w":"obbligato","def":"a persistent but subordinate motif","ex":"The violin played a delicate obbligato beneath the soprano's soaring melody throughout the aria.","say":"ob-lih-GAH-toh","hook":"","o":"Italian"},{"w":"sforzando","def":"an accented chord","ex":"The pianist struck a sforzando on the final beat that made the audience jump in their seats.","say":"sfort-SAHN-doh","hook":"SFORZANDO: FORCE it—sforZANDO contains FORCE, which means play it HARD","o":"Italian"}]},{"title":"At the Microphone — what each question rules out","category":"Championship Procedure","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"You may ask for the language of origin, the definition, the part of speech, alternate pronunciations, and use in a sentence. Each one eliminates a different class of spelling, so the order you ask in should depend on the ambiguity you are actually facing rather than on habit.","method":"<div class='trick'><b>THE FIVE QUESTIONS</b></div>\n<b>Origin.</b> fixes which orthography applies\n<b>Root.</b> fixes internal vowels and doubled letters\n<b>Definition.</b> separates homophones\n<b>Part of speech.</b> settles -ance / -ant and -ence / -ent\n<b>Alternate pronunciation.</b> can move the stress and expose a schwa\n<b>Sentence.</b> confirms sense when the definition was abstract","cards":[{"title":"Origin first, almost always","body":"Origin is the highest-information question because it selects the whole spelling system, not one letter. Every other question operates inside the system that origin picks. The exception is a suspected homophone, where the definition has to come first or you will spell the wrong word perfectly."},{"title":"Part of speech is underrated","body":"English distinguishes noun and adjective endings that sound identical. -ance and -ence, -ant and -ent, -cy and -sy, -ise and -ice. Knowing whether you are spelling a thing or a description of a thing frequently settles the last letter, and it is a question almost nobody asks."},{"title":"Use your time deliberately","body":"Asking questions is not stalling; it is the mechanism the rules give you for gathering evidence. But ask with a purpose. Decide what you do not know, ask the question that resolves it, and spell. Asking all five in sequence signals that you have no hypothesis, and burns the clock you may need."},{"title":"Spelling out loud","body":"Say the word, spell at a steady rhythm, say it again. The rhythm matters more at this level than it does at a school bee: it stops you compressing a doubled letter, and it gives you a beat in which to catch an error while a correction is still allowed. A rushed string of letters cannot be self-audited."}],"words":[{"w":"principal","def":"the original amount of a debt on which interest is calculated","ex":"She sent unruly pupils to see the principal.","say":"PRIH-nsuh-puhl","hook":"Your school PRINCIPAL is your PAL — principAL ends in A-L like PAL.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"principle","def":"a basic generalization that is accepted as true and that can be used as a basis for reasoning or conduct","ex":"Their principles of composition characterized all their works.","say":"PRIH-nsuh-puhl","hook":"A PRINCIPLE is a RULE — principLE ends in L-E like RULE reversed... and rules end things.","o":"French"},{"w":"palate","def":"the roof of the mouth","ex":"The chef designed the menu to appeal to a wide range of tastes and satisfy every palate.","say":"p-A-l-uh-t","hook":"Your PALATE tastes with one L — one taste at a time.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"palette","def":"a board with a thumb hole used by painters to mix colors","ex":"The artist squeezed bright colors onto her palette before painting the sunset.","say":"p-A-l-uh-t","hook":"A PALETTE has one 'l' and ends in -ette — remember: one layer of paint needs one 'l,' then -ette like 'kitchenette.'","o":"French"},{"w":"pallet","def":"the range of colour characteristic of a particular artist or painting or school of art","ex":"The warehouse workers stacked the heavy boxes neatly on a wooden pallet for easy transport.","say":"PA-luht","hook":"","o":"Anglo-Norman"},{"w":"complement","def":"a word or phrase used to complete a grammatical construction","ex":"The bright orange scarf was a perfect complement to her navy blue coat, and the grammar teacher also noted that a predicate adjective functions as a complement to the subject of a sentence.","say":"KAH-mpluh-muhnt","hook":"A complEment makEs things complEte — both have an E.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"compliment","def":"a remark (or act) expressing praise and admiration","ex":"He complimented her on her last physics paper.","say":"KAH-mpluh-mehnt","hook":"A compliment is something nice said to I — remember 'complIment' has an I, because it's about ME feeling good.","o":"French"},{"w":"discreet","def":"marked by prudence or modesty and wise self-restraint","ex":"His trusted discreet aide.","say":"dih-SKREET","hook":"","o":"French"},{"w":"discrete","def":"constituting a separate entity or part","ex":"A government with three discrete divisions.","say":"dih-SKREET","hook":"","o":"French"},{"w":"stationary","def":"standing still","ex":"The car remained stationary with the engine running.","say":"STAY-shuh-neh-ree","hook":"StationARY means stAying — both have 'A'; you stAy in one plAce.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"stationery","def":"is writing paper","ex":"She chose elegant floral stationery to write her thank-you notes after the party.","say":"s-t-AY-sh-uh-n-eh-r-ee","hook":"StationERy is for lettERs — both have 'ER'; you write a lettER on stationERy.","o":"English"},{"w":"canvas","def":"a heavy, closely woven fabric (used for clothing or chairs or sails or tents)","ex":"The crowded canvas of history.","say":"KA-nvuhs","hook":"","o":"Latin"},{"w":"canvass","def":"is to solicit votes","ex":"Volunteers went door to door to canvass the neighborhood before the school election.","say":"k-A-n-v-uh-s","hook":"CANVASS the voters with double SS — you need two feet to STOMP the pavement soliciting.","o":"English"},{"w":"affect","def":"to influence something or produce a change in it","ex":"Missing sleep can affect how well you do on a test.","say":"uh-FEKT","hook":"To affect something is to act on it and change it.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"effect","def":"A result; a change that something causes.","ex":"Extra sleep before the bee had a great effect on my focus.","say":"ih-FEKT","hook":"Cause and Effect: the effect is what happens BECAUSE of the cause.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"allusion","def":"passing reference or indirect mention","ex":"When the teacher said 'don't be an Ebenezer,' the students recognized the allusion to Scrooge immediately.","say":"uh-LOO-zhuhn","hook":"An allusion is an ILLUSION of meaning — both words share -llusion; double L, one S.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"illusion","def":"an erroneous mental representation","ex":"They have the illusion that I am very wealthy.","say":"ih-LOO-zhuhn","hook":"An ILLUSION tricks you twice — just like those two L's trick you into thinking there's only one.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"elicit","def":"call forth (emotions, feelings, and responses)","ex":"The teacher asked a surprising, thought-provoking question designed to elicit a genuine, emotional response from every student sitting quietly in the classroom.","say":"ih-LIH-siht","hook":"","o":"Latin"},{"w":"illicit","def":"is not legally permitted","ex":"An illicit association with his secretary.","say":"ih-l-IH-s-uh-t","hook":"ILLICIT has two L's — doubly forbidden; find LICIT (permitted) inside and slap IL- in front.","o":"French"},{"w":"eminent","def":"standing above others in quality or position","ex":"The eminent scientist was invited to speak at universities all over the world because of her groundbreaking research.","say":"EH-muh-nuhnt","hook":"","o":"Latin"},{"w":"imminent","def":"close in time; about to occur","ex":"Dark clouds gathered overhead, warning hikers that a thunderstorm was imminent.","say":"IH-muh-nuhnt","hook":"IMMINENt danger is IN MINutes away.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"immanent","def":"of a mental act performed entirely within the mind","ex":"A cognition is an immanent act of mind.","say":"IH-muh-nuhnt","hook":"IMMANENT means dwelling within — a MAN is IN it; IMMINENT means coming soon — very different!","o":"Latin"},{"w":"precede","def":"is to go before","ex":"Stone tools precede bronze tools.","say":"p-r-ih-s-EE-d","hook":"To preCEDE is to go before — remember: cede ends in E not EE.","o":"English"},{"w":"proceed","def":"continue talking;  he continued,","ex":"After the principal finished her morning announcements and the hallways had quieted down, she gave the signal for the science fair participants to proceed with setting up their colorful display boards.","say":"pruh-SEED","hook":"","o":"French"},{"w":"cite","def":"to quote or mention something as proof or an example","ex":"For her report, Zoe had to cite three different books.","say":"SYT","hook":"To cite is to call up a source as your evidence.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"site","def":"The place where something is located, happens, or will be built","ex":"We visited the site where the new school gym will be built.","say":"SYT","hook":"A SITE is a spot — a campsite is the place where you camp.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"sight","def":"an instance of visual perception","ex":"The sight of his wife brought him back to reality.","say":"SEYET","hook":"","o":"Old English"},{"w":"desert","def":"arid land with little or no vegetation","ex":"The mother deserted her children.","say":"DEH-zert","hook":"","o":"French"}]},{"title":"Greek Openings — ps, pn, mn, chth, phth, rh, bd","category":"Advanced Orthography","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"Greek allowed consonant clusters at the start of a word that English does not pronounce. English kept the spelling and dropped the first sound. So an unexplained initial consonant is almost always a Greek fingerprint, and the clusters are a closed set you can memorise outright.","method":"<div class='trick'><b>PNEUMONIA</b></div>\n<b>Heard.</b> noo-MOHN-yuh — starts with an n sound\n<b>Origin.</b> Greek\n<b>Candidates.</b> n, kn, gn, pn\n<b>Greek default.</b> pn — from pneuma = breath\n<b>Spell.</b> P N E U M O N I A\n<b>Family.</b> pneumatic, pneumograph, pneumothorax","cards":[{"title":"The closed set","body":"ps as in psalm and pseudonym. pn as in pneumonia and pneumatic. mn as in mnemonic. chth as in chthonic. phth as in phthisis. rh as in rhetoric and rhinoceros. bd as in bdellium and bdelloid. Also gn in gnomon and pt in ptomaine. That is nearly the whole inventory."},{"title":"Which silent letter, though","body":"Hearing an /n/ at the start leaves you choosing between kn, gn, pn and plain n. Origin decides it: kn and gn are Old English (knife, gnaw), pn is Greek (pneumonia). Hearing an /s/ leaves ps against sc against plain s, and ps is Greek. The silent letter is a label telling you which language you are in."},{"title":"rh and the doubled rr","body":"Greek rho takes an h after it at the start of a word, and when a prefix puts that rho inside a compound it doubles: rhythm but arrhythmia, rhea but diarrhoea, rhage but haemorrhage. If you hear a Greek word with an internal /r/ after a prefix, suspect rrh."},{"title":"Our library holds 170 of these","body":"The core competition library carries 170 words with one of these openings and the full library carries 427, so this is not a curiosity — it is a pattern worth an hour of drill. rhinorrhagia, pneumatocyst, bdelloid and phthisis all sit in the word pool you are already studying."}],"words":[{"w":"vertebrae","def":"are bones composing the spinal column","ex":"The doctor pointed to the X-ray and explained which vertebrae had been affected by the football injury.","say":"v-UR-t-uh-b-r-ay","hook":"VERTEBRAE ends in AE — the fancy Latin plural; think 'All Excellent bones' for AE at the end of vertebrAE.","o":"English"},{"w":"maxillae","def":"the paired upper jaw bones in vertebrates that are fused to the cranium","ex":"The paleontologist carefully brushed away sediment to reveal the perfectly preserved maxillae of the ancient crocodilian.","say":"mak-SIL-ee","hook":"MAXILLae — MAXI (big) + LL jaw; the -ae is the Latin plural","o":"Latin"},{"w":"larvae","def":"the immature free-living form of most invertebrates and amphibians and fish which at hatching from the egg is fundamentally unlike its parent and must metamorphose","ex":"The pond was teeming with frog larvae, all wriggling in the shallow water near the muddy bank.","say":"LAH-rvee","hook":"","o":"Latin"},{"w":"alumnae","def":"a person who has received a degree from a school (high school or college or university)","ex":"The alumnae of the women's college gathered every five years to celebrate their shared memories and achievements.","say":"uh-LUH-mnay","hook":"","o":"Latin"},{"w":"antennae","def":"an electrical device that sends or receives radio or television signals","ex":"The old radio's antennae had to be adjusted to catch the weather station clearly.","say":"a-NTEH-nee","hook":"ANTENNAE ends in -ae, the classy Latin plural — like alumni; bugs deserve their Latin plural too: antennAE.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"lacunae","def":"a blank gap or missing part","ex":"The ancient manuscript was difficult to interpret because of the many lacunae where entire paragraphs had faded away.","say":"luh-KYOO-nee","hook":"","o":"Latin"},{"w":"vortices","def":"the shape of something rotating rapidly","ex":"The science documentary showed dramatic vortices forming in the ocean as two powerful currents collided near the coast.","say":"VOR-tih-seez","hook":"Vortices is the Latin plural of vortex — like indices for index, the -ex becomes -ices.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"auspices","def":"kindly endorsement and guidance","ex":"The science fair was held under the auspices of the local university, which provided equipment and expert judges.","say":"AW-spih-sihz","hook":"AUSPICes — AUS(picious) birds SPICed up Roman fortune-telling","o":"Latin"},{"w":"appendices","def":"supplementary material that is collected and appended at the back of a book","ex":"The science textbook included several appendices with charts, data tables, and reference materials for students.","say":"uh-PEH-ndih-seez","hook":"APPENDICES is the fancy Latin plural — think of ICE at the end, like cold hard Latin freezing the word.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"indices","def":"a numerical scale used to compare variables with one another or with some reference number","ex":"The scientist checked multiple indices to determine how air quality had changed over the decade.","say":"IH-ndih-seez","hook":"","o":"Latin"},{"w":"matrices","def":"(mathematics) a rectangular array of quantities or expressions set out by rows and columns; treated as a single element and manipulated according to rules","ex":"The student filled several pages with matrices, carefully multiplying rows and columns to solve the linear equations.","say":"MAY-trih-sihz","hook":"MATRICES uses the Latin plural — like one vertex, many vertICES — swap X for ICES to go classical.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"codices","def":"an official list of chemicals or medicines etc.","ex":"Scholars gathered to study the ancient codices, hoping to unlock secrets hidden for centuries.","say":"KOH-dih-seez","hook":"","o":"Latin"},{"w":"apices","def":"the highest point (of something)","ex":"","say":"AY-pih-seez","hook":"","o":"Latin"},{"w":"fungi","def":"the taxonomic kingdom including yeast, molds, smuts, mushrooms, and toadstools; distinct from the green plants","ex":"Scientists discovered several new species of fungi thriving deep in the tropical rainforest soil.","say":"FUH-njeye","hook":"","o":"Latin"},{"w":"alumni","def":"a person who has received a degree from a school (high school or college or university)","ex":"Hundreds of alumni gathered on campus for the reunion, sharing memories of their years at the school.","say":"uh-LUH-mneye","hook":"","o":"Latin"},{"w":"stimuli","def":"any stimulating information or event that acts to arouse action","ex":"The psychologist tested how quickly subjects responded to visual stimuli, such as flashing lights and moving shapes.","say":"STIH-myuh-leye","hook":"","o":"Latin"},{"w":"nuclei","def":"a part of the cell containing DNA and RNA and responsible for growth and reproduction","ex":"","say":"NOO-klee-eye","hook":"","o":"Latin"},{"w":"phenomena","def":"any state or process known through the senses rather than by intuition or reasoning","ex":"The northern lights are among the most breathtaking natural phenomena anyone can witness.","say":"fuh-NAH-muh-nuh","hook":"PHENOMENA is already plural — it ends in -A not -AS, just like data and criteria.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"criteria","def":"a basis for comparison; a reference point against which other things can be evaluated","ex":"The judges explained the criteria for the science fair: originality, presentation, and scientific accuracy all counted equally.","say":"kreye-TIH-ree-uh","hook":"CRITERIA is already plural — never say 'criterias'; one CRITERION, many CRITERIA","o":"Greek"},{"w":"stigmata","def":"marks resembling the wounds on the crucified body of Christ","ex":"The medieval painting depicted a saint who bore stigmata on his hands as a sign of deep devotion.","say":"stih-GMAH-tuh","hook":"STIGMATA has the same root as STIGMA—both mean marks that are hard to ignore","o":"Greek"},{"w":"schemata","def":"an internal representation of the world; an organization of concepts and actions that can be revised by new information about the world","ex":"The psychology professor explained that children build mental schemata to make sense of new experiences.","say":"skih-MA-tuh","hook":"SCHEMATA is the fancy Greek plural—think schema plus 'ta' for 'totally academic.'","o":"Greek"},{"w":"dogmata","def":"a religious doctrine that is proclaimed as true without proof","ex":"","say":"DAWG-muh-tuh","hook":"","o":"Greek"},{"w":"miasmata","def":"an unwholesome atmosphere","ex":"Ancient physicians blamed miasmata rising from rotting vegetation for spreading deadly fevers.","say":"my-AZ-muh-tuh","hook":"MIASMATA takes a Greek plural -ATA ending, like STIGMATA — ancient scholars gave ancient smells ancient plurals.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"genera","def":"a general kind of something","ex":"","say":"JEH-ner-uh","hook":"","o":"Latin"},{"w":"corrigenda","def":"a list of printing errors in a book along with their corrections","ex":"The publisher included a page of corrigenda at the front of the textbook to alert students to the misprints found after printing.","say":"kor-ih-JEN-duh","hook":"CORRECT the AGENDA — corrigENDA needs correction","o":"Latin"},{"w":"erratum","def":"a mistake in printed matter resulting from mechanical failures of some kind","ex":"The publisher inserted a slip of paper noting the erratum on page forty-seven, where two paragraphs had been swapped.","say":"ih-RAH-tum","hook":"","o":"Latin"},{"w":"addenda","def":"textual matter that is added onto a publication; usually at the end","ex":"The professor asked students to read the addenda at the back of the textbook before the exam.","say":"uh-DEN-duh","hook":"","o":"Latin"}]},{"title":"Classical Plurals — ae, i, a, ices, mata","category":"Advanced Orthography","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"Words borrowed whole from Latin and Greek often kept their original plural, and the plural ending you hear tells you which declension the singular belonged to, which in turn tells you how to spell the singular. The relationship runs both ways and is worth knowing in both directions.","method":"<div class='trick'><b>VORTICES</b></div>\n<b>Heard.</b> VOR-tih-seez\n<b>Ending.</b> the /seez/ sound points to -ices\n<b>Rule.</b> Latin nouns in -ex or -ix take -ices\n<b>Singular.</b> vortex\n<b>Spell.</b> V O R T I C E S\n<b>Family.</b> index/indices, appendix/appendices, matrix/matrices","cards":[{"title":"The five main patterns","body":"Latin -a becomes -ae: vertebra, vertebrae. Latin -us becomes -i: fungus, fungi. Latin -um becomes -a: datum, data. Latin -ex or -ix becomes -ices: vortex, vortices. Greek -ma becomes -mata: stigma, stigmata. Learn the pair, not the single form."},{"title":"Hearing the plural first","body":"At the microphone you are often given the plural, and the plural is the more informative form. -ae tells you the singular ended in -a. -ices tells you it ended in -ex or -ix, and that a c appears where you might have written an x. -mata tells you Greek, and that the singular ends -ma not -mat."},{"title":"Where English overrode the original","body":"Not every borrowing kept its classical plural, and some now take both. Some words moved fully into English endings, and a few plurals became singulars in the move: agenda and data both began as Latin plurals. When both forms are valid the bee will accept the one it asked for, so listen to the ending you were given."},{"title":"Beyond the classics","body":"French contributes -eaux for singulars in -eau: bureau, bureaux; tableau, tableaux. Italian contributes -i for -o: virtuoso, virtuosi. Hebrew contributes -im: cherub, cherubim; seraph, seraphim. Each is a small closed set and each is worth memorising as a set."}],"words":[{"w":"psalm","def":"A sacred song or poem, especially from the Bible.","ex":"He psalms the works of God.","say":"SAHLM","hook":"","o":"Greek"},{"w":"pseudonym","def":"a fictitious name used when the person performs a particular social role","ex":"The famous author used a pseudonym so readers wouldn't know her real identity.","say":"SOO-duh-nihm","hook":"A PSEUDONYM is a FALSE NAME — PSEUDO+NYM; think 'Sue Doh Nim' uses a fake name to hide from Sue.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"psaltery","def":"an ancient stringed instrument similar to the lyre or zither but having a trapezoidal sounding board under the strings","ex":"The musician plucked the strings of a psaltery to recreate the haunting sounds of medieval court music.","say":"SAL-tuh-ree","hook":"","o":"Greek"},{"w":"psoriasis","def":"a chronic skin disease characterized by dry red patches covered with scales; occurs especially on the scalp and ears and genitalia and the skin over bony prominences","ex":"The dermatologist explained that psoriasis could be managed with medicated creams and lifestyle changes, though there was currently no cure.","say":"s-ur-AY-uh-s-uh-s","hook":"PSORIASIS starts with silent PS — like PSYCHOLOGY and PSYCHIC, the P is quiet","o":"Greek"},{"w":"pneumonia","def":"is inflammation of the lungs with congestion","ex":"After being caught in the freezing rain for hours, she developed pneumonia and had to be hospitalized.","say":"n-oo-m-OH-n-y-uh","hook":"PNEUMonia attacks the lungs — the silent P-N-E-U-M is the hardest part; picture a PNEUMATIC pump filling diseased lungs.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"pneumatic","def":"of or relating to or using air (or a similar gas)","ex":"The mechanic used a powerful pneumatic drill to remove the rusted bolts from the engine, the compressed air driving the tool with so much force that each bolt came loose within seconds.","say":"noo-MA-tihk","hook":"PNEUMatic has a silent P — like PNEUMONIA, both start with PNEUM meaning breath of air.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"pneumothorax","def":"abnormal presence of air in the pleural cavity resulting in the collapse of the lung; may be spontaneous (due to injury to the chest) or induced (as a treatment for tuberculosis)","ex":"The collapsed lung caused by pneumothorax required emergency treatment in the hospital.","say":"noo-moh-THOR-aks","hook":"PNEUMO (air) + THORAX (chest) — unwanted AIR in the CHEST: PNEUMOTHORAX.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"mnemonic","def":"is assisting or intended to assist the memory","ex":"Our science teacher taught us a clever mnemonic to remember the order of the planets, and within just ten minutes the entire class could recite them perfectly from Mercury all the way out to Neptune.","say":"n-ih-m-AH-n-ih-k","hook":"Mnemonic starts with a silent M — M is for Memory, hiding quietly at the front.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"mnemosyne","def":"(Greek mythology) the Titaness who was goddess of memory; mother of the Muses","ex":"The ancient Greeks believed that Mnemosyne granted poets the ability to recall all the events of the past and weave them into song.","say":"neh-MOS-ih-nee","hook":"Mnemosyne: 'M-NEMO swims through your mind' — the silent M dives in first, then NEE-mo-SY-nee flows out.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"chthonic","def":"dwelling beneath the surface of the earth","ex":"The archaeologist believed the carved stone altar was dedicated to a chthonic deity, one of the dark underground gods worshipped by the ancient civilization she was studying.","say":"THON-ik","hook":"The silent 'ch' hides underground, just like chthonic creatures do — the word itself burrows into the earth.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"phthisis","def":"involving the lungs with progressive wasting of the body","ex":"Before antibiotics were available, phthisis ravaged communities and was often called consumption by those who feared it.","say":"TY-sis","hook":"","o":"Latin"},{"w":"rhetoric","def":"is bombast or the undue use of exaggeration or display","ex":"The speaker's fiery rhetoric sounded grand, but it contained very few actual facts.","say":"r-EH-t-ur-ih-k","hook":"RHETORIC has RHETOR at its heart — a RHETOR speaks, so keep the RH and the hidden H inside.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"rhinoceros","def":"massive powerful herbivorous odd-toed ungulate of southeast Asia and Africa having very thick skin and one or two horns on the snout","ex":"The massive rhinoceros charged across the dusty plain, sending smaller animals scattering in every direction.","say":"reye-NAH-ser-uhs","hook":"A rhinoCERos has a nose-HORN — no 'u' sneaking into 'ceros', just like no extra horn on its nose.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"rhapsody","def":"an epic poem adapted for recitation","ex":"The composer wrote a rhapsody that swept the audience away with its wild, emotional melodies.","say":"RA-psuh-dee","hook":"A RHAPSODY stitches epic ODE sections together — RH-AP-SO-DY, four clear syllables.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"rhododendron","def":"An evergreen shrub with leathery leaves and showy clusters of bell-shaped flowers.","ex":"The hillside was breathtaking in spring when the purple rhododendron bushes burst into bloom along every trail.","say":"roh-duh-DEH-ndruhn","hook":"RHODO (rose) + DENDRON (tree) = 'rose tree.' Remember dendrology is the study of trees.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"bdellium","def":"aromatic gum resin; similar to myrrh","ex":"The ancient traders prized bdellium for its rich fragrance used in sacred rituals.","say":"DEL-ee-um","hook":"The silent B leads the word like a quiet priest guarding a fragrant resin — B-DELL-ium, don't drop that B!","o":"Latin"},{"w":"bdelloid","def":"Relating to or resembling a leech; belonging to the class Bdelloidea, a group of microscopic aquatic invertebrates.","ex":"Under the microscope, the bdelloid rotifers writhed through the pond water with a creeping motion resembling tiny leeches.","say":"DEL-oyd","hook":"The silent B is tricky — BDELLOID starts like BDELL (leech in Greek), the B is silent","o":"Modern Greek"},{"w":"gnomon","def":"indicator provided by the stationary arm whose shadow indicates the time on the sundial","ex":"At noon the gnomon cast almost no shadow at all, indicating the sun was directly overhead in the midsummer sky.","say":"NOH-mon","hook":"The G is silent — the gnomon KNOWS the time silently from the sun's shadow","o":"Latin"},{"w":"gnostic","def":"an advocate of Gnosticism","ex":"The professor explained that a gnostic believes salvation comes through secret spiritual knowledge rather than faith alone, and that these ancient thinkers often clashed sharply with the mainstream religious authorities of their time.","say":"NOS-tik","hook":"","o":"Greek"},{"w":"ptomaine","def":"any of various amines (such as putrescine or cadaverine) formed by the action of putrefactive bacteria","ex":"Before modern food science, people blamed ptomaine for most cases of food poisoning traced to spoiled meat.","say":"TOH-mayn","hook":"","o":"French"},{"w":"pterodactyl","def":"extinct flying reptile","ex":"The museum exhibit featured a full-scale model of a pterodactyl swooping over a prehistoric swamp.","say":"teh-ruh-DAK-til","hook":"Remember the silent P: 'Pterodactyl' — Please Try Every Route Over Dangerous Ancient Cliffs To Your Lair","o":"Greek"},{"w":"xylophone","def":"a percussion instrument with wooden bars tuned to produce a chromatic scale and with resonators; played with small mallets","ex":"The kindergartner proudly played a simple tune on the colorful xylophone during the school recital.","say":"ZEYE-luh-fohn","hook":"A XYLOPHONE makes PHONe sounds from XYLO (wooden) bars — always start with X, not Z!","o":"Greek"},{"w":"xenophobia","def":"a fear of foreigners or strangers","ex":"The community worked hard to overcome xenophobia by hosting cultural festivals that celebrated their diverse neighbors.","say":"zeh-nuh-FOH-bee-uh","hook":"XENO means stranger, PHOBIA means fear — xenophobia is fear of strangers, spelled just as it sounds: x-e-n-o-p-h-o-b-i-a.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"psychology","def":"the science of mental life","ex":"Her fascination with why people behave the way they do led her to pursue a career in psychology.","say":"seye-KAH-luh-jee","hook":"PSYCHOlogy — the PSYCHO at the start is the mind gone wild; don't forget the silent P.","o":"French"},{"w":"psalter","def":"a collection of Psalms for liturgical use","ex":"The monk carefully illuminated each page of the psalter with gold leaf and vivid blue ink.","say":"SAW-lter","hook":"","o":"Greek"},{"w":"rheumatism","def":"a disorder of the extremities or back characterized by stiffness","ex":"The old carpenter's rheumatism made it painful to grip his tools on cold winter mornings.","say":"r-OO-m-uh-t-ih-z-uh-m","hook":"RHEUMATISM was thought to be bad humors FLOWING into joints — RHEUM + AT + ISM.","o":"English"},{"w":"rhizome","def":"a horizontal plant stem with shoots above and roots below serving as a reproductive structure","ex":"The gardener discovered that the grass had spread so widely because its rhizome crept beneath the entire lawn.","say":"REYE-zohm","hook":"RHIZOME — RHI like RHINO, but this underground root ZOOMs along","o":"Greek"}]},{"title":"-ise / -ize / -yse — the transatlantic split","category":"Advanced Orthography","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"The largest single spelling split in English. Most of these verbs come from Greek -izein and are spelled -ize in American usage; British publishing respells almost all of them -ise. That would be simple, except a third group is spelled -ise on both sides of the Atlantic because the s is part of the root and never was a Greek suffix at all. Those are the ones that catch people: you cannot choose by accent, you have to know whether a suffix is present.","method":"<div class='trick'>ADVERTISE</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> AD-ver-tyz — ends in the /ize/ sound\n<b>Origin.</b> French avertir, not Greek -izein\n<b>Test.</b> Strip the ending — is there a word left? ADVERT is a clipping of this, not its root\n<b>Verdict.</b> no Greek suffix, so no z is available\n<b>Spell.</b> A D V E R T I S E\n<b>Family.</b> advertisement, advertiser — all s","cards":[{"title":"The default and the exception","body":"In American spelling the suffix is -ize: patronize, canonize, itemize, eulogize, immunize. The exceptions are the words where -ise is not a suffix at all — advertise, surprise, compromise, exercise, supervise, improvise, despise, disguise, revise. There are only about a dozen and they are worth memorising outright, because for those, -ize is simply wrong everywhere."},{"title":"What the pronouncer will tell you","body":"If a bee accepts both spellings the pronouncer will usually say so, or the word will be flagged as having a variant. If no variant is offered, the dictionary of record decides. Merriam-Webster, the Scripps dictionary, lists -ize first and -ise as a chiefly British variant. So on an American stage -ize is the safer default whenever the ending really is a suffix."},{"title":"-yse is the same story one letter over","body":"Analyse, paralyse, catalyse and hydrolyse take -yse in British spelling and -yze in American. Here the root is Greek lusis, meaning a loosening, so there is no root-s exception to worry about — it is a clean British-American pair. If you can spell one of the four you can spell all of them."},{"title":"Why the -isation forms follow","body":"Whatever the verb does, the noun copies: civilize gives civilization, civilise gives civilisation. Mixed spellings like civilisization are never right. Decide the verb once and the whole family falls into line — which is why this pattern is worth an hour: our library carries 755 words ending this way in the core list and 1,614 in the full library."}],"words":[{"w":"patronize","def":"to adopt an air of superiority and condescension toward someone.","ex":"We patronize this store.","say":"PAY-truh-neyez","hook":"Greek -izein gave us -ize. British house style respells nearly all of them -ise. American bees spell -ize unless the s belongs to the root.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"gorgonize","def":"to paralyse someone with a terrifying stare","ex":"One look from the headmaster could gorgonize a whole corridor.","say":"GOR-guh-nyz","hook":"Greek -izein gave us -ize. British house style respells nearly all of them -ise. American bees spell -ize unless the s belongs to the root.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"bowdlerize","def":"to cut passages thought improper out of a book or play","ex":"Editors used to bowdlerize the play before schoolchildren were allowed to read it.","say":"BOHD-luh-ryz","hook":"Greek -izein gave us -ize. British house style respells nearly all of them -ise. American bees spell -ize unless the s belongs to the root.","o":"English"},{"w":"canonize","def":"declare (a dead person) to be a saint","ex":"After he was shown to have performed a miracle, the priest was canonized.","say":"KA-nuh-neyez","hook":"Greek -izein gave us -ize. British house style respells nearly all of them -ise. American bees spell -ize unless the s belongs to the root.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"eulogize","def":"praise formally and eloquently","ex":"The dead woman was eulogized at the funeral.","say":"YOO-luh-jeyez","hook":"Greek -izein gave us -ize. British house style respells nearly all of them -ise. American bees spell -ize unless the s belongs to the root.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"immunize","def":"law: grant immunity from prosecution","ex":"The nurse came to immunize the students against the flu before winter.","say":"IH-myoo-neyez","hook":"Greek -izein gave us -ize. British house style respells nearly all of them -ise. American bees spell -ize unless the s belongs to the root.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"itemize","def":"specify individually","ex":"Please itemize everything you packed so we don't forget anything for the trip.","say":"EYE-tuh-meyez","hook":"Greek -izein gave us -ize. British house style respells nearly all of them -ise. American bees spell -ize unless the s belongs to the root.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"lionize","def":"assign great social importance to; treat as a celebrity","ex":"After scoring the winning goal in the championship, Marcus was lionized by his teammates and the entire school.","say":"LEYE-uh-neyez","hook":"Greek -izein gave us -ize. British house style respells nearly all of them -ise. American bees spell -ize unless the s belongs to the root.","o":"French"},{"w":"amortize","def":"liquidate gradually","ex":"The bank advised the young couple to amortize their home loan over thirty years to keep their monthly payments manageable.","say":"A-mer-teyez","hook":"Greek -izein gave us -ize. British house style respells nearly all of them -ise. American bees spell -ize unless the s belongs to the root.","o":"French"},{"w":"deputize","def":"act as a substitute","ex":"The sheriff decided to deputize a brave volunteer to help guard the quiet town.","say":"DEH-pyuh-teyez","hook":"Greek -izein gave us -ize. British house style respells nearly all of them -ise. American bees spell -ize unless the s belongs to the root.","o":"French"},{"w":"advertise","def":"call attention to","ex":"Please don't advertise the fact that he has AIDS.","say":"A-dver-teyez","hook":"Greek -izein gave us -ize. British house style respells nearly all of them -ise. American bees spell -ize unless the s belongs to the root.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"surprise","def":"the astonishment you feel when something totally unexpected happens to you","ex":"The news really surprised me.","say":"ser-PREYEZ","hook":"Greek -izein gave us -ize. British house style respells nearly all of them -ise. American bees spell -ize unless the s belongs to the root.","o":"French"},{"w":"compromise","def":"a middle way between two extremes","ex":"The newly elected congressmen rejected a compromise because they considered it `business as usual'.","say":"KAH-mpruh-meyez","hook":"Greek -izein gave us -ize. British house style respells nearly all of them -ise. American bees spell -ize unless the s belongs to the root.","o":"French"},{"w":"exercise","def":"the activity of exerting your muscles in various ways to keep fit","ex":"The physical education teacher designed a fun exercise routine that included jumping jacks, push-ups, and relay races around the gym.","say":"EH-kser-seyez","hook":"Greek -izein gave us -ize. British house style respells nearly all of them -ise. American bees spell -ize unless the s belongs to the root.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"supervise","def":"watch and direct","ex":"Two teachers will supervise the students during the exciting science fair.","say":"SOO-per-veyez","hook":"Greek -izein gave us -ize. British house style respells nearly all of them -ise. American bees spell -ize unless the s belongs to the root.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"improvise","def":"perform without preparation","ex":"When she forgot her lines, she had to improvise and make up new ones.","say":"IH-mpruh-veyez","hook":"Greek -izein gave us -ize. British house style respells nearly all of them -ise. American bees spell -ize unless the s belongs to the root.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"catalyse","def":"change by catalysis or cause to catalyze","ex":"Scientists discovered that the new compound could catalyse the reaction at a much lower temperature than before.","say":"KAT-uh-lyz","hook":"Greek -izein gave us -ize. British house style respells nearly all of them -ise. American bees spell -ize unless the s belongs to the root.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"catalyze","def":"To spark or speed up a change or reaction.","ex":"The inspiring speech seemed to catalyze a wave of community action across the city.","say":"KA-tuh-leyez","hook":"Greek -izein gave us -ize. British house style respells nearly all of them -ise. American bees spell -ize unless the s belongs to the root.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"hydrolyze","def":"undergo hydrolysis; decompose by reacting with water","ex":"Adding warm water caused the starch to hydrolyze rapidly into smaller sugar molecules.","say":"HEYE-druh-leyez","hook":"Greek -izein gave us -ize. British house style respells nearly all of them -ise. American bees spell -ize unless the s belongs to the root.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"civilize","def":"teach or refine to be discriminative in taste or judgment","ex":"Good books can civilize our tastes, teaching us to enjoy finer music and art.","say":"SIH-vuh-leyez","hook":"Greek -izein gave us -ize. British house style respells nearly all of them -ise. American bees spell -ize unless the s belongs to the root.","o":"French"}]},{"title":"The /shəs/ endings — ceous, cious, tious, scious, xious","category":"Advanced Orthography","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"Five different spellings share one ending sound, roughly shus. Guessing between them is a coin flip unless you use the word's family. The related noun almost always exposes the answer: a noun in -ce points to -cious, a noun in -tion points to -tious, an x in the root points to -xious, and -ceous is the botanical and material ending meaning made of or resembling.","method":"<div class='trick'>ADVENTITIOUS</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> ad-ven-TISH-us\n<b>Candidates.</b> ceous, cious, tious, scious, xious\n<b>Family.</b> adventitious relates to advent and addition — a -tion noun\n<b>Rule.</b> -tion in the family means the ending is -tious\n<b>Spell.</b> A D V E N T I T I O U S\n<b>Family.</b> adventitiously, adventitiousness","cards":[{"title":"Let the noun choose","body":"Malice gives malicious. Space gives spacious. Grace gives gracious. Avarice gives avaricious. When the family noun ends in -ce, the adjective ends -cious. Now the other way: ambition gives ambitious, caution gives cautious, superstition gives superstitious, nutrition gives nutritious. A -tion noun means -tious. This one test settles most of the pattern."},{"title":"-ceous is the material ending","body":"Latin -aceus meant made of or having the nature of, and English kept it for substances and plant families. Sebaceous, cetaceous, cretaceous, herbaceous, saponaceous, gallinaceous, foliaceous, farinaceous. If the word describes what something is made of, or names a botanical or zoological family, reach for -ceous first."},{"title":"The two tiny groups","body":"-scious appears in only a handful of words, all built on Latin scire, to know: conscious, unconscious, subconscious, prescious. -xious is even smaller: anxious, noxious, obnoxious. Both are closed sets. If the root is about knowing, it is -scious; if you can hear a k-s in the root, it is -xious."},{"title":"When there is no helpful relative","body":"Some words give you nothing to work with — meretricious, adscititious, tralatitious, pervicacious, obreptitious. For these, origin is the tiebreak: Latin verbs in -tus and -tio push toward -tious, Latin nouns in -ax and -acis push toward -acious. Our core library holds 140 of these words and the full library 235, so drilling the family test pays for itself quickly."}],"words":[{"w":"adventitious","def":"associated by chance and not an integral part; - Frederick W. Robertson","ex":"By adventitious luck, she found her lost bracelet gleaming in the sand at the beach.","say":"ad-ven-TISH-us","hook":"Five spellings, one sound. The noun you can build from it usually names the ending: -ce → cious, -tion → tious, -x → xious, plant or substance → ceous.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"meretricious","def":"attractive in a flashy, showy way but having no real value","ex":"The judge warned that a meretricious science-fair display, all glitter and no data, would never win.","say":"mer-uh-TRISH-us","hook":"Five spellings, one sound. The noun you can build from it usually names the ending: -ce → cious, -tion → tious, -x → xious, plant or substance → ceous.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"adscititious","def":"added or derived from something outside; not inherent","ex":"The editor argued that the lengthy footnotes were adscititious and detracted from the flow of the main argument.","say":"ad-sih-TISH-us","hook":"Five spellings, one sound. The noun you can build from it usually names the ending: -ce → cious, -tion → tious, -x → xious, plant or substance → ceous.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"tralatitious","def":"having been passed along from generation to generation","ex":"The tralatitious folk remedy for hiccups had been handed down through five generations of the family.","say":"tral-uh-TISH-us","hook":"Five spellings, one sound. The noun you can build from it usually names the ending: -ce → cious, -tion → tious, -x → xious, plant or substance → ceous.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"pervicacious","def":"Stubbornly or willfully obstinate; characterized by an extreme and unreasonable refusal to change one's mind or behavior.","ex":"The pervicacious student refused to change his answer even after the teacher explained the error.","say":"per-vih-KAY-shus","hook":"Five spellings, one sound. The noun you can build from it usually names the ending: -ce → cious, -tion → tious, -x → xious, plant or substance → ceous.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"obreptitious","def":"Obtained or done by concealing the truth or by misrepresentation; characterized by the act of sneaking something past someone in authority through deliberate deceit or suppression of key facts.","ex":"Her obreptitious claim to the inheritance was exposed when the full will was revealed.","say":"ob-rep-TISH-us","hook":"Five spellings, one sound. The noun you can build from it usually names the ending: -ce → cious, -tion → tious, -x → xious, plant or substance → ceous.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"saponaceous","def":"resembling or having the qualities of soap","ex":"The saponaceous texture of the aloe gel made it feel like liquid soap as it spread across her sunburned skin.","say":"sap-uh-NAY-shus","hook":"Five spellings, one sound. The noun you can build from it usually names the ending: -ce → cious, -tion → tious, -x → xious, plant or substance → ceous.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"gallinaceous","def":"of or relating to or resembling a gallinacean","ex":"Chickens, turkeys, and pheasants are all gallinaceous birds commonly raised on farms.","say":"gal-ih-NAY-shus","hook":"Five spellings, one sound. The noun you can build from it usually names the ending: -ce → cious, -tion → tious, -x → xious, plant or substance → ceous.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"sebaceous","def":"containing an unusual amount of grease or oil","ex":"The dermatologist explained that sebaceous glands in the skin produce an oily substance that can clog pores and cause acne.","say":"suh-BAY-shuhs","hook":"Five spellings, one sound. The noun you can build from it usually names the ending: -ce → cious, -tion → tious, -x → xious, plant or substance → ceous.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"cetaceous","def":"of or relating to whales and dolphins etc","ex":"The museum exhibit focused on cetaceous mammals, displaying skeletons of whales and dolphins side by side.","say":"sih-TAY-shus","hook":"Five spellings, one sound. The noun you can build from it usually names the ending: -ce → cious, -tion → tious, -x → xious, plant or substance → ceous.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"facetious","def":"means not to be taken seriously","ex":"When Tyler suggested that the class should take a field trip to the moon for their science project, his teacher smiled and said he was being completely facetious and needed a more realistic idea.","say":"f-uh-s-EE-sh-uh-s","hook":"Five spellings, one sound. The noun you can build from it usually names the ending: -ce → cious, -tion → tious, -x → xious, plant or substance → ceous.","o":"French"},{"w":"judicious","def":"marked by the exercise of good judgment or common sense in practical matters","ex":"Judicious use of one's money.","say":"j-oo-d-IH-sh-uh-s","hook":"Five spellings, one sound. The noun you can build from it usually names the ending: -ce → cious, -tion → tious, -x → xious, plant or substance → ceous.","o":"English"},{"w":"sagacious","def":"showing acute mental discernment or being shrewd","ex":"The sagacious old owl in the story always knew exactly which path was safest.","say":"suh-GAY-shus","hook":"Five spellings, one sound. The noun you can build from it usually names the ending: -ce → cious, -tion → tious, -x → xious, plant or substance → ceous.","o":"English"},{"w":"voracious","def":"excessively eager avid insatiable","ex":"The voracious caterpillar munched through every leaf on the little plant.","say":"v-aw-r-AY-sh-uh-s","hook":"Five spellings, one sound. The noun you can build from it usually names the ending: -ce → cious, -tion → tious, -x → xious, plant or substance → ceous.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"malicious","def":"marked by evil intention","ex":"The malicious rumor spread through the school within hours, and the damage it caused to the student's reputation took weeks of honest conversations to undo and repair completely.","say":"m-uh-l-IH-sh-uh-s","hook":"Five spellings, one sound. The noun you can build from it usually names the ending: -ce → cious, -tion → tious, -x → xious, plant or substance → ceous.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"ferocious","def":"marked by extreme and violent energy","ex":"The ferocious thunderstorm snapped ancient oak trees like twigs and flooded the entire valley overnight.","say":"fer-OH-shuhs","hook":"Five spellings, one sound. The noun you can build from it usually names the ending: -ce → cious, -tion → tious, -x → xious, plant or substance → ceous.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"ambitious","def":"having a strong desire and determination to succeed; requiring exceptional effort or ability","ex":"Maya was so ambitious that she signed up for three advanced classes, joined the debate team, and started her own tutoring business all in the same semester.","say":"a-MBIH-shuhs","hook":"Five spellings, one sound. The noun you can build from it usually names the ending: -ce → cious, -tion → tious, -x → xious, plant or substance → ceous.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"vexatious","def":"causing irritation or annoyance","ex":"The constant buzzing of the broken fire alarm was so vexatious that the entire class lost focus during the math test.","say":"veh-KSAY-shuhs","hook":"Five spellings, one sound. The noun you can build from it usually names the ending: -ce → cious, -tion → tious, -x → xious, plant or substance → ceous.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"conscious","def":"is being aware of one's own existence","ex":"A conscious effort to speak more slowly.","say":"k-AH-n-sh-uh-s","hook":"Five spellings, one sound. The noun you can build from it usually names the ending: -ce → cious, -tion → tious, -x → xious, plant or substance → ceous.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"obnoxious","def":"is highly objectionable or offensive","ex":"The obnoxious car alarm blared all morning, annoying everyone on the whole quiet street.","say":"ah-b-n-AH-k-sh-uh-s","hook":"Five spellings, one sound. The noun you can build from it usually names the ending: -ce → cious, -tion → tious, -x → xious, plant or substance → ceous.","o":"Latin"}]},{"title":"-oid and -oidal — resembling, but not the thing","category":"Advanced Orthography","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"Greek eidos meant form or shape, and -oeides meant having the form of. English borrowed it wholesale as -oid, and it is now one of the most productive scientific endings in the language: anything that resembles something else without being it. The spelling never varies — always o-i-d, never oyd, never ode — and the adjective form is regularly -oidal, which is where the mistakes actually happen.","method":"<div class='trick'>SOLENOID</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> SOH-luh-noyd — the ending sounds like oyd\n<b>Origin.</b> Greek solen = pipe or channel, plus -oeides = shaped like\n<b>Trap.</b> the oy sound tempts o-y-d; Greek never wrote it that way\n<b>Spell.</b> S O L E N O I D\n<b>Family.</b> solenoidal, solenocyte","cards":[{"title":"The ending never changes","body":"Whatever the root, the ending is o-i-d. Asteroid, spheroid, alkaloid, cardioid, trapezoid, crystalloid, humanoid, thyroid, amoeboid, botryoid. The oy sound in speech tempts an o-y spelling, and English does write oy elsewhere — boy, joy, employ — but never in this Greek suffix. If the meaning is resembling, the letters are o i d."},{"title":"The adjective is -oidal, not -oidial","body":"Add -al and you get the adjective: toroidal, cuboidal, adenoidal, amyloidal, sinusoidal, spheroidal, colloidal. There is no i before the al. Sinusoidial and spheroidial are the classic slips. The suffix -oid already ends in a consonant, so plain -al is all that is needed."},{"title":"The root tells you the whole word","body":"Because -oid is transparent, the root does all the work and the word becomes guessable. Solenoid, from solen, a pipe. Rhabdoid, from rhabdos, a rod. Botryoid, from botrys, a bunch of grapes. Cotyloid, from kotyle, a cup. Xiphoid, from xiphos, a sword. Learn the Greek shape-noun and the spelling comes free."},{"title":"Two lookalikes that are not this suffix","body":"Watch out for words that end in the same letters for different reasons. Avoid and devoid are not shape words at all — they come from French vuide, empty. Sangfroid is French sang-froid, cold blood, and its d is silent. Neither belongs to this pattern, so neither takes an -oidal adjective. Our library carries 112 true -oid words in core and 389 in full."}],"words":[{"w":"solenoid","def":"a coil of wire around an iron core; becomes a magnet when current passes through the coil","ex":"When the science teacher connected the solenoid to the battery, it instantly attracted a pile of iron filings from across the lab table.","say":"SOH-luh-noyd","hook":"Greek -oeides = having the form of. The ending is always oid, never oyd or ode, and the adjective adds -al, not -ial.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"rhabdoid","def":"shaped like a rod; also describing an aggressive tumor with rod-like cells","ex":"The pathologist noted the rhabdoid appearance of the tumor cells, which had large nuclei and prominent nucleoli unlike typical kidney cells.","say":"RAB-doyd","hook":"Greek -oeides = having the form of. The ending is always oid, never oyd or ode, and the adjective adds -al, not -ial.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"bdelloid","def":"Relating to or resembling a leech; belonging to the class Bdelloidea, a group of microscopic aquatic invertebrates.","ex":"Under the microscope, the bdelloid rotifers writhed through the pond water with a creeping motion resembling tiny leeches.","say":"DEL-oyd","hook":"Greek -oeides = having the form of. The ending is always oid, never oyd or ode, and the adjective adds -al, not -ial.","o":"Modern Greek"},{"w":"botryoid","def":"resembling a cluster of grapes in form","ex":"The mineral deposit had a botryoid shape, with rounded lumps bunched together like grapes.","say":"BOT-ree-oid","hook":"Greek -oeides = having the form of. The ending is always oid, never oyd or ode, and the adjective adds -al, not -ial.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"cotyloid","def":"of the cup-shaped socket that receives the head of the thigh bone","ex":"The surgeon carefully examined the cotyloid cavity before proceeding with the hip replacement.","say":"KOT-ih-loid","hook":"Greek -oeides = having the form of. The ending is always oid, never oyd or ode, and the adjective adds -al, not -ial.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"crystalloid","def":"A substance that, when dissolved in a liquid, forms a true solution and can pass through a semipermeable membrane; also refers to a crystal-like protein body found in plant cells.","ex":"The nurse administered a crystalloid solution to help rehydrate the patient quickly.","say":"KRIS-tuh-loid","hook":"Greek -oeides = having the form of. The ending is always oid, never oyd or ode, and the adjective adds -al, not -ial.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"alkaloid","def":"natural bases containing nitrogen found in plants","ex":"Caffeine is an alkaloid found naturally in coffee beans and tea leaves.","say":"A-lkuh-loyd","hook":"Greek -oeides = having the form of. The ending is always oid, never oyd or ode, and the adjective adds -al, not -ial.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"asteroid","def":"any of numerous small celestial bodies composed of rock and metal that move around the sun (mainly between the orbits of Mars and Jupiter)","ex":"Scientists tracked the massive asteroid as it hurtled through space between Mars and Jupiter.","say":"A-ster-oyd","hook":"Greek -oeides = having the form of. The ending is always oid, never oyd or ode, and the adjective adds -al, not -ial.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"sinusoid","def":"tiny endothelium-lined passages for blood in the tissue of an organ","ex":"The biology student studied the diagram of a sinusoid in the liver under her microscope.","say":"SEYE-nuh-soyd","hook":"Greek -oeides = having the form of. The ending is always oid, never oyd or ode, and the adjective adds -al, not -ial.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"trapezoid","def":"a quadrilateral with two parallel sides","ex":"The art teacher asked students to draw a trapezoid and label its two parallel sides before shading the figure.","say":"TRAP-uh-zoyd","hook":"Greek -oeides = having the form of. The ending is always oid, never oyd or ode, and the adjective adds -al, not -ial.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"cardioid","def":"an epicycloid in which the rolling circle equals the fixed circle","ex":"The math student carefully plotted a cardioid curve on her graph paper, noticing its heart-like shape.","say":"KAR-dee-oyd","hook":"Greek -oeides = having the form of. The ending is always oid, never oyd or ode, and the adjective adds -al, not -ial.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"amoeboid","def":"like an amoeba (especially in having a variable irregular shape)","ex":"The amoeboid movement of the white blood cell allowed it to squeeze between tissues to fight infection.","say":"ah-MEE-boyd","hook":"Greek -oeides = having the form of. The ending is always oid, never oyd or ode, and the adjective adds -al, not -ial.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"toroidal","def":"of or relating to or shaped like a toroid; doughnut shaped","ex":"The physicist explained that the toroidal shape of the fusion reactor allowed plasma to circulate in a continuous loop without touching the walls.","say":"tuh-ROY-dul","hook":"Greek -oeides = having the form of. The ending is always oid, never oyd or ode, and the adjective adds -al, not -ial.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"cuboidal","def":"shaped like a cube","ex":"The cuboidal cells lining the kidney tubules were clearly visible under the high-powered microscope.","say":"kyoo-BOY-dul","hook":"Greek -oeides = having the form of. The ending is always oid, never oyd or ode, and the adjective adds -al, not -ial.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"adenoidal","def":"of or pertaining to the adenoids","ex":"His adenoidal voice made it sound as though he always had a stuffy nose.","say":"ad-eh-NOY-dul","hook":"Greek -oeides = having the form of. The ending is always oid, never oyd or ode, and the adjective adds -al, not -ial.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"amyloidal","def":"resembling starch","ex":"Under the microscope, the tissue showed an amyloidal structure that puzzled the researchers.","say":"am-ih-LOY-dul","hook":"Greek -oeides = having the form of. The ending is always oid, never oyd or ode, and the adjective adds -al, not -ial.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"arytenoid","def":"either of two small cartilages at the back of the larynx to which the vocal folds are attached","ex":"The surgeon carefully examined the arytenoid cartilage to determine why the patient was losing his voice.","say":"air-ih-TEE-noid","hook":"Greek -oeides = having the form of. The ending is always oid, never oyd or ode, and the adjective adds -al, not -ial.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"thyroid","def":"a large endocrine gland in the base of the neck that influences growth and development","ex":"After feeling unusually tired and cold for several weeks, the doctor ordered a blood test and discovered that Mara's thyroid was not producing enough hormones to keep her body's energy levels normal.","say":"th-AY-r-oy-d","hook":"Greek -oeides = having the form of. The ending is always oid, never oyd or ode, and the adjective adds -al, not -ial.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"sangfroid","def":"Coolness and composure, especially in difficult or dangerous situations; the ability to remain calm under pressure.","ex":"Despite the chaos erupting around him during the fire drill, the teacher directed students to safety with remarkable sangfroid.","say":"sah-NFROW","hook":"Greek -oeides = having the form of. The ending is always oid, never oyd or ode, and the adjective adds -al, not -ial.","o":"French"},{"w":"avoid","def":"stay clear from; keep away from; keep out of the way of someone or something","ex":"Her former friends now avoid her.","say":"uh-VOYD","hook":"Greek -oeides = having the form of. The ending is always oid, never oyd or ode, and the adjective adds -al, not -ial.","o":"Anglo-Norman"}]},{"title":"-esce, -escent, -escence — the becoming words","category":"Advanced Orthography","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"Latin had a whole verb class for beginning to be something: -escere, called the inchoative. English inherited the trio intact. The verb ends -esce, the adjective -escent, the noun -escence. Because the sound is just ess, spellers reach for -ess or -esent, and because the noun ends in a soft s sound they reach for -essence. The c never leaves and never doubles, and knowing the trio means one word gives you three.","method":"<div class='trick'>ERUBESCENT</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> er-oo-BESS-unt — beginning to redden\n<b>Origin.</b> Latin rubescere, from ruber = red, plus -escere = beginning to\n<b>Rule.</b> process starting → e s c; adjective takes -escent\n<b>Spell.</b> E R U B E S C E N T\n<b>Family.</b> erubescence, rubescent, rubicund","cards":[{"title":"One root, three words","body":"Learn the trio and you triple your return. Coalesce, coalescent, coalescence. Obsolesce, obsolescent, obsolescence. Effervesce, effervescent, effervescence. Convalesce, convalescent, convalescence. Luminesce, luminescent, luminescence. The moment you can spell any one member, you can spell the other two."},{"title":"The c is doing real work","body":"That c is not decoration. It is the visible remains of Latin -escere, and it is what tells you the word means a process getting under way rather than a finished state. Compare senescent, growing old, with senile, already old. Compare liquescent, becoming liquid, with liquid. The c marks the becoming."},{"title":"Colour and light words cluster here","body":"A surprising number of these describe something changing shade or glow, because Latin loved forming them from colour adjectives. Albescent, turning white. Canescent, turning grey. Nigrescent, turning black. Flavescent, turning yellow. Rubescent and erubescent, turning red. Iridescent, taking on rainbow colours. Virescent, turning green. If the definition says turning some colour, expect -escent."},{"title":"Where the traps are","body":"Three slips account for nearly all errors. Writing -essent for -escent, which loses the Latin marker. Writing -essence for -escence, which confuses it with the unrelated word essence. And doubling the s in the verb, as in coalessce. The c stays single, the s stays single, and they always appear in that order. Sixty-seven of these words sit in the core library, a hundred and fifteen in the full."}],"words":[{"w":"erubescent","def":"turning red or blushing, often from embarrassment or shyness","ex":"His erubescent cheeks betrayed his embarrassment when the teacher read his poem aloud to the entire class.","say":"er-oo-BES-ent","hook":"Latin -escere marked a process starting. Verb -esce, adjective -escent, noun -escence. The c is always there and never doubles.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"effervescence","def":"the process of bubbling as gas escapes","ex":"The effervescence of the sparkling water tickled Maya's nose as she took her first sip.","say":"ef-er-VES-ents","hook":"Latin -escere marked a process starting. Verb -esce, adjective -escent, noun -escence. The c is always there and never doubles.","o":"French"},{"w":"arborescence","def":"The quality of branching or growing in a tree-like pattern; a structure or growth that resembles the branching of a tree.","ex":"The arborescence of the river delta looked like a giant tree spreading across the land.","say":"ar-bor-ES-ents","hook":"Latin -escere marked a process starting. Verb -esce, adjective -escent, noun -escence. The c is always there and never doubles.","o":"French"},{"w":"invalescence","def":"The process or condition of becoming weak, feeble, or invalid; a gradual decline into a state of chronic illness or physical disability.","ex":"The doctor noted the patient's gradual invalescence as he moved from active life to chronic rest.","say":"in-vuh-LES-ents","hook":"Latin -escere marked a process starting. Verb -esce, adjective -escent, noun -escence. The c is always there and never doubles.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"pinguescence","def":"The process or state of becoming fat or greasy; the gradual accumulation of fatty matter in a substance or organism.","ex":"The pinguescence of the hibernating bear's body fat helped it survive the long winter without food.","say":"ping-GWES-ence","hook":"Latin -escere marked a process starting. Verb -esce, adjective -escent, noun -escence. The c is always there and never doubles.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"alkalescent","def":"tending to become alkaline; slightly alkaline","ex":"The alkalescent solution turned the litmus paper a faint blue, showing a mild base.","say":"al-kuh-LEH-sent","hook":"Latin -escere marked a process starting. Verb -esce, adjective -escent, noun -escence. The c is always there and never doubles.","o":"French"},{"w":"frondescent","def":"In the process of putting forth fronds or leaves; becoming leafy, especially in reference to ferns or plants developing their characteristic large leaf structures.","ex":"The frondescent hillside turned brilliant green as the ferns and leafy plants burst into spring growth.","say":"fron-DES-unt","hook":"Latin -escere marked a process starting. Verb -esce, adjective -escent, noun -escence. The c is always there and never doubles.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"intumescent","def":"Abnormally swollen or distended, especially by fluids or gas; describing something that swells up when exposed to heat or other stimuli.","ex":"The intumescent paint swells into a protective foam whenever it gets too hot.","say":"in-too-MES-unt","hook":"Latin -escere marked a process starting. Verb -esce, adjective -escent, noun -escence. The c is always there and never doubles.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"juvenescent","def":"Becoming young again or growing youthful; displaying the process of returning to or developing the characteristics associated with youth and vitality.","ex":"The spring rain gave the old garden a juvenescent quality, as if it had become young again.","say":"joo-vuh-NES-unt","hook":"Latin -escere marked a process starting. Verb -esce, adjective -escent, noun -escence. The c is always there and never doubles.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"acaulescent","def":"(of plants) having no apparent stem above ground","ex":"The dandelion is acaulescent because its leaves seem to rise directly from the ground with no visible stem.","say":"ay-kaw-LES-ent","hook":"Latin -escere marked a process starting. Verb -esce, adjective -escent, noun -escence. The c is always there and never doubles.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"hyalescence","def":"The process or quality of becoming glassy or transparent; a gradual transition toward a clear, glass-like appearance in a substance or material.","ex":"The scientist noted a subtle hyalescence in the mineral sample as it became increasingly glassy under heat.","say":"hy-uh-LES-unts","hook":"Latin -escere marked a process starting. Verb -esce, adjective -escent, noun -escence. The c is always there and never doubles.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"quiescent","def":"is being at rest quiet inactive","ex":"The quiescent level of centimeter wave-length solar radiation.","say":"k-w-ay-EH-s-uh-n-t","hook":"Latin -escere marked a process starting. Verb -esce, adjective -escent, noun -escence. The c is always there and never doubles.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"liquescent","def":"becoming liquid","ex":"The liquescent ice cream dripped down the cone faster than she could lick it.","say":"lih-KWES-ent","hook":"Latin -escere marked a process starting. Verb -esce, adjective -escent, noun -escence. The c is always there and never doubles.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"iridescent","def":"is displaying a play of bright colors like a rainbow","ex":"The hummingbird hovered just inches from the flower, its iridescent throat feathers shifting from deep emerald to brilliant violet as it tilted its tiny head, catching the afternoon sunlight at a slightly different angle.","say":"ih-r-uh-d-EH-s-uh-n-t","hook":"Latin -escere marked a process starting. Verb -esce, adjective -escent, noun -escence. The c is always there and never doubles.","o":"English"},{"w":"obsolescent","def":"means passing out of use as a word","ex":"With smartphones everywhere, the pay phone has become an obsolescent relic of a previous generation.","say":"ah-b-s-uh-l-EH-s-uh-n-t","hook":"Latin -escere marked a process starting. Verb -esce, adjective -escent, noun -escence. The c is always there and never doubles.","o":"English"},{"w":"senescent","def":"growing old.","ex":"The senescent oak tree, though gnarled and slow-growing, still sheltered dozens of birds each spring.","say":"sih-NES-unt","hook":"Latin -escere marked a process starting. Verb -esce, adjective -escent, noun -escence. The c is always there and never doubles.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"acquiesce","def":"to agree or express agreement","ex":"After hours of debate, Marcus finally decided to acquiesce to his teammates' plan even though he still had doubts about it.","say":"a-kwee-EHS","hook":"Latin -escere marked a process starting. Verb -esce, adjective -escent, noun -escence. The c is always there and never doubles.","o":"French"},{"w":"coalesce","def":"mix together different elements","ex":"The small streams slowly coalesce into one wide river down in the valley.","say":"koh-uh-LEHS","hook":"Latin -escere marked a process starting. Verb -esce, adjective -escent, noun -escence. The c is always there and never doubles.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"evanesce","def":"disappear gradually","ex":"Like morning fog, her childhood memories began to evanesce as the years passed.","say":"ev-uh-NES","hook":"Latin -escere marked a process starting. Verb -esce, adjective -escent, noun -escence. The c is always there and never doubles.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"putrescent","def":"becoming putrid","ex":"The putrescent smell of the forgotten lunch bag made the entire classroom groan and hold their noses.","say":"pyoo-TRES-ent","hook":"Latin -escere marked a process starting. Verb -esce, adjective -escent, noun -escence. The c is always there and never doubles.","o":"Latin"}]},{"title":"-efy or -ify — only four take the e","category":"Advanced Orthography","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"Both endings come from Latin facere, to make, and both are pronounced the same. English standardised on -ify for essentially the whole language — and then left exactly four verbs behind with -efy. That is the entire exception list, which makes this one of the few patterns you can master completely in one sitting. The noun forms are the second half of the story, because -efy words take -efaction, not -ification.","method":"<div class='trick'>LIQUEFY</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> LICK-wuh-fye — to make liquid\n<b>Candidates.</b> -efy or -ify\n<b>Rule.</b> the only four with e are liquefy, rarefy, putrefy, stupefy\n<b>Spell.</b> L I Q U E F Y\n<b>Noun.</b> liquefaction — never liquification","cards":[{"title":"The complete exception list","body":"Liquefy, rarefy, putrefy, stupefy. That is all of them. Every other English verb in this family takes -ify: classify, magnify, terrify, justify, testify, ratify, mollify, nullify, vilify, edify, codify, deify, typify, verify, purify, qualify, amplify, glorify. Four words against hundreds, and the four are the ones examiners ask."},{"title":"The noun exposes the verb","body":"This is the elegant part. The four -efy verbs form nouns in -efaction: liquefaction, rarefaction, putrefaction, stupefaction. Every -ify verb forms a noun in -ification: classification, magnification, justification. So if you can remember the noun, the verb follows automatically — and if you can remember the verb, you already know the noun."},{"title":"Where -faction comes from","body":"Latin facere gave a noun factio, an act of making. That is why the noun has an a where the verb has none: liquefy but liquefaction, satisfy but satisfaction. The f-a-c is the visible root, meaning make, and it also shows up in factory, manufacture, benefaction and malefaction. Same root, doing the same job."},{"title":"Why this pattern earns its hour","body":"Our core library holds 142 words ending in this family and the full library 364, so the -ify side is enormous. But the value is concentrated: because -ify is the default, the only thing you actually have to store is the list of four. Learn four words and a rule about nouns, and a 364-word pattern becomes safe."}],"words":[{"w":"liquefy","def":"become liquid","ex":"","say":"LIH-kwuh-feye","hook":"Latin facere = to make. Only liquefy, rarefy, putrefy and stupefy keep the e. Every other verb takes -ify. The noun always turns -efy into -efaction.","o":"Anglo-Norman"},{"w":"rarefy","def":"lessen the density or solidity of","ex":"","say":"REH-ruh-feye","hook":"Latin facere = to make. Only liquefy, rarefy, putrefy and stupefy keep the e. Every other verb takes -ify. The noun always turns -efy into -efaction.","o":"French"},{"w":"putrefy","def":"become putrid; decay with an offensive smell","ex":"Without proper refrigeration, the organic waste will putrefy within hours.","say":"PYOO-truh-fy","hook":"Latin facere = to make. Only liquefy, rarefy, putrefy and stupefy keep the e. Every other verb takes -ify. The noun always turns -efy into -efaction.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"stupefy","def":"make dull or stupid or muddle with drunkenness or infatuation","ex":"The magician's final trick—making a live elephant disappear—managed to absolutely stupefy the entire audience, leaving everyone speechless and blinking in disbelief.","say":"STOO-puh-fy","hook":"Latin facere = to make. Only liquefy, rarefy, putrefy and stupefy keep the e. Every other verb takes -ify. The noun always turns -efy into -efaction.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"liquefaction","def":"the conversion of a solid or a gas into a liquid","ex":"During the earthquake, liquefaction caused the sandy ground to behave like quicksand, swallowing up roads.","say":"lih-kwuh-FA-kshuhn","hook":"Latin facere = to make. Only liquefy, rarefy, putrefy and stupefy keep the e. Every other verb takes -ify. The noun always turns -efy into -efaction.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"benefaction","def":"a contribution of money or assistance","ex":"The school library was built thanks to a generous benefaction from a local philanthropist.","say":"ben-ih-FAK-shun","hook":"Latin facere = to make. Only liquefy, rarefy, putrefy and stupefy keep the e. Every other verb takes -ify. The noun always turns -efy into -efaction.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"labefaction","def":"the gradual weakening or shaking of something, such as an institution or moral standard","ex":"The historian argued that corruption led to the labefaction of the once-mighty empire.","say":"lab-ih-FAK-shun","hook":"Latin facere = to make. Only liquefy, rarefy, putrefy and stupefy keep the e. Every other verb takes -ify. The noun always turns -efy into -efaction.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"patefaction","def":"The act of opening, disclosing, or making something manifest; the state of being revealed or laid open.","ex":"The diplomat's speech resulted in the patefaction of previously secret trade negotiations.","say":"pat-ih-FAK-shun","hook":"Latin facere = to make. Only liquefy, rarefy, putrefy and stupefy keep the e. Every other verb takes -ify. The noun always turns -efy into -efaction.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"mystification","def":"The state of being completely puzzled or confused.","ex":"The magician's vanishing act left the audience in total mystification.","say":"mis-tih-fih-KAY-shun","hook":"Latin facere = to make. Only liquefy, rarefy, putrefy and stupefy keep the e. Every other verb takes -ify. The noun always turns -efy into -efaction.","o":"French"},{"w":"vivification","def":"quality of being active or spirited or alive and vigorous","ex":"The director spoke about the vivification of the old script, saying new actors had breathed fresh life into it.","say":"viv-ih-fih-KAY-shun","hook":"Latin facere = to make. Only liquefy, rarefy, putrefy and stupefy keep the e. Every other verb takes -ify. The noun always turns -efy into -efaction.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"horrification","def":"The act or process of causing horror, dread, or extreme fright; the state of being horrified or the experience of terror spreading through a person or group.","ex":"The horrification on the crowd's faces was clear when the magician's trick went wrong.","say":"hor-ih-fih-KAY-shun","hook":"Latin facere = to make. Only liquefy, rarefy, putrefy and stupefy keep the e. Every other verb takes -ify. The noun always turns -efy into -efaction.","o":"French"},{"w":"aridification","def":"The process by which a region becomes increasingly dry or arid over time, often due to climate change or environmental degradation.","ex":"Scientists warned that the aridification of the region would turn fertile farmland into desert within decades.","say":"uh-rid-ih-fih-KAY-shun","hook":"Latin facere = to make. Only liquefy, rarefy, putrefy and stupefy keep the e. Every other verb takes -ify. The noun always turns -efy into -efaction.","o":"French"},{"w":"massification","def":"The process of making something available to or characteristic of the mass population, often referring to the widespread expansion of education, culture, or consumer goods to large groups.","ex":"The massification of higher education over the past fifty years has allowed millions of first-generation students to attend university, though critics debate whether quality has suffered as a result.","say":"mas-ih-fih-KAY-shun","hook":"Latin facere = to make. Only liquefy, rarefy, putrefy and stupefy keep the e. Every other verb takes -ify. The noun always turns -efy into -efaction.","o":"French"},{"w":"typify","def":"To be a perfect example of something; to show its typical qualities","ex":"Her kindness to new students typifies the friendly spirit of our school.","say":"TIH-pih-fy","hook":"Latin facere = to make. Only liquefy, rarefy, putrefy and stupefy keep the e. Every other verb takes -ify. The noun always turns -efy into -efaction.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"gamification","def":"The process of applying game-design elements such as points, levels, badges, and leaderboards to non-game contexts, such as education or workplace tasks, in order to increase engagement and motivation.","ex":"The teacher used gamification in the classroom by turning spelling practice into a point-scoring competition.","say":"gay-mih-fih-KAY-shun","hook":"Latin facere = to make. Only liquefy, rarefy, putrefy and stupefy keep the e. Every other verb takes -ify. The noun always turns -efy into -efaction.","o":"French"},{"w":"ludification","def":"The act of mocking, ridiculing, or making a fool of someone; the process of turning something into an object of sport or derision.","ex":"The court jester's ludification of the pompous nobleman made the whole audience burst into laughter.","say":"loo-dih-fih-KAY-shun","hook":"Latin facere = to make. Only liquefy, rarefy, putrefy and stupefy keep the e. Every other verb takes -ify. The noun always turns -efy into -efaction.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"domification","def":"In astrology, the division of the celestial sphere into twelve houses, each representing a different area of life; the process of assigning planets to specific houses.","ex":"The astrologer explained that domification determines which area of a person's life each planet most powerfully influences at the time of birth.","say":"doh-mih-fih-KAY-shun","hook":"Latin facere = to make. Only liquefy, rarefy, putrefy and stupefy keep the e. Every other verb takes -ify. The noun always turns -efy into -efaction.","o":"French"},{"w":"mallification","def":"The process by which an area, culture, or community is transformed to resemble or be dominated by shopping malls and commercial consumer culture.","ex":"Residents complained about the mallification of their historic downtown neighborhood.","say":"maw-lih-fih-KAY-shun","hook":"Latin facere = to make. Only liquefy, rarefy, putrefy and stupefy keep the e. Every other verb takes -ify. The noun always turns -efy into -efaction.","o":"French"},{"w":"abstractify","def":"To make something more abstract or theoretical; to transform a concrete idea or object into a vague or generalized concept.","ex":"The professor tended to abstractify simple ideas until students could barely understand the original concept.","say":"ab-STRAK-tih-fy","hook":"Latin facere = to make. Only liquefy, rarefy, putrefy and stupefy keep the e. Every other verb takes -ify. The noun always turns -efy into -efaction.","o":"French"},{"w":"disqualification","def":"unfitness that bars you from participation","ex":"The runner faced disqualification after officials discovered she had taken a shortcut during the race.","say":"dih-skwah-luh-fuh-KAY-shuhn","hook":"Latin facere = to make. Only liquefy, rarefy, putrefy and stupefy keep the e. Every other verb takes -ify. The noun always turns -efy into -efaction.","o":"French"}]},{"title":"Bearing, eating, birthing — iferous, ivorous, parous, fugal, fluous","category":"Advanced Orthography","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"Five Latin verbs became productive endings, and they behave as a family. Ferre, to bear, gives -iferous. Vorare, to devour, gives -ivorous. Parere, to bring forth, gives -parous. Fugere, to flee, gives -fugal. Fluere, to flow, gives -fluous. The spelling problem is almost never the ending itself but the vowel that joins it to the root, and that vowel is i far more often than anything else.","method":"<div class='trick'>FOSSILIFEROUS</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> foss-ill-IFF-er-us — bearing fossils\n<b>Parse.</b> fossil + i + ferous\n<b>Rule.</b> Latin joins root to ending with a connecting i, not an a or an o\n<b>Spell.</b> F O S S I L I F E R O U S\n<b>Family.</b> carboniferous, cupriferous, auriferous","cards":[{"title":"The five endings and their verbs","body":"-iferous, from ferre, to bear or carry: coniferous bears cones, auriferous bears gold, fossiliferous bears fossils. -ivorous, from vorare, to devour: carnivorous, herbivorous, piscivorous, insectivorous. -parous, from parere, to bring forth: oviparous lays eggs, viviparous bears live young, multiparous has borne several. -fugal, from fugere, to flee: centrifugal flees the centre, lucifugal flees light. -fluous, from fluere, to flow: mellifluous flows like honey, superfluous overflows."},{"title":"The connecting vowel is i","body":"This is where the marks are lost. Latin joined a noun root to a verb ending with a short connecting vowel, and in this family it is almost always i. Cupriferous, not cupraferous. Plumbiferous, not plumbeferous. Laticiferous. Cruciferous. Nitriferous. When you hear the ending, assume i and then look for a reason to think otherwise."},{"title":"The metals give you a free set","body":"Mineralogy loves -iferous, and the roots are the Latin metal names you may already know. Aurum gold gives auriferous. Argentum silver gives argentiferous. Cuprum copper gives cupriferous. Plumbum lead gives plumbiferous. Ferrum iron gives ferriferous. Stannum tin gives stanniferous. Learn six metals and you have earned six long words."},{"title":"Where the family breaks pattern","body":"A few members join with a different vowel because the Latin root already ended in one. Omnivorous keeps the i of omni. Septemfluous keeps the e of septem, seven. Frugivorous keeps the i of frugi. And a small group uses -ferous without the i where the root supplies its own vowel, as in odiferous beside odoriferous. Fifty-five of these sit in core and a hundred and eleven in the full library."}],"words":[{"w":"fossiliferous","def":"bearing or containing fossils","ex":"The geologist's eyes lit up when she discovered a fossiliferous shale bed packed with tiny trilobite shells millions of years old.","say":"fah-suh-LIH-fer-uhs","hook":"Latin combining verbs: ferre bear, vorare devour, parere bring forth, fugere flee, fluere flow. The connecting vowel is almost always i.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"quartziferous","def":"Containing or bearing quartz; used to describe rocks or geological formations in which quartz is a significant mineral component.","ex":"The geologist noted that the quartziferous rock sample sparkled brilliantly in the afternoon sunlight.","say":"kwort-ZIF-er-us","hook":"Latin combining verbs: ferre bear, vorare devour, parere bring forth, fugere flee, fluere flow. The connecting vowel is almost always i.","o":"German"},{"w":"plutoniferous","def":"Containing, producing, or bearing plutonium; used in scientific contexts to describe materials or geological formations associated with plutonium deposits or contamination.","ex":"Scientists mapped the plutoniferous zones in the region before planning any mining operations.","say":"ploo-toh-NIF-er-us","hook":"Latin combining verbs: ferre bear, vorare devour, parere bring forth, fugere flee, fluere flow. The connecting vowel is almost always i.","o":"Malay"},{"w":"strontiferous","def":"Containing or yielding strontium; used to describe rocks or minerals that bear the element strontium.","ex":"The geologist was excited to discover strontiferous rock layers deep in the Scottish hillside.","say":"stron-TIF-ur-us","hook":"Latin combining verbs: ferre bear, vorare devour, parere bring forth, fugere flee, fluere flow. The connecting vowel is almost always i.","o":"Scots"},{"w":"petaliferous","def":"Bearing or producing petals; used to describe plants or flowers that have petals as part of their structure.","ex":"The garden was full of petaliferous plants that bloomed in brilliant colors every spring.","say":"pet-ul-IF-er-us","hook":"Latin combining verbs: ferre bear, vorare devour, parere bring forth, fugere flee, fluere flow. The connecting vowel is almost always i.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"laticiferous","def":"Containing or conveying latex; describing plant ducts, cells, or vessels that produce and carry latex or milky sap throughout a plant's tissues.","ex":"The botanist noted that the laticiferous ducts of the milkweed plant oozed a sticky white sap when cut.","say":"lat-ih-SIF-er-us","hook":"Latin combining verbs: ferre bear, vorare devour, parere bring forth, fugere flee, fluere flow. The connecting vowel is almost always i.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"plumbiferous","def":"Containing, yielding, or bearing lead; used to describe ores, rocks, or geological formations that carry lead deposits.","ex":"The environmental report warned that the plumbiferous soil near the old factory posed serious health risks.","say":"plum-BIF-er-us","hook":"Latin combining verbs: ferre bear, vorare devour, parere bring forth, fugere flee, fluere flow. The connecting vowel is almost always i.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"arseniferous","def":"Containing or yielding arsenic; used to describe minerals, soils, or geological deposits that bear arsenic compounds.","ex":"Workers were warned not to mine in the arseniferous zone without proper protective gear.","say":"ar-sen-IF-er-us","hook":"Latin combining verbs: ferre bear, vorare devour, parere bring forth, fugere flee, fluere flow. The connecting vowel is almost always i.","o":"French"},{"w":"cupuliferous","def":"Bearing or producing cupules, the small cup-shaped structures that partially enclose the nuts of trees such as oaks, beeches, and chestnuts.","ex":"The forest path was lined with cupuliferous oaks whose little acorn cups littered the ground, making a satisfying crunching sound under the hikers' boots.","say":"kyoo-pyoo-LIF-er-us","hook":"Latin combining verbs: ferre bear, vorare devour, parere bring forth, fugere flee, fluere flow. The connecting vowel is almost always i.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"laminiferous","def":"Bearing, producing, or composed of thin layers or laminae; having a structure made up of multiple flat, sheet-like layers.","ex":"The geologist described the rock as laminiferous because it contained thin, flat mineral layers throughout.","say":"lam-ih-NIF-er-us","hook":"Latin combining verbs: ferre bear, vorare devour, parere bring forth, fugere flee, fluere flow. The connecting vowel is almost always i.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"cruciferous","def":"of or relating to or belonging to the plant family Cruciferae","ex":"Broccoli, cauliflower, and cabbage are all cruciferous vegetables known for their health benefits.","say":"kroo-SIH-fur-uhs","hook":"Latin combining verbs: ferre bear, vorare devour, parere bring forth, fugere flee, fluere flow. The connecting vowel is almost always i.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"coniferous","def":"of or relating to or part of trees or shrubs bearing cones and evergreen leaves","ex":"The hikers walked through a dense coniferous forest where towering pines blocked out most of the sunlight.","say":"kuh-NIH-fer-uhs","hook":"Latin combining verbs: ferre bear, vorare devour, parere bring forth, fugere flee, fluere flow. The connecting vowel is almost always i.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"vociferous","def":"is crying out noisily","ex":"The vociferous crowd outside the city hall meeting chanted slogans, waved handmade signs, and shouted their demands so loudly and persistently that the council members inside could barely hear each other speak.","say":"v-oh-s-IH-f-ur-uh-s","hook":"Latin combining verbs: ferre bear, vorare devour, parere bring forth, fugere flee, fluere flow. The connecting vowel is almost always i.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"piscivorous","def":"feeding on fishes","ex":"The osprey is a piscivorous bird of prey, diving talons-first into rivers and lakes to catch its scaly meals.","say":"pih-SIV-uh-rus","hook":"Latin combining verbs: ferre bear, vorare devour, parere bring forth, fugere flee, fluere flow. The connecting vowel is almost always i.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"omnivorous","def":"feeding on both plants and animals","ex":"Bears are omnivorous creatures, eating everything from berries and honey to fish and insects.","say":"ah-MNIH-ver-uhs","hook":"Latin combining verbs: ferre bear, vorare devour, parere bring forth, fugere flee, fluere flow. The connecting vowel is almost always i.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"oviparous","def":"Describing animals that reproduce by laying eggs that hatch outside the mother's body, such as birds, reptiles, and most fish.","ex":"Unlike mammals, most reptiles are oviparous creatures that bury their eggs in warm sand and leave them to hatch on their own.","say":"oh-VIH-per-uhs","hook":"Latin combining verbs: ferre bear, vorare devour, parere bring forth, fugere flee, fluere flow. The connecting vowel is almost always i.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"viviparous","def":"producing living young (not eggs)","ex":"Unlike most fish, sharks are viviparous, giving birth to live pups instead of laying eggs.","say":"veye-VIH-per-uhs","hook":"Latin combining verbs: ferre bear, vorare devour, parere bring forth, fugere flee, fluere flow. The connecting vowel is almost always i.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"centrifugal","def":"tending to move away from a center","ex":"When the washing machine spun at full speed, the centrifugal force pushed the wet clothes outward against the drum wall, squeezing out most of the water.","say":"SEH-ntrih-fyoo-guhl","hook":"Latin combining verbs: ferre bear, vorare devour, parere bring forth, fugere flee, fluere flow. The connecting vowel is almost always i.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"mellifluous","def":"is sweetly or smoothly flowing","ex":"The singer's mellifluous voice flowed through the hall like warm honey.","say":"meh-LIF-loo-us","hook":"Latin combining verbs: ferre bear, vorare devour, parere bring forth, fugere flee, fluere flow. The connecting vowel is almost always i.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"lucifugal","def":"avoiding or keeping away from light, as some insects do","ex":"The lucifugal cockroaches scattered the moment she switched on the kitchen light.","say":"loo-SIF-yoo-gul","hook":"Latin combining verbs: ferre bear, vorare devour, parere bring forth, fugere flee, fluere flow. The connecting vowel is almost always i.","o":"Latin"}]},{"title":"-ance or -ence after soft c and g","category":"Advanced Orthography","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"English spells the same unstressed ending two ways, and after a c or g the choice is not arbitrary at all — it is forced by pronunciation. A c or g stays soft only when the next letter is e, i or y. So if you hear a soft sound just before the ending, the ending must begin with e. If you hear a hard sound, an a is available. This single piece of phonics resolves a whole class of words that otherwise have to be memorised one at a time.","method":"<div class='trick'>INSURGENCE</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> in-SUR-junce — the g is soft, like j\n<b>Rule.</b> a soft g needs e, i or y after it to stay soft\n<b>Test.</b> insurgance would force a hard g, giving in-sur-GANCE\n<b>Spell.</b> I N S U R G E N C E\n<b>Family.</b> insurgent, insurgency, resurgence","cards":[{"title":"Why the rule exists","body":"In English a c before a, o or u is hard, as in cat, cot, cut. Before e, i or y it is soft, as in cent, city, cycle. The same holds for g: gate and got are hard, gem and gin are soft. So the vowel after the letter is not decoration, it is the instruction that tells you how to pronounce the letter before it."},{"title":"Apply it to the ending","body":"Say the word and listen to the consonant just before the ending. Soft, like j or s? Then the ending must start with e. Negligence, intelligence, diligence, indulgence, divergence, emergence, reticence, innocence, beneficence, magnificence. Hard? Then a is allowed. Arrogance, elegance, extravagance, significance, vacancy — note that in significance and vacancy the c is hard, a k sound."},{"title":"The -ency and -ancy nouns follow","body":"The same logic governs the shorter noun forms. Soft g gives -gency: emergency, exigency, stringency, insurgency, plangency, tangency. Hard c gives -cancy: vacancy, mendicancy. And the adjective agrees too — insurgent, divergent, indulgent all keep the e for the same reason the noun does."},{"title":"What this does not settle","body":"Be honest about the limits. The rule only bites when the letter before the ending is c or g. For everything else — resistance against existence, persistence against assistance — you still need the Latin conjugation or plain memory. But c and g cover 65 words in the core library and 119 in the full library, and those are exactly the ones that look unguessable until you know the trick."}],"words":[{"w":"obliviscence","def":"The process or condition of forgetting; the gradual fading of memories from the mind over time. It refers to the natural tendency of the brain to lose stored information.","ex":"He blamed his obliviscence on old age, but his doctor suspected it might be something more serious.","say":"ob-lih-VIS-ens","hook":"A soft c or g needs a following e or i to stay soft. So a soft sound before the ending forces -ence, and a hard sound allows -ance.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"effervescence","def":"the process of bubbling as gas escapes","ex":"The effervescence of the sparkling water tickled Maya's nose as she took her first sip.","say":"ef-er-VES-ents","hook":"A soft c or g needs a following e or i to stay soft. So a soft sound before the ending forces -ence, and a hard sound allows -ance.","o":"French"},{"w":"arborescence","def":"The quality of branching or growing in a tree-like pattern; a structure or growth that resembles the branching of a tree.","ex":"The arborescence of the river delta looked like a giant tree spreading across the land.","say":"ar-bor-ES-ents","hook":"A soft c or g needs a following e or i to stay soft. So a soft sound before the ending forces -ence, and a hard sound allows -ance.","o":"French"},{"w":"invalescence","def":"The process or condition of becoming weak, feeble, or invalid; a gradual decline into a state of chronic illness or physical disability.","ex":"The doctor noted the patient's gradual invalescence as he moved from active life to chronic rest.","say":"in-vuh-LES-ents","hook":"A soft c or g needs a following e or i to stay soft. So a soft sound before the ending forces -ence, and a hard sound allows -ance.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"pinguescence","def":"The process or state of becoming fat or greasy; the gradual accumulation of fatty matter in a substance or organism.","ex":"The pinguescence of the hibernating bear's body fat helped it survive the long winter without food.","say":"ping-GWES-ence","hook":"A soft c or g needs a following e or i to stay soft. So a soft sound before the ending forces -ence, and a hard sound allows -ance.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"beneficence","def":"the doing of good","ex":"The hospital wing was built entirely through the beneficence of a local family who donated their entire fortune.","say":"b-uh-n-EH-f-uh-s-uh-n-s","hook":"A soft c or g needs a following e or i to stay soft. So a soft sound before the ending forces -ence, and a hard sound allows -ance.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"insurgence","def":"an act of rebellion","ex":"The government struggled to contain the sudden insurgence that swept through the northern provinces.","say":"in-SUR-junss","hook":"A soft c or g needs a following e or i to stay soft. So a soft sound before the ending forces -ence, and a hard sound allows -ance.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"divergence","def":"the act of moving away in different direction from a common point","ex":"An angle is formed by the divergence of two straight lines.","say":"deye-VUR-juhns","hook":"A soft c or g needs a following e or i to stay soft. So a soft sound before the ending forces -ence, and a hard sound allows -ance.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"indulgence","def":"an inability to resist the gratification of whims and desires","ex":"Too much indulgence spoils a child.","say":"ih-NDUH-ljuhns","hook":"A soft c or g needs a following e or i to stay soft. So a soft sound before the ending forces -ence, and a hard sound allows -ance.","o":"French"},{"w":"negligence","def":"failure to act with the prudence that a reasonable person would exercise under the same circumstances","ex":"The company was sued for negligence after failing to repair the broken staircase that injured a customer.","say":"NEH-gluh-juhns","hook":"A soft c or g needs a following e or i to stay soft. So a soft sound before the ending forces -ence, and a hard sound allows -ance.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"reticence","def":"the trait of being uncommunicative; not volunteering anything more than necessary","ex":"His reticence during the interview made the employer wonder whether he was shy or simply uninterested in the position.","say":"REH-tih-suhns","hook":"A soft c or g needs a following e or i to stay soft. So a soft sound before the ending forces -ence, and a hard sound allows -ance.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"plangency","def":"having the character of a loud deep sound; the quality of being resonant","ex":"The plangency of the cathedral bells rolled across the valley and sent chills down the hikers' spines.","say":"PLAN-jen-see","hook":"A soft c or g needs a following e or i to stay soft. So a soft sound before the ending forces -ence, and a hard sound allows -ance.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"stringency","def":"a state occasioned by scarcity of money and a shortage of credit","ex":"The financial stringency of the Great Depression forced many families to grow their own food and mend their own clothes.","say":"STRIH-njuh-nsee","hook":"A soft c or g needs a following e or i to stay soft. So a soft sound before the ending forces -ence, and a hard sound allows -ance.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"exigency","def":"a pressing or urgent situation","ex":"During the sudden rainstorm, the exigency of finding shelter sent us dashing under the porch.","say":"eh-KSIH-juh-nsee","hook":"A soft c or g needs a following e or i to stay soft. So a soft sound before the ending forces -ence, and a hard sound allows -ance.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"tangency","def":"The state of touching, especially of a line touching a curve at one point","ex":"In math class we marked the point of tangency where the line touched the circle.","say":"TAN-jen-see","hook":"A soft c or g needs a following e or i to stay soft. So a soft sound before the ending forces -ence, and a hard sound allows -ance.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"displacency","def":"A state of displeasure or dissatisfaction; the feeling of being uncomfortable or ill at ease in a situation.","ex":"The ambassador's displacency at the treaty's unfair terms was obvious to everyone seated around the long negotiating table that afternoon.","say":"dis-PLAY-sen-see","hook":"A soft c or g needs a following e or i to stay soft. So a soft sound before the ending forces -ence, and a hard sound allows -ance.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"significance","def":"the quality of being significant","ex":"Do not underestimate the significance of nuclear power.","say":"suh-GNIH-fih-kuhns","hook":"A soft c or g needs a following e or i to stay soft. So a soft sound before the ending forces -ence, and a hard sound allows -ance.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"insignificance","def":"the quality of having little or no significance","ex":"Standing beneath the vast night sky filled her with a humbling sense of her own insignificance.","say":"ih-nsih-GNYIH-fih-kuhns","hook":"A soft c or g needs a following e or i to stay soft. So a soft sound before the ending forces -ence, and a hard sound allows -ance.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"elegance","def":"a refined quality of gracefulness and good taste","ex":"She conveys an aura of elegance and gentility.","say":"EH-luh-guhns","hook":"A soft c or g needs a following e or i to stay soft. So a soft sound before the ending forces -ence, and a hard sound allows -ance.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"arrogance","def":"overbearing pride evidenced by a superior manner toward inferiors","ex":"The new student's arrogance quickly made him unpopular, as he constantly bragged about his achievements.","say":"EH-ruh-guhns","hook":"A soft c or g needs a following e or i to stay soft. So a soft sound before the ending forces -ence, and a hard sound allows -ance.","o":"Latin"}]},{"title":"Who rules — archy, cracy, arch and the government words","category":"Advanced Orthography","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"Two Greek roots produce almost every word for a form of government, and they are not interchangeable. Arkhein meant to rule or to be first, and gives -arch for the ruler and -archy for the system. Kratos meant strength or power, and gives -crat for the person and -cracy for the system. The spelling trap is the ch: it is a Greek chi, always ch and never k, and the same root sits inside architect, archive and archaeology.","method":"<div class='trick'>KAKISTOCRACY</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> kack-iss-TOCK-ruh-see — government by the worst people\n<b>Parse.</b> kakistos = worst, plus o, plus kratos = power\n<b>Rule.</b> a group holding power → cracy; the connecting vowel is o in Greek\n<b>Spell.</b> K A K I S T O C R A C Y\n<b>Family.</b> kakistocrat, kakistocratic","cards":[{"title":"Ruler versus system","body":"-arch names a person: monarch, patriarch, matriarch, oligarch, ecclesiarch, gymnasiarch, heresiarch. -archy names the system that person heads: monarchy, oligarchy, patriarchy, anarchy, autarchy, gynarchy. -crat names a person too — democrat, technocrat, autocrat — and -cracy names their system. Deciding person or system chooses your ending before you write a letter."},{"title":"The ch is a Greek chi","body":"Every one of the -arch words spells the k sound c-h, because the Greek letter was chi. Monarch, anarchy, hierarchy, archetype, architect, archaeology, archive, archaic. There is no monark or anarky. The moment you hear a k sound in a word about ruling, being first or being ancient, write ch."},{"title":"The connecting o","body":"Greek joined a root to kratos with an o, and that o is the letter people drop. Theocracy, not theacracy. Ochlocracy, from ochlos, a crowd. Technocracy, from techne, skill. Plutocracy, from ploutos, wealth. Gerontocracy, from geron, an old man. Kakistocracy, from kakistos, worst. Assume the joint is o."},{"title":"How productive it is","body":"This pattern is still generating words, which is why the library is full of unusual ones: squattocracy, punditocracy, kindergarchy, cryptocracy, ergatocracy, mobocracy, adhocracy, e-democracy. Sixty-one sit in the core competition list and a hundred and fifty-two in the full library. If a definition names a group and says they hold the power, build it with o-c-r-a-c-y and you will usually be right."}],"words":[{"w":"kakistocracy","def":"A system of government in which the least qualified, most unprincipled, or most corrupt people hold power and make decisions that affect the rest of society.","ex":"The political commentator argued that the corrupt election had produced a true kakistocracy.","say":"kak-ih-STOK-ruh-see","hook":"Greek arkhein = to rule or be first, kratos = power. -archy is rule by one kind of ruler, -cracy is power held by a group.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"squattocracy","def":"A powerful ruling class made up of large landowners or squatters, especially in early Australian history, who controlled vast territories and wielded great political influence.","ex":"In the novel, the squattocracy controlled vast stretches of land and looked down on newcomers.","say":"skwot-OK-ruh-see","hook":"Greek arkhein = to rule or be first, kratos = power. -archy is rule by one kind of ruler, -cracy is power held by a group.","o":"French"},{"w":"punditocracy","def":"A society or system in which media commentators and expert pundits hold excessive influence over public opinion and political decisions.","ex":"Critics argued that the punditocracy on cable news had more influence than elected officials.","say":"pun-dih-TOK-ruh-see","hook":"Greek arkhein = to rule or be first, kratos = power. -archy is rule by one kind of ruler, -cracy is power held by a group.","o":"Hindi"},{"w":"kindergarchy","def":"A social condition in which children are treated as the center of family and cultural life to such a degree that they effectively hold power over adult decisions and behavior.","ex":"Critics argued that the kindergarchy of modern parenting gave children far too much power over family decisions.","say":"KIN-der-gar-kee","hook":"Greek arkhein = to rule or be first, kratos = power. -archy is rule by one kind of ruler, -cracy is power held by a group.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"ergatocracy","def":"A system of government or social organization in which the working class holds political power and controls the mechanisms of governance.","ex":"The revolutionary pamphlet called for an ergatocracy where factory workers controlled the government.","say":"er-guh-TOK-ruh-see","hook":"Greek arkhein = to rule or be first, kratos = power. -archy is rule by one kind of ruler, -cracy is power held by a group.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"cryptocracy","def":"A system of government or control that operates in secret, with the true rulers or decision-makers hidden from public view.","ex":"Critics claimed the shadowy committee had turned the government into a cryptocracy operating behind closed doors.","say":"krip-TOK-ruh-see","hook":"Greek arkhein = to rule or be first, kratos = power. -archy is rule by one kind of ruler, -cracy is power held by a group.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"ochlocracy","def":"a political system in which a mob is the source of control; government by the masses","ex":"The philosopher warned that without strong laws, a democracy could collapse into ochlocracy, where angry crowds made all the decisions.","say":"ok-LOK-ruh-see","hook":"Greek arkhein = to rule or be first, kratos = power. -archy is rule by one kind of ruler, -cracy is power held by a group.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"technocracy","def":"a form of government in which scientists and technical experts are in control","ex":"Technocracy was described as that society in which those who govern justify themselves by appeal to technical experts who justify themselves by appeal to scientific forms of knowledge.","say":"tek-NOK-ruh-see","hook":"Greek arkhein = to rule or be first, kratos = power. -archy is rule by one kind of ruler, -cracy is power held by a group.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"ecclesiarch","def":"A leader or ruling official of a church or ecclesiastical assembly, responsible for overseeing religious governance and administrative affairs.","ex":"The ecclesiarch presided over the cathedral's daily services and administrative affairs.","say":"ih-KLEE-zee-ark","hook":"Greek arkhein = to rule or be first, kratos = power. -archy is rule by one kind of ruler, -cracy is power held by a group.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"gymnasiarch","def":"The director or superintendent of a gymnasium in ancient Greece, responsible for overseeing the training, discipline, and administration of athletic exercises and education held there.","ex":"The gymnasiarch oversaw all athletic training and competitions at the ancient Greek school.","say":"jim-NAY-zee-ark","hook":"Greek arkhein = to rule or be first, kratos = power. -archy is rule by one kind of ruler, -cracy is power held by a group.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"theocracy","def":"a form of government in which a deity is recognized as the supreme civil ruler","ex":"In a theocracy, religious leaders make the laws of the land based on sacred texts and divine authority.","say":"th-ee-AH-k-r-uh-s-ee","hook":"Greek arkhein = to rule or be first, kratos = power. -archy is rule by one kind of ruler, -cracy is power held by a group.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"hierarchy","def":"a system of things ranked above each other","ex":"Put honesty first in her hierarchy of values.","say":"h-AY-ur-ah-r-k-ee","hook":"Greek arkhein = to rule or be first, kratos = power. -archy is rule by one kind of ruler, -cracy is power held by a group.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"matriarch","def":"the female head of a tribal or family line","ex":"The matriarch of the family gathered all her grandchildren around the fireplace to share stories of the past.","say":"m-AY-t-r-ee-ah-r-k","hook":"Greek arkhein = to rule or be first, kratos = power. -archy is rule by one kind of ruler, -cracy is power held by a group.","o":"English"},{"w":"patriarch","def":"the male head of a family or tribal line","ex":"The elderly patriarch gathered his large family around the dinner table every Sunday evening.","say":"p-AY-t-r-ee-ah-r-k","hook":"Greek arkhein = to rule or be first, kratos = power. -archy is rule by one kind of ruler, -cracy is power held by a group.","o":"English"},{"w":"oligarchy","def":"a political system governed by a few people","ex":"The revolution was sparked by citizens who were tired of living under an oligarchy where three wealthy families made all the laws.","say":"AH-luh-gah-rkee","hook":"Greek arkhein = to rule or be first, kratos = power. -archy is rule by one kind of ruler, -cracy is power held by a group.","o":"French"},{"w":"gynarchy","def":"a political system governed by a woman","ex":"The fantasy novel described a gynarchy where queens and empresses had ruled the land for a thousand years.","say":"GY-nar-kee","hook":"Greek arkhein = to rule or be first, kratos = power. -archy is rule by one kind of ruler, -cracy is power held by a group.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"autarchy","def":"economic independence as a national policy","ex":"The country's autarchy left its citizens cut off from cheaper goods available abroad.","say":"AW-tar-kee","hook":"Greek arkhein = to rule or be first, kratos = power. -archy is rule by one kind of ruler, -cracy is power held by a group.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"monarchy","def":"an autocracy governed by a monarch who usually inherits the authority","ex":"The United Kingdom operates as a constitutional monarchy, where the royal family shares power with an elected parliament.","say":"MAH-nah-rkee","hook":"Greek arkhein = to rule or be first, kratos = power. -archy is rule by one kind of ruler, -cracy is power held by a group.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"anarchy","def":"a state of lawlessness and disorder (usually resulting from a failure of government)","ex":"After the king was overthrown, the kingdom fell into anarchy, with rival warlords fighting over every village.","say":"A-ner-kee","hook":"Greek arkhein = to rule or be first, kratos = power. -archy is rule by one kind of ruler, -cracy is power held by a group.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"meritocracy","def":"a form of social system in which power goes to those with superior intellects","ex":"In a true meritocracy, the hardest-working and most talented people rise to the top.","say":"meh-rih-TAW-kruh-see","hook":"Greek arkhein = to rule or be first, kratos = power. -archy is rule by one kind of ruler, -cracy is power held by a group.","o":"French"}]},{"title":"-itude — the quality-of-being nouns","category":"Advanced Orthography","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"Latin -itudo turned an adjective into a noun naming the quality itself: from altus, high, came altitudo, height. English kept the suffix as -itude and it now names abstract qualities across the language. Two things trip spellers. The connecting vowel is i, not a, so it is never -atude. And the adjective is built on the Latin oblique stem itudin-, giving -itudinous and -itudinal, which is why longitude becomes longitudinal rather than longitudal.","method":"<div class='trick'>PULCHRITUDE</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> PULL-kruh-tood — physical beauty\n<b>Origin.</b> Latin pulcher = beautiful, plus -itudo = the quality of\n<b>Trap.</b> the ch is a Latin ch, said like k\n<b>Spell.</b> P U L C H R I T U D E\n<b>Family.</b> pulchritudinous","cards":[{"title":"How the noun is built","body":"Take a Latin adjective, add -itudo, get the quality. Altus high gives altitude. Latus wide gives latitude. Longus long gives longitude. Certus sure gives certitude. Aptus fit gives aptitude. Gratus pleasing gives gratitude. Servus a slave gives servitude. The connecting vowel is i every time — there is no -atude in English."},{"title":"The stem is itudin-","body":"In Latin the suffix declined, and its stem was itudin-. That stem is what English uses for further derivatives, which is why the adjectives look longer than expected. Longitude gives longitudinal. Latitude gives latitudinal. Attitude gives attitudinal. Multitude gives multitudinous. Pulchritude gives pulchritudinous. Vicissitude gives vicissitudinous. Never longitudal, never multitudous."},{"title":"The hard ones and their roots","body":"The competition words in this family are all transparent once you have the Latin. Pulchritude from pulcher, beautiful. Turpitude from turpis, shameful. Vicissitude from vicis, a turn or change. Lassitude from lassus, weary. Decrepitude from decrepitus, broken down. Solicitude from sollicitus, anxious. Ineptitude from ineptus, unsuitable. Rectitude from rectus, straight."},{"title":"Small pattern, high hit rate","body":"Only 35 of these are in the core library and 61 in the full library, so this is a small family. But its members appear at the hard end of word lists out of proportion to their number, because the roots are unusual Latin adjectives and the -itudinous derivatives are long. A short list worth owning completely."}],"words":[{"w":"verisimilitude","def":"the appearance of truth; the quality of seeming to be true","ex":"The novelist researched Medieval armor for months to give her fantasy story enough verisimilitude that readers would forget they were reading fiction.","say":"v-eh-r-uh-s-uh-m-IH-l-uh-t-oo-d","hook":"Latin -itudo made an abstract noun from an adjective. The connecting vowel is i, never a, and the adjective form is -itudinous from the Latin stem itudin-.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"pulchritude","def":"is physical beauty comeliness","ex":"The judges at the art competition marveled at the statue's pulchritude, saying it captured human beauty perfectly.","say":"PUL-krih-tyood","hook":"Latin -itudo made an abstract noun from an adjective. The connecting vowel is i, never a, and the adjective form is -itudinous from the Latin stem itudin-.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"vicissitude","def":"means a change in the course of things","ex":"The project was subject to the usual vicissitudes of exploratory research.","say":"v-ih-s-IH-s-ih-t-oo-d","hook":"Latin -itudo made an abstract noun from an adjective. The connecting vowel is i, never a, and the adjective form is -itudinous from the Latin stem itudin-.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"decrepitude","def":"a state of deterioration due to old age or long use","ex":"The old mansion had fallen into such decrepitude that the floorboards creaked with every step and the roof let in rain.","say":"dih-KREP-ih-tood","hook":"Latin -itudo made an abstract noun from an adjective. The connecting vowel is i, never a, and the adjective form is -itudinous from the Latin stem itudin-.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"solicitude","def":"a feeling of excessive concern","ex":"Her solicitude for the injured bird was touching as she carefully wrapped it in a soft cloth.","say":"suh-LIH-sih-tood","hook":"Latin -itudo made an abstract noun from an adjective. The connecting vowel is i, never a, and the adjective form is -itudinous from the Latin stem itudin-.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"correctitude","def":"correct or appropriate behavior","ex":"The ambassador was known for his flawless correctitude in every social and diplomatic situation.","say":"kuh-REK-tih-tood","hook":"Latin -itudo made an abstract noun from an adjective. The connecting vowel is i, never a, and the adjective form is -itudinous from the Latin stem itudin-.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"perfectitude","def":"The state or quality of being perfectly complete or flawless; an absolute degree of excellence or faultlessness in character or execution.","ex":"She pursued perfectitude in every sketch, erasing and redrawing each line until it was flawless.","say":"per-FEK-tih-tood","hook":"Latin -itudo made an abstract noun from an adjective. The connecting vowel is i, never a, and the adjective form is -itudinous from the Latin stem itudin-.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"turpitude","def":"a corrupt or depraved or degenerate act or practice","ex":"The judge declared that the politician's crimes showed such moral turpitude that he was unfit to ever hold public office again.","say":"TUR-pih-tood","hook":"Latin -itudo made an abstract noun from an adjective. The connecting vowel is i, never a, and the adjective form is -itudinous from the Latin stem itudin-.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"lassitude","def":"a state of comatose torpor (as found in sleeping sickness)","ex":"After two weeks of brutal final exams, a profound lassitude settled over the students, and they spent the entire weekend doing nothing but sleeping.","say":"LAS-ih-tyood","hook":"Latin -itudo made an abstract noun from an adjective. The connecting vowel is i, never a, and the adjective form is -itudinous from the Latin stem itudin-.","o":"French"},{"w":"habitude","def":"habitual mode of behavior","ex":"His habitude of rising before dawn made him unusually productive compared to his classmates.","say":"HAB-ih-tood","hook":"Latin -itudo made an abstract noun from an adjective. The connecting vowel is i, never a, and the adjective form is -itudinous from the Latin stem itudin-.","o":"French"},{"w":"ineptitude","def":"unskillfulness resulting from a lack of training","ex":"The new player's ineptitude on the basketball court was obvious, but the coach assured everyone that practice would fix it.","say":"ih-NEH-ptih-tood","hook":"Latin -itudo made an abstract noun from an adjective. The connecting vowel is i, never a, and the adjective form is -itudinous from the Latin stem itudin-.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"exactitude","def":"the quality of being exact","ex":"With great exactitude, the watchmaker fit each tiny gear precisely into place.","say":"ih-GZA-ktuh-tood","hook":"Latin -itudo made an abstract noun from an adjective. The connecting vowel is i, never a, and the adjective form is -itudinous from the Latin stem itudin-.","o":"French"},{"w":"beatitude","def":"a state of supreme happiness","ex":"Her favorite Beatitude is `Blessed are the meek for they shall inherit the earth'.","say":"bee-AT-ih-tood","hook":"Latin -itudo made an abstract noun from an adjective. The connecting vowel is i, never a, and the adjective form is -itudinous from the Latin stem itudin-.","o":"French"},{"w":"rectitude","def":"righteousness as a consequence of being honorable and honest","ex":"The judge was admired throughout the city for her unwavering rectitude and fair treatment of every case.","say":"REH-ktih-tood","hook":"Latin -itudo made an abstract noun from an adjective. The connecting vowel is i, never a, and the adjective form is -itudinous from the Latin stem itudin-.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"platitude","def":"a trite or obvious remark","ex":"Instead of giving real advice, the coach just offered platitudes like 'hard work always pays off,' which frustrated the team who needed a specific strategy.","say":"PLA-tih-tood","hook":"Latin -itudo made an abstract noun from an adjective. The connecting vowel is i, never a, and the adjective form is -itudinous from the Latin stem itudin-.","o":"French"},{"w":"negritude","def":"an ideological position that holds Black culture to be independent and valid on its own terms; an affirmation of the African cultural heritage","ex":"The poet drew on negritude to celebrate the beauty and resilience of African cultural heritage.","say":"NEG-rih-tood","hook":"Latin -itudo made an abstract noun from an adjective. The connecting vowel is i, never a, and the adjective form is -itudinous from the Latin stem itudin-.","o":"French"},{"w":"longitudinal","def":"of or relating to lines of longitude","ex":"Longitudinal reckoning by the navigator.","say":"lah-njuh-TOO-duh-nuhl","hook":"Latin -itudo made an abstract noun from an adjective. The connecting vowel is i, never a, and the adjective form is -itudinous from the Latin stem itudin-.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"attitudinal","def":"of or relating to attitudes","ex":"The coach noticed an attitudinal shift in the team after they won their first game of the season.","say":"a-tuh-TOO-duh-nuhl","hook":"Latin -itudo made an abstract noun from an adjective. The connecting vowel is i, never a, and the adjective form is -itudinous from the Latin stem itudin-.","o":"French"},{"w":"latitudinal","def":"of or relating to latitudes north or south","ex":"The scientists studied the latitudinal differences in temperature between the equator and the Arctic Circle.","say":"la-tuh-TOO-duh-nuhl","hook":"Latin -itudo made an abstract noun from an adjective. The connecting vowel is i, never a, and the adjective form is -itudinous from the Latin stem itudin-.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"amplitude","def":"(physics) the maximum displacement of a periodic wave","ex":"The science teacher drew a wave on the board and showed students how to measure its amplitude from the center line to its peak.","say":"A-mpluh-tood","hook":"Latin -itudo made an abstract noun from an adjective. The connecting vowel is i, never a, and the adjective form is -itudinous from the Latin stem itudin-.","o":"French"}]},{"title":"-mancy — divination by absolutely anything","category":"Advanced Orthography","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"Greek manteia meant prophecy or divination, and English used it to name a method of foretelling by any object at all. It is one of the most reliably spellable families on a bee stage: the ending never varies, the connecting vowel is o, and the root is a plain Greek noun for whatever you are reading the future in. Once you know the roots, words nobody has ever seen become buildable.","method":"<div class='trick'>AILUROMANCY</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> eye-LOOR-uh-man-see — divination by watching cats\n<b>Parse.</b> ailouros = cat, plus o, plus manteia = prophecy\n<b>Rule.</b> the joint is o; the ending is m a n c y\n<b>Spell.</b> A I L U R O M A N C Y\n<b>Family.</b> ailuromantic, ailurophile, ailurophobia","cards":[{"title":"The three forms","body":"The method is -mancy: necromancy, pyromancy, chiromancy. The practitioner is -mancer: necromancer, geomancer. The adjective is -mantic: necromantic, chiromantic. All three keep the m-a-n, because Greek manteia had an a. There is no -mency and no -mantik."},{"title":"Learn the roots and build the words","body":"Every one of these is a Greek noun plus the suffix. Cheir hand gives chiromancy, palm reading. Ge earth gives geomancy. Pyr fire gives pyromancy. Selene moon gives selenomancy. Oneiros dream gives oneiromancy. Ichthys fish gives ichthyomancy. Biblion book gives bibliomancy. Aleuron flour gives aleuromancy. Daktylos finger gives dactylomancy."},{"title":"The strange ones are the best ones","body":"The library is full of methods nobody practises any more, which makes them perfect competition words. Enoptromancy, by mirror. Arachnomancy, by spiders. Cephalomancy, by a skull. Maculomancy, by spots or blemishes. Crithomancy, by barley. Lecanomancy, by a basin of water. Botanomancy, by burning plants. Ailuromancy, by the behaviour of cats."},{"title":"Do not confuse it with romantic","body":"Two words end in these letters without belonging to the family. Romantic comes from Rome by way of the Romance languages and has nothing to do with prophecy. Semantic comes from Greek sema, a sign. Dormancy comes from Latin dormire, to sleep. None of them takes a -mancer form, which is your test. Thirty of the real ones are in core, forty-six in the full library."}],"words":[{"w":"ailuromancy","def":"A form of divination or fortune-telling based on the observation of a cat's movements and behavior.","ex":"In ancient times, people practiced ailuromancy by observing how cats moved to predict the weather.","say":"ay-LYUR-oh-man-see","hook":"Greek manteia = prophecy. The joint is almost always o, and the practitioner is -mancer, the adjective -mantic.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"enoptromancy","def":"A form of divination in which a person gazes into a mirror to receive prophetic visions or answers about the future.","ex":"The fortune teller practiced enoptromancy, gazing into a mirror by candlelight to reveal visions of the future.","say":"en-OP-troh-man-see","hook":"Greek manteia = prophecy. The joint is almost always o, and the practitioner is -mancer, the adjective -mantic.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"arachnomancy","def":"A form of divination or fortune-telling that uses the behavior, appearance, or webs of spiders to predict the future or reveal hidden truths. Practitioners interpret spider movements and web patterns as omens or signs.","ex":"In old folklore, arachnomancy involved watching a spider's web to predict the weather.","say":"uh-RAK-noh-man-see","hook":"Greek manteia = prophecy. The joint is almost always o, and the practitioner is -mancer, the adjective -mantic.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"cephalomancy","def":"A form of divination in which predictions or omens are interpreted by examining a head, historically often a boiled or roasted animal skull.","ex":"In medieval times, practitioners of cephalomancy claimed to divine the future by examining a boiled donkey's head.","say":"SEF-uh-loh-man-see","hook":"Greek manteia = prophecy. The joint is almost always o, and the practitioner is -mancer, the adjective -mantic.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"maculomancy","def":"A form of divination or fortune-telling based on the interpretation of spots, marks, or blemishes on the skin or on objects, using their patterns to predict future events.","ex":"The fortune teller claimed to practice maculomancy, reading the patterns of birthmarks and freckles on her clients' skin to reveal hidden truths about their personality, destiny, and the challenges that lay ahead.","say":"MAK-yoo-loh-man-see","hook":"Greek manteia = prophecy. The joint is almost always o, and the practitioner is -mancer, the adjective -mantic.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"crithomancy","def":"A form of divination in which predictions or omens are interpreted by examining the grains, dough, or cakes used in sacrificial rituals.","ex":"The ancient ritual included crithomancy, where priests scattered grain over a sacrificed animal to read omens.","say":"KRITH-oh-man-see","hook":"Greek manteia = prophecy. The joint is almost always o, and the practitioner is -mancer, the adjective -mantic.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"lecanomancy","def":"A form of divination in which a person attempts to foretell the future by observing the surface of water in a basin, sometimes with oil or precious stones added.","ex":"The ancient seer practiced lecanomancy, gazing at the ripples in a bronze basin filled with water to predict the future.","say":"leh-KAN-oh-man-see","hook":"Greek manteia = prophecy. The joint is almost always o, and the practitioner is -mancer, the adjective -mantic.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"botanomancy","def":"The practice of attempting to predict the future or gain hidden knowledge by interpreting signs and patterns found in plants, such as the shapes of leaves, the burning of herbs, or the movements of branches.","ex":"The ancient priestess practiced botanomancy by reading omens in the patterns of burning herbs.","say":"boh-TAN-oh-man-see","hook":"Greek manteia = prophecy. The joint is almost always o, and the practitioner is -mancer, the adjective -mantic.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"dactylomancy","def":"A form of divination or fortune-telling in which rings worn on the fingers or the fingers themselves are used to predict future events.","ex":"In ancient times, some people practiced dactylomancy, believing that rings on fingers could reveal the future.","say":"dak-tuh-loh-MAN-see","hook":"Greek manteia = prophecy. The joint is almost always o, and the practitioner is -mancer, the adjective -mantic.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"ichthyomancy","def":"Divination or the attempt to foretell the future by examining fish, particularly their entrails, behavior, or appearance.","ex":"The ancient priest practiced ichthyomancy, studying the entrails of fish to predict the outcome of the battle.","say":"IK-thee-oh-man-see","hook":"Greek manteia = prophecy. The joint is almost always o, and the practitioner is -mancer, the adjective -mantic.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"aleuromancy","def":"A form of divination or fortune-telling that uses flour or meal, such as interpreting messages baked inside dough.","ex":"In ancient times, priests practiced aleuromancy by baking messages in dough balls and drawing them at random to tell fortunes.","say":"ah-LYUR-oh-man-see","hook":"Greek manteia = prophecy. The joint is almost always o, and the practitioner is -mancer, the adjective -mantic.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"selenomancy","def":"Divination or fortune-telling performed by observing and interpreting the phases, appearance, and movements of the moon.","ex":"Ancient mystics practiced selenomancy, believing the moon's phases could reveal the future.","say":"sel-EE-noh-man-see","hook":"Greek manteia = prophecy. The joint is almost always o, and the practitioner is -mancer, the adjective -mantic.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"oneiromancy","def":"divination through the interpretation of dreams","ex":"She practiced oneiromancy, keeping a detailed dream journal and consulting it to predict important life events.","say":"oh-NY-roh-man-see","hook":"Greek manteia = prophecy. The joint is almost always o, and the practitioner is -mancer, the adjective -mantic.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"bibliomancy","def":"The practice of telling fortunes or predicting the future by randomly selecting and interpreting passages from a book, especially the Bible or another sacred text.","ex":"In bibliomancy, a person opens a sacred book to a random page and reads the first line as a prophecy.","say":"BIB-lee-oh-man-see","hook":"Greek manteia = prophecy. The joint is almost always o, and the practitioner is -mancer, the adjective -mantic.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"chartomancy","def":"Divination or fortune-telling performed by interpreting maps, charts, written papers, or other documents to predict future events.","ex":"The fortune teller practiced chartomancy, reading old maps and papers to predict the traveler's fate.","say":"KAR-toh-man-see","hook":"Greek manteia = prophecy. The joint is almost always o, and the practitioner is -mancer, the adjective -mantic.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"chronomancy","def":"A form of divination in which a person attempts to predict the future or determine lucky and unlucky times by interpreting the significance of specific hours, days, or seasons.","ex":"The fortune teller claimed her chronomancy could reveal the exact date of future disasters.","say":"KROH-noh-man-see","hook":"Greek manteia = prophecy. The joint is almost always o, and the practitioner is -mancer, the adjective -mantic.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"chiromancy","def":"telling fortunes by lines on the palm of the hand","ex":"At the fair, a costumed fortune teller pretended to practice chiromancy by tracing the lines on palms.","say":"KY-roh-man-see","hook":"Greek manteia = prophecy. The joint is almost always o, and the practitioner is -mancer, the adjective -mantic."},{"w":"necromancer","def":"one who practices magic or sorcery","ex":"In the fantasy novel, the necromancer raised an army of skeletons from the ancient graveyard to do his bidding.","say":"NEH-kruh-ma-nser","hook":"Greek manteia = prophecy. The joint is almost always o, and the practitioner is -mancer, the adjective -mantic.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"necromancy","def":"the belief in magical spells that harness occult forces or evil spirits to produce unnatural effects in the world","ex":"The wizard's book of necromancy was locked away because its spells were too dangerous for anyone to use.","say":"NEH-kruh-ma-nsee","hook":"Greek manteia = prophecy. The joint is almost always o, and the practitioner is -mancer, the adjective -mantic.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"pyromancy","def":"divination by fire or flames","ex":"The ancient oracle practiced pyromancy, reading futures in the dancing flames of a torch.","say":"PY-roh-man-see","hook":"Greek manteia = prophecy. The joint is almost always o, and the practitioner is -mancer, the adjective -mantic.","o":"Greek"}]},{"title":"Silent finishes — words ending -gm and -gn","category":"Advanced Orthography","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"A small set of words ends in a written g you cannot hear: paradigm, phlegm, benign, campaign, impugn. The g is not decoration — it was pronounced in Latin and Greek, and it comes back the moment a suffix is added. That is the whole strategy for this pattern. If you can find a relative in which the g is audible, the g belongs in the base word, and if you cannot, it probably does not.","method":"<div class='trick'>PARADIGM</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> PAIR-uh-dime — the final g is silent\n<b>Test.</b> say the adjective — paradigmatic, and the g comes back\n<b>Verdict.</b> the audible g in the relative proves the silent g in the base\n<b>Spell.</b> P A R A D I G M\n<b>Family.</b> paradigmatic, paradigmatically","cards":[{"title":"The derivative test","body":"This is the only tool you need. Paradigm hides its g, but paradigmatic says it. Phlegm hides it, phlegmatic says it. Diaphragm hides it, diaphragmatic says it. Malign hides it, malignant says it. Benign hides it, benignity says it. Sign hides it, signature and signal say it. Resign hides it, resignation says it."},{"title":"The -gm group","body":"The -gm words are Greek, from roots where gamma and mu sat together: paradigm from paradeigma, an example; phlegm from phlegma, inflammation; diaphragm from diaphragma, a partition; apothegm from apophthegma, a terse saying; borborygm from borborygmos, a rumbling of the stomach. All silent, all recoverable by adding -atic."},{"title":"The -gn group","body":"The -gn words are mostly Latin. Benign and malign from benignus and malignus. Deign from dignare, to think worthy. Feign from fingere, to shape. Impugn and oppugn from pugnare, to fight. Arraign from ad rationem, to call to account. Campaign from campus, a field. Sovereign is the odd one out — its g was never pronounced at all, and it was inserted by scribes who wrongly linked it to reign."},{"title":"Sovereign and foreign are traps","body":"Two words in the group have a g that is historically fake. Sovereign comes from Latin super and had no g; foreign comes from foras, outdoors, and had no g either. Scribes added the g to both by false analogy with reign. So the derivative test fails for these — sovereignty keeps the silent g, and there is no relative that pronounces it. Those two you memorise. Twenty-four of these words are in the core library and 66 in the full library."}],"words":[{"w":"paradigm","def":"systematic arrangement of all the inflected forms of a word","ex":"Our grammar book shows the full paradigm of the verb, listing every changed form.","say":"PEH-ruh-deyem","hook":"The g is silent at the end but speaks again when a suffix follows: paradigm but paradigmatic, malign but malignant. The derivative proves the g.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"diaphragm","def":"a part of the human body","ex":"The new cameras adjust the diaphragm automatically.","say":"d-AY-uh-f-r-a-m","hook":"The g is silent at the end but speaks again when a suffix follows: paradigm but paradigmatic, malign but malignant. The derivative proves the g.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"apothegm","def":"a short pithy instructive saying","ex":"Benjamin Franklin was famous for crafting an apothegm that could teach a lesson in just a few clever words.","say":"AP-uh-them","hook":"The g is silent at the end but speaks again when a suffix follows: paradigm but paradigmatic, malign but malignant. The derivative proves the g.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"phlegm","def":"apathy demonstrated by an absence of emotional reactions","ex":"","say":"FLEHM","hook":"The g is silent at the end but speaks again when a suffix follows: paradigm but paradigmatic, malign but malignant. The derivative proves the g.","o":"French"},{"w":"borborygm","def":"A rumbling or gurgling noise produced by gas moving through the intestines; the technical term for what is commonly called a stomach growl.","ex":"During the completely silent exam, the student's loud borborygm echoed through the room, causing several classmates to look up from their papers with poorly concealed amusement.","say":"BOR-boh-rigm","hook":"The g is silent at the end but speaks again when a suffix follows: paradigm but paradigmatic, malign but malignant. The derivative proves the g.","o":"French"},{"w":"benign","def":"is having a kindly disposition","ex":"The doctor reassured the worried family that the small growth was completely benign, explaining that it posed no threat to the patient's health and required no further treatment whatsoever.","say":"b-ih-n-AY-n","hook":"The g is silent at the end but speaks again when a suffix follows: paradigm but paradigmatic, malign but malignant. The derivative proves the g.","o":"English"},{"w":"malign","def":"is to speak harmful untruths about","ex":"It is never kind to malign a classmate with rumors that aren't true.","say":"m-uh-l-AY-n","hook":"The g is silent at the end but speaks again when a suffix follows: paradigm but paradigmatic, malign but malignant. The derivative proves the g.","o":"English"},{"w":"condign","def":"fitting or appropriate and deserved; used especially of punishment","ex":"The judge delivered condign punishment to the swindler who had robbed dozens of elderly victims.","say":"kun-DYNE","hook":"The g is silent at the end but speaks again when a suffix follows: paradigm but paradigmatic, malign but malignant. The derivative proves the g.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"deign","def":"to think fit or to condescend","ex":"The famous chef did not deign to answer questions from food bloggers he considered beneath his level of expertise.","say":"d-AY-n","hook":"The g is silent at the end but speaks again when a suffix follows: paradigm but paradigmatic, malign but malignant. The derivative proves the g.","o":"French"},{"w":"feign","def":"is to represent fictitiously","ex":"He feigned that he was ill.","say":"f-AY-n","hook":"The g is silent at the end but speaks again when a suffix follows: paradigm but paradigmatic, malign but malignant. The derivative proves the g.","o":"French"},{"w":"impugn","def":"attack as false or wrong","ex":"The defense lawyer tried to impugn the witness's testimony by pointing out several contradictions.","say":"ih-MPYOON","hook":"The g is silent at the end but speaks again when a suffix follows: paradigm but paradigmatic, malign but malignant. The derivative proves the g.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"oppugn","def":"challenge the accuracy, probity, or propriety of","ex":"The defense attorney rose to oppugn every claim the witness made, pointing out inconsistencies in the testimony.","say":"uh-PYOON","hook":"The g is silent at the end but speaks again when a suffix follows: paradigm but paradigmatic, malign but malignant. The derivative proves the g.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"eloign","def":"To remove or carry away to a distance; in legal contexts, to convey property or a person beyond the jurisdiction of a court to prevent seizure or process.","ex":"The thief tried to eloign the stolen goods by hiding them far outside the city.","say":"ih-LOIN","hook":"The g is silent at the end but speaks again when a suffix follows: paradigm but paradigmatic, malign but malignant. The derivative proves the g.","o":"Anglo-Norman"},{"w":"arraign","def":"call before a court to answer an indictment","ex":"The suspect was arraigned on Monday morning, and the judge read the charges aloud before setting a bail amount.","say":"er-AYN","hook":"The g is silent at the end but speaks again when a suffix follows: paradigm but paradigmatic, malign but malignant. The derivative proves the g.","o":"French"},{"w":"campaign","def":"a military operation for a specific objective","ex":"I managed his campaign for governor.","say":"k-a-m-p-AY-n","hook":"The g is silent at the end but speaks again when a suffix follows: paradigm but paradigmatic, malign but malignant. The derivative proves the g.","o":"French"},{"w":"ensign","def":"a flag or banner","ex":"The ship's ensign snapped proudly in the ocean breeze as it sailed into the harbor at dawn.","say":"EH-n-s-uh-n","hook":"The g is silent at the end but speaks again when a suffix follows: paradigm but paradigmatic, malign but malignant. The derivative proves the g.","o":"English"},{"w":"countersign","def":"a secret word or phrase known only to a restricted group","ex":"The clubhouse guards let you in only if you whispered the correct countersign at the door.","say":"KOWN-ter-syne","hook":"The g is silent at the end but speaks again when a suffix follows: paradigm but paradigmatic, malign but malignant. The derivative proves the g.","o":"French"},{"w":"sovereign","def":"a monarch or a king","ex":"The wise sovereign ruled the peaceful kingdom fairly, listening carefully to every villager's concern.","say":"s-AH-v-r-uh-n","hook":"The g is silent at the end but speaks again when a suffix follows: paradigm but paradigmatic, malign but malignant. The derivative proves the g.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"foreign","def":"of concern to or concerning the affairs of other nations (other than your own)","ex":"Learning a foreign language can open doors to new cultures, friendships, and opportunities that most people in your hometown never get the chance to experience.","say":"FAW-ruhn","hook":"The g is silent at the end but speaks again when a suffix follows: paradigm but paradigmatic, malign but malignant. The derivative proves the g.","o":"French"},{"w":"reassign","def":"transfer somebody to a different position or location of work","ex":"The principal decided to reassign the experienced teacher to the struggling class that needed more support.","say":"ree-uh-SEYEN","hook":"The g is silent at the end but speaks again when a suffix follows: paradigm but paradigmatic, malign but malignant. The derivative proves the g.","o":"French"}]},{"title":"-cide — the killing words","category":"Advanced Orthography","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"Latin caedere meant to cut down, and by extension to kill. English uses -cide both for the act and for the agent that does it, joined to the root with an i. The adjective is regularly -cidal. The single real confusion is that a different Latin verb, cadere, to fall, produces the same letters in words like coincide and decide, where nothing is being killed at all — and those words never take a -cidal form.","method":"<div class='trick'>MOLLUSCICIDE</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> muh-LUSS-ih-side — a substance that kills molluscs\n<b>Parse.</b> mollusc + i + cide\n<b>Trap.</b> the c of mollusc stays, so there are two c letters before the ending\n<b>Spell.</b> M O L L U S C I C I D E\n<b>Family.</b> molluscicidal, mollusc, molluscan","cards":[{"title":"Agent or act","body":"The same ending does two jobs. As an act: regicide the killing of a king, matricide of a mother, patricide of a father, parricide of a close relative, fratricide of a brother, liberticide of liberty. As a substance that kills: pesticide, herbicide, fungicide, insecticide, germicide, larvicide, vermicide, acaricide, molluscicide. The ending is identical either way."},{"title":"The joint is i","body":"Latin joined the root to caedere with an i, and that i is the letter to watch. Herbicide, not herbacide. Fungicide, not fungacide. Larvicide, not larvacide. Acaricide, from acarus, a mite. Vermicide, from vermis, a worm. When you build one of these, put an i before the c."},{"title":"The adjective is -cidal","body":"Add -al and the c stays soft because the i is still there: homicidal, suicidal, genocidal, fungicidal, herbicidal, bactericidal, germicidal, molluscicidal, barmecidal. There is no -cideal and no -cidial. Just drop the final e and add al."},{"title":"The other -cide","body":"Latin cadere, to fall, is a different verb entirely, and it gives coincide, to fall together, and decide, to cut off deliberation — plus incident, accident and occident, which are all about falling. Coincide and decide have no -cidal form, and nothing in them is killed. If the definition has no victim, you are looking at cadere, not caedere. Twenty-six killing words sit in the core library and 56 in the full library."}],"words":[{"w":"molluscicide","def":"A chemical substance or agent used to kill mollusks, such as snails and slugs, particularly those that damage crops or transmit disease.","ex":"The farmer applied a molluscicide to the garden to protect his crops from slugs and snails.","say":"moh-LUS-ih-syd","hook":"Latin caedere = to cut down or kill. The joint is i, the noun is -cide, the adjective -cidal. Do not confuse it with -cide from cadere, to fall.","o":"French"},{"w":"liberticide","def":"The destruction or killing of liberty; also, a person who destroys or suppresses the freedoms and rights of others through tyrannical or oppressive actions.","ex":"The historian called the oppressive new law a liberticide that crushed the citizens' basic freedoms.","say":"lih-BER-tih-syd","hook":"Latin caedere = to cut down or kill. The joint is i, the noun is -cide, the adjective -cidal. Do not confuse it with -cide from cadere, to fall.","o":"French"},{"w":"parasiticide","def":"A substance or agent that destroys parasites; any chemical, drug, or treatment used to kill parasitic organisms living on or within a host organism.","ex":"The veterinarian prescribed a parasiticide to rid the dog of fleas and ticks.","say":"pair-uh-SIT-ih-syd","hook":"Latin caedere = to cut down or kill. The joint is i, the noun is -cide, the adjective -cidal. Do not confuse it with -cide from cadere, to fall.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"cancericidal","def":"Capable of destroying or killing cancer cells; describing an agent or treatment that acts directly against cancerous growth.","ex":"Researchers were excited to discover that the new compound had powerful cancericidal properties in laboratory tests on tumor cells.","say":"kan-SAIR-ih-SY-dul","hook":"Latin caedere = to cut down or kill. The joint is i, the noun is -cide, the adjective -cidal. Do not confuse it with -cide from cadere, to fall.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"barmecidal","def":"generous in appearance but giving nothing at all","ex":"The offer turned out to be barmecidal.","say":"bar-mih-SY-duhl","hook":"Latin caedere = to cut down or kill. The joint is i, the noun is -cide, the adjective -cidal. Do not confuse it with -cide from cadere, to fall.","o":"Arabic"},{"w":"monosilicide","def":"a compound of one silicon atom joined to one metal atom","ex":"Engineers used monosilicide coatings to protect the surface of the microchip from heat damage.","say":"mon-oh-SIL-ih-syd","hook":"Latin caedere = to cut down or kill. The joint is i, the noun is -cide, the adjective -cidal. Do not confuse it with -cide from cadere, to fall.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"insecticide","def":"a chemical used to kill insects","ex":"The farmer sprayed insecticide on the crops to protect them from the swarms of beetles.","say":"ih-NSEH-ktuh-seyed","hook":"Latin caedere = to cut down or kill. The joint is i, the noun is -cide, the adjective -cidal. Do not confuse it with -cide from cadere, to fall.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"pesticide","def":"a chemical preparation to destroy pests","ex":"The farmer carefully applied pesticide to protect his crops from the destructive beetles.","say":"p-EH-s-t-uh-s-ay-d","hook":"Latin caedere = to cut down or kill. The joint is i, the noun is -cide, the adjective -cidal. Do not confuse it with -cide from cadere, to fall.","o":"English"},{"w":"acaricide","def":"a chemical agent used to kill mites","ex":"The farmer applied an acaricide to protect the apple trees from a damaging mite outbreak.","say":"uh-KAR-ih-syd","hook":"Latin caedere = to cut down or kill. The joint is i, the noun is -cide, the adjective -cidal. Do not confuse it with -cide from cadere, to fall.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"vermicide","def":"an agent that kills worms (especially those in the intestines)","ex":"The veterinarian prescribed a vermicide to rid the puppy of the roundworms living in its intestines.","say":"VUR-mih-syd","hook":"Latin caedere = to cut down or kill. The joint is i, the noun is -cide, the adjective -cidal. Do not confuse it with -cide from cadere, to fall.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"fungicide","def":"any agent that destroys or prevents the growth of fungi","ex":"The farmer sprayed a powerful fungicide on his grape vines to prevent mold from ruining the harvest.","say":"FUH-njuh-seyed","hook":"Latin caedere = to cut down or kill. The joint is i, the noun is -cide, the adjective -cidal. Do not confuse it with -cide from cadere, to fall.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"germicide","def":"an agent (as heat or radiation or a chemical) that destroys microorganisms that might carry disease","ex":"The nurse wiped the counter with a powerful germicide to ensure no harmful bacteria could survive.","say":"JUR-muh-seyed","hook":"Latin caedere = to cut down or kill. The joint is i, the noun is -cide, the adjective -cidal. Do not confuse it with -cide from cadere, to fall.","o":"French"},{"w":"herbicide","def":"a chemical agent that destroys plants or inhibits their growth","ex":"The farmer applied herbicide carefully along the field's edge to keep weeds from spreading into the crop rows.","say":"HUR-bih-seyed","hook":"Latin caedere = to cut down or kill. The joint is i, the noun is -cide, the adjective -cidal. Do not confuse it with -cide from cadere, to fall.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"larvicide","def":"a chemical used to kill larval pests","ex":"The health department applied larvicide to the standing water in the park to prevent mosquito populations from exploding.","say":"LAR-vuh-syd","hook":"Latin caedere = to cut down or kill. The joint is i, the noun is -cide, the adjective -cidal. Do not confuse it with -cide from cadere, to fall.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"matricide","def":"a person who murders their mother","ex":"The ancient Greek play explored the tragic theme of matricide and its devastating consequences for an entire family.","say":"MAY-truh-syd","hook":"Latin caedere = to cut down or kill. The joint is i, the noun is -cide, the adjective -cidal. Do not confuse it with -cide from cadere, to fall.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"parricide","def":"someone who kills his or her parent","ex":"The ancient myth described a parricide who later suffered great guilt and punishment.","say":"PAIR-ih-syde","hook":"Latin caedere = to cut down or kill. The joint is i, the noun is -cide, the adjective -cidal. Do not confuse it with -cide from cadere, to fall.","o":"French"},{"w":"patricide","def":"a person who murders their father","ex":"The shocking trial revealed that the accused had committed patricide after years of abuse.","say":"PAY-trih-syd","hook":"Latin caedere = to cut down or kill. The joint is i, the noun is -cide, the adjective -cidal. Do not confuse it with -cide from cadere, to fall.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"regicide","def":"The killing of a king, or the person who kills one.","ex":"Shakespeare's Macbeth explores how one act of regicide unleashes guilt and chaos that destroys an entire kingdom.","say":"REJ-ih-syd","hook":"Latin caedere = to cut down or kill. The joint is i, the noun is -cide, the adjective -cidal. Do not confuse it with -cide from cadere, to fall.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"lousicide","def":"A chemical agent or treatment designed to kill lice.","ex":"The school nurse recommended a gentle lousicide shampoo to treat the outbreak of head lice in the class.","say":"LOO-sih-syd","hook":"Latin caedere = to cut down or kill. The joint is i, the noun is -cide, the adjective -cidal. Do not confuse it with -cide from cadere, to fall.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"coincide","def":"go with, fall together","ex":"The two events coincided.","say":"koh-ih-NSEYED","hook":"Latin caedere = to cut down or kill. The joint is i, the noun is -cide, the adjective -cidal. Do not confuse it with -cide from cadere, to fall.","o":"French"}]},{"title":"-phagous and -phagy — the eating words","category":"Advanced Orthography","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"Greek phagein, to eat, produces a family of four related endings that spellers routinely swap. The adjective describing a diet is -phagous. The name of the habit is -phagy. A medical condition of swallowing is -phagia. A thing that eats is -phage. All four spell the f sound p-h, because the Greek letter was phi, and all four join to the root with an o.","method":"<div class='trick'>MYRMECOPHAGY</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> mur-muh-COFF-uh-jee — the habit of eating ants\n<b>Parse.</b> myrmex = ant, plus o, plus phagy = eating\n<b>Rule.</b> the f sound is p h; the habit ends -phagy\n<b>Spell.</b> M Y R M E C O P H A G Y\n<b>Family.</b> myrmecophagous, myrmecophage, myrmecology","cards":[{"title":"Four endings, four jobs","body":"-phagous is the adjective: saprophagous eats decaying matter, arachnophagous eats spiders, geophagous eats earth. -phagy is the habit: entomophagy, mycophagy, geophagy, autophagy. -phagia is the medical condition: dysphagia difficult swallowing, aphagia inability to swallow, hyperphagia excessive eating. -phage is the eater itself: macrophage, coliphage, bacteriophage."},{"title":"The ph is not optional","body":"Greek phi is always written p-h in English, never f. Phagein, phone, photo, physics, philosophy, sphere, graph. So the eating words are phagous and phagy, never fagous or fagy. If a word is Greek and you hear an f, reach for p-h first."},{"title":"What is being eaten","body":"The root is the diet. Sapros rotten gives saprophagous. Entomon insect gives entomophagy. Mykes fungus gives mycophagy. Ge earth gives geophagy. Xylon wood gives xylophagy. Ophis snake gives ophiophagy. Ichthys fish gives ichthyophagy. Anthropos man gives anthropophagy, the technical word for cannibalism. Autos self gives autophagy, the cell's recycling of its own parts."},{"title":"The medical -phagia group","body":"In medicine the ending is -phagia and it names a swallowing disorder rather than a diet. Dysphagia, difficulty swallowing. Aphagia, inability to swallow. Odynophagia, painful swallowing. Hyperphagia, eating far too much. Aerophagia, swallowing air. If the definition sounds clinical, the ending is i a. Seventeen of these words are in core and 36 in the full library."}],"words":[{"w":"myrmecophagy","def":"The dietary practice or habit of feeding on ants, characteristic of various animals such as anteaters, pangolins, and aardvarks that have evolved to consume ants as a primary food source.","ex":"Myrmecophagy is a fascinating dietary strategy shared by animals as different as pangolins and echidnas.","say":"mur-meh-KOF-uh-jee","hook":"Greek phagein = to eat. Adjective -phagous, the habit -phagy, a disorder -phagia, an eater -phage. The ph is a Greek phi, never f.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"myrmecophage","def":"An animal that feeds primarily or exclusively on ants and termites, such as an anteater or aardvark, equipped with specialized adaptations for this diet.","ex":"The anteater is the most famous myrmecophage, using its long sticky tongue to devour thousands of ants daily.","say":"MUR-meh-koh-fayj","hook":"Greek phagein = to eat. Adjective -phagous, the habit -phagy, a disorder -phagia, an eater -phage. The ph is a Greek phi, never f.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"arachnophagous","def":"Describing an organism that feeds on spiders and other arachnids as a regular part of its diet.","ex":"The peculiar arachnophagous lizard darted across the desert sand, snapping up every spider in its path.","say":"uh-RAK-nuh-fay-guhs","hook":"Greek phagein = to eat. Adjective -phagous, the habit -phagy, a disorder -phagia, an eater -phage. The ph is a Greek phi, never f.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"saprophagous","def":"(of certain animals) feeding on dead or decaying animal matter","ex":"Vultures are saprophagous creatures that clean up carcasses left on the savanna.","say":"sap-ROF-ah-gus","hook":"Greek phagein = to eat. Adjective -phagous, the habit -phagy, a disorder -phagia, an eater -phage. The ph is a Greek phi, never f.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"entomophagy","def":"The practice of eating insects as food, which is common in many cultures around the world and is increasingly studied as a sustainable protein source.","ex":"Advocates of entomophagy argue that eating insects is a sustainable protein source that could help feed a growing global population.","say":"en-toh-MOF-uh-jee","hook":"Greek phagein = to eat. Adjective -phagous, the habit -phagy, a disorder -phagia, an eater -phage. The ph is a Greek phi, never f.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"heterophagy","def":"The process by which a cell digests material taken in from outside itself, such as food particles or foreign bodies, by enclosing them in a membrane-bound sac called a phagosome.","ex":"During heterophagy, a cell digests material it has absorbed from outside itself rather than from its own components.","say":"het-er-OF-uh-jee","hook":"Greek phagein = to eat. Adjective -phagous, the habit -phagy, a disorder -phagia, an eater -phage. The ph is a Greek phi, never f.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"glottophagy","def":"The process by which dominant languages absorb, suppress, or cause the extinction of smaller or minority languages, metaphorically described as one language eating another.","ex":"Glottophagy occurs when a dominant language slowly causes smaller languages to disappear.","say":"glot-OF-uh-jee","hook":"Greek phagein = to eat. Adjective -phagous, the habit -phagy, a disorder -phagia, an eater -phage. The ph is a Greek phi, never f.","o":"French"},{"w":"omophagia","def":"the eating of raw food","ex":"Ancient religious rituals sometimes involved omophagia as a symbol of primal connection to nature.","say":"oh-moh-FAY-jee-ah","hook":"Greek phagein = to eat. Adjective -phagous, the habit -phagy, a disorder -phagia, an eater -phage. The ph is a Greek phi, never f.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"dysphagia","def":"condition in which swallowing is difficult or painful","ex":"The elderly patient's dysphagia made every meal a slow and careful challenge requiring a specially thickened diet.","say":"dis-FAY-jee-uh","hook":"Greek phagein = to eat. Adjective -phagous, the habit -phagy, a disorder -phagia, an eater -phage. The ph is a Greek phi, never f.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"mycophagy","def":"the practice of eating fungi (especially mushrooms collected in the wild)","ex":"Her passion for mycophagy led her to spend every autumn weekend foraging for wild mushrooms.","say":"my-KOF-uh-jee","hook":"Greek phagein = to eat. Adjective -phagous, the habit -phagy, a disorder -phagia, an eater -phage. The ph is a Greek phi, never f.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"aphagia","def":"loss of the ability to swallow","ex":"After the stroke, the patient developed aphagia and required a feeding tube to receive nutrition.","say":"ay-FAY-jee-uh","hook":"Greek phagein = to eat. Adjective -phagous, the habit -phagy, a disorder -phagia, an eater -phage. The ph is a Greek phi, never f.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"hyperphagia","def":"An abnormal and excessive increase in appetite and food intake, often caused by a medical condition, brain injury, or certain medications.","ex":"The neurologist explained that hyperphagia in some patients results from damage to the hypothalamus, the brain region that normally signals when a person is full.","say":"hy-per-FAY-jee-uh","hook":"Greek phagein = to eat. Adjective -phagous, the habit -phagy, a disorder -phagia, an eater -phage. The ph is a Greek phi, never f.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"autophagy","def":"The biological process by which a cell breaks down and recycles its own damaged or unnecessary components.","ex":"Scientists discovered that autophagy helps cells survive periods of starvation by digesting their own proteins for energy.","say":"aw-TOF-uh-jee","hook":"Greek phagein = to eat. Adjective -phagous, the habit -phagy, a disorder -phagia, an eater -phage. The ph is a Greek phi, never f.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"macrophage","def":"a large phagocyte; some are fixed and other circulate in the blood stream","ex":"The macrophage engulfed the harmful bacteria like a tiny vacuum cleaner cleaning up the bloodstream.","say":"MA-kroh-fayj","hook":"Greek phagein = to eat. Adjective -phagous, the habit -phagy, a disorder -phagia, an eater -phage. The ph is a Greek phi, never f.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"geophagia","def":"eating earth, clay, chalk; occurs in some primitive tribes, sometimes in cases of nutritional deficiency or obsessive behavior","ex":"Archaeologists studying ancient tribes discovered evidence of geophagia, as certain types of clay were found stored near food supplies.","say":"jee-oh-FAY-jee-uh","hook":"Greek phagein = to eat. Adjective -phagous, the habit -phagy, a disorder -phagia, an eater -phage. The ph is a Greek phi, never f.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"coliphage","def":"a bacteriophage that infects the bacterium Escherichia coli","ex":"Scientists used a coliphage in the experiment to study how viruses attack the E. coli bacterium.","say":"KOH-lih-fayj","hook":"Greek phagein = to eat. Adjective -phagous, the habit -phagy, a disorder -phagia, an eater -phage. The ph is a Greek phi, never f.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"geophagy","def":"eating earth, clay, chalk; occurs in some primitive tribes, sometimes in cases of nutritional deficiency or obsessive behavior","ex":"Scientists believe that geophagy in some animals may help neutralize plant toxins in their diet.","say":"jee-OF-uh-jee","hook":"Greek phagein = to eat. Adjective -phagous, the habit -phagy, a disorder -phagia, an eater -phage. The ph is a Greek phi, never f.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"oligophagy","def":"The habit or condition of feeding on only a limited variety of foods, particularly in animals or insects that are restricted to eating just a few specific plant species or food types.","ex":"The caterpillar's oligophagy meant it would only eat leaves from two or three specific plant species.","say":"ol-ih-GOF-ah-jee","hook":"Greek phagein = to eat. Adjective -phagous, the habit -phagy, a disorder -phagia, an eater -phage. The ph is a Greek phi, never f.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"ophiophagy","def":"The practice or habit of eating snakes, whether by animals such as secretary birds and mongooses, or in certain human cultural practices.","ex":"The king cobra is famous for its ophiophagy, regularly hunting and consuming other snake species for meals.","say":"oh-fee-OF-ah-jee","hook":"Greek phagein = to eat. Adjective -phagous, the habit -phagy, a disorder -phagia, an eater -phage. The ph is a Greek phi, never f.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"xerophagy","def":"A strict religious diet of only dry, uncooked food and water.","ex":"Xerophagy was observed in the monastery during Lent, with monks surviving on minimal dry food.","say":"zeer-OF-ah-jee","hook":"Greek phagein = to eat. Adjective -phagous, the habit -phagy, a disorder -phagia, an eater -phage. The ph is a Greek phi, never f.","o":"Greek"}]},{"title":"-latry — worship of anything","category":"Advanced Orthography","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"Greek latreia meant service, especially religious service, and English uses -latry to name the worship of whatever is in the root. It is a compact and very regular family: joint of o, adjective in -latrous, worshipper in -later. The only real hazard is that a handful of unrelated words end in -later because they are agent nouns from Latin verbs, and those follow different rules entirely.","method":"<div class='trick'>ICHTHYOLATRY</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> ick-thee-OL-uh-tree — worship of fish\n<b>Parse.</b> ichthys = fish, plus o, plus latreia = worship\n<b>Trap.</b> ichthys begins i-c-h-t-h — two h letters, both silent-ish\n<b>Spell.</b> I C H T H Y O L A T R Y\n<b>Family.</b> ichthyolatrous, ichthyology, ichthyosaur","cards":[{"title":"The three forms","body":"The practice is -latry: idolatry, zoolatry, pyrolatry, bardolatry. The adjective is -latrous: idolatrous, monolatrous, ichthyolatrous. The person is -later: idolater. Note the vowel change — the noun has -latry with no e, but the person has -later. Idolatry and idolater, never idolatry and idolatrer."},{"title":"The objects of worship","body":"The root is what is worshipped. Eidolon an image gives idolatry. Zoon an animal gives zoolatry. Pyr fire gives pyrolatry. Ichthys fish gives ichthyolatry. Helios sun gives heliolatry. Lithos stone gives litholatry. Nekros the dead gives necrolatry. Kyon a dog gives cynolatry. Autos self gives autolatry. And Bardolatry, coined for the worship of Shakespeare, uses the English word bard."},{"title":"Idolatry loses a syllable","body":"Idolatry looks as if it should be idololatry, from eidolon plus latreia, and in fact that longer form exists in older writing. English contracted it, dropping one of the o-l sequences. So the modern spelling is i-d-o-l-a-t-r-y with a single l — the commonest error is doubling it to idollatry by analogy with idol plus latry."},{"title":"The -later imposters","body":"Several words end in -later without belonging to this family, because they are simply agent nouns from Latin verbs. Electroplater plates things. Desolater desolates. Inflater inflates. Dilater dilates. None of them worships anything, and none takes a -latrous adjective. Fourteen genuine worship words are in the core library and 30 in the full library."}],"words":[{"w":"ichthyolatry","def":"the worship of fish","ex":"The ancient coastal tribe practiced ichthyolatry, holding elaborate ceremonies to honor the sacred fish gods.","say":"ik-thee-OL-uh-tree","hook":"Greek latreia = worship or service. The joint is o, the worshipper is -later, the adjective -latrous. Watch out for -later words that are really agent nouns.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"martyrolatry","def":"The excessive veneration or worship of martyrs, often criticized as going beyond proper religious honor into idolatrous devotion.","ex":"Church reformers warned that martyrolatry had replaced genuine faith in some communities where shrines to martyrs outnumbered ordinary chapels.","say":"mar-tih-ROL-ah-tree","hook":"Greek latreia = worship or service. The joint is o, the worshipper is -later, the adjective -latrous. Watch out for -later words that are really agent nouns.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"demonolatry","def":"the acts or rites of worshiping devils","ex":"The ancient text described demonolatry as a forbidden practice that whole villages once feared.","say":"deh-mon-OL-uh-tree","hook":"Greek latreia = worship or service. The joint is o, the worshipper is -later, the adjective -latrous. Watch out for -later words that are really agent nouns.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"bardolatry","def":"the idolization of William Shakespeare","ex":"The professor warned his students that bardolatry could cloud their ability to critically analyze Shakespeare's flaws as a writer.","say":"bar-DOL-uh-tree","hook":"Greek latreia = worship or service. The joint is o, the worshipper is -later, the adjective -latrous. Watch out for -later words that are really agent nouns.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"idolatrous","def":"relating to or practicing idolatry","ex":"The ancient tribe was condemned by the prophet for its idolatrous practices, which included bowing before golden statues and offering elaborate sacrifices to carved figures made of wood and stone.","say":"eye-DAH-luh-truhs","hook":"Greek latreia = worship or service. The joint is o, the worshipper is -later, the adjective -latrous. Watch out for -later words that are really agent nouns.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"idiolatry","def":"the worship of yourself","ex":"His constant selfies and self-praise bordered on idiolatry, making his friends uncomfortable.","say":"id-ee-OL-uh-tree","hook":"Greek latreia = worship or service. The joint is o, the worshipper is -later, the adjective -latrous. Watch out for -later words that are really agent nouns.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"autolatry","def":"the worship of yourself","ex":"His friends joked that his constant selfies and mirror-gazing had crossed the line into pure autolatry.","say":"aw-TOL-uh-tree","hook":"Greek latreia = worship or service. The joint is o, the worshipper is -later, the adjective -latrous. Watch out for -later words that are really agent nouns.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"monolatry","def":"the worship of a single god but without claiming that it is the only god","ex":"Some scholars argue that early Israelite religion was a form of monolatry before becoming strict monotheism.","say":"moh-NOL-ah-tree","hook":"Greek latreia = worship or service. The joint is o, the worshipper is -later, the adjective -latrous. Watch out for -later words that are really agent nouns.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"pyrolatry","def":"the worship of fire","ex":"Ancient tribes practiced pyrolatry, believing flames connected them to the gods.","say":"py-ROL-uh-tree","hook":"Greek latreia = worship or service. The joint is o, the worshipper is -later, the adjective -latrous. Watch out for -later words that are really agent nouns.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"zoolatry","def":"the worship of animals","ex":"Ancient Egyptian religion included elements of zoolatry, as gods like Anubis and Bastet were depicted with the heads of animals.","say":"zoh-OL-uh-tree","hook":"Greek latreia = worship or service. The joint is o, the worshipper is -later, the adjective -latrous. Watch out for -later words that are really agent nouns.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"idolatry","def":"religious zeal; the willingness to serve God","ex":"Ancient prophets warned their people against idolatry, urging them to abandon the worship of carved stone figures.","say":"eye-DAH-luh-tree","hook":"Greek latreia = worship or service. The joint is o, the worshipper is -later, the adjective -latrous. Watch out for -later words that are really agent nouns.","o":"French"},{"w":"idolater","def":"a person who worships idols","ex":"The ancient text described how the idolater bowed before a golden statue each morning at sunrise.","say":"eye-DOL-uh-ter","hook":"Greek latreia = worship or service. The joint is o, the worshipper is -later, the adjective -latrous. Watch out for -later words that are really agent nouns.","o":"French"},{"w":"heliolatry","def":"the worship of the sun","ex":"Ancient Egyptians practiced a form of heliolatry, building grand temples to honor the sun god Ra.","say":"hee-lee-OL-uh-tree","hook":"Greek latreia = worship or service. The joint is o, the worshipper is -later, the adjective -latrous. Watch out for -later words that are really agent nouns.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"litholatry","def":"The worship of stones or rocks, practiced in certain ancient or indigenous religions that regarded particular stones as sacred or divine objects.","ex":"Archaeologists discovered evidence of litholatry at the site, where ancient people had worshipped a sacred standing stone.","say":"lih-THOL-ah-tree","hook":"Greek latreia = worship or service. The joint is o, the worshipper is -later, the adjective -latrous. Watch out for -later words that are really agent nouns.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"necrolatry","def":"The worship of or excessive reverence for the dead, including rituals, practices, or beliefs centered on honoring deceased individuals as sacred.","ex":"The anthropology class studied ancient cultures that practiced necrolatry, holding elaborate rituals to honor the dead.","say":"neh-KROL-uh-tree","hook":"Greek latreia = worship or service. The joint is o, the worshipper is -later, the adjective -latrous. Watch out for -later words that are really agent nouns.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"cynolatry","def":"The worship or excessive devotion to dogs, treating them as sacred or divine beings; extreme reverence for dogs beyond what is considered ordinary affection.","ex":"Her dedication to her ten rescue dogs led friends to joke about her cynolatry.","say":"sy-NOL-uh-tree","hook":"Greek latreia = worship or service. The joint is o, the worshipper is -later, the adjective -latrous. Watch out for -later words that are really agent nouns.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"hierolatry","def":"the worship of saints","ex":"The reformer condemned hierolatry as a distraction from worshipping God directly.","say":"hy-er-OL-ah-tree","hook":"Greek latreia = worship or service. The joint is o, the worshipper is -later, the adjective -latrous. Watch out for -later words that are really agent nouns.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"goniolatry","def":"The worship of or excessive reverence for angles; an obsessive devotion to geometric angles, used humorously or critically to describe an extreme focus on angular measurement.","ex":"The architect's colleagues jokingly accused him of goniolatry because he spent hours debating the precise angles of every corner in his designs before approving anything.","say":"goh-nee-OL-uh-tree","hook":"Greek latreia = worship or service. The joint is o, the worshipper is -later, the adjective -latrous. Watch out for -later words that are really agent nouns.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"electroplater","def":"a plater who uses electrolysis","ex":"The electroplater dipped the silver spoon into the solution and watched a thin gold layer form on its surface.","say":"ih-LEK-troh-play-ter","hook":"Greek latreia = worship or service. The joint is o, the worshipper is -later, the adjective -latrous. Watch out for -later words that are really agent nouns.","o":"French"},{"w":"desolater","def":"providing no shelter or sustenance","ex":"The fierce hurricane was a true desolater, tearing through the coastal village and leaving every building, every garden, and every road completely stripped of life and shelter.","say":"DEZ-uh-lay-ter","hook":"Greek latreia = worship or service. The joint is o, the worshipper is -later, the adjective -latrous. Watch out for -later words that are really agent nouns.","o":"Latin"}]},{"title":"Old English trades — monger, wright and smith","category":"Advanced Orthography","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"Amid a curriculum dominated by Latin and Greek, this family is entirely native. Old English mangere meant a trader, wryhta meant a maker or worker, and smitha meant one who works metal. All three still form compounds, and they behave completely unlike classical endings: no connecting vowel, no hyphen, and the first element is a plain English noun. Recognising that a word is Germanic rather than classical is itself the spelling decision.","method":"<div class='trick'>WHEELWRIGHT</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> WEEL-rite — a maker of wheels\n<b>Origin.</b> Old English hweol plus wryhta, a maker\n<b>Trap.</b> the maker ending is w-r-i-g-h-t, not r-i-t-e or w-r-i-t-e\n<b>Spell.</b> W H E E L W R I G H T\n<b>Family.</b> cartwright, playwright, shipwright, wainwright","cards":[{"title":"No connecting vowel","body":"This is the giveaway. Latin and Greek compounds need a joint — cupriferous, ochlocracy, myrmecophagy. Old English compounds simply push the two words together. Wheelwright. Playwright. Blacksmith. Coppersmith. Fishmonger. Scandalmonger. If you find yourself wanting to insert an o or an i, you are treating a Germanic word as a classical one."},{"title":"-wright is not -write","body":"A wright is a maker, from wryhta, and it is spelled w-r-i-g-h-t. A playwright makes plays; the word has nothing to do with writing them, which is why playwrite is always wrong. Cartwright, wheelwright, shipwright, wainwright, millwright. The gh is a fossil of a sound English stopped making centuries ago."},{"title":"-monger carries a sneer","body":"Mangere just meant trader, and the neutral sense survives in fishmonger, ironmonger and costermonger. But English gradually made the ending disapproving, so most modern coinages are hostile: scandalmonger, warmonger, rumourmonger, phrasemonger, meritmonger, powermonger, statemonger, poisonmonger. The spelling is always m-o-n-g-e-r, with the e."},{"title":"-smith outlived its metal","body":"A smith worked metal — blacksmith with iron, goldsmith, silversmith, coppersmith, whitesmith with tin. Then the ending broke free of the forge and now means simply a skilled maker: wordsmith, tunesmith, jokesmith, gunsmith, locksmith. Eleven of these three endings are in the core library and 32 in the full library."}],"words":[{"w":"wheelwright","def":"a person whose trade is to make wheels","ex":"The village wheelwright spent three days crafting the sturdy oak wheels for the farmer's new cart.","say":"w-EE-l-r-ay-t","hook":"Three native agent endings: mangere a trader, wryhta a maker, smitha a metalworker. Old English compounds join with no vowel and no hyphen.","o":"English"},{"w":"playwright","def":"someone who writes plays","ex":"The young playwright spent all summer writing a one-act comedy that her drama club would perform in the fall.","say":"PLAY-reyet","hook":"Three native agent endings: mangere a trader, wryhta a maker, smitha a metalworker. Old English compounds join with no vowel and no hyphen.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"cartwright","def":"English clergyman who invented the power loom (1743-1823)","ex":"We learned that Cartwright's invention of the power loom transformed the textile industry forever.","say":"KAH-rtreyet","hook":"Three native agent endings: mangere a trader, wryhta a maker, smitha a metalworker. Old English compounds join with no vowel and no hyphen.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"wagonwright","def":"a wagon maker","ex":"The wagonwright carefully fitted iron rims onto the wooden wheels in his workshop.","say":"WAG-un-ryt","hook":"Three native agent endings: mangere a trader, wryhta a maker, smitha a metalworker. Old English compounds join with no vowel and no hyphen.","o":"Dutch"},{"w":"coppersmith","def":"someone who makes articles from copper","ex":"The colonial coppersmith hammered glowing metal into pots and kettles for the townspeople.","say":"KAH-per-smihth","hook":"Three native agent endings: mangere a trader, wryhta a maker, smitha a metalworker. Old English compounds join with no vowel and no hyphen.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"blacksmith","def":"a smith who forges and shapes iron with a hammer and anvil","ex":"The blacksmith hammered the glowing iron into a perfect horseshoe while sparks flew everywhere.","say":"BLA-ksmihth","hook":"Three native agent endings: mangere a trader, wryhta a maker, smitha a metalworker. Old English compounds join with no vowel and no hyphen.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"arrowsmith","def":"a maker of arrows","ex":"The museum exhibit featured the tools and finished work of a skilled arrowsmith from the medieval period.","say":"A-roh-smihth","hook":"Three native agent endings: mangere a trader, wryhta a maker, smitha a metalworker. Old English compounds join with no vowel and no hyphen.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"jewelsmith","def":"A craftsperson who makes or works with jewels and fine jewelry; a jeweler skilled in metalwork and gemstone setting.","ex":"The jewelsmith spent weeks crafting an intricate gold ring set with tiny sapphires.","say":"JOO-ul-smith","hook":"Three native agent endings: mangere a trader, wryhta a maker, smitha a metalworker. Old English compounds join with no vowel and no hyphen.","o":"Anglo-Norman"},{"w":"tunesmith","def":"A person who composes or writes tunes and melodies, especially popular or commercial songs; a skilled creator of memorable musical pieces, much like a wordsmith crafts memorable language.","ex":"The famous tunesmith wrote over two hundred songs before he turned thirty.","say":"TYOON-smith","hook":"Three native agent endings: mangere a trader, wryhta a maker, smitha a metalworker. Old English compounds join with no vowel and no hyphen.","o":"French"},{"w":"jokesmith","def":"A person who is skilled at crafting or inventing jokes; someone who creates humor professionally or with great talent and ingenuity.","ex":"The class clown was such a skilled jokesmith that he always had a perfect punchline ready.","say":"JOHK-smith","hook":"Three native agent endings: mangere a trader, wryhta a maker, smitha a metalworker. Old English compounds join with no vowel and no hyphen.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"wordsmith","def":"a fluent and prolific writer","ex":"The poet was a true wordsmith, crafting phrases that lingered in your memory for days.","say":"WUR-dsmihth","hook":"Three native agent endings: mangere a trader, wryhta a maker, smitha a metalworker. Old English compounds join with no vowel and no hyphen.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"scandalmonger","def":"a person who spreads malicious gossip","ex":"The neighborhood scandalmonger couldn't resist whispering rumors about everyone on the street.","say":"SKAN-dl-mung-ger","hook":"Three native agent endings: mangere a trader, wryhta a maker, smitha a metalworker. Old English compounds join with no vowel and no hyphen.","o":"French"},{"w":"carpetmonger","def":"A person who deals in or sells carpets, or one who meddles excessively in carpet trade affairs.","ex":"The market was full of every carpetmonger in the city, each claiming to sell the finest rugs.","say":"KAR-pet-mung-ger","hook":"Three native agent endings: mangere a trader, wryhta a maker, smitha a metalworker. Old English compounds join with no vowel and no hyphen.","o":"French"},{"w":"poisonmonger","def":"A person who deals in, spreads, or promotes poison, either literally or figuratively, such as someone who spreads harmful rumors or toxic ideas.","ex":"The villain in the story was a notorious poisonmonger who sold deadly concoctions to the highest bidder.","say":"POY-zun-mung-gur","hook":"Three native agent endings: mangere a trader, wryhta a maker, smitha a metalworker. Old English compounds join with no vowel and no hyphen.","o":"French"},{"w":"phrasemonger","def":"A person who uses showy, empty, or overused phrases rather than expressing genuine or original ideas; one who deals in hollow rhetoric.","ex":"Critics called the politician a phrasemonger who offered slogans instead of real solutions.","say":"FRAYZ-mung-ger","hook":"Three native agent endings: mangere a trader, wryhta a maker, smitha a metalworker. Old English compounds join with no vowel and no hyphen.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"barbermonger","def":"A person excessively devoted to visiting barbers or obsessively concerned with personal grooming and appearance.","ex":"The vain nobleman was called a barbermonger because he visited the barber twice a day.","say":"BAR-bur-mung-gur","hook":"Three native agent endings: mangere a trader, wryhta a maker, smitha a metalworker. Old English compounds join with no vowel and no hyphen.","o":"Anglo-Norman"},{"w":"meritmonger","def":"A person who boasts excessively about their own merits, achievements, or virtues, or who trades on their reputation for personal gain.","ex":"The classmate was called a meritmonger for constantly boasting about his awards and honors.","say":"MAIR-it-mung-ger","hook":"Three native agent endings: mangere a trader, wryhta a maker, smitha a metalworker. Old English compounds join with no vowel and no hyphen.","o":"French"},{"w":"powermonger","def":"A person who aggressively seeks to gain, expand, or manipulate power over others, especially in political or social settings, often in a selfish or ruthless way.","ex":"The school's student council president was accused of being a powermonger who refused to share decisions with anyone.","say":"POW-ur-mung-gur","hook":"Three native agent endings: mangere a trader, wryhta a maker, smitha a metalworker. Old English compounds join with no vowel and no hyphen.","o":"French"},{"w":"statemonger","def":"A person who deals excessively or manipulatively in political affairs, scheming about matters of state for personal gain or influence.","ex":"The statemonger constantly meddled in other nations' politics to expand his own influence.","say":"STAYT-mung-gur","hook":"Three native agent endings: mangere a trader, wryhta a maker, smitha a metalworker. Old English compounds join with no vowel and no hyphen.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"fishmonger","def":"someone who sells fish","ex":"The fishmonger at the harbor market proudly displayed the morning's fresh catch on a bed of ice.","say":"FIH-shmah-ngger","hook":"Three native agent endings: mangere a trader, wryhta a maker, smitha a metalworker. Old English compounds join with no vowel and no hyphen.","o":"Old English"}]},{"title":"-gogue and -gogy — the leading words","category":"Advanced Orthography","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"Greek agein meant to lead, and agogos meant leading. English keeps three forms: the person is a -gogue, with a silent u-e borrowed through French; the practice is -gogy; and the adjective is -gogic. It is a tiny family, but every member is a trap, because the silent ue in gogue looks wrong, the gy in gogy looks wrong, and the two g letters can each be doubled by mistake.","method":"<div class='trick'>PEDAGOGUE</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> PED-uh-gog — a teacher\n<b>Origin.</b> Greek pais, a child, plus agogos, leading — one who leads children\n<b>Trap.</b> the word ends in a silent u-e that French added on the way in\n<b>Spell.</b> P E D A G O G U E\n<b>Family.</b> pedagogy, pedagogic, pedagogical","cards":[{"title":"Person, practice, adjective","body":"The person ends -gogue: pedagogue, demagogue, synagogue. The practice ends -gogy: pedagogy, demagogy. The adjective ends -gogic: pedagogic, demagogic, anagogic, hypnagogic. Three shapes, and you need all three, because a bee will ask for whichever one is least familiar."},{"title":"The silent ue is French","body":"Greek had no u-e on the end. It arrived when these words came through French, which used -gue to keep the g hard before a final e. The same thing gives us catalogue, dialogue, monologue, epilogue, analogue, fatigue, intrigue and vogue. So the ue is not decoration — without it the g would go soft."},{"title":"What each one leads","body":"Pais, a child, gives pedagogue, one who leads children. Demos, the people, gives demagogue, one who leads the mob. Syn, together, gives synagogue, a leading-together, hence a place of assembly. Ana, up, gives anagogic, leading the mind upward to spiritual meaning. Hypnos, sleep, gives hypnagogic, leading into sleep. Sialon, saliva, gives sialagogue, a substance that brings on saliva."},{"title":"A tiny family, entirely learnable","body":"Only eight of these are in the core library and 12 in the full, which makes this the smallest pattern in the course. That is precisely why it is worth a chapter — a set this small can be learned completely, and then it is simply never a risk again. Note synagogue in particular: the syn- prefix means it has no e after the n."}],"words":[{"w":"pedagogue","def":"someone who educates young people","ex":"The old pedagogue paced the classroom, quizzing students on Latin grammar.","say":"PED-uh-gog","hook":"Greek agogos = leading. The person ends -gogue with a silent ue; the practice ends -gogy; the adjective -gogic. All three keep the double g of the root.","o":"French"},{"w":"demagogue","def":"a political leader who seeks support by appealing to popular passions and prejudices","ex":"The historian warned that the fiery speaker was a demagogue who stirred up fear rather than offering real solutions.","say":"DEH-muh-gahg","hook":"Greek agogos = leading. The person ends -gogue with a silent ue; the practice ends -gogy; the adjective -gogic. All three keep the double g of the root.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"synagogue","def":"(Judaism) the place of worship for a Jewish congregation","ex":"The family walked to their synagogue every Friday evening to celebrate the start of the Sabbath.","say":"SIH-nuh-gawg","hook":"Greek agogos = leading. The person ends -gogue with a silent ue; the practice ends -gogy; the adjective -gogic. All three keep the double g of the root.","o":"French"},{"w":"pedagogy","def":"the principles and methods of instruction","ex":"The new teacher studied pedagogy to learn the best ways to explain math.","say":"PEH-duh-goh-jee","hook":"Greek agogos = leading. The person ends -gogue with a silent ue; the practice ends -gogy; the adjective -gogic. All three keep the double g of the root.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"demagogy","def":"impassioned appeals to the prejudices and emotions of the populace","ex":"The professor argued that demagogy thrives when citizens stop thinking critically about what their leaders say.","say":"DEH-muh-gah-jee","hook":"Greek agogos = leading. The person ends -gogue with a silent ue; the practice ends -gogy; the adjective -gogic. All three keep the double g of the root.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"pedagogic","def":"of or relating to pedagogy","ex":"The teacher tried a new pedagogic trick, turning the spelling lesson into a game.","say":"ped-uh-GOJ-ik","hook":"Greek agogos = leading. The person ends -gogue with a silent ue; the practice ends -gogy; the adjective -gogic. All three keep the double g of the root.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"demagogic","def":"characteristic of or resembling a demagogue","ex":"The editorial warned readers to recognize demagogic tactics when politicians use fear and oversimplified slogans to manipulate large crowds rather than presenting well-reasoned arguments supported by reliable facts and evidence.","say":"deh-muh-GAH-jihk","hook":"Greek agogos = leading. The person ends -gogue with a silent ue; the practice ends -gogy; the adjective -gogic. All three keep the double g of the root.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"anagogic","def":"based on or exemplifying anagoge","ex":"The priest offered an anagogic reading of the psalm, connecting its words to the promise of eternal life.","say":"an-ah-GOJ-ik","hook":"Greek agogos = leading. The person ends -gogue with a silent ue; the practice ends -gogy; the adjective -gogic. All three keep the double g of the root.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"hypnagogic","def":"sleep inducing","ex":"The warm bath had a hypnagogic effect, and she drifted off to sleep within minutes.","say":"hip-nuh-GOJ-ik","hook":"Greek agogos = leading. The person ends -gogue with a silent ue; the practice ends -gogy; the adjective -gogic. All three keep the double g of the root.","o":"French"},{"w":"ochlagogue","def":"A leader or agitator who stirs up, guides, or incites a mob or crowd, often for disruptive or political purposes.","ex":"The fiery speaker was accused of being an ochlagogue, using emotion to lead the crowd rather than reason.","say":"OK-luh-gog","hook":"Greek agogos = leading. The person ends -gogue with a silent ue; the practice ends -gogy; the adjective -gogic. All three keep the double g of the root.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"agogic","def":"Relating to slight changes in speed or emphasis used to bring out a piece of music's rhythm.","ex":"The pianist used agogic accents, slightly lengthening certain notes to give the melody more emotional weight.","say":"uh-GOJ-ik","hook":"Greek agogos = leading. The person ends -gogue with a silent ue; the practice ends -gogy; the adjective -gogic. All three keep the double g of the root.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"antisialagogue","def":"A substance that reduces or inhibits the production and flow of saliva.","ex":"The doctor prescribed an antisialagogue to reduce excess saliva before the procedure.","say":"an-tee-sy-AL-uh-gog","hook":"Greek agogos = leading. The person ends -gogue with a silent ue; the practice ends -gogy; the adjective -gogic. All three keep the double g of the root.","o":"Greek"}]},{"title":"-cede, -ceed, -sede — three, one and one","category":"Advanced Orthography","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"One of the most quoted spelling problems in English, and one of the most completely solvable. All of these words come from Latin cedere, to go or to yield, so the default is -cede. Exactly three verbs took a doubled e in English — succeed, proceed and exceed — and exactly one word is spelled with an s, supersede, because it comes from a different Latin verb altogether. Learn four words and the whole family is safe forever.","method":"<div class='trick'>SUPERSEDE</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> soo-per-SEED — to take the place of\n<b>Trap.</b> every relative is spelled c-e-d-e, so c looks right\n<b>Origin.</b> not cedere but sedere, to sit — to sit above, hence to replace\n<b>Spell.</b> S U P E R S E D E\n<b>Family.</b> supersession, supersedure — both keep the s","cards":[{"title":"The default is -cede","body":"Precede, recede, secede, concede, accede, intercede, antecede, retrocede, cede itself. All from Latin cedere, to go or yield, and all spelled c-e-d-e. If you have no specific reason to think otherwise, this is the answer, and it is the answer most of the time."},{"title":"Three take -ceed","body":"Succeed, proceed, exceed. That is the complete list. There is no rule behind it — they simply came into English through French forms that had a doubled e, and the spelling stuck. Three words, worth memorising as a trio: succeed, proceed, exceed."},{"title":"One takes -sede, and here is why","body":"Supersede is not from cedere at all. It is from Latin sedere, to sit — super plus sedere, to sit above, hence to take the place of. Different verb, different letter. That is why supercede, which looks so reasonable, is wrong: it would mean yielding above rather than sitting above. The related nouns keep the s too: supersession, supersedure."},{"title":"What the nouns do","body":"The nouns are the last piece and they are irregular in a helpful way. Precede gives precession and precedence. Succeed gives succession. Proceed gives procession and procedure — note that procedure loses the second e, so it is not proceedure. Exceed gives excess. Recede gives recession. If you know the noun you can usually work back to the verb."}],"words":[{"w":"supersede","def":"is to replace in power or acceptance","ex":"The bright new tablets will soon supersede the heavy old computers in our classroom.","say":"s-oo-p-ur-s-EE-d","hook":"Latin cedere = to go or yield. Only succeed, proceed and exceed take -ceed; only supersede takes -sede; everything else is -cede.","o":"French"},{"w":"intercede","def":"is to act in behalf of someone in difficulty","ex":"He interceded in the family dispute.","say":"ih-n-t-ur-s-EE-d","hook":"Latin cedere = to go or yield. Only succeed, proceed and exceed take -ceed; only supersede takes -sede; everything else is -cede.","o":"English"},{"w":"antecede","def":"be earlier in time; go back further","ex":"Experts agree that the invention of writing did antecede the rise of complex civilization by centuries.","say":"an-tih-SEED","hook":"Latin cedere = to go or yield. Only succeed, proceed and exceed take -ceed; only supersede takes -sede; everything else is -cede.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"precede","def":"is to go before","ex":"Stone tools precede bronze tools.","say":"p-r-ih-s-EE-d","hook":"Latin cedere = to go or yield. Only succeed, proceed and exceed take -ceed; only supersede takes -sede; everything else is -cede.","o":"English"},{"w":"concede","def":"to admit something is true, or to give in and accept defeat","ex":"After the final point, the other team conceded the match.","say":"kuhn-SEED","hook":"Latin cedere = to go or yield. Only succeed, proceed and exceed take -ceed; only supersede takes -sede; everything else is -cede.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"recede","def":"to move back or away to withdraw","ex":"After three days of heavy rainfall, the townspeople anxiously watched the floodwaters finally begin to recede, slowly pulling back from the streets and leaving a thick layer of mud behind.","say":"r-ih-s-EE-d","hook":"Latin cedere = to go or yield. Only succeed, proceed and exceed take -ceed; only supersede takes -sede; everything else is -cede.","o":"French"},{"w":"secede","def":"is to withdraw formally from an alliance","ex":"The tiny island once tried to secede and form its own independent little nation.","say":"s-ih-s-EE-d","hook":"Latin cedere = to go or yield. Only succeed, proceed and exceed take -ceed; only supersede takes -sede; everything else is -cede.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"succeed","def":"attain success or reach a desired goal","ex":"","say":"suh-KSEED","hook":"Latin cedere = to go or yield. Only succeed, proceed and exceed take -ceed; only supersede takes -sede; everything else is -cede.","o":"French"},{"w":"proceed","def":"continue talking;  he continued,","ex":"After the principal finished her morning announcements and the hallways had quieted down, she gave the signal for the science fair participants to proceed with setting up their colorful display boards.","say":"pruh-SEED","hook":"Latin cedere = to go or yield. Only succeed, proceed and exceed take -ceed; only supersede takes -sede; everything else is -cede.","o":"French"},{"w":"exceed","def":"be greater in scope or size than some standard","ex":"Their loyalty exceeds their national bonds.","say":"ih-KSEED","hook":"Latin cedere = to go or yield. Only succeed, proceed and exceed take -ceed; only supersede takes -sede; everything else is -cede.","o":"French"},{"w":"accede","def":"yield to another's wish or opinion","ex":"","say":"a-KSEED","hook":"Latin cedere = to go or yield. Only succeed, proceed and exceed take -ceed; only supersede takes -sede; everything else is -cede.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"cede","def":"give over; surrender or relinquish to the physical control of another","ex":"","say":"SEED","hook":"Latin cedere = to go or yield. Only succeed, proceed and exceed take -ceed; only supersede takes -sede; everything else is -cede.","o":"French"},{"w":"epicede","def":"A funeral song, elegy, or poem composed and performed in honor of a deceased person; a mournful ode sung at or about a grave.","ex":"The poet composed a moving epicede for her mentor, reading it aloud at the graveside while mourners stood silently in the cold autumn air.","say":"EP-ih-seed","hook":"Latin cedere = to go or yield. Only succeed, proceed and exceed take -ceed; only supersede takes -sede; everything else is -cede.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"supercede","def":"take the place or move into the position of","ex":"","say":"soo-per-SEED","hook":"Latin cedere = to go or yield. Only succeed, proceed and exceed take -ceed; only supersede takes -sede; everything else is -cede.","o":"French"},{"w":"unconcede","def":"To retract or take back a concession that was previously made; to reverse an earlier agreement or admission.","ex":"The debater tried to unconcede the point she had accidentally agreed to earlier.","say":"un-kun-SEED","hook":"Latin cedere = to go or yield. Only succeed, proceed and exceed take -ceed; only supersede takes -sede; everything else is -cede.","o":"French"}]},{"title":"Dutch — the largest untaught origin","category":"Origins Beyond the Big Four","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"Dutch is the biggest gap in the general course: the full library holds 1,550 words of Dutch origin, more than any other language outside the classical four and French. Four centuries of shipping, trade and shared North Sea coastline pushed them in. They are recognisable — short, concrete, and clustered around the sea, the kitchen and money — and they follow a small set of spelling habits that resolve most of the choices English otherwise leaves open.","method":"<div class='trick'>CABOOSE</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> kuh-BOOSS — the last car of a train\n<b>Origin.</b> Dutch kabuis, a ship's galley\n<b>Marker.</b> Dutch writes a long oo sound as double o, not as ou or u\n<b>Spell.</b> C A B O O S E\n<b>Family.</b> schooner, sloop, cruiser — the same oo habit","cards":[{"title":"Double o is the signature","body":"Dutch writes a long oo sound as oo, and English kept it. Caboose from kabuis. Schooner from schoener. Sloop from sloep. Cruiser from kruiser. Snoop from snoepen. Spook from spook. Stoop from stoep, a doorstep. Poppycock from pappekak. If a homely, concrete word has a doubled o, Dutch is a strong first guess."},{"title":"The sea vocabulary is almost all Dutch","body":"The Dutch ran the seas in the seventeenth century and English took the words wholesale. Yacht from jacht. Sloop, schooner, cruiser, keelhaul, maelstrom, hoist, smuggle, freight, deck, skipper, dock, buoy, walrus, bow, avast. Yacht is worth its own note — j-a-c-h-t became y-a-c-h-t, keeping a Dutch ch that English does not pronounce at all."},{"title":"The kitchen and the household too","body":"Alongside the sea came the home. Cookie from koekje, a little cake — note the -je diminutive, which English flattened to -ie. Coleslaw from koolsalade, cabbage salad. Waffle from wafel. Gherkin from gurkijn. Brandy from brandewijn, burnt wine. Scone, easel from ezel meaning donkey, and offal. Plain words for plain things."},{"title":"Dutch versus German — the tell","body":"Both use sch-, both build compounds, and confusing them is easy. Dutch words in English are usually short and old and about ships, food or money — sloop, cookie, guilder, stiver, polder. German loans are usually longer, later, and about ideas or science — zeitgeist, schadenfreude, gestalt, doppelganger, bildungsroman. Length and subject matter separate them more reliably than spelling does."}],"words":[{"w":"caboose","def":"the car at the rear of a freight train, used by the train crew","ex":"The sailors crowded around the caboose hoping to grab an extra bowl of stew before the storm hit.","say":"kuh-BOOS","hook":"Dutch words are short, concrete and nautical or domestic. Markers: oo for a long vowel, sch-, -kje/-je diminutives, aa, and hard g where English would soften it.","o":"Dutch"},{"w":"cruiser","def":"a car in which policemen cruise the streets; equipped with radiotelephonic communications to headquarters","ex":"The police cruiser rolled slowly down the foggy boulevard, its spotlight sweeping across darkened doorways.","say":"KROO-zer","hook":"Dutch words are short, concrete and nautical or domestic. Markers: oo for a long vowel, sch-, -kje/-je diminutives, aa, and hard g where English would soften it.","o":"Dutch"},{"w":"landscape","def":"an expanse of scenery that can be seen in a single view","ex":"The political landscape looks bleak without a change of administration.","say":"LA-ndskayp","hook":"Dutch words are short, concrete and nautical or domestic. Markers: oo for a long vowel, sch-, -kje/-je diminutives, aa, and hard g where English would soften it.","o":"Dutch"},{"w":"measles","def":"an acute and highly contagious viral disease marked by distinct red spots followed by a rash; occurs primarily in children","ex":"Before the vaccine was widely available, measles spread rapidly through schools and neighborhoods every winter.","say":"MEE-zuhlz","hook":"Dutch words are short, concrete and nautical or domestic. Markers: oo for a long vowel, sch-, -kje/-je diminutives, aa, and hard g where English would soften it.","o":"Dutch"},{"w":"furlough","def":"a leave of absence granted to an enlisted person","ex":"The soldier came home on furlough just in time for his sister's birthday.","say":"f-UR-l-oh","hook":"Dutch words are short, concrete and nautical or domestic. Markers: oo for a long vowel, sch-, -kje/-je diminutives, aa, and hard g where English would soften it.","o":"Dutch"},{"w":"boulevard","def":"a wide street or thoroughfare","ex":"Tall oak trees lined both sides of the grand boulevard leading to the city center.","say":"BUU-luh-vahrd","hook":"Dutch words are short, concrete and nautical or domestic. Markers: oo for a long vowel, sch-, -kje/-je diminutives, aa, and hard g where English would soften it.","o":"French"},{"w":"brandy","def":"distilled from wine or fermented fruit juice","ex":"The innkeeper warmed his cold hands around a small glass of brandy by the fireplace.","say":"BRA-ndee","hook":"Dutch words are short, concrete and nautical or domestic. Markers: oo for a long vowel, sch-, -kje/-je diminutives, aa, and hard g where English would soften it.","o":"Dutch"},{"w":"cookie","def":"any of various small flat sweet cakes (`biscuit' is the British term)","ex":"He grabbed a warm chocolate chip cookie from the tray as soon as it came out of the oven.","say":"KUU-kee","hook":"Dutch words are short, concrete and nautical or domestic. Markers: oo for a long vowel, sch-, -kje/-je diminutives, aa, and hard g where English would soften it.","o":"Dutch"},{"w":"coleslaw","def":"basically shredded cabbage","ex":"He piled tangy coleslaw on top of his pulled pork sandwich at the summer cookout.","say":"KOH-lslah","hook":"Dutch words are short, concrete and nautical or domestic. Markers: oo for a long vowel, sch-, -kje/-je diminutives, aa, and hard g where English would soften it.","o":"Dutch"},{"w":"gherkin","def":"any of various small cucumbers pickled whole","ex":"She placed a crisp gherkin on the side of her sandwich for a tangy crunch.","say":"GUR-kin","hook":"Dutch words are short, concrete and nautical or domestic. Markers: oo for a long vowel, sch-, -kje/-je diminutives, aa, and hard g where English would soften it.","o":"Dutch"},{"w":"knapsack","def":"a bag carried by a strap on your back or shoulder","ex":"She slung her knapsack over both shoulders and headed down the dusty path toward camp.","say":"NA-psak","hook":"Dutch words are short, concrete and nautical or domestic. Markers: oo for a long vowel, sch-, -kje/-je diminutives, aa, and hard g where English would soften it.","o":"German"},{"w":"mannequin","def":"a woman who wears clothes to display fashions","ex":"She was too fat to be a mannequin.","say":"MA-nuh-kihn","hook":"Dutch words are short, concrete and nautical or domestic. Markers: oo for a long vowel, sch-, -kje/-je diminutives, aa, and hard g where English would soften it.","o":"Dutch"},{"w":"onslaught","def":"a sudden and severe onset of trouble","ex":"","say":"AW-nslawt","hook":"Dutch words are short, concrete and nautical or domestic. Markers: oo for a long vowel, sch-, -kje/-je diminutives, aa, and hard g where English would soften it.","o":"Dutch"},{"w":"poppycock","def":"senseless talk","ex":"","say":"PAH-pee-kawk","hook":"Dutch words are short, concrete and nautical or domestic. Markers: oo for a long vowel, sch-, -kje/-je diminutives, aa, and hard g where English would soften it.","o":"Dutch"},{"w":"yacht","def":"a private cruising vessel","ex":"The billionaire's gleaming white yacht sailed smoothly into the harbor as the sun set.","say":"y-AH-t","hook":"Dutch words are short, concrete and nautical or domestic. Markers: oo for a long vowel, sch-, -kje/-je diminutives, aa, and hard g where English would soften it.","o":"English"},{"w":"waffle","def":"a batter cake baked in a special griddle that presses a grid pattern into it","ex":"He topped his crispy waffle with strawberries and a swirl of cream.","say":"WAH-fuhl","hook":"Dutch words are short, concrete and nautical or domestic. Markers: oo for a long vowel, sch-, -kje/-je diminutives, aa, and hard g where English would soften it.","o":"Dutch"},{"w":"walrus","def":"either of two large northern marine mammals having ivory tusks and tough hide over thick blubber","ex":"The enormous walrus hauled itself onto the rocky shore and let out a deep, rumbling bellow that echoed across the bay.","say":"WAW-lruhs","hook":"Dutch words are short, concrete and nautical or domestic. Markers: oo for a long vowel, sch-, -kje/-je diminutives, aa, and hard g where English would soften it.","o":"Danish"},{"w":"wainscot","def":"panel forming the lower part of an interior wall when it is finished differently from the rest of the wall","ex":"The dining room featured a dark oak wainscot that contrasted beautifully with the cream-colored plaster above it.","say":"WAY-nskuht","hook":"Dutch words are short, concrete and nautical or domestic. Markers: oo for a long vowel, sch-, -kje/-je diminutives, aa, and hard g where English would soften it.","o":"German"},{"w":"burgomaster","def":"a mayor of a municipality in Germany or Holland or Flanders or Austria","ex":"The burgomaster addressed the townspeople from the steps of the old city hall each week.","say":"BUR-guh-ma-ster","hook":"Dutch words are short, concrete and nautical or domestic. Markers: oo for a long vowel, sch-, -kje/-je diminutives, aa, and hard g where English would soften it.","o":"Dutch"},{"w":"beleaguer","def":"is to surround with troubles","ex":"Endless questions began to beleaguer the tired tour guide as the curious visitors crowded around.","say":"b-ih-l-EE-g-ur","hook":"Dutch words are short, concrete and nautical or domestic. Markers: oo for a long vowel, sch-, -kje/-je diminutives, aa, and hard g where English would soften it.","o":"Dutch"},{"w":"bantam","def":"any of various small breeds of fowl","ex":"The little bantam hen strutted proudly around the yard, smaller than all the other chickens.","say":"BA-ntuhm","hook":"Dutch words are short, concrete and nautical or domestic. Markers: oo for a long vowel, sch-, -kje/-je diminutives, aa, and hard g where English would soften it.","o":"Dutch"},{"w":"polder","def":"low-lying land that has been reclaimed and is protected by dikes (especially in the Netherlands)","ex":"Dutch farmers have cultivated the flat polder for generations, relying on a network of dikes and pumps to keep the sea at bay.","say":"POH-lder","hook":"Dutch words are short, concrete and nautical or domestic. Markers: oo for a long vowel, sch-, -kje/-je diminutives, aa, and hard g where English would soften it.","o":"Dutch"},{"w":"schooner","def":"a sailing vessel with two or more masts, with the foremast shorter than the mainmast","ex":"The tall schooner glided gracefully across the harbor, its white sails billowing in the summer breeze.","say":"SKOO-ner","hook":"Dutch words are short, concrete and nautical or domestic. Markers: oo for a long vowel, sch-, -kje/-je diminutives, aa, and hard g where English would soften it."},{"w":"keelhaul","def":"To punish a sailor by dragging them under the keel of a ship from one side to the other as a severe maritime punishment. The term is also used figuratively to mean to reprimand someone very harshly.","ex":"The fearsome captain threatened to keelhaul any sailor caught stealing food from the ship's dwindling supplies.","say":"KEEL-hawl","hook":"Dutch words are short, concrete and nautical or domestic. Markers: oo for a long vowel, sch-, -kje/-je diminutives, aa, and hard g where English would soften it.","o":"Dutch"},{"w":"maelstrom","def":"a large powerful or violent whirlpool","ex":"The tiny fishing boat was nearly swallowed by the churning maelstrom that formed between the rocky islands.","say":"m-AY-l-s-t-r-uh-m","hook":"Dutch words are short, concrete and nautical or domestic. Markers: oo for a long vowel, sch-, -kje/-je diminutives, aa, and hard g where English would soften it.","o":"English"},{"w":"stoop","def":"an inclination of the top half of the body forward and downward","ex":"Grandpa developed a noticeable stoop after years of bending over his workbench, his shoulders curving forward as if permanently hunched in concentration.","say":"STOOP","hook":"Dutch words are short, concrete and nautical or domestic. Markers: oo for a long vowel, sch-, -kje/-je diminutives, aa, and hard g where English would soften it.","o":"Dutch"},{"w":"snoop","def":"a spy who makes uninvited inquiries into the private affairs of others","ex":"The nosy neighbor was a known snoop who peeked through curtains whenever a car pulled up.","say":"SNOOP","hook":"Dutch words are short, concrete and nautical or domestic. Markers: oo for a long vowel, sch-, -kje/-je diminutives, aa, and hard g where English would soften it.","o":"Dutch"},{"w":"sloop","def":"a sailing vessel with a single mast set about one third of the boat's length aft of the bow","ex":"The sailor guided his small sloop through the narrow channel as the tide began to rise.","say":"SLOOP","hook":"Dutch words are short, concrete and nautical or domestic. Markers: oo for a long vowel, sch-, -kje/-je diminutives, aa, and hard g where English would soften it.","o":"Dutch"}]},{"title":"Yiddish and Hebrew — two layers, one alphabet","category":"Origins Beyond the Big Four","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"Two related but distinct sources, and telling them apart decides the spelling. Yiddish arrived through everyday speech in the last hundred and fifty years, so its words are informal and use German-style clusters: sch-, kn-, tz-. Hebrew arrived much earlier through scripture, so its words are formal and carry Hebrew grammar — the masculine plural -im, the feminine -oth, and a ch that stands for a sound English does not have. Together they account for over eight hundred words in the full library.","method":"<div class='trick'>CHUTZPAH</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> HOOTS-puh — extreme nerve\n<b>Origin.</b> Yiddish khutspe, from Hebrew hutspah\n<b>Markers.</b> the h sound is written ch; the ts is written tz; the final schwa is written a-h\n<b>Spell.</b> C H U T Z P A H\n<b>Family.</b> chutzpadik","cards":[{"title":"Yiddish clusters","body":"Yiddish freely begins words with clusters English otherwise avoids. Sch-: schlep, schmooze, schmaltz, schtick, schlock. Kn- with the k sounded: knish, knaidel. Ts written tz: chutzpah, kibitz, klutz, matzo, tchotchke. And a great many end in -el or -ke: bagel, dreidel, yarmulke, tchotchke. Recognise the cluster and you have the language."},{"title":"Hebrew plurals are the giveaway","body":"Hebrew brought its own grammar. The masculine plural is -im: seraphim, cherubim, kibbutzim, goyim, teraphim. The feminine plural is -oth or -ot: sephiroth, mitzvoth. So a word ending -im or -oth in a religious or scholarly context is almost certainly Hebrew, and the singular usually ends -ah or plain consonant: seraph, cherub, kibbutz."},{"title":"The ch that is not a ch","body":"Hebrew and Yiddish have a throat sound English lacks, and both are conventionally written ch. Chutzpah, challah, chanukah, chazan, brachah. It is never the ch of church and never the ch of chorus — it is closer to clearing your throat. When a Hebrew or Yiddish word begins with an h sound, ask whether it should be written ch."},{"title":"Scripture put them in early","body":"The Hebrew layer is a thousand years older than the Yiddish one, and it came through Bible translation, which is why those words feel formal. Jubilee, sabbath, shibboleth, behemoth, leviathan, messiah, jeremiad, cabal, hallelujah, amen, seraphim. Several arrived by way of Greek and Latin, which is why they look less foreign than the Yiddish ones do."}],"words":[{"w":"chutzpah","def":"(Yiddish) unbelievable gall; insolence; audacity","ex":"It took real chutzpah for the student to argue with the professor in front of the entire class.","say":"CHUH-tspah","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"Hebrew"},{"w":"shibboleth","def":"a favorite saying of a sect or political group","ex":"The politician's speech was full of every familiar shibboleth, but offered no new ideas for solving the problem.","say":"SHIH-buh-lehth","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"Hebrew"},{"w":"hallelujah","def":"means praise ye the Lord","ex":"The crowd shouted hallelujah when the long-awaited rain finally arrived after the drought.","say":"h-a-l-uh-l-OO-y-uh","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"Hebrew"},{"w":"seraphim","def":"an angel of the first order; usually portrayed as the winged head of a child","ex":"Medieval artists painted the seraphim as radiant winged beings surrounding the throne of heaven.","say":"sur-ah-FEEM","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"cherubim","def":"a sweet innocent baby","ex":"The painting on the ceiling was filled with rosy-cheeked cherubim floating among the clouds.","say":"CHAIR-uh-bim","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"Hebrew"},{"w":"behemoth","def":"someone or something that is abnormally large and powerful","ex":"The new aircraft carrier was a steel behemoth that dwarfed every other vessel in the harbor.","say":"buh-HEE-muhth","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"Hebrew"},{"w":"leviathan","def":"the largest or most massive thing of its kind","ex":"The cargo ship was a leviathan of the sea, so enormous it blocked the entire view of the harbor entrance.","say":"luh-VEYE-uh-thuhn","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"Hebrew"},{"w":"jeremiad","def":"a long and mournful complaint","ex":"A jeremiad against any form of government.","say":"jeh-ruh-MEYE-uhd","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"French"},{"w":"kosher","def":"food that fulfills the requirements of Jewish dietary law","ex":"The deli on the corner proudly displayed a certificate in the window confirming that every ingredient and every preparation method used in the kitchen met the strict standards required to be kosher.","say":"KOH-sher","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"Yiddish"},{"w":"nosh","def":"(Yiddish) a snack or light meal","ex":"After school, Marcus liked to nosh on crackers and cheese while doing his homework at the kitchen table.","say":"NAWSH","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"Yiddish"},{"w":"schlep","def":"(Yiddish) an awkward and stupid person","ex":"","say":"SHLEP","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"German"},{"w":"schmooze","def":"an informal conversation","ex":"At the company party, he managed to schmooze with every important executive in the room before dessert was served.","say":"SHMOOZ","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"Yiddish"},{"w":"glitch","def":"a defect or malfunction in a plan or machine","ex":"A tiny software glitch caused the entire school computer network to crash right before final exams.","say":"g-l-IH-ch","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"English"},{"w":"kibitz","def":"make unwanted and intrusive comments","ex":"Uncle Harold loved to kibitz during our card games, offering advice nobody asked for.","say":"KIB-its","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"Yiddish"},{"w":"klutz","def":"(Yiddish) a clumsy dolt","ex":"Being a total klutz, he managed to knock over the display of soup cans on his very first day at the store.","say":"KLUHTS","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel."},{"w":"maven","def":"someone who is dazzlingly skilled in any field","ex":"The food maven could identify every spice in the dish after just one bite.","say":"MAY-vuhn","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"Yiddish"},{"w":"mensch","def":"a decent responsible person with admirable characteristics","ex":"Everyone in the neighborhood agreed that Mr. Cohen was a true mensch because he always helped others without being asked.","say":"MEHNSH","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"German"},{"w":"kvetch","def":"(Yiddish) a constant complainer","ex":"","say":"KVEHCH","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"Yiddish"},{"w":"bialy","def":"flat crusty-bottomed onion roll","ex":"She grabbed a warm bialy from the bakery basket and spread it generously with cream cheese.","say":"bee-AH-lee","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"Yiddish"},{"w":"latke","def":"made of grated potato and egg with a little flour","ex":"At the Hanukkah party, Grandma served crispy latke with applesauce and sour cream on the side.","say":"LAHT-kuh","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"Yiddish"},{"w":"knish","def":"(Yiddish) a baked or fried turnover filled with potato or meat or cheese; often eaten as a snack","ex":"He bought a warm potato knish from the street cart and ate it while walking through the busy market.","say":"kuh-NISH","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"Yiddish"},{"w":"shtick","def":"(Yiddish) a little; a piece","ex":"","say":"SHTIHK","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"Yiddish"},{"w":"tchotchke","def":"(Yiddish) an attractive, unconventional woman","ex":"Every shelf in her room was cluttered with a tchotchke she had collected from her travels.","say":"CHAHCH-kee","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"Hebrew"},{"w":"yarmulke","def":"a skullcap worn by religious Jews (especially at prayer)","ex":"He carefully placed his yarmulke on his head before entering the synagogue for services.","say":"YAH-rmuh-lkuh","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"Yiddish"},{"w":"matzo","def":"brittle flat bread eaten at Passover","ex":"She crumbled matzo into the soup to make traditional matzo ball broth.","say":"MAHT-soh","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"Hebrew"},{"w":"challah","def":"(Judaism) a loaf of white bread containing eggs and leavened with yeast; often formed into braided loaves and glazed with eggs before baking","ex":"The family gathered around the table as grandmother sliced the braided challah for the Sabbath meal.","say":"HAH-luh","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"Yiddish"},{"w":"golem","def":"(Jewish folklore) an artificially created human being that is given life by supernatural means","ex":"In the old legend, the rabbi breathed life into a golem of clay to protect the people of the village.","say":"GOH-luhm","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"Hebrew"},{"w":"verklempt","def":"so overcome with emotion that you can hardly speak","ex":"When the trophy was placed in her hands, the champion went completely verklempt and could only wave at her parents.","say":"fer-KLEMPT","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"Yiddish"},{"w":"dybbuk","def":"(Jewish folklore) a demon that enters the body of a living person and controls that body's behavior","ex":"In the old tale, the rabbi performed a ceremony to drive the dybbuk out of the possessed girl.","say":"DIB-uk","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"Hebrew"},{"w":"cabal","def":"a clique (often secret) that seeks power usually through intrigue","ex":"In the mystery novel, a secret cabal met at midnight to plot their clever scheme.","say":"kuh-BAHL","hook":"Yiddish markers: initial sch-, kn-, ts-/tz-, final -el and -ke. Hebrew markers: -im and -oth plurals, ch for a throat sound, h for a final vowel.","o":"French"}]},{"title":"Russian and Slavic — suffixes that name things","category":"Origins Beyond the Big Four","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"Slavic words entered English in identifiable waves — Tsarist court and countryside, then Soviet politics, then space and weapons — and they are unusually easy to spell once you know the suffixes, because Slavic languages build meaning by stacking endings that survive transliteration intact. The second useful fact is that Slavic tolerates consonant clusters English does not, so a word with an improbable pile of consonants at the start is a strong Slavic signal.","method":"<div class='trick'>PERESTROIKA</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> pair-uh-STROY-kuh — restructuring\n<b>Parse.</b> pere- (re-) + stroi (build) + -ka (noun ending)\n<b>Markers.</b> the oy sound is written o-i; the ending is -ka\n<b>Spell.</b> P E R E S T R O I K A\n<b>Family.</b> glasnost, apparatchik — the same Soviet wave","cards":[{"title":"The suffixes do the work","body":"-nik names a person or adherent: sputnik a fellow traveller, refusenik, beatnik, kibitznik. -ka makes a noun, often small or feminine: babushka, matryoshka, balalaika, troika, perestroika, dacha. -ov and -ev end surnames that became words: molotov, kalashnikov, stroganoff, pavlova. -ism and -ist attach to leaders: stalinism, leninism, trotskyism."},{"title":"oi, not oy","body":"Russian transliteration writes the oy sound as o-i. Perestroika, not perestroyka. Troika, not troyka. Coulibiac keeps its own oddity. This is a small point that decides several words, because English instinct reaches for o-y in this position — boy, toy, employ — and Slavic transliteration never does."},{"title":"Consonant clusters English would refuse","body":"Slavic languages happily begin words with clusters English avoids: zd-, vz-, chsh-, shch-. Chastushka carries a shch cluster in the middle. Borscht ends in three consonants. Sputnik, glasnost and gulag stack sounds English would break up with a vowel. If a word looks unpronounceable at the start or end, Slavic is a reasonable guess."},{"title":"Three waves, three subjects","body":"The oldest layer is rural and courtly: steppe, tundra, samovar, sable, kopeck, ruble, czar, vodka, kasha. The Soviet layer is political: commissar, gulag, apparatchik, glasnost, perestroika, agitprop, nomenklatura. The newest is technical and military: sputnik, cosmonaut, kalashnikov, molotov. Twenty-three sit in the core library and 438 in the full library."}],"words":[{"w":"perestroika","def":"an economic policy adopted in the former Soviet Union; intended to increase automation and labor efficiency but it led eventually to the end of central planning in the Russian economy","ex":"The policy of perestroika brought sweeping economic reforms to the Soviet Union in the 1980s.","say":"peh-ruh-STROY-kuh","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"Russian"},{"w":"intelligentsia","def":"an educated and intellectual elite","ex":"The city's intelligentsia met weekly to discuss literature, science, and the future of their country.","say":"ih-nteh-luh-JEH-ntsee-uh","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"Russian"},{"w":"apparatchik","def":"a humorous but derogatory term for an official of a large organization (especially a political organization)","ex":"In the play, a fussy apparatchik stamped every form twice before letting anyone pass.","say":"a-per-A-chihk","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"Russian"},{"w":"kalashnikov","def":"a type of submachine gun made in Russia","ex":"The documentary showed how the Kalashnikov became one of the most widely distributed firearms in the world after World War II.","say":"kuh-LA-shnih-kahv","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"Russian"},{"w":"chastushka","def":"a short, witty Russian folk song, usually funny or teasing","ex":"The performer sang a witty chastushka that had the entire village crowd roaring with laughter at its clever rhymes.","say":"chah-STOOSH-kah","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"Russian"},{"w":"matryoshka","def":"A set of brightly painted hollow wooden Russian dolls of decreasing size, each one placed inside the next larger one; also called a nesting doll or Russian doll.","ex":"The matryoshka my grandmother brought from Moscow had seven dolls nested inside one another.","say":"mah-TRYOSH-kuh","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"Russian"},{"w":"balalaika","def":"a Russian musical instrument","ex":"The musician plucked the strings of his balalaika and filled the room with its distinctive, twangy melody.","say":"bal-uh-LY-kuh","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"Russian"},{"w":"babushka","def":"a woman's scarf","ex":"The elderly woman tied a floral babushka tightly under her chin before stepping out into the cold wind.","say":"b-uh-b-UH-sh-k-uh","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"Russian"},{"w":"stroganoff","def":"A Russian dish of sautéed pieces of beef served in a sauce of sour cream, named after the Stroganov family.","ex":"After a cold afternoon of raking leaves, nothing warmed the family up like a steaming bowl of beef stroganoff over egg noodles.","say":"s-t-r-OH-g-uh-n-aw-f","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"English"},{"w":"coulibiac","def":"A Russian dish of fish, especially salmon, baked inside a flaky pastry crust along with rice, mushrooms, onions, and hard-boiled eggs, often served at formal occasions.","ex":"The chef prepared an elegant coulibiac, wrapping salmon and rice in a golden pastry crust for the banquet.","say":"koo-lee-BYAK","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"French"},{"w":"cosmonaut","def":"a person trained to travel in a spacecraft","ex":"The Russians called their astronauts cosmonauts.","say":"KAW-zmuh-nawt","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"commissar","def":"an official of the Communist Party who was assigned to teach party principles to a military unit","ex":"The commissar arrived at the regiment to ensure that the soldiers remained loyal to the party's ideology.","say":"KAH-muh-sahr","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"Russian"},{"w":"samovar","def":"a metal urn with a spigot at the base; used in Russia to boil water for tea","ex":"The gleaming copper samovar sat at the center of the table, keeping tea hot for all the guests throughout the long winter evening.","say":"SA-muh-vahr","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"Russian"},{"w":"sputnik","def":"a Russian artificial satellite","ex":"Sputnik was the first man-made satellite to orbit the earth.","say":"SPUH-tnihk","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"Russian"},{"w":"glasnost","def":"a policy of the Soviet government allowing freer discussion of social problems","ex":"Under glasnost, Soviet citizens began to speak more openly about problems that had been hidden from public view for decades.","say":"GLA-snah-st","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"Russian"},{"w":"troika","def":"a team of 3 horses driven abreast","ex":"A troika of decorated horses pulled the sleigh across the snow-covered Russian countryside at full gallop.","say":"t-r-OY-k-uh","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"Russian"},{"w":"tundra","def":"a vast treeless plain in the Arctic regions where the subsoil is permanently frozen","ex":"The caribou migrated across the frozen tundra in a massive herd that stretched as far as the eye could see.","say":"TUH-ndruh","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"Russian"},{"w":"steppe","def":"extensive plain without trees (associated with eastern Russia and Siberia)","ex":"Nomadic tribes crossed the vast steppe on horseback, traveling for days without seeing a single tree.","say":"STEHP","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"Russian"},{"w":"borscht","def":"a Russian or Polish soup usually containing beet juice as a foundation","ex":"My grandmother ladled a steaming bowl of borscht, its deep red color coming from fresh beets.","say":"BAWRSHT","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"Russian"},{"w":"kasha","def":"boiled or baked buckwheat","ex":"Grandmother served steaming bowls of kasha topped with caramelized onions on cold winter nights.","say":"KAH-shuh","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"Russian"},{"w":"beluga","def":"valuable source of caviar and isinglass; found in Black and Caspian seas","ex":"The chef placed a small spoonful of beluga caviar on the cracker and announced it was among the most prized delicacies in the world.","say":"bih-LOO-guh","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"Russian"},{"w":"mammoth","def":"Huge; enormous in size. Also a giant hairy elephant of the Ice Age.","ex":"Cleaning the whole garage turned into a mammoth job.","say":"MAM-uth","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"English"},{"w":"dacha","def":"Russian country house","ex":"Every summer, the family escaped the heat of Moscow by retreating to their cozy dacha in the pine forest outside the city.","say":"DAH-chuh","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"Russian"},{"w":"kopeck","def":"A small Russian coin; a hundred of them equal one ruble.","ex":"He found a single kopeck on the sidewalk, worth so little it barely bought a moment's thought.","say":"KOH-pek","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"Russian"},{"w":"Soviet","def":"an elected government council in a communist country","ex":"During the Cold War, a Soviet council made sweeping decisions that affected millions of citizens living under communist rule across a vast territory.","say":"SOH-vee-uht","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"Russian"},{"w":"Bolshevik","def":"emotionally charged terms used to refer to extreme radicals or revolutionaries","ex":"","say":"BOH-lshuh-vihk","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"Russian"},{"w":"polka","def":"music with a fast, bouncy beat played for a lively couples' dance","ex":"The accordion struck up a lively polka and the whole village flooded onto the dance floor.","say":"POH-lkah","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"Czech"},{"w":"robot","def":"a mechanism that can move automatically","ex":"The factory installed a new robot that could assemble car parts three times faster than any human worker.","say":"ROH-baht","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"Czech"},{"w":"goulash","def":"a rich meat stew highly seasoned with paprika","ex":"On a cold winter evening, nothing was more comforting than a steaming bowl of beef goulash.","say":"GOO-lahsh","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"Hungarian"},{"w":"kielbasa","def":"A type of Polish smoked sausage, typically made from pork and seasoned with garlic and spices, widely enjoyed throughout Eastern Europe and Polish-American communities. It is commonly grilled, boiled, or fried.","ex":"My grandmother brought a spicy kielbasa to the family barbecue, and it disappeared in minutes.","say":"kee-LBAH-suh","hook":"Slavic suffixes are the whole game: -nik a person, -ka a small thing, -ov/-ev a name, -shchina a condition. Sound clusters English avoids are normal here.","o":"Polish"}]},{"title":"Portuguese — the first global vocabulary","category":"Origins Beyond the Big Four","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"Portuguese sailors reached Africa, Brazil, India, China and Japan before any other Europeans, and English took a great deal of its first global vocabulary from them second-hand. So a Portuguese loanword is often not originally Portuguese at all — it is a Tupi, Malay, Bantu or Konkani word wearing Portuguese spelling. That is the key insight: you spell the Portuguese route, not the ultimate source.","method":"<div class='trick'>ALBATROSS</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> AL-buh-tross\n<b>Origin.</b> Portuguese alcatraz, a pelican, reshaped by Latin albus meaning white\n<b>Marker.</b> Portuguese al- from Arabic, then an English respelling toward albus\n<b>Spell.</b> A L B A T R O S S\n<b>Family.</b> Alcatraz — the same word, unchanged","cards":[{"title":"The -ão ending","body":"Portuguese has a nasal ending written -ão, and English had no way to write it. It came out as -oon or -on. Galleon from galeão. Monsoon by way of monção. Festoon, dragoon and cartoon reached English through the same French and Portuguese route. When a word ends -oon and is about ships, weather or the tropics, suspect Portuguese."},{"title":"Portuguese as a courier","body":"Many of these words are only Portuguese in transit. Jacaranda and piranha are Tupi from Brazil. Cobra is Portuguese cobra de capelo, hooded snake. Mango is Tamil mankay, Portuguese manga. Teak is Malayalam tekka. Pagoda came through Portuguese pagode. Molasses from melaço. The Portuguese spelling is the one English copied, so that is the one to learn."},{"title":"Brazilian words keep their shape","body":"Words that came straight from Brazil are unusually well preserved. Capoeira, the martial art and dance. Jacaranda, the tree. Anhinga, the darter bird. Piranha, with its Portuguese nh for the ny sound. Feijoada, the bean stew. Samba. Note nh and lh — Portuguese writes the ny and ly sounds that way, where Spanish uses ñ and ll."},{"title":"Portuguese versus Spanish","body":"The two look alike and the tell is in a few letters. Portuguese uses nh and lh where Spanish uses ñ and ll: piranha against señor. Portuguese uses ç and ão, Spanish does not. Portuguese words in English cluster around Brazil, Africa and the sea routes to Asia; Spanish ones cluster around Mexico, the American Southwest and the Caribbean. Twenty-two are in the core library and 271 in the full library."}],"words":[{"w":"albatross","def":"a very large white seabird with long narrow wings that soars over the ocean","ex":"She was an albatross around his neck.","say":"A-lbuh-trahs","hook":"Portuguese -ão became English -oon or -on. Look for -inho diminutives, final -a, and words that Portuguese carried from Africa, Brazil and Asia into English.","o":"Portuguese"},{"w":"jacaranda","def":"an important Brazilian timber tree yielding a heavy hard dark-colored wood streaked with black","ex":"Every spring the streets of Pretoria are canopied in violet as thousands of jacaranda trees burst into bloom.","say":"jak-uh-RAN-duh","hook":"Portuguese -ão became English -oon or -on. Look for -inho diminutives, final -a, and words that Portuguese carried from Africa, Brazil and Asia into English.","o":"Portuguese"},{"w":"capoeira","def":"A Brazilian martial art that combines elements of dance, acrobatics, and music, developed by enslaved Africans in Brazil as a disguised form of self-defense.","ex":"The performers blended dance and martial arts in a dazzling display of capoeira that left the audience breathless.","say":"kap-oh-AY-rah","hook":"Portuguese -ão became English -oon or -on. Look for -inho diminutives, final -a, and words that Portuguese carried from Africa, Brazil and Asia into English.","o":"Portuguese"},{"w":"flamingo","def":"large pink to scarlet web-footed wading bird with down-bent bill; inhabits brackish lakes","ex":"A solitary flamingo stood on one leg at the edge of the shallow lagoon, its bright pink feathers vivid against the blue water.","say":"fluh-MIH-nggoh","hook":"Portuguese -ão became English -oon or -on. Look for -inho diminutives, final -a, and words that Portuguese carried from Africa, Brazil and Asia into English.","o":"Portuguese"},{"w":"molasses","def":"thick dark syrup produced by boiling down juice from sugar cane; especially during sugar refining","ex":"Grandma's gingerbread cookies were extraordinarily chewy because she used an extra spoonful of dark molasses in the batter.","say":"m-uh-l-A-s-uh-z","hook":"Portuguese -ão became English -oon or -on. Look for -inho diminutives, final -a, and words that Portuguese carried from Africa, Brazil and Asia into English.","o":"English"},{"w":"marmalade","def":"a preserve made of the pulp and rind of citrus fruits","ex":"She spread a thick layer of orange marmalade on her toast and watched the sunlight pour through the kitchen global.window.","say":"MAH-rmuh-layd","hook":"Portuguese -ão became English -oon or -on. Look for -inho diminutives, final -a, and words that Portuguese carried from Africa, Brazil and Asia into English.","o":"French"},{"w":"commando","def":"a member of a military unit trained as shock troops for hit-and-run raids","ex":"The lone commando slipped behind enemy lines under cover of darkness to complete the mission.","say":"kuh-MA-ndoh","hook":"Portuguese -ão became English -oon or -on. Look for -inho diminutives, final -a, and words that Portuguese carried from Africa, Brazil and Asia into English.","o":"Afrikaans"},{"w":"monsoon","def":"a seasonal wind in southern Asia; blows from the southwest (bringing rain) in summer and from the northeast in winter","ex":"The monsoon arrived in July, flooding the streets and filling the dry riverbeds overnight.","say":"mah-NSOON","hook":"Portuguese -ão became English -oon or -on. Look for -inho diminutives, final -a, and words that Portuguese carried from Africa, Brazil and Asia into English.","o":"Arabic"},{"w":"piranha","def":"a small South American fish","ex":"The explorer nervously watched the piranha swarm beneath the surface before deciding not to cross the river.","say":"p-ih-r-A-n-h-uh","hook":"Portuguese -ão became English -oon or -on. Look for -inho diminutives, final -a, and words that Portuguese carried from Africa, Brazil and Asia into English.","o":"English"},{"w":"anhinga","def":"fish-eating bird of warm inland waters having a long flexible neck and slender sharp-pointed bill","ex":"The anhinga perched on a cypress branch with its wings spread wide, drying its feathers in the Florida sun after a dive.","say":"an-HING-gah","hook":"Portuguese -ão became English -oon or -on. Look for -inho diminutives, final -a, and words that Portuguese carried from Africa, Brazil and Asia into English.","o":"Portuguese"},{"w":"feijoada","def":"Brazil's most celebrated national dish, a hearty stew of black beans slow-cooked with a variety of salted and smoked pork and beef cuts, traditionally served with rice, collard greens, and orange slices.","ex":"The family gathered every Saturday around a huge pot of feijoada, the black bean and pork stew simmering for hours.","say":"fay-ZHWAH-dah","hook":"Portuguese -ão became English -oon or -on. Look for -inho diminutives, final -a, and words that Portuguese carried from Africa, Brazil and Asia into English.","o":"Portuguese"},{"w":"palaver","def":"flattery intended to persuade","ex":"Coach Williams had no patience for palaver before the game and told the team to stop chattering and focus on their strategy.","say":"puh-LAV-er","hook":"Portuguese -ão became English -oon or -on. Look for -inho diminutives, final -a, and words that Portuguese carried from Africa, Brazil and Asia into English.","o":"Portuguese"},{"w":"mangrove","def":"a tropical tree or shrub bearing fruit that germinates while still on the tree and having numerous prop roots that eventually form an impenetrable mass and are important in land building","ex":"The mangrove forest along the coastline provided shelter for hundreds of fish species among its tangled, stilted roots.","say":"MA-ngrohv","hook":"Portuguese -ão became English -oon or -on. Look for -inho diminutives, final -a, and words that Portuguese carried from Africa, Brazil and Asia into English.","o":"Portuguese"},{"w":"tapioca","def":"granular preparation of cassava starch used to thicken especially puddings","ex":"Grandma's tapioca pudding was thick and creamy, dotted with soft white pearls that burst pleasantly on the tongue.","say":"tap-ee-OH-kuh","hook":"Portuguese -ão became English -oon or -on. Look for -inho diminutives, final -a, and words that Portuguese carried from Africa, Brazil and Asia into English.","o":"Portuguese"},{"w":"veranda","def":"a porch along the outside of a building (sometimes partly enclosed)","ex":"The family gathered on the wide veranda each evening to watch the sun sink behind the distant hills.","say":"ver-A-nduh","hook":"Portuguese -ão became English -oon or -on. Look for -inho diminutives, final -a, and words that Portuguese carried from Africa, Brazil and Asia into English.","o":"Hindi"},{"w":"cuspidor","def":"a receptacle for spit (usually in a public place)","ex":"A brass cuspidor sat in the corner of the old saloon, a relic of a less refined era.","say":"KUS-pih-dor","hook":"Portuguese -ão became English -oon or -on. Look for -inho diminutives, final -a, and words that Portuguese carried from Africa, Brazil and Asia into English.","o":"Portuguese"},{"w":"pagoda","def":"an Asian temple; usually a pyramidal tower with an upward curving roof","ex":"The ancient pagoda rose above the treetops, its curved golden rooftops shimmering in the morning light.","say":"puh-GOH-duh","hook":"Portuguese -ão became English -oon or -on. Look for -inho diminutives, final -a, and words that Portuguese carried from Africa, Brazil and Asia into English.","o":"Portuguese"},{"w":"madeira","def":"a Brazilian river; tributary of the Amazon River","ex":"The Madeira River winds through the Amazon basin carrying nutrients from distant mountains.","say":"muh-DIH-ruh","hook":"Portuguese -ão became English -oon or -on. Look for -inho diminutives, final -a, and words that Portuguese carried from Africa, Brazil and Asia into English.","o":"Portuguese"},{"w":"labrador","def":"a rugged mainland region of eastern Canada","ex":"The rugged wilderness of Labrador attracts adventurers who love ice fishing and remote hiking trails.","say":"LA-bruh-dawr","hook":"Portuguese -ão became English -oon or -on. Look for -inho diminutives, final -a, and words that Portuguese carried from Africa, Brazil and Asia into English.","o":"Portuguese"},{"w":"jaguar","def":"the largest cat in the western hemisphere","ex":"The jaguar crept silently through the dense rainforest, its spotted coat blending perfectly with the shadows.","say":"j-A-g-w-ah-r","hook":"Portuguese -ão became English -oon or -on. Look for -inho diminutives, final -a, and words that Portuguese carried from Africa, Brazil and Asia into English.","o":"Portuguese"},{"w":"pataca","def":"the basic unit of money in Macao","ex":"The tourist exchanged her dollars for patacas before exploring the bustling markets of Macao.","say":"puh-TAH-kuh","hook":"Portuguese -ão became English -oon or -on. Look for -inho diminutives, final -a, and words that Portuguese carried from Africa, Brazil and Asia into English.","o":"Portuguese"},{"w":"jacana","def":"A tropical wading bird known for its remarkably long toes and claws, which allow it to walk across floating vegetation on the surface of ponds and marshes.","ex":"The jacana walked effortlessly across the lily pads, its long toes spreading its weight perfectly.","say":"zhah-suh-NAH","hook":"Portuguese -ão became English -oon or -on. Look for -inho diminutives, final -a, and words that Portuguese carried from Africa, Brazil and Asia into English.","o":"Portuguese"},{"w":"macaw","def":"long-tailed brilliantly colored parrot of Central America and South America; among the largest and showiest of parrots","ex":"The brilliant scarlet macaw perched on a branch and screeched loudly at the tourists passing below.","say":"muh-KAW","hook":"Portuguese -ão became English -oon or -on. Look for -inho diminutives, final -a, and words that Portuguese carried from Africa, Brazil and Asia into English.","o":"Portuguese"},{"w":"mango","def":"large evergreen tropical tree cultivated for its large oval fruit","ex":"She bit into the ripe mango and let the sweet, golden juice drip down her chin.","say":"MA-nggoh","hook":"Portuguese -ão became English -oon or -on. Look for -inho diminutives, final -a, and words that Portuguese carried from Africa, Brazil and Asia into English.","o":"Portuguese"},{"w":"teak","def":"hard, strong yellowish-brown wood used for furniture and ships, resistant to insects","ex":"The captain chose teak for the ship's deck because it could withstand moisture and heavy use.","say":"TEEK","hook":"Portuguese -ão became English -oon or -on. Look for -inho diminutives, final -a, and words that Portuguese carried from Africa, Brazil and Asia into English.","o":"Portuguese"},{"w":"cobra","def":"venomous Asiatic and African elapid snakes that can expand the skin of the neck into a hood","ex":"The cobra rose from the basket and spread its impressive hood as the charmer played his flute.","say":"KOH-bruh","hook":"Portuguese -ão became English -oon or -on. Look for -inho diminutives, final -a, and words that Portuguese carried from Africa, Brazil and Asia into English.","o":"Portuguese"},{"w":"samba","def":"large west African tree having large palmately lobed leaves and axillary cymose panicles of small white flowers and one-winged seeds; yields soft white to pale yellow wood","ex":"The towering samba tree spread its enormous lobed leaves over the village clearing, providing welcome shade.","say":"SAH-mbuh","hook":"Portuguese -ão became English -oon or -on. Look for -inho diminutives, final -a, and words that Portuguese carried from Africa, Brazil and Asia into English.","o":"Portuguese"},{"w":"dodo","def":"someone whose style is out of fashion","ex":"My uncle still uses a flip phone and refuses to text, making him a real dodo in the eyes of his teenage nephews.","say":"DOH-doh","hook":"Portuguese -ão became English -oon or -on. Look for -inho diminutives, final -a, and words that Portuguese carried from Africa, Brazil and Asia into English.","o":"Portuguese"}]},{"title":"Celtic — Irish, Welsh and Gaelic spelling logic","category":"Origins Beyond the Big Four","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"Celtic loanwords look impossible because Irish and Welsh use the Roman alphabet for sounds it was never designed for, and they solved that with letter pairs rather than new letters. Once you know what those pairs mean, the words stop being arbitrary. Irish adds an h to soften a consonant and inserts an i to signal the quality of the one before it. Welsh uses ll for a sound English lacks entirely, dd for th, and treats w and y as vowels.","method":"<div class='trick'>BODHRAN</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> BOW-rawn — an Irish frame drum\n<b>Origin.</b> Irish bodhrán, from bodhar meaning deaf or dull\n<b>Marker.</b> Irish d-h is lenited — the d goes silent, so you hear a vowel where letters sit\n<b>Spell.</b> B O D H R A N\n<b>Family.</b> bodhranai — the player","cards":[{"title":"Irish lenition — the added h","body":"Irish softens a consonant by writing an h after it, and the result often sounds nothing like the letters. bh and mh sound like v or w. dh and gh often go silent. th sounds like h. So bodhran sounds bow-rawn, taoiseach sounds TEE-shukh, and Siobhan sounds shiv-AWN. When an Irish word has a consonant plus h, expect to hear less than you see."},{"title":"Irish slender vowels","body":"Irish marks whether a consonant is slender or broad by the vowel written beside it, which is why Irish words carry vowels you do not pronounce. Taoiseach has a-o-i together. Aoife, Caoimhe, saoirse all use that a-o-i cluster. These vowels are grammar, not sound. Do not try to hear them — learn the cluster as a unit."},{"title":"Welsh ll, dd and w","body":"Welsh double l is a single sound made by blowing air past the tongue, and English has nothing like it — llan, Llewellyn, Llanfair. Welsh dd is the th of this. Welsh w is a vowel, giving cwm and crwth. Welsh y is also a vowel. That is why Welsh place names look vowel-less to English eyes: the vowels are w and y."},{"title":"The words English actually took","body":"Most Celtic loans are informal and vivid: hooligan, banshee, brogue, galore, smithereens, shillelagh, slew, whiskey from uisce beatha meaning water of life, leprechaun, colcannon, sporran, plaid, clan, slogan from sluagh-ghairm, a battle cry. Penguin may be Welsh pen gwyn, white head. Thirteen are in the core library and 292 in the full library."}],"words":[{"w":"leprechaun","def":"a dwarf or sprite in Ireland","ex":"According to Irish legend, if you catch a leprechaun, he must lead you to his pot of gold.","say":"LEH-per-kown","hook":"Irish spells a slender consonant with a following i and lenites with h: -bh- and -dh- sound like v and silence. Welsh uses ll, dd, w as a vowel, and y.","o":"Irish"},{"w":"donnybrook","def":"a noisy fight or free-for-all","ex":"The argument turned into a donnybrook.","say":"DON-ee-bruuk","hook":"Irish spells a slender consonant with a following i and lenites with h: -bh- and -dh- sound like v and silence. Welsh uses ll, dd, w as a vowel, and y.","o":"Irish"},{"w":"smithereens","def":"a collection of small fragments considered as a whole","ex":"The piñata exploded into smithereens when Marcus finally landed a solid hit, sending candy flying across the gym.","say":"smih-ther-EENZ","hook":"Irish spells a slender consonant with a following i and lenites with h: -bh- and -dh- sound like v and silence. Welsh uses ll, dd, w as a vowel, and y.","o":"Irish"},{"w":"colcannon","def":"A traditional Irish dish made from mashed potatoes mixed with cooked cabbage or kale and typically seasoned with butter and onions.","ex":"On a cold autumn evening, nothing warmed her more than a steaming bowl of colcannon made with kale and butter.","say":"kol-KAN-un","hook":"Irish spells a slender consonant with a following i and lenites with h: -bh- and -dh- sound like v and silence. Welsh uses ll, dd, w as a vowel, and y.","o":"Irish"},{"w":"taoiseach","def":"the prime minister of the Irish Republic","ex":"The taoiseach addressed the nation with a calm and reassuring speech during the economic crisis.","say":"TEE-shuhk","hook":"Irish spells a slender consonant with a following i and lenites with h: -bh- and -dh- sound like v and silence. Welsh uses ll, dd, w as a vowel, and y."},{"w":"eisteddfod","def":"any of several annual Welsh festivals involving artistic competitions (especially in singing)","ex":"Maya practiced her harp piece for months before performing at the eisteddfod in Cardiff.","say":"eye-STETH-vod","hook":"Irish spells a slender consonant with a following i and lenites with h: -bh- and -dh- sound like v and silence. Welsh uses ll, dd, w as a vowel, and y.","o":"Welsh"},{"w":"gallowglass","def":"a heavily armed Scottish or Irish mercenary soldier serving Gaelic Irish chiefs from the 1200s to 1500s.","ex":"The chieftain's gallowglass stood guard with their heavy axes, ready to defend the castle gates.","say":"GAL-oh-glas","hook":"Irish spells a slender consonant with a following i and lenites with h: -bh- and -dh- sound like v and silence. Welsh uses ll, dd, w as a vowel, and y.","o":"Irish"},{"w":"bodhran","def":"A shallow, single-headed Irish frame drum held in one hand and struck with a double-headed beater called a tipper, central to traditional Irish folk music.","ex":"The folk musician struck the bodhran with a quick flick of the tipper, filling the pub with a deep, rhythmic beat.","say":"BOW-rawn","hook":"Irish spells a slender consonant with a following i and lenites with h: -bh- and -dh- sound like v and silence. Welsh uses ll, dd, w as a vowel, and y.","o":"Irish"},{"w":"sporran","def":"a fur or leather pouch worn at the front of the kilt as part of the traditional dress of Scottish Highlanders","ex":"Cameron adjusted his sporran so it hung squarely in front of his kilt before marching in the Highland games parade.","say":"SPOR-un","hook":"Irish spells a slender consonant with a following i and lenites with h: -bh- and -dh- sound like v and silence. Welsh uses ll, dd, w as a vowel, and y.","o":"Scottish Gaelic"},{"w":"shamrock","def":"creeping European clover having white to pink flowers and bright green leaves; naturalized in United States; widely grown for forage","ex":"On Saint Patrick's Day, Brendan wore a fresh shamrock pinned to his jacket in honor of his Irish heritage.","say":"SHA-mrahk","hook":"Irish spells a slender consonant with a following i and lenites with h: -bh- and -dh- sound like v and silence. Welsh uses ll, dd, w as a vowel, and y.","o":"Irish"},{"w":"banshee","def":"(Irish folklore) a female spirit who wails to warn of impending death","ex":"In Irish legend, hearing a banshee wail outside your window meant someone nearby would soon die.","say":"ba-NSHEE","hook":"Irish spells a slender consonant with a following i and lenites with h: -bh- and -dh- sound like v and silence. Welsh uses ll, dd, w as a vowel, and y."},{"w":"hooligan","def":"a cruel and brutal fellow","ex":"The hooligan smashed the shop window and ran off laughing before the police arrived.","say":"HOO-lih-guhn","hook":"Irish spells a slender consonant with a following i and lenites with h: -bh- and -dh- sound like v and silence. Welsh uses ll, dd, w as a vowel, and y."},{"w":"brogue","def":"a thick and heavy shoe","ex":"He polished his leather brogues until they gleamed like mirrors before striding into the job interview.","say":"BROHG","hook":"Irish spells a slender consonant with a following i and lenites with h: -bh- and -dh- sound like v and silence. Welsh uses ll, dd, w as a vowel, and y.","o":"Irish"},{"w":"galore","def":"in great numbers","ex":"The end-of-year festival featured games galore, with ring-toss booths, pie-eating contests, and bouncy castles stretching from one end of the school field to the other in a riot of color and noise.","say":"guh-LAWR","hook":"Irish spells a slender consonant with a following i and lenites with h: -bh- and -dh- sound like v and silence. Welsh uses ll, dd, w as a vowel, and y.","o":"Irish"},{"w":"whiskey","def":"a liquor made from fermented mash of grain","ex":"The old distillery had been producing its famous whiskey using the same recipe for over a hundred years.","say":"WIH-skee","hook":"Irish spells a slender consonant with a following i and lenites with h: -bh- and -dh- sound like v and silence. Welsh uses ll, dd, w as a vowel, and y.","o":"Irish"},{"w":"cromlech","def":"a prehistoric megalithic tomb typically having two large upright stones and a capstone","ex":"The hikers stumbled upon an ancient cromlech hidden among the overgrown fields of Wales.","say":"KROM-lek","hook":"Irish spells a slender consonant with a following i and lenites with h: -bh- and -dh- sound like v and silence. Welsh uses ll, dd, w as a vowel, and y.","o":"Welsh"},{"w":"dolmen","def":"a prehistoric megalithic tomb typically having two large upright stones and a capstone","ex":"The ancient dolmen stood on the hillside, its massive capstone balanced on two upright boulders.","say":"DOL-men","hook":"Irish spells a slender consonant with a following i and lenites with h: -bh- and -dh- sound like v and silence. Welsh uses ll, dd, w as a vowel, and y.","o":"French"},{"w":"slogan","def":"a favorite saying of a sect or political group","ex":"The candidate's campaign slogan was printed on every banner, button, and bumper sticker in the city.","say":"SLOH-guhn","hook":"Irish spells a slender consonant with a following i and lenites with h: -bh- and -dh- sound like v and silence. Welsh uses ll, dd, w as a vowel, and y."},{"w":"plaid","def":"a cloth having a crisscross design","ex":"She wore a cozy plaid flannel shirt on the crisp autumn morning hike.","say":"PLAD","hook":"Irish spells a slender consonant with a following i and lenites with h: -bh- and -dh- sound like v and silence. Welsh uses ll, dd, w as a vowel, and y.","o":"Scots"},{"w":"penguin","def":"short-legged flightless birds of cold southern especially Antarctic regions having webbed feet and wings modified as flippers","ex":"The penguin waddled across the icy shore and then plunged gracefully into the freezing Antarctic water.","say":"PEH-nggwuhn","hook":"Irish spells a slender consonant with a following i and lenites with h: -bh- and -dh- sound like v and silence. Welsh uses ll, dd, w as a vowel, and y."},{"w":"flannel","def":"a soft light woolen fabric; used for clothing","ex":"Every winter, Marcus wrapped himself in his grandfather's old flannel shirt, which smelled faintly of pine and woodsmoke.","say":"FLA-nuhl","hook":"Irish spells a slender consonant with a following i and lenites with h: -bh- and -dh- sound like v and silence. Welsh uses ll, dd, w as a vowel, and y.","o":"Anglo-Norman"},{"w":"cairn","def":"a mound of stones piled up as a memorial or to mark a boundary or path","ex":"The hikers built a small cairn at the summit to mark their achievement and guide future climbers.","say":"KEHRN","hook":"Irish spells a slender consonant with a following i and lenites with h: -bh- and -dh- sound like v and silence. Welsh uses ll, dd, w as a vowel, and y.","o":"Scots"},{"w":"loch","def":"a long narrow inlet of the sea in Scotland (especially when it is nearly landlocked)","ex":"Tourists gathered at the edge of the loch hoping to spot the legendary creature beneath its dark surface.","say":"LAHK","hook":"Irish spells a slender consonant with a following i and lenites with h: -bh- and -dh- sound like v and silence. Welsh uses ll, dd, w as a vowel, and y.","o":"French"},{"w":"glen","def":"a narrow secluded valley (in the mountains)","ex":"The hikers discovered a quiet glen hidden between two steep rocky ridges.","say":"GLEHN","hook":"Irish spells a slender consonant with a following i and lenites with h: -bh- and -dh- sound like v and silence. Welsh uses ll, dd, w as a vowel, and y.","o":"Scottish Gaelic"},{"w":"crag","def":"a steep rugged rock or cliff","ex":"The hawk perched on a jagged crag high above the valley, surveying the land below.","say":"KRAG","hook":"Irish spells a slender consonant with a following i and lenites with h: -bh- and -dh- sound like v and silence. Welsh uses ll, dd, w as a vowel, and y."},{"w":"clan","def":"group of people related by blood or marriage","ex":"Every summer the entire clan gathered at the old farmhouse for a weekend of storytelling and laughter.","say":"KLAN","hook":"Irish spells a slender consonant with a following i and lenites with h: -bh- and -dh- sound like v and silence. Welsh uses ll, dd, w as a vowel, and y.","o":"Scottish Gaelic"},{"w":"spree","def":"a brief indulgence of your impulses","ex":"After saving her allowance for months, she went on a shopping spree at the bookstore and left with a huge bag.","say":"SPREE","hook":"Irish spells a slender consonant with a following i and lenites with h: -bh- and -dh- sound like v and silence. Welsh uses ll, dd, w as a vowel, and y."}]},{"title":"Turkish and Persian — the road words","category":"Origins Beyond the Big Four","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"The Silk Road and the Ottoman court pushed a long list of words westward, and nearly all of them reached English through at least one other language, which is why their spellings look inconsistent. Persian supplied poetry, gardens, textiles and chess; Turkish supplied the court, the military and the kitchen. The practical skill is recognising which language the word passed through last, because that decides whether you write k or c, and ee or i.","method":"<div class='trick'>SERENDIPITY</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> ser-un-DIP-ih-tee — a happy accident\n<b>Origin.</b> Serendip, the Persian name for Sri Lanka, plus an English suffix\n<b>Note.</b> coined by Horace Walpole from a Persian fairy tale, so the root is a place name\n<b>Spell.</b> S E R E N D I P I T Y\n<b>Family.</b> serendipitous","cards":[{"title":"Persian building blocks","body":"-stan means land: Afghanistan, Hindustan. -abad means city. bad- relates to wind, giving badgir a wind-catcher. Persian also gave English its chess vocabulary: checkmate from shah mat, the king is helpless; rook from rukh. And the garden words: paradise from pairidaeza, a walled enclosure; jasmine, lilac, tulip, azure from lazhward, spinach, julep, and candy from qand."},{"title":"Turkish suffixes","body":"-lik makes an abstract noun or a container. -ci or -ji marks a trade: the origin of words like the Ottoman title. And Turkish gave English the court and the camp: pasha, vizier, janissary, seraglio, horde from ordu meaning army camp, khan, fez from the city of Fez, kiosk from kösk a garden pavilion, and turban."},{"title":"The food came with them","body":"Yogurt from yoğurt, kebab, baklava, sherbet from şerbet, coffee by way of Turkish kahve from Arabic qahwa, and pilaf. Sherbet and syrup and shrub are all the same Arabic root sharba, a drink, arriving by three different routes — which is exactly why their spellings look unrelated."},{"title":"Why the spellings look inconsistent","body":"Almost none of these came straight into English. Kiosk came through French, which is why it ends -sk and not -shk. Turban came through French and Italian. Azure came through Latin and lost its initial l. Tulip came through French tulipe, itself from Turkish tülbend, a turban, because the flower resembled one. Trace the route and the spelling stops looking random. Twelve are in core and 294 in the full library."}],"words":[{"w":"serendipity","def":"means good fortune or luck","ex":"By pure serendipity, she found the missing puzzle piece tucked inside an old library book.","say":"s-eh-r-uh-n-d-IH-p-ih-t-ee","hook":"Persian gave English -stan, bad-, -abad and the garden words. Turkish gave -lik, -ci and the court words. Both often arrived wrapped in Arabic or French spelling.","o":"French"},{"w":"janissary","def":"a loyal supporter","ex":"The young prince trusted his faithful janissary to guard the castle gates each night.","say":"JAN-ih-sair-ee","hook":"Persian gave English -stan, bad-, -abad and the garden words. Turkish gave -lik, -ci and the court words. Both often arrived wrapped in Arabic or French spelling.","o":"French"},{"w":"Achaemenian","def":"Of or relating to the Achaemenid dynasty, the ruling Persian royal house founded by Achaemenes that governed the Persian Empire from the sixth to the fourth centuries BCE.","ex":"The Achaemenian empire under Cyrus the Great stretched from Persia to the edges of India.","say":"ak-ih-MEE-nee-an","hook":"Persian gave English -stan, bad-, -abad and the garden words. Turkish gave -lik, -ci and the court words. Both often arrived wrapped in Arabic or French spelling.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"zumbooruk","def":"a small cannon fired from a swivel on a camel's back","ex":"A zumbooruk was carried by camel.","say":"ZUM-buh-ruk","hook":"Persian gave English -stan, bad-, -abad and the garden words. Turkish gave -lik, -ci and the court words. Both often arrived wrapped in Arabic or French spelling.","o":"Persian"},{"w":"tambourine","def":"a small drum consisting of a circular frame with skin stretched over it","ex":"She shook the tambourine enthusiastically during the school's folk music performance.","say":"t-a-m-b-ur-EE-n","hook":"Persian gave English -stan, bad-, -abad and the garden words. Turkish gave -lik, -ci and the court words. Both often arrived wrapped in Arabic or French spelling.","o":"French"},{"w":"checkmate","def":"complete victory","ex":"Kasparov checkmated his opponent after only a few moves.","say":"CHEH-kmayt","hook":"Persian gave English -stan, bad-, -abad and the garden words. Turkish gave -lik, -ci and the court words. Both often arrived wrapped in Arabic or French spelling.","o":"French"},{"w":"paradise","def":"any place of complete bliss and delight and peace","ex":"After weeks of freezing rain, the warm sunny beach felt like pure paradise to the exhausted travelers.","say":"PEH-ruh-deyes","hook":"Persian gave English -stan, bad-, -abad and the garden words. Turkish gave -lik, -ci and the court words. Both often arrived wrapped in Arabic or French spelling.","o":"French"},{"w":"caravan","def":"a procession (of wagons or mules or camels) traveling together in single file","ex":"As the sun set over the Sahara, a caravan of twelve camels silhouetted against the orange sky made its slow way toward the distant oasis.","say":"KA-ruh-van","hook":"Persian gave English -stan, bad-, -abad and the garden words. Turkish gave -lik, -ci and the court words. Both often arrived wrapped in Arabic or French spelling.","o":"Persian"},{"w":"jackal","def":"a wild dog of Asia and Africa","ex":"The lean jackal crept silently along the desert's edge, searching for scraps left by lions.","say":"j-A-k-uh-l","hook":"Persian gave English -stan, bad-, -abad and the garden words. Turkish gave -lik, -ci and the court words. Both often arrived wrapped in Arabic or French spelling.","o":"Turkish"},{"w":"mogul","def":"a bump on a ski slope","ex":"The fearless snowboarder launched off every mogul on the steep slope with breathtaking confidence, catching air on each icy bump before landing smoothly and racing on toward the finish line.","say":"MOH-guhl","hook":"Persian gave English -stan, bad-, -abad and the garden words. Turkish gave -lik, -ci and the court words. Both often arrived wrapped in Arabic or French spelling.","o":"Persian"},{"w":"bazaar","def":"a marketplace especially in the Middle East","ex":"Walking through the colorful bazaar, the travelers were dazzled by stalls overflowing with handwoven rugs, fragrant spices, gleaming copper pots, and vendors calling out enthusiastically in a dozen different languages.","say":"b-uh-z-AH-r","hook":"Persian gave English -stan, bad-, -abad and the garden words. Turkish gave -lik, -ci and the court words. Both often arrived wrapped in Arabic or French spelling.","o":"Italian"},{"w":"dervish","def":"an ascetic Muslim monk; a member of an order noted for devotional exercises involving bodily movements","ex":"The whirling dervish spun gracefully in circles as part of a centuries-old spiritual ceremony.","say":"DUR-vihsh","hook":"Persian gave English -stan, bad-, -abad and the garden words. Turkish gave -lik, -ci and the court words. Both often arrived wrapped in Arabic or French spelling.","o":"Turkish"},{"w":"turban","def":"a headdress consisting of a long cloth that is wrapped around a cap or directly around the head","ex":"The elder wrapped his colorful turban carefully around his head before joining the festival celebration.","say":"t-UR-b-uh-n","hook":"Persian gave English -stan, bad-, -abad and the garden words. Turkish gave -lik, -ci and the court words. Both often arrived wrapped in Arabic or French spelling.","o":"French"},{"w":"baklava","def":"rich Middle Eastern cake made of thin layers of flaky pastry filled with nuts and honey","ex":"The baklava was dripping with honey and packed with crunchy pistachios.","say":"BAH-kluh-vah","hook":"Persian gave English -stan, bad-, -abad and the garden words. Turkish gave -lik, -ci and the court words. Both often arrived wrapped in Arabic or French spelling.","o":"Turkish"},{"w":"sherbet","def":"a frozen dessert made primarily of fruit juice and sugar, but also containing milk or egg-white or gelatin","ex":"The bright orange sherbet tasted like a creamy blend of tangerines and vanilla on a sweltering July afternoon.","say":"SHUR-buht","hook":"Persian gave English -stan, bad-, -abad and the garden words. Turkish gave -lik, -ci and the court words. Both often arrived wrapped in Arabic or French spelling.","o":"Turkish"},{"w":"naphtha","def":"any of various volatile flammable liquid hydrocarbon mixtures; used chiefly as solvents","ex":"The mechanic warned us that naphtha is highly flammable and must be stored away from any open flame.","say":"NA-fthuh","hook":"Persian gave English -stan, bad-, -abad and the garden words. Turkish gave -lik, -ci and the court words. Both often arrived wrapped in Arabic or French spelling.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"satrap","def":"a governor of a province in ancient Persia","ex":"The satrap collected taxes from the surrounding villages and sent them to the king.","say":"SAY-trap","hook":"Persian gave English -stan, bad-, -abad and the garden words. Turkish gave -lik, -ci and the court words. Both often arrived wrapped in Arabic or French spelling.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"vizier","def":"a high official in a Muslim government (especially in the Ottoman Empire)","ex":"The sultan trusted his grand vizier to handle the empire's daily affairs with wisdom.","say":"vih-ZEER","hook":"Persian gave English -stan, bad-, -abad and the garden words. Turkish gave -lik, -ci and the court words. Both often arrived wrapped in Arabic or French spelling.","o":"Turkish"},{"w":"pasha","def":"a civil or military authority in Turkey or Egypt","ex":"The pasha arrived at the palace gates with a grand procession of soldiers and attendants.","say":"puh-SHAH","hook":"Persian gave English -stan, bad-, -abad and the garden words. Turkish gave -lik, -ci and the court words. Both often arrived wrapped in Arabic or French spelling.","o":"Turkish"},{"w":"kiosk","def":"small area set off by walls for special use","ex":"The student bought a bottle of water from the kiosk in the school hallway.","say":"KEE-awsk","hook":"Persian gave English -stan, bad-, -abad and the garden words. Turkish gave -lik, -ci and the court words. Both often arrived wrapped in Arabic or French spelling.","o":"French"},{"w":"divan","def":"a long backless sofa (usually with pillows against a wall)","ex":"The sultan reclined on a silk-covered divan, surrounded by colorful cushions and the scent of jasmine.","say":"dih-VAN","hook":"Persian gave English -stan, bad-, -abad and the garden words. Turkish gave -lik, -ci and the court words. Both often arrived wrapped in Arabic or French spelling.","o":"Arabic"},{"w":"tiara","def":"A small, jeweled crown worn on the head, especially by women","ex":"The pageant winner wore a sparkling tiara during the parade.","say":"tee-AR-uh","hook":"Persian gave English -stan, bad-, -abad and the garden words. Turkish gave -lik, -ci and the court words. Both often arrived wrapped in Arabic or French spelling.","o":"Persian"},{"w":"horde","def":"a large group a multitude","ex":"A horde of excited fans rushed toward the stage the moment the band appeared.","say":"h-AW-r-d","hook":"Persian gave English -stan, bad-, -abad and the garden words. Turkish gave -lik, -ci and the court words. Both often arrived wrapped in Arabic or French spelling.","o":"English"},{"w":"abdest","def":"The ritual washing of hands, face, and feet performed by Muslims before prayer; the Islamic practice of ritual purification with water.","ex":"Before entering the mosque for the noon prayer, he carefully performed his abdest at the fountain in the courtyard.","say":"AB-dest","hook":"Persian gave English -stan, bad-, -abad and the garden words. Turkish gave -lik, -ci and the court words. Both often arrived wrapped in Arabic or French spelling.","o":"Turkish"},{"w":"yogurt","def":"a custard-like food made from curdled milk","ex":"She topped her granola with a thick spoonful of creamy Greek yogurt and fresh blueberries.","say":"YOH-gert","hook":"Persian gave English -stan, bad-, -abad and the garden words. Turkish gave -lik, -ci and the court words. Both often arrived wrapped in Arabic or French spelling.","o":"Turkish"},{"w":"tulip","def":"any of numerous perennial bulbous herbs having linear or broadly lanceolate leaves and usually a single showy flower","ex":"Every spring, the park fills with rows of colorful tulips in red, yellow, and purple.","say":"TOO-luhp","hook":"Persian gave English -stan, bad-, -abad and the garden words. Turkish gave -lik, -ci and the court words. Both often arrived wrapped in Arabic or French spelling.","o":"French"},{"w":"azure","def":"a light shade of blue","ex":"Morning azured the village.","say":"A-zher","hook":"Persian gave English -stan, bad-, -abad and the garden words. Turkish gave -lik, -ci and the court words. Both often arrived wrapped in Arabic or French spelling.","o":"French"},{"w":"jasmine","def":"any of several shrubs and vines of the genus Jasminum chiefly native to Asia","ex":"Every evening, the scent of jasmine drifted through the open window and filled the room with a sweet floral perfume.","say":"JA-zmuhn","hook":"Persian gave English -stan, bad-, -abad and the garden words. Turkish gave -lik, -ci and the court words. Both often arrived wrapped in Arabic or French spelling.","o":"French"}]},{"title":"Malay and the Austronesian words","category":"Origins Beyond the Big Four","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"Malay was the trade language of the whole East Indies, so English took its Southeast Asian vocabulary mostly through Malay, often via Portuguese or Dutch shipping. The words are unusually easy to spell for two reasons: Malay syllables are simple, generally a consonant plus a vowel, and Malay tolerates almost no consonant clusters. So a Malay loanword tends to alternate consonant and vowel neatly all the way through.","method":"<div class='trick'>BINTURONG</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> BIN-tuh-rong — a bearcat of Southeast Asia\n<b>Origin.</b> Malay binturung\n<b>Marker.</b> simple syllables — bin, tu, rong — and the final n-g typical of Malay\n<b>Spell.</b> B I N T U R O N G\n<b>Family.</b> linsang, pangolin — the same regional set","cards":[{"title":"Simple open syllables","body":"Malay builds words from consonant-vowel syllables with almost no clusters, so the spelling follows the sound closely. Rattan, batik, sarong, sago, kapok, parang, proa, tempeh, durian. Read it aloud slowly and you can usually write it. That regularity is the opposite of French or Irish, and it is why these words reward careful listening rather than etymological knowledge."},{"title":"The -ng ending","body":"Malay ends a great many words in -ng, and English kept it. Sarong, gong, binturong, linsang, parang, orangutan from orang hutan meaning forest person. When you hear a final -ng on a tropical or animal word, Malay is a strong guess — and note that the g is written but the sound is a single nasal."},{"title":"Which came through Dutch","body":"The Dutch controlled the East Indies for three centuries, so some of these arrived in English wearing Dutch spelling. Batik, from Javanese, came through Dutch. Gutta-percha, from getah perca. Amok, from amuk, came through Portuguese. Ketchup, one of the great travellers, came from Hokkien through Malay kicap and then English. Knowing the courier explains the letters."},{"title":"The animals are the competition words","body":"The bee-level vocabulary here is mostly natural history. Binturong, linsang, pangolin, babirusa, dugong, cassowary, cockatoo, gecko, mangosteen, rambutan, casuarina, pandanus. All simply spelled, all unfamiliar. Twelve are in the core library and 259 in the full library, and the full-library set is where the real drill value sits."}],"words":[{"w":"binturong","def":"arboreal civet of Asia having a long prehensile tail and shaggy black hair","ex":"The binturong draped its long prehensile tail around the branch like a fifth limb as it munched on figs high in the rainforest canopy.","say":"BIN-tuh-rong","hook":"Malay syllables are simple and open, so the words look regular: rattan, batik, sarong. Watch for doubled vowels, final -ng, and prefixes ber- and meng-.","o":"Malay"},{"w":"orangutan","def":"large long-armed ape of Borneo and Sumatra having arboreal habits","ex":"The orangutan swung effortlessly from branch to branch high in the rainforest canopy.","say":"aw-RA-nguh-tan","hook":"Malay syllables are simple and open, so the words look regular: rattan, batik, sarong. Watch for doubled vowels, final -ng, and prefixes ber- and meng-.","o":"Malay"},{"w":"mangosteen","def":"East Indian tree with thick leathery leaves and edible fruit","ex":"When she bit into the juicy white flesh of the mangosteen, she immediately understood why it's called the queen of fruits.","say":"MA-nggoh-steen","hook":"Malay syllables are simple and open, so the words look regular: rattan, batik, sarong. Watch for doubled vowels, final -ng, and prefixes ber- and meng-.","o":"Dutch"},{"w":"cassowary","def":"large black flightless bird of Australia and New Guinea having a horny head crest","ex":"The cassowary at the wildlife park towered over us and regarded us with its bright orange eyes.","say":"KA-suh-weh-ree","hook":"Malay syllables are simple and open, so the words look regular: rattan, batik, sarong. Watch for doubled vowels, final -ng, and prefixes ber- and meng-.","o":"Malay"},{"w":"casuarina","def":"A tree with jointed stems and tiny scale-like leaves; some give heavy hardwood.","ex":"The casuarina tree swayed gracefully in the sea breeze, its slender drooping branches resembling the feathers of a cassowary.","say":"kazh-oo-uh-REE-nuh","hook":"Malay syllables are simple and open, so the words look regular: rattan, batik, sarong. Watch for doubled vowels, final -ng, and prefixes ber- and meng-.","o":"Malay"},{"w":"pandanus","def":"a tropical tree whose leaves are woven into mats and baskets","ex":"The artisan wove a beautiful basket from pandanus fibers, using a technique passed down for generations.","say":"pa-NDAY-nuhs","hook":"Malay syllables are simple and open, so the words look regular: rattan, batik, sarong. Watch for doubled vowels, final -ng, and prefixes ber- and meng-.","o":"Malay"},{"w":"babirusa","def":"Indonesian wild pig with enormous curved canine teeth","ex":"The babirusa stunned visitors at the wildlife sanctuary with its bizarre, curved tusks that curled back toward its forehead.","say":"bah-bih-ROO-suh","hook":"Malay syllables are simple and open, so the words look regular: rattan, batik, sarong. Watch for doubled vowels, final -ng, and prefixes ber- and meng-.","o":"Malay"},{"w":"rambutan","def":"Malayan tree bearing spiny red fruit","ex":"At the tropical market, she cracked open a rambutan and marveled at its sweet, juicy flesh.","say":"ram-BOO-tan","hook":"Malay syllables are simple and open, so the words look regular: rattan, batik, sarong. Watch for doubled vowels, final -ng, and prefixes ber- and meng-.","o":"Malay"},{"w":"pangolin","def":"toothless mammal of southern Africa and Asia having a body covered with horny scales and a long snout for feeding on ants and termites","ex":"The pangolin curled into a tight armored ball the moment it sensed danger approaching through the tall grass.","say":"PANG-guh-lin","hook":"Malay syllables are simple and open, so the words look regular: rattan, batik, sarong. Watch for doubled vowels, final -ng, and prefixes ber- and meng-.","o":"Malay"},{"w":"cockatoo","def":"white or light-colored crested parrot of the Australian region; often kept as cage birds","ex":"The bright white cockatoo spread its yellow crest wide and shrieked whenever a stranger approached the cage.","say":"KAH-kuh-too","hook":"Malay syllables are simple and open, so the words look regular: rattan, batik, sarong. Watch for doubled vowels, final -ng, and prefixes ber- and meng-.","o":"Malay"},{"w":"linsang","def":"a small, catlike, tree-dwelling mammal of Asia and Africa","ex":"A linsang crept along the branch.","say":"LIN-sang","hook":"Malay syllables are simple and open, so the words look regular: rattan, batik, sarong. Watch for doubled vowels, final -ng, and prefixes ber- and meng-.","o":"Javanese"},{"w":"gingham","def":"a clothing fabric in a plaid weave","ex":"She wore a cheerful blue and white gingham dress to the summer picnic.","say":"GIH-nguhm","hook":"Malay syllables are simple and open, so the words look regular: rattan, batik, sarong. Watch for doubled vowels, final -ng, and prefixes ber- and meng-.","o":"Malay"},{"w":"rattan","def":"climbing palm of Sri Lanka and southern India remarkable for the great length of the stems which are used for malacca canes","ex":"The porch was furnished with a comfortable rattan chair that creaked pleasantly when you sat down.","say":"ra-TAN","hook":"Malay syllables are simple and open, so the words look regular: rattan, batik, sarong. Watch for doubled vowels, final -ng, and prefixes ber- and meng-.","o":"Malay"},{"w":"sarong","def":"a loose skirt consisting of brightly colored fabric wrapped around the body; worn by both women and men in the South Pacific","ex":"The tourist bought a vibrant batik sarong at the market and wore it wrapped around her waist on the beach.","say":"ser-AWNG","hook":"Malay syllables are simple and open, so the words look regular: rattan, batik, sarong. Watch for doubled vowels, final -ng, and prefixes ber- and meng-.","o":"Malay"},{"w":"batik","def":"a dyed fabric; a removable wax is used where the dye is not wanted","ex":"Indonesian fabrics are often batiked.","say":"buh-TEEK","hook":"Malay syllables are simple and open, so the words look regular: rattan, batik, sarong. Watch for doubled vowels, final -ng, and prefixes ber- and meng-.","o":"Javanese"},{"w":"parang","def":"a stout straight knife used in Malaysia and Indonesia","ex":"The guide used his parang to cut a path through the dense Malaysian rainforest.","say":"pah-RANG","hook":"Malay syllables are simple and open, so the words look regular: rattan, batik, sarong. Watch for doubled vowels, final -ng, and prefixes ber- and meng-.","o":"Malay"},{"w":"dugong","def":"sirenian tusked mammal found from eastern Africa to Australia; the flat tail is bilobate","ex":"The marine biologist spent weeks observing a dugong grazing peacefully on sea grass near the coral reef.","say":"DOO-gong","hook":"Malay syllables are simple and open, so the words look regular: rattan, batik, sarong. Watch for doubled vowels, final -ng, and prefixes ber- and meng-.","o":"Tagalog"},{"w":"tempeh","def":"A fermented soy product originating from Indonesia, made by culturing cooked soybeans with a mold to form a firm, protein-rich cake.","ex":"The vegetarian chef sliced the tempeh into thin strips and marinated it in soy sauce before grilling it to golden perfection.","say":"TEM-pay","hook":"Malay syllables are simple and open, so the words look regular: rattan, batik, sarong. Watch for doubled vowels, final -ng, and prefixes ber- and meng-."},{"w":"durian","def":"tree of southeastern Asia having edible oval fruit with a hard spiny rind","ex":"The durian fruit smells so strong that it is banned in many hotels across Southeast Asia.","say":"DYOOR-ee-un","hook":"Malay syllables are simple and open, so the words look regular: rattan, batik, sarong. Watch for doubled vowels, final -ng, and prefixes ber- and meng-.","o":"Malay"},{"w":"gecko","def":"any of various small chiefly tropical and usually nocturnal insectivorous terrestrial lizards typically with immovable eyelids; completely harmless","ex":"A tiny gecko clung to the hotel wall, its sticky toe pads gripping the smooth surface with ease.","say":"GEH-koh","hook":"Malay syllables are simple and open, so the words look regular: rattan, batik, sarong. Watch for doubled vowels, final -ng, and prefixes ber- and meng-.","o":"Malay"},{"w":"kapok","def":"A fluffy plant fiber used to stuff cushions and life jackets.","ex":"The old life jacket was stuffed with kapok, making it light enough to keep a swimmer afloat.","say":"KAY-pahk","hook":"Malay syllables are simple and open, so the words look regular: rattan, batik, sarong. Watch for doubled vowels, final -ng, and prefixes ber- and meng-.","o":"Malay"},{"w":"raffia","def":"A strong fiber from palm leaves, used to weave baskets, mats, and hats.","ex":"The craft teacher showed students how to weave colorful raffia into sturdy market baskets.","say":"RAF-ee-uh","hook":"Malay syllables are simple and open, so the words look regular: rattan, batik, sarong. Watch for doubled vowels, final -ng, and prefixes ber- and meng-."},{"w":"amok","def":"frenzied as if possessed by a demon","ex":"","say":"uh-MUHK","hook":"Malay syllables are simple and open, so the words look regular: rattan, batik, sarong. Watch for doubled vowels, final -ng, and prefixes ber- and meng-.","o":"Malay"},{"w":"sago","def":"a starchy food from palm stems, used to thicken soups and puddings","ex":"The cook stirred sago into the soup to give it a thick, satisfying texture on the cold winter evening.","say":"SAY-goh","hook":"Malay syllables are simple and open, so the words look regular: rattan, batik, sarong. Watch for doubled vowels, final -ng, and prefixes ber- and meng-.","o":"Malay"},{"w":"proa","def":"A type of fast, narrow outrigger sailing canoe or boat originating in the Malay Archipelago, traditionally built with one or two outrigger floats for stability.","ex":"The fisherman paddled his proa swiftly across the calm lagoon before sunrise.","say":"PROH-ah","hook":"Malay syllables are simple and open, so the words look regular: rattan, batik, sarong. Watch for doubled vowels, final -ng, and prefixes ber- and meng-.","o":"Malay"},{"w":"bamboo","def":"the tall, hard, hollow woody stems of a tropical grass plant","ex":"The giant panda lazily chewed through a thick stalk of bamboo while tourists snapped photos from behind the glass.","say":"ba-MBOO","hook":"Malay syllables are simple and open, so the words look regular: rattan, batik, sarong. Watch for doubled vowels, final -ng, and prefixes ber- and meng-.","o":"Dutch"},{"w":"paddy","def":"A flooded field where rice is grown; also rice still in its husk.","ex":"From the train we saw workers planting seedlings in a rice paddy.","say":"PA-dee","hook":"Malay syllables are simple and open, so the words look regular: rattan, batik, sarong. Watch for doubled vowels, final -ng, and prefixes ber- and meng-.","o":"English"},{"w":"ikat","def":"A dyeing technique used to create fabric in which the yarn is resist-dyed in patterns before weaving, producing designs with characteristically blurred or feathered edges.","ex":"The weaver's ikat fabric featured stunning geometric patterns created by resist-dyeing the threads before weaving.","say":"EE-kaht","hook":"Malay syllables are simple and open, so the words look regular: rattan, batik, sarong. Watch for doubled vowels, final -ng, and prefixes ber- and meng-.","o":"Malay"}]},{"title":"Chinese and Korean — two romanisations, one language","category":"Origins Beyond the Big Four","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"The hardest thing about Chinese loanwords is that English has used two rival romanisation systems, so the same syllable can be spelled two entirely different ways depending on when the word arrived. Older borrowings use Wade-Giles or the older postal spellings; newer ones use Pinyin. Knowing which system produced a word is the whole spelling decision. Korean adds a third system with its own conventions.","method":"<div class='trick'>CHEONGSAM</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> chong-SAM — a fitted dress\n<b>Origin.</b> Cantonese cheuhngsaam, a long gown\n<b>Marker.</b> Cantonese romanisation keeps the eo vowel cluster English would not use\n<b>Spell.</b> C H E O N G S A M\n<b>Family.</b> samfu — the related garment","cards":[{"title":"Wade-Giles against Pinyin","body":"The same sounds, two systems. Wade-Giles wrote ch where Pinyin writes zh or j. Wade-Giles ts is Pinyin z or c. Wade-Giles hs is Pinyin x. Wade-Giles k is often Pinyin g. So Peking became Beijing, Mao Tse-tung became Mao Zedong, Taoism became Daoism, and Chungking became Chongqing. Both spellings still appear in dictionaries."},{"title":"Which words came in when","body":"Old borrowings kept their old spellings even after Pinyin arrived, which is why the vocabulary looks mixed. Tea, typhoon, kowtow, ketchup, tycoon, sampan, oolong, souchong, pekoe, litchi, kumquat, ginseng and kaolin all predate Pinyin. Renminbi, pinyin, qi, Beijing and Chongqing came in with it. Old and homely means the old system."},{"title":"Cantonese did a lot of the work","body":"Much early borrowing happened through the southern ports, so the words reflect Cantonese rather than Mandarin. Cheongsam, wok, chop suey, dim sum, bok choy, kumquat, loquat and souchong are Cantonese. If a food or clothing word feels southern, expect Cantonese vowels — the eo of cheongsam, the oy of choy."},{"title":"Korean has its own system","body":"Korean romanisation gives you eo, eu and ae for vowels English writes otherwise. Bibimbap, mixed rice. Naengmyeon, cold noodles. Taekwondo. Hangul, the alphabet. Kimchi. Bulgogi. Note the doubled vowels in naengmyeon — those are single Korean vowels written with two letters. Four Chinese and Korean words are in core, 175 in the full library."}],"words":[{"w":"cheongsam","def":"A form-fitting, high-collared traditional Chinese dress with side slits, originating from Manchu clothing styles and widely worn as elegant formal or everyday attire, especially by women.","ex":"She wore a beautifully embroidered cheongsam to the Lunar New Year banquet, drawing admiring glances from every guest.","say":"chong-SAM","hook":"The same Chinese sound has two spellings: Wade-Giles wrote ch, ts, hs, k; Pinyin writes zh, z, x, g. Which system a word arrived under decides its letters.","o":"Yue Chinese"},{"w":"bibimbap","def":"a Korean dish of rice topped with vegetables, meat, egg, and chili paste, mixed before eating","ex":"At the Korean restaurant, Jess ordered bibimbap and loved stirring the colorful vegetables, egg, and gochujang sauce together in the hot stone bowl.","say":"BEE-bim-bap","hook":"The same Chinese sound has two spellings: Wade-Giles wrote ch, ts, hs, k; Pinyin writes zh, z, x, g. Which system a word arrived under decides its letters.","o":"Korean"},{"w":"taekwondo","def":"a Korean martial art similar to karate","ex":"She earned her black belt in taekwondo after years of early morning practice.","say":"TA-kwah-NDOH","hook":"The same Chinese sound has two spellings: Wade-Giles wrote ch, ts, hs, k; Pinyin writes zh, z, x, g. Which system a word arrived under decides its letters.","o":"Korean"},{"w":"souchong","def":"a fine quality of black tea native to China","ex":"Grandmother brewed a pot of smoky souchong every afternoon, claiming the rich black tea was the perfect remedy for a rainy day.","say":"SOO-shong","hook":"The same Chinese sound has two spellings: Wade-Giles wrote ch, ts, hs, k; Pinyin writes zh, z, x, g. Which system a word arrived under decides its letters.","o":"Chinese"},{"w":"renminbi","def":"The official currency of the People's Republic of China, issued by the People's Bank of China, with the yuan as its basic unit of denomination.","ex":"The tourist exchanged her dollars for renminbi before exploring the bustling markets of Beijing.","say":"reh-NMIH-nbee","hook":"The same Chinese sound has two spellings: Wade-Giles wrote ch, ts, hs, k; Pinyin writes zh, z, x, g. Which system a word arrived under decides its letters.","o":"Mandarin Chinese"},{"w":"kaoliang","def":"sorghums of China and Manchuria having small white or brown grains (used for food) and dry pithy stalks (used for fodder, fuel and thatching)","ex":"Farmers in Manchuria harvested kaoliang to make both food and a potent local spirit.","say":"KAY-oh-lee-ang","hook":"The same Chinese sound has two spellings: Wade-Giles wrote ch, ts, hs, k; Pinyin writes zh, z, x, g. Which system a word arrived under decides its letters.","o":"Chinese"},{"w":"chopsticks","def":"one of a pair of slender sticks used as oriental tableware to eat food with","ex":"It took many tries before she could expertly pick up a single grain of rice using chopsticks.","say":"CHAH-pstihks","hook":"The same Chinese sound has two spellings: Wade-Giles wrote ch, ts, hs, k; Pinyin writes zh, z, x, g. Which system a word arrived under decides its letters."},{"w":"kowtow","def":"is to show servile deference","ex":"He is always kowtowing to his boss.","say":"k-OW-t-OW","hook":"The same Chinese sound has two spellings: Wade-Giles wrote ch, ts, hs, k; Pinyin writes zh, z, x, g. Which system a word arrived under decides its letters.","o":"English"},{"w":"hangul","def":"The alphabetic writing system created in the 15th century for the Korean language, consisting of characters that represent phonetic sounds.","ex":"During Korean culture week, our teacher showed us how to write our names in hangul, which looks very different from the Roman alphabet.","say":"HA-nguul","hook":"The same Chinese sound has two spellings: Wade-Giles wrote ch, ts, hs, k; Pinyin writes zh, z, x, g. Which system a word arrived under decides its letters."},{"w":"pinyin","def":"a system for romanizing Chinese ideograms in which tones are indicated by diacritics.","ex":"Using pinyin, students could sound out Mandarin words before they had learned to read the actual characters.","say":"PIN-yin","hook":"The same Chinese sound has two spellings: Wade-Giles wrote ch, ts, hs, k; Pinyin writes zh, z, x, g. Which system a word arrived under decides its letters."},{"w":"Taoism","def":"a religion and philosophy of China traditionally founded by Laozi in the sixth century B.C.E.","ex":"A central teaching of Taoism is that living in harmony with the natural flow of the universe brings peace.","say":"DOW-ih-zuhm","hook":"The same Chinese sound has two spellings: Wade-Giles wrote ch, ts, hs, k; Pinyin writes zh, z, x, g. Which system a word arrived under decides its letters."},{"w":"taoist","def":"an adherent of any branch of Taoism","ex":"The Taoist monk explained to the visiting students that his spiritual practice centered on living in harmony with nature and following the natural flow of the universe without resistance.","say":"DOW-ihst","hook":"The same Chinese sound has two spellings: Wade-Giles wrote ch, ts, hs, k; Pinyin writes zh, z, x, g. Which system a word arrived under decides its letters.","o":"Chinese"},{"w":"typhoon","def":"a tropical cyclone occurring in the western Pacific or Indian oceans","ex":"The fishermen rushed back to shore when the weather radar showed a typhoon building strength over the Pacific.","say":"teye-FOON","hook":"The same Chinese sound has two spellings: Wade-Giles wrote ch, ts, hs, k; Pinyin writes zh, z, x, g. Which system a word arrived under decides its letters.","o":"Arabic"},{"w":"kumquat","def":"any of several trees or shrubs of the genus Fortunella bearing small orange-colored edible fruits with thick sweet-flavored skin and sour pulp","ex":"She popped a whole kumquat into her mouth and was surprised by its sweet skin and tart center.","say":"KUH-mkwaht","hook":"The same Chinese sound has two spellings: Wade-Giles wrote ch, ts, hs, k; Pinyin writes zh, z, x, g. Which system a word arrived under decides its letters.","o":"Chinese"},{"w":"litchi","def":"Chinese tree cultivated especially in Philippines and India for its edible fruit; sometimes placed in genus Nephelium","ex":"She peeled open the spiky red skin of a litchi and popped the sweet, juicy fruit into her mouth.","say":"LEE-chee","hook":"The same Chinese sound has two spellings: Wade-Giles wrote ch, ts, hs, k; Pinyin writes zh, z, x, g. Which system a word arrived under decides its letters.","o":"Chinese"},{"w":"loquat","def":"evergreen tree of warm regions having fuzzy yellow olive-sized fruit with a large free stone; native to China and Japan","ex":"She reached up and plucked a ripe loquat from the branch, biting into its sweet, tangy flesh.","say":"LOH-kwaht","hook":"The same Chinese sound has two spellings: Wade-Giles wrote ch, ts, hs, k; Pinyin writes zh, z, x, g. Which system a word arrived under decides its letters.","o":"Chinese"},{"w":"ginseng","def":"a plant whose root is used in teas and folk medicine","ex":"The herbal tea blended ginseng with honey and lemon to create a warm, energizing drink on cold mornings.","say":"JIH-nsehng","hook":"The same Chinese sound has two spellings: Wade-Giles wrote ch, ts, hs, k; Pinyin writes zh, z, x, g. Which system a word arrived under decides its letters.","o":"Mandarin Chinese"},{"w":"kaolin","def":"a fine usually white clay formed by the weathering of aluminous minerals (as feldspar); used in ceramics and as an absorbent and as a filler (e.g., in paper)","ex":"The porcelain artist mixed kaolin with other minerals to create delicate, snow-white ceramic bowls.","say":"KOW-lihn","hook":"The same Chinese sound has two spellings: Wade-Giles wrote ch, ts, hs, k; Pinyin writes zh, z, x, g. Which system a word arrived under decides its letters.","o":"Mandarin Chinese"},{"w":"oolong","def":"Chinese tea leaves that have been partially fermented before being dried","ex":"She brewed a pot of fragrant oolong tea and sat by the window watching the rain fall.","say":"OO-lawng","hook":"The same Chinese sound has two spellings: Wade-Giles wrote ch, ts, hs, k; Pinyin writes zh, z, x, g. Which system a word arrived under decides its letters.","o":"Chinese"},{"w":"sampan","def":"an Asian skiff usually propelled by two oars","ex":"The fisherman steered his wooden sampan through the misty river delta at dawn, nets ready for the morning catch.","say":"SA-mpan","hook":"The same Chinese sound has two spellings: Wade-Giles wrote ch, ts, hs, k; Pinyin writes zh, z, x, g. Which system a word arrived under decides its letters.","o":"Chinese"},{"w":"tycoon","def":"a very wealthy or powerful businessman","ex":"The oil tycoon arrived at the conference in a private helicopter, then announced plans to invest hundreds of millions of dollars in renewable energy projects across five different countries over the next decade.","say":"teye-KOON","hook":"The same Chinese sound has two spellings: Wade-Giles wrote ch, ts, hs, k; Pinyin writes zh, z, x, g. Which system a word arrived under decides its letters.","o":"Japanese"},{"w":"nankeen","def":"a durable fabric formerly loomed by hand in China from natural cotton having a yellowish color","ex":"The tailor stitched the boy a sturdy pair of trousers from pale yellow nankeen cloth.","say":"nan-KEEN","hook":"The same Chinese sound has two spellings: Wade-Giles wrote ch, ts, hs, k; Pinyin writes zh, z, x, g. Which system a word arrived under decides its letters."},{"w":"taipan","def":"large highly venomous snake of northeastern Australia","ex":"The herpetologist warned that a taipan's venom can kill a human within hours.","say":"TY-pan","hook":"The same Chinese sound has two spellings: Wade-Giles wrote ch, ts, hs, k; Pinyin writes zh, z, x, g. Which system a word arrived under decides its letters."},{"w":"pekoe","def":"a superior grade of black tea; grown in India and Sri Lanka and Java","ex":"Grandmother carefully brewed a pot of pekoe tea each afternoon, saying the delicate flavor was nothing like ordinary grocery-store brands.","say":"PEE-koh","hook":"The same Chinese sound has two spellings: Wade-Giles wrote ch, ts, hs, k; Pinyin writes zh, z, x, g. Which system a word arrived under decides its letters."},{"w":"yuloh","def":"A large, single sculling oar used on Chinese boats, mounted at the stern and worked in a figure-eight motion to propel the vessel forward.","ex":"The fisherman guided his small boat through the narrow channel using a single yuloh with practiced ease.","say":"YOO-loh","hook":"The same Chinese sound has two spellings: Wade-Giles wrote ch, ts, hs, k; Pinyin writes zh, z, x, g. Which system a word arrived under decides its letters.","o":"Chinese"},{"w":"wok","def":"a large bowl-shaped cooking utensil used especially in stir-frying.","ex":"He tossed the vegetables high in the air over the blazing hot wok to keep them from burning.","say":"WAHK","hook":"The same Chinese sound has two spellings: Wade-Giles wrote ch, ts, hs, k; Pinyin writes zh, z, x, g. Which system a word arrived under decides its letters.","o":"Yue Chinese"},{"w":"kudzu","def":"fast-growing vine from eastern Asia having tuberous starchy roots and hairy trifoliate leaves and racemes of purple flowers followed by long hairy pods containing many seeds; grown for fodder and forage and root starch; widespread in the southern United States","ex":"The kudzu vine spread so rapidly through the forest that it smothered entire trees within just a few seasons.","say":"KUH-dzoo","hook":"The same Chinese sound has two spellings: Wade-Giles wrote ch, ts, hs, k; Pinyin writes zh, z, x, g. Which system a word arrived under decides its letters.","o":"Japanese"},{"w":"tofu","def":"cheeselike food made of curdled soybean milk","ex":"She cubed the silken tofu and added it to the bubbling miso broth.","say":"TOH-foo","hook":"The same Chinese sound has two spellings: Wade-Giles wrote ch, ts, hs, k; Pinyin writes zh, z, x, g. Which system a word arrived under decides its letters.","o":"Japanese"}]},{"title":"Hawaiian and Polynesian — thirteen letters, no clusters","category":"Origins Beyond the Big Four","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"Hawaiian has the smallest alphabet of any language English borrows from: five vowels, seven consonants, and the glottal stop. Every syllable ends in a vowel and no two consonants ever touch. That single fact makes Hawaiian words the most predictable in the entire course — if you can hear the syllables, you can spell the word, because there is no other legal way to write it. The same rules broadly hold across Polynesian languages.","method":"<div class='trick'>PAHOEHOE</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> puh-HOY-hoy — smooth, ropy lava\n<b>Origin.</b> Hawaiian pāhoehoe\n<b>Marker.</b> every syllable ends in a vowel — pa, ho, e, ho, e — so no consonant can double\n<b>Spell.</b> P A H O E H O E\n<b>Family.</b> aa — the rough lava, the shortest word in the set","cards":[{"title":"The whole alphabet","body":"Hawaiian uses a, e, i, o, u and h, k, l, m, n, p, w, plus the glottal stop written as an apostrophe. That is it. There is no b, c, d, f, g, j, q, r, s, t, v, x, y or z. So the moment you know a word is Hawaiian, three quarters of the alphabet is eliminated — a stronger constraint than any rule elsewhere in this course."},{"title":"Every syllable ends in a vowel","body":"No Hawaiian syllable ends in a consonant and no two consonants ever sit together. Pahoehoe, kipuka, wahine, haupia, laulau, kakapo, muumuu, ukulele, humuhumunukunukuapuaa. Say it slowly, write consonant-vowel, consonant-vowel, and you will be right. Doubled vowels are not a mistake — aa, aalii, muumuu are all real."},{"title":"The famous long one","body":"Humuhumunukunukuapuaa is the Hawaiian triggerfish, and it is 21 letters of pure pattern: humu humu nuku nuku a puaa. Two doubled words, a connector, and a word meaning pig — the fish was said to grunt like one. It is in your library, and it is far easier than a 21-letter Latin word, because every syllable is legal in only one way."},{"title":"Wider Polynesian","body":"Related languages behave similarly with slightly larger alphabets. Maori gives tuatara and moa and allows t and r. Tongan and Samoan give tapa and kava. Tuatara, kakapo and moa come from New Zealand. Taboo is Tongan tabu and tattoo is Tahitian tatau — both reached English through Captain Cook's voyages. Eight of these are in core and 82 in the full library."}],"words":[{"w":"humuhumunukunukuapuaa","def":"The reef triggerfish, Hawaii's state fish, a small colorful fish native to the coral reefs of the Pacific Ocean, known for its distinctive grunting sound and its ability to lock itself into crevices with a spine on its back.","ex":"The humuhumunukunukuapuaa, Hawaii's state fish, darted between the coral reef and disappeared in a flash of color.","say":"hoo-moo-hoo-moo-noo-koo-noo-koo-ah-poo-AH-ah","hook":"Hawaiian uses only 13 letters and every syllable ends in a vowel. No consonant ever touches another. Doubled and tripled vowels are normal.","o":"Hawaiian"},{"w":"pahoehoe","def":"freely flowing lava","ex":"As the volcano erupted, scientists watched the pahoehoe flow slowly downhill, its surface hardening into shiny, ropy coils while molten rock still moved beneath.","say":"pah-HOY-hoy","hook":"Hawaiian uses only 13 letters and every syllable ends in a vowel. No consonant ever touches another. Doubled and tripled vowels are normal.","o":"Hawaiian"},{"w":"ukulele","def":"a small guitar having four strings","ex":"She strummed her ukulele on the beach and sang a cheerful Hawaiian song to her friends.","say":"yoo-kuh-LAY-lee","hook":"Hawaiian uses only 13 letters and every syllable ends in a vowel. No consonant ever touches another. Doubled and tripled vowels are normal.","o":"Hawaiian"},{"w":"tuatara","def":"only extant member of the order Rhynchocephalia of large spiny lizard-like diapsid reptiles of coastal islands off New Zealand","ex":"The tuatara, which looks like a large iguana, has survived since the age of dinosaurs and is found only in New Zealand.","say":"too-uh-TAR-uh","hook":"Hawaiian uses only 13 letters and every syllable ends in a vowel. No consonant ever touches another. Doubled and tripled vowels are normal.","o":"Maori"},{"w":"kakapo","def":"A large, flightless, nocturnal parrot native to New Zealand, known for its greenish plumage and distinctive musty odor.","ex":"The wildlife documentary showed a kakapo waddling through the forest undergrowth on its stubby legs, hooting softly into the night air.","say":"KAH-kuh-poh","hook":"Hawaiian uses only 13 letters and every syllable ends in a vowel. No consonant ever touches another. Doubled and tripled vowels are normal.","o":"Maori"},{"w":"wahine","def":"A Hawaiian or Polynesian woman or girl; also used in surfing slang to refer to a female surfer.","ex":"The documentary featured a skilled wahine who had been surfing the big waves of the North Shore since she was ten years old.","say":"wuh-HEE-nee","hook":"Hawaiian uses only 13 letters and every syllable ends in a vowel. No consonant ever touches another. Doubled and tripled vowels are normal.","o":"Maori"},{"w":"kipuka","def":"An area of land surrounded by lava flows that has been left untouched, forming an island of older vegetation within a lava field.","ex":"The botanists hiked carefully across the hardened lava field to reach the kipuka, where rare native ferns still thrived undisturbed.","say":"kih-POO-kah","hook":"Hawaiian uses only 13 letters and every syllable ends in a vowel. No consonant ever touches another. Doubled and tripled vowels are normal."},{"w":"haupia","def":"A traditional Hawaiian dessert made from coconut milk thickened with cornstarch or arrowroot, resulting in a smooth, firm, white pudding often served at luaus.","ex":"At the luau, the creamy haupia coconut pudding was the first dessert to disappear from the table.","say":"how-PEE-ah","hook":"Hawaiian uses only 13 letters and every syllable ends in a vowel. No consonant ever touches another. Doubled and tripled vowels are normal."},{"w":"laulau","def":"A traditional Hawaiian dish consisting of pork, fish, or other ingredients wrapped in taro leaves and ti leaves, then steamed or baked.","ex":"At the luau, the steaming laulau bundles were unwrapped to reveal tender pork and buttery taro leaves that melted in your mouth.","say":"LOW-low","hook":"Hawaiian uses only 13 letters and every syllable ends in a vowel. No consonant ever touches another. Doubled and tripled vowels are normal."},{"w":"mahimahi","def":"the lean flesh of a saltwater fish found in warm waters (especially in Hawaii)","ex":"The restaurant served grilled mahimahi with a mango salsa that tasted like a tropical vacation.","say":"mah-hee-MAH-hee","hook":"Hawaiian uses only 13 letters and every syllable ends in a vowel. No consonant ever touches another. Doubled and tripled vowels are normal."},{"w":"lavalava","def":"a skirt consisting of a rectangle of calico or printed cotton; worn by Polynesians (especially Samoans)","ex":"He wore a brightly patterned lavalava to the island festival, moving gracefully as he danced.","say":"lah-vah-LAH-vah","hook":"Hawaiian uses only 13 letters and every syllable ends in a vowel. No consonant ever touches another. Doubled and tripled vowels are normal."},{"w":"makomako","def":"graceful deciduous shrub or small tree having attractive foliage and small red berries that turn black at maturity and are used for making wine","ex":"The hikers admired the makomako tree's delicate red berries glowing against its bright green leaves.","say":"mah-koh-MAH-koh","hook":"Hawaiian uses only 13 letters and every syllable ends in a vowel. No consonant ever touches another. Doubled and tripled vowels are normal."},{"w":"poroporo","def":"Australian annual sometimes cultivated for its racemes of purple flowers and edible yellow egg-shaped fruit","ex":"The gardener planted poroporo along the fence, admiring its clusters of vivid purple flowers each spring.","say":"por-oh-POR-oh","hook":"Hawaiian uses only 13 letters and every syllable ends in a vowel. No consonant ever touches another. Doubled and tripled vowels are normal."},{"w":"aalii","def":"A small Hawaiian tree or shrub (Dodonaea viscosa) with tough wood and sticky, winged seed pods, native to Hawaii and other tropical regions.","ex":"The botanist pointed out the aalii shrub growing on the dry lava slopes, its papery seed pods rattling in the trade wind.","say":"ah-LAH-ee","hook":"Hawaiian uses only 13 letters and every syllable ends in a vowel. No consonant ever touches another. Doubled and tripled vowels are normal.","o":"Hawaiian"},{"w":"muumuu","def":"a woman's loose unbelted dress","ex":"She wore a brightly patterned muumuu to the beach luau, swaying comfortably in the ocean breeze.","say":"MOO-moo","hook":"Hawaiian uses only 13 letters and every syllable ends in a vowel. No consonant ever touches another. Doubled and tripled vowels are normal.","o":"Hawaiian"},{"w":"lanai","def":"an island of central Hawaii; a pineapple-growing area","ex":"They sipped fresh pineapple juice on the lanai as the warm Hawaiian breeze swept in from the ocean.","say":"luh-NEYE","hook":"Hawaiian uses only 13 letters and every syllable ends in a vowel. No consonant ever touches another. Doubled and tripled vowels are normal.","o":"Hawaiian"},{"w":"heiau","def":"A traditional Hawaiian place of worship or temple, typically constructed of stacked stone walls or terraces and used for religious ceremonies, offerings, and rituals honoring Hawaiian gods.","ex":"The class visited an ancient heiau on the hillside and learned about its importance to Native Hawaiian spiritual life.","say":"HEY-ow","hook":"Hawaiian uses only 13 letters and every syllable ends in a vowel. No consonant ever touches another. Doubled and tripled vowels are normal.","o":"Hawaiian"},{"w":"aloha","def":"a Hawaiian word used to say hello or goodbye","ex":"The smiling flight attendant greeted every passenger with a warm 'aloha' as they stepped off the plane in Honolulu.","say":"uh-LOH-hah","hook":"Hawaiian uses only 13 letters and every syllable ends in a vowel. No consonant ever touches another. Doubled and tripled vowels are normal.","o":"Hawaiian"},{"w":"taboo","def":"Something forbidden by custom or society; not allowed to be done or discussed","ex":"In some families, talking with your mouth full is strictly taboo.","say":"tuh-BOO","hook":"Hawaiian uses only 13 letters and every syllable ends in a vowel. No consonant ever touches another. Doubled and tripled vowels are normal.","o":"English"},{"w":"tattoo","def":"a drumbeat or bugle call that signals the military to return to their quarters","ex":"At dusk, the soldiers stopped their games and marched back to camp when they heard the sharp tattoo of the drum.","say":"ta-TOO","hook":"Hawaiian uses only 13 letters and every syllable ends in a vowel. No consonant ever touches another. Doubled and tripled vowels are normal.","o":"Dutch"},{"w":"kahuna","def":"a Hawaiian priest, expert, or shaman regarded as having special powers","ex":"The village elder, considered the big kahuna of local knowledge, guided the ceremony with quiet authority.","say":"kuh-HOO-nuh","hook":"Hawaiian uses only 13 letters and every syllable ends in a vowel. No consonant ever touches another. Doubled and tripled vowels are normal."},{"w":"kava","def":"A calming drink made from the ground roots of a Pacific island shrub.","ex":"The islanders shared a bowl of kava during the welcoming ceremony, honoring their guests with the traditional drink.","say":"KAH-vuh","hook":"Hawaiian uses only 13 letters and every syllable ends in a vowel. No consonant ever touches another. Doubled and tripled vowels are normal.","o":"Tonga"},{"w":"tiki","def":"In Māori culture, a carved figurine representing the first human being or an ancestral figure, often worn as a greenstone pendant and regarded as a powerful good-luck charm and symbol of fertility.","ex":"She wore a small greenstone tiki around her neck, a carving passed down from her grandmother.","say":"TEE-KEE","hook":"Hawaiian uses only 13 letters and every syllable ends in a vowel. No consonant ever touches another. Doubled and tripled vowels are normal.","o":"Maori"},{"w":"poi","def":"Hawaiian dish of taro root pounded to a paste and often allowed to ferment","ex":"At the luau, guests tried poi for the first time, surprised by its thick, sticky texture and tangy taste.","say":"POY","hook":"Hawaiian uses only 13 letters and every syllable ends in a vowel. No consonant ever touches another. Doubled and tripled vowels are normal."},{"w":"luau","def":"an elaborate Hawaiian feast or party (especially one accompanied by traditional foods and entertainment)","ex":"The resort hosted a spectacular luau on the beach, complete with roasted pig, hula dancers, and fire twirlers.","say":"LOO-ow","hook":"Hawaiian uses only 13 letters and every syllable ends in a vowel. No consonant ever touches another. Doubled and tripled vowels are normal.","o":"Hawaiian"},{"w":"hula","def":"a Polynesian rain dance performed by a woman","ex":"The performers swayed gracefully as they danced the hula on the beach at sunset.","say":"HOO-luh","hook":"Hawaiian uses only 13 letters and every syllable ends in a vowel. No consonant ever touches another. Doubled and tripled vowels are normal.","o":"Hawaiian"},{"w":"moa","def":"extinct flightless bird of New Zealand","ex":"The museum displayed a towering skeleton of the extinct moa next to a human figure for scale.","say":"MOH-uh","hook":"Hawaiian uses only 13 letters and every syllable ends in a vowel. No consonant ever touches another. Doubled and tripled vowels are normal.","o":"Maori"},{"w":"kiwi","def":"a flightless bird of New Zealand","ex":"The kiwi uses its long beak to probe the forest floor for worms and insects in the dark of night.","say":"k-EE-w-ee","hook":"Hawaiian uses only 13 letters and every syllable ends in a vowel. No consonant ever touches another. Doubled and tripled vowels are normal.","o":"Maori"}]},{"title":"African languages — Afrikaans, Bantu and the rest","category":"Origins Beyond the Big Four","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"Two very different sources under one heading. Afrikaans is a daughter of Dutch, so it compounds the same way and its words are transparent once you know a handful of elements. The Bantu languages work by prefixes: a noun's class is marked at the front, so the m, n, ki or vu you hear at the start of a word is grammar rather than part of the root. Recognising a prefix as a prefix is what makes these spellable.","method":"<div class='trick'>AARDWOLF</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> ARD-woolf — a small striped hyena\n<b>Origin.</b> Afrikaans aardwolf, earth wolf\n<b>Marker.</b> Afrikaans doubles the a for a long vowel — aard, from Dutch aarde meaning earth\n<b>Spell.</b> A A R D W O L F\n<b>Family.</b> aardvark, aasvogel — the same doubled a","cards":[{"title":"Afrikaans elements","body":"Learn a short list and the compounds open up. aard- earth: aardvark earth-pig, aardwolf earth-wolf. -veld field: veld, bushveld, highveld. -kop head or hill: koppie. -bok buck: springbok, steenbok, gemsbok, reebok. -slang snake: boomslang, tree-snake. aas- carrion: aasvogel, a vulture. The doubled a is Afrikaans marking a long vowel, exactly as Dutch does."},{"title":"Bantu prefixes are grammar","body":"Bantu languages mark a noun's class with a prefix, and English often swallowed the prefix into the word. Swahili safari is a journey; simba a lion; bwana a master. Kalimba and marimba both carry an instrument prefix. Vuvuzela, jenga meaning to build, and impala all begin with a class marker. Do not try to find a root in those first letters — they are grammar."},{"title":"The words that travelled by ship","body":"A separate route brought West African words to the Americas and back into English through the Caribbean and the American South. Banjo, banana, jazz, jumbo, goober meaning peanut, gumbo, jambalaya, tote, yam, okra, voodoo, zombie. These arrived without their original spellings and got English ones, so they look native — which is exactly why their origin surprises people."},{"title":"Where to expect them","body":"Afrikaans words in English are almost all natural history and landscape: the animals of the veld and the shape of the land. Bantu words are mostly music, food and social terms. Twenty-eight of these are in the core library and 111 in the full library. And apartheid is worth its own note — Afrikaans apart plus heid, matching English -hood, so it means apart-hood."}],"words":[{"w":"aardwolf","def":"A small, insect-eating African mammal related to the hyena, with striped fur, that feeds primarily on termites using its sticky tongue.","ex":"Unlike its fierce hyena cousins, the aardwolf is a shy, gentle creature that spends its nights licking up termites from the African plains.","say":"AHRD-woolf","hook":"Afrikaans compounds like Dutch: aard-, -kop, -veld, -bok. Bantu languages use prefix classes: the m-, n-, ki- and vu- at the start is grammar, not a root.","o":"Afrikaans"},{"w":"aardvark","def":"A large, nocturnal African mammal with a long snout and sticky tongue that feeds almost entirely on ants and termites.","ex":"Under the beam of the ranger's flashlight, the aardvark pressed its long snout deep into the termite mound and lapped up thousands of insects.","say":"AH-rdvahrk","hook":"Afrikaans compounds like Dutch: aard-, -kop, -veld, -bok. Bantu languages use prefix classes: the m-, n-, ki- and vu- at the start is grammar, not a root.","o":"Afrikaans"},{"w":"apartheid","def":"a rigid policy of segregation in South Africa","ex":"Nelson Mandela spent decades fighting against apartheid before becoming South Africa's first democratically elected president.","say":"uh-p-AH-r-t-ay-t","hook":"Afrikaans compounds like Dutch: aard-, -kop, -veld, -bok. Bantu languages use prefix classes: the m-, n-, ki- and vu- at the start is grammar, not a root.","o":"Afrikaans"},{"w":"wildebeest","def":"large African antelope having a head with horns like an ox and a long tufted tail","ex":"The wildebeest herd stretched across the savanna for miles as the animals migrated in search of fresh grass.","say":"WIL-duh-beest","hook":"Afrikaans compounds like Dutch: aard-, -kop, -veld, -bok. Bantu languages use prefix classes: the m-, n-, ki- and vu- at the start is grammar, not a root.","o":"German"},{"w":"springbok","def":"a South African gazelle noted for springing lightly into the air","ex":"The springbok leaped gracefully over the dry African scrubland, its white rump flashing in the sunlight.","say":"SPRING-bok","hook":"Afrikaans compounds like Dutch: aard-, -kop, -veld, -bok. Bantu languages use prefix classes: the m-, n-, ki- and vu- at the start is grammar, not a root.","o":"Afrikaans"},{"w":"boomslang","def":"A large, venomous tree snake native to sub-Saharan Africa, known for its potent hemotoxic venom and striking large eyes.","ex":"The wildlife photographer was warned to stay alert for the boomslang hiding among the acacia branches overhead.","say":"BOOM-slang","hook":"Afrikaans compounds like Dutch: aard-, -kop, -veld, -bok. Bantu languages use prefix classes: the m-, n-, ki- and vu- at the start is grammar, not a root.","o":"Afrikaans"},{"w":"aasvogel","def":"A large vulture native to southern Africa, especially the Cape vulture, known for feeding on carrion.","ex":"The aasvogel circled lazily on the thermals above the savanna, its keen eyes scanning for the remains of a wildebeest.","say":"AHS-foh-ghul","hook":"Afrikaans compounds like Dutch: aard-, -kop, -veld, -bok. Bantu languages use prefix classes: the m-, n-, ki- and vu- at the start is grammar, not a root."},{"w":"jambalaya","def":"spicy Creole dish of rice and ham, sausage, chicken, or shellfish with tomatoes, peppers, onions, and celery","ex":"The aroma of sizzling sausage and spiced tomatoes drifted across the school cafeteria when the cook prepared a giant pot of jambalaya for the Louisiana Heritage Day celebration.","say":"juh-mbuh-LEYE-uh","hook":"Afrikaans compounds like Dutch: aard-, -kop, -veld, -bok. Bantu languages use prefix classes: the m-, n-, ki- and vu- at the start is grammar, not a root.","o":"Louisiana Creole"},{"w":"chimpanzee","def":"intelligent somewhat arboreal ape of equatorial African forests","ex":"The chimpanzee at the zoo used a stick to dig termites out of a mound, amazing the watching crowd.","say":"chih-MPA-nzee","hook":"Afrikaans compounds like Dutch: aard-, -kop, -veld, -bok. Bantu languages use prefix classes: the m-, n-, ki- and vu- at the start is grammar, not a root."},{"w":"vuvuzela","def":"A long plastic horn, associated with South African football culture, that produces an extremely loud, droning buzz when blown and became internationally famous during the 2010 FIFA World Cup.","ex":"The stadium erupted in a deafening drone as thousands of fans blew their vuvuzelas during the World Cup match.","say":"voo-voo-ZEH-lah","hook":"Afrikaans compounds like Dutch: aard-, -kop, -veld, -bok. Bantu languages use prefix classes: the m-, n-, ki- and vu- at the start is grammar, not a root.","o":"Zulu"},{"w":"biltong","def":"strips of salted, sun-dried meat, a popular snack in South Africa","ex":"On the long hike through the savanna, she snacked on strips of biltong to keep her energy up.","say":"BIL-tong","hook":"Afrikaans compounds like Dutch: aard-, -kop, -veld, -bok. Bantu languages use prefix classes: the m-, n-, ki- and vu- at the start is grammar, not a root.","o":"Dutch"},{"w":"meerkat","def":"a mongoose-like viverrine of South Africa having a face like a lemur and only four toes","ex":"The meerkat stood upright on its hind legs, scanning the savanna horizon for predators.","say":"MEER-kat","hook":"Afrikaans compounds like Dutch: aard-, -kop, -veld, -bok. Bantu languages use prefix classes: the m-, n-, ki- and vu- at the start is grammar, not a root.","o":"Afrikaans"},{"w":"commando","def":"a member of a military unit trained as shock troops for hit-and-run raids","ex":"The lone commando slipped behind enemy lines under cover of darkness to complete the mission.","say":"kuh-MA-ndoh","hook":"Afrikaans compounds like Dutch: aard-, -kop, -veld, -bok. Bantu languages use prefix classes: the m-, n-, ki- and vu- at the start is grammar, not a root.","o":"Afrikaans"},{"w":"marimba","def":"a percussion instrument with wooden bars tuned to produce a chromatic scale and with resonators; played with small mallets","ex":"The school band's performance was electrifying when the marimba player launched into a rapid, resonant solo that filled the auditorium.","say":"mer-IH-mbuh","hook":"Afrikaans compounds like Dutch: aard-, -kop, -veld, -bok. Bantu languages use prefix classes: the m-, n-, ki- and vu- at the start is grammar, not a root."},{"w":"kalimba","def":"A small African musical instrument consisting of a wooden board with metal tines of varying lengths that are plucked with the thumbs, also called a thumb piano.","ex":"He plucked the metal tines of his kalimba, filling the small room with bright, bell-like African melodies.","say":"kah-LIM-bah","hook":"Afrikaans compounds like Dutch: aard-, -kop, -veld, -bok. Bantu languages use prefix classes: the m-, n-, ki- and vu- at the start is grammar, not a root."},{"w":"macaque","def":"short-tailed monkey of rocky regions of Asia and Africa","ex":"The Japanese macaque, sometimes called the snow monkey, was photographed sitting peacefully in a steaming hot spring.","say":"muh-KAK","hook":"Afrikaans compounds like Dutch: aard-, -kop, -veld, -bok. Bantu languages use prefix classes: the m-, n-, ki- and vu- at the start is grammar, not a root.","o":"French"},{"w":"impala","def":"African antelope with ridged curved horns; moves with enormous leaps","ex":"The impala leaped gracefully over the tall grass as the cheetah gave chase across the savanna.","say":"ih-MPAH-luh","hook":"Afrikaans compounds like Dutch: aard-, -kop, -veld, -bok. Bantu languages use prefix classes: the m-, n-, ki- and vu- at the start is grammar, not a root.","o":"Zulu"},{"w":"safari","def":"an overland journey by hunters (especially in Africa)","ex":"On their African safari, the family spotted a pride of lions resting under an acacia tree.","say":"suh-FAH-ree","hook":"Afrikaans compounds like Dutch: aard-, -kop, -veld, -bok. Bantu languages use prefix classes: the m-, n-, ki- and vu- at the start is grammar, not a root.","o":"Swahili"},{"w":"banana","def":"any of several tropical and subtropical treelike herbs of the genus Musa having a terminal crown of large entire leaves and usually bearing hanging clusters of elongated fruits","ex":"He peeled the banana so quickly that the fruit flew out and landed on his teacher's desk.","say":"buh-NA-nuh","hook":"Afrikaans compounds like Dutch: aard-, -kop, -veld, -bok. Bantu languages use prefix classes: the m-, n-, ki- and vu- at the start is grammar, not a root.","o":"Wolof"},{"w":"banjo","def":"a stringed instrument of the guitar family that has long neck and circular body","ex":"The musician plucked his banjo so fast during the bluegrass festival that his fingers were a blur.","say":"BA-njoh","hook":"Afrikaans compounds like Dutch: aard-, -kop, -veld, -bok. Bantu languages use prefix classes: the m-, n-, ki- and vu- at the start is grammar, not a root."},{"w":"jumbo","def":"of great mass; huge and bulky","ex":"Excited for the long flight, Priya's dad picked up a jumbo bag of pretzels at the airport snack shop that was so enormous it barely fit inside the carry-on bag.","say":"JUH-mboh","hook":"Afrikaans compounds like Dutch: aard-, -kop, -veld, -bok. Bantu languages use prefix classes: the m-, n-, ki- and vu- at the start is grammar, not a root."},{"w":"gumbo","def":"any of various fine-grained silty soils that become waxy and very sticky mud when saturated with water","ex":"The Louisiana chef stirred a rich, fragrant gumbo filled with shrimp, okra, and spicy sausage.","say":"GUH-mboh","hook":"Afrikaans compounds like Dutch: aard-, -kop, -veld, -bok. Bantu languages use prefix classes: the m-, n-, ki- and vu- at the start is grammar, not a root."},{"w":"kraal","def":"a village of huts for native Africans in southern Africa; usually surrounded by a stockade","ex":"The cattle were safely enclosed within the kraal as the sun set over the African savanna.","say":"KRAHL","hook":"Afrikaans compounds like Dutch: aard-, -kop, -veld, -bok. Bantu languages use prefix classes: the m-, n-, ki- and vu- at the start is grammar, not a root.","o":"Dutch"},{"w":"mamba","def":"arboreal snake of central and southern Africa whose bite is often fatal","ex":"The black mamba is one of the world's most feared snakes because of its speed and deadly venom.","say":"MAH-mbuh","hook":"Afrikaans compounds like Dutch: aard-, -kop, -veld, -bok. Bantu languages use prefix classes: the m-, n-, ki- and vu- at the start is grammar, not a root.","o":"Zulu"},{"w":"braai","def":"A South African barbecue in which meat is grilled over an open fire or hot coals; also used as a verb meaning to grill meat in this manner.","ex":"The whole family gathered around the braai on Saturday afternoon to grill boerewors and chicken.","say":"BRY","hook":"Afrikaans compounds like Dutch: aard-, -kop, -veld, -bok. Bantu languages use prefix classes: the m-, n-, ki- and vu- at the start is grammar, not a root.","o":"Afrikaans"},{"w":"dagga","def":"relatively nontoxic South African herb smoked like tobacco","ex":"The herbalist explained that dagga had been used in traditional South African rituals for centuries.","say":"DAH-gah","hook":"Afrikaans compounds like Dutch: aard-, -kop, -veld, -bok. Bantu languages use prefix classes: the m-, n-, ki- and vu- at the start is grammar, not a root."},{"w":"jenga","def":"a game of pulling wooden blocks from a tower","ex":"The jenga tower wobbled and fell.","say":"JENG-guh","hook":"Afrikaans compounds like Dutch: aard-, -kop, -veld, -bok. Bantu languages use prefix classes: the m-, n-, ki- and vu- at the start is grammar, not a root.","o":"Swahili"},{"w":"trek","def":"a journey by ox wagon (especially an organized migration by a group of settlers)","ex":"We spent the summer trekking in the foothills of the Himalayas.","say":"TREHK","hook":"Afrikaans compounds like Dutch: aard-, -kop, -veld, -bok. Bantu languages use prefix classes: the m-, n-, ki- and vu- at the start is grammar, not a root.","o":"Afrikaans"}]},{"title":"Native American — the -tl that gives it away","category":"Origins Beyond the Big Four","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"The clearest fingerprint in this chapter belongs to Nahuatl, the language of the Aztecs: it permits a t-l cluster that English finds nearly unpronounceable, and it uses it constantly at the end of words. Everything else in the group mostly arrived through Spanish, French or English colonists, who reshaped the words to fit their own spelling, which is why words like raccoon and skunk look completely English despite being Algonquian.","method":"<div class='trick'>AXOLOTL</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> AK-suh-lot-ul — a salamander that never grows up\n<b>Origin.</b> Nahuatl axolotl, from atl water plus xolotl a servant or monster\n<b>Marker.</b> the Nahuatl t-l ending, and x for a sh-like sound\n<b>Spell.</b> A X O L O T L\n<b>Family.</b> atlatl, quetzal, nopales — all Nahuatl","cards":[{"title":"Nahuatl -tl","body":"Nahuatl ends a great many words in t-l, and English kept the letters even though it cannot really pronounce them. Atlatl a spear-thrower, axolotl, quetzal from quetzalli, nahuatl itself, ocelotl giving ocelot, tomatl giving tomato, ahuacatl giving avocado. When a word about Mexico ends in a t-l cluster, or once did, it is Nahuatl."},{"title":"Spanish softened most of them","body":"Nahuatl reached English through Spanish, which trimmed the endings to fit its own patterns. Tomatl became tomate then tomato. Ahuacatl became aguacate then avocado. Chocolatl became chocolate. Xocolatl kept its x, pronounced sh. Coyotl became coyote. So the English form is usually the Spanish reshaping, not the Nahuatl original."},{"title":"The Algonquian words look English","body":"Along the Atlantic coast, English colonists borrowed and then thoroughly anglicised. Raccoon, skunk, moose, opossum, squash, hickory, hominy, pecan, persimmon, succotash, terrapin, moccasin, powwow, toboggan, wigwam, wampum, caucus. Because English speakers did the respelling, these carry no foreign marker at all — which is what makes their origin a surprise rather than a spelling problem."},{"title":"Quechua and the Andes","body":"A third group came from Quechua through Spanish: llama, alpaca, vicuna, condor, guano, pampa, quinine from quina, quinoa, jerky from charqui, puma, coca. Note llama with its double l, which is Spanish spelling, and quinoa which keeps a Quechua vowel sequence. Two of these are in the core library and 46 in the full library."}],"words":[{"w":"axolotl","def":"larval salamander of mountain lakes of Mexico that usually lives without metamorphosing","ex":"The axolotl amazed scientists because it can regrow lost limbs and rarely transforms into its adult form.","say":"AK-suh-lot-ul","hook":"Nahuatl ends words in -tl and often in -atl: atlatl, axolotl, quetzal. Most others reached English through Spanish, which reshaped them.","o":"nah"},{"w":"xocomecatlite","def":"A rare copper tellurite mineral, found in very few locations worldwide, notable for its striking green color and its unusual chemical composition involving tellurium.","ex":"The mineralogist carefully catalogued a rare sample of xocomecatlite found in the Mexican copper mine.","say":"shoh-koh-meh-KAT-lyt","hook":"Nahuatl ends words in -tl and often in -atl: atlatl, axolotl, quetzal. Most others reached English through Spanish, which reshaped them.","o":"nah"},{"w":"massasauga","def":"small pygmy rattlesnake","ex":"The hiker froze when she spotted a massasauga coiled under a log beside the trail.","say":"mas-uh-SAW-guh","hook":"Nahuatl ends words in -tl and often in -atl: atlatl, axolotl, quetzal. Most others reached English through Spanish, which reshaped them.","o":"Ojibwa"},{"w":"saskatoon","def":"a city in Saskatchewan, Canada; also a shrub with sweet purple berries","ex":"During our road trip through Canada, we stopped in Saskatoon and tried a warm saskatoon berry pie at a local diner.","say":"SA-skuh-toon","hook":"Nahuatl ends words in -tl and often in -atl: atlatl, axolotl, quetzal. Most others reached English through Spanish, which reshaped them.","o":"Cree"},{"w":"succotash","def":"fresh corn and lima beans with butter or cream","ex":"Grandma's summer succotash overflowed with sweet corn kernels and tender lima beans glistening with melted butter.","say":"SUK-uh-tash","hook":"Nahuatl ends words in -tl and often in -atl: atlatl, axolotl, quetzal. Most others reached English through Spanish, which reshaped them.","o":"Narragansett"},{"w":"persimmon","def":"any of several tropical trees of the genus Diospyros","ex":"She bit into the ripe persimmon and savored its honey-sweet flavor on a crisp October afternoon.","say":"per-SIM-un","hook":"Nahuatl ends words in -tl and often in -atl: atlatl, axolotl, quetzal. Most others reached English through Spanish, which reshaped them."},{"w":"toboggan","def":"a long narrow sled without runners; boards curve upward in front","ex":"Six kids piled onto the toboggan and flew down the snowy hill, screaming with delight.","say":"tuh-BAH-guhn","hook":"Nahuatl ends words in -tl and often in -atl: atlatl, axolotl, quetzal. Most others reached English through Spanish, which reshaped them."},{"w":"moccasin","def":"a heelless shoe made entirely of soft leather","ex":"She slipped on a soft leather moccasin that fit perfectly around her foot.","say":"m-AH-k-uh-s-ih-n","hook":"Nahuatl ends words in -tl and often in -atl: atlatl, axolotl, quetzal. Most others reached English through Spanish, which reshaped them.","o":"Powhatan"},{"w":"tamarack","def":"medium-sized larch of Canada and northern United States including Alaska having a broad conic crown and rust-brown scaly bark","ex":"The tamarack stood out in autumn because, unlike most conifers, it dropped its golden needles before the first snowfall.","say":"TAM-uh-rak","hook":"Nahuatl ends words in -tl and often in -atl: atlatl, axolotl, quetzal. Most others reached English through Spanish, which reshaped them."},{"w":"huapango","def":"A lively traditional Mexican folk music and dance style originating in the Huasteca region, characterized by complex rhythms, high-pitched vocals, and rapid footwork.","ex":"The musicians stomped on the wooden platform as they launched into a lively huapango that drew dancers from every corner of the plaza.","say":"wah-PAHN-goh","hook":"Nahuatl ends words in -tl and often in -atl: atlatl, axolotl, quetzal. Most others reached English through Spanish, which reshaped them.","o":"Spanish"},{"w":"wickiup","def":"a lodge consisting of a frame covered with matting or brush; used by nomadic American Indians in the southwestern United States","ex":"The museum exhibit featured a full-size replica of a wickiup, showing visitors how nomadic tribes built quick, sturdy shelters from local materials.","say":"WIK-ee-up","hook":"Nahuatl ends words in -tl and often in -atl: atlatl, axolotl, quetzal. Most others reached English through Spanish, which reshaped them."},{"w":"nopales","def":"young tender stem segments of the prickly pear cactus that are used as food.","ex":"The street vendor grilled nopales on a hot iron skillet and served them with salsa and warm tortillas.","say":"noh-PAH-leyz","hook":"Nahuatl ends words in -tl and often in -atl: atlatl, axolotl, quetzal. Most others reached English through Spanish, which reshaped them.","o":"Spanish"},{"w":"atlatl","def":"A device, typically a flat board or stick with a hooked end, used to hurl a spear or dart with greater force and distance than by hand alone.","ex":"The museum volunteer demonstrated how an atlatl could launch a dart nearly twice as far as an arm throw, astonishing the crowd of students.","say":"AT-latl","hook":"Nahuatl ends words in -tl and often in -atl: atlatl, axolotl, quetzal. Most others reached English through Spanish, which reshaped them.","o":"Classical Nahuatl"},{"w":"quetzal","def":"the basic unit of money in Guatemala; equal to 100 centavos","ex":"The merchant accepted fifty quetzal for the handwoven Guatemalan blanket.","say":"ket-SAHL","hook":"Nahuatl ends words in -tl and often in -atl: atlatl, axolotl, quetzal. Most others reached English through Spanish, which reshaped them.","o":"Spanish"},{"w":"peyote","def":"a small spineless globe-shaped cactus; source of mescal buttons","ex":"The botany textbook included a photograph of peyote, a small round cactus native to the deserts of Mexico.","say":"pih-YAW-tee","hook":"Nahuatl ends words in -tl and often in -atl: atlatl, axolotl, quetzal. Most others reached English through Spanish, which reshaped them.","o":"Spanish"},{"w":"mesquite","def":"a spiny tree found in western North America","ex":"The cowboys gathered mesquite wood to build a fire that gave the grilled meat a smoky flavor.","say":"m-EH-s-k-ee-t","hook":"Nahuatl ends words in -tl and often in -atl: atlatl, axolotl, quetzal. Most others reached English through Spanish, which reshaped them.","o":"Spanish"},{"w":"ocelot","def":"nocturnal wildcat of Central America and South America having a dark-spotted buff-brown coat","ex":"The ocelot crept silently through the jungle undergrowth, its spotted coat perfectly camouflaged in the dappled moonlight.","say":"AH-suh-laht","hook":"Nahuatl ends words in -tl and often in -atl: atlatl, axolotl, quetzal. Most others reached English through Spanish, which reshaped them.","o":"nah"},{"w":"coyote","def":"a smaller wolf-like animal","ex":"A lone coyote howled mournfully from the ridge above our campsite as the full moon rose over the hills.","say":"k-ay-OH-t-ee","hook":"Nahuatl ends words in -tl and often in -atl: atlatl, axolotl, quetzal. Most others reached English through Spanish, which reshaped them.","o":"Spanish"},{"w":"avocado","def":"a pear-shaped tropical fruit with green or blackish skin and rich yellowish pulp enclosing a single large seed","ex":"She mashed a ripe avocado with lime juice and salt to make a creamy guacamole.","say":"a-vuh-KAH-doh","hook":"Nahuatl ends words in -tl and often in -atl: atlatl, axolotl, quetzal. Most others reached English through Spanish, which reshaped them.","o":"Spanish"},{"w":"chocolate","def":"a beverage made from cocoa powder and milk and sugar; usually drunk hot","ex":"After building a snowman, the children rushed inside for mugs of steaming hot chocolate topped with marshmallows.","say":"CHAW-kluht","hook":"Nahuatl ends words in -tl and often in -atl: atlatl, axolotl, quetzal. Most others reached English through Spanish, which reshaped them.","o":"Spanish"},{"w":"raccoon","def":"a clever night animal of North America with a black face mask and a ringed tail","ex":"A curious raccoon tipped over the trash can in the backyard searching for scraps of food.","say":"r-a-k-OO-n","hook":"Nahuatl ends words in -tl and often in -atl: atlatl, axolotl, quetzal. Most others reached English through Spanish, which reshaped them.","o":"Powhatan"},{"w":"opossum","def":"small furry Australian arboreal marsupials having long usually prehensile tails","ex":"The opossum froze in the beam of the flashlight, then played dead until we walked away.","say":"oh-PAH-suhm","hook":"Nahuatl ends words in -tl and often in -atl: atlatl, axolotl, quetzal. Most others reached English through Spanish, which reshaped them."},{"w":"powwow","def":"(informal) a quick private conference","ex":"The coaches held a quick powwow on the sideline before deciding on the final play.","say":"POW-wow","hook":"Nahuatl ends words in -tl and often in -atl: atlatl, axolotl, quetzal. Most others reached English through Spanish, which reshaped them."},{"w":"hominy","def":"hulled corn with the bran and germ removed","ex":"Grandma stirred a big pot of hominy grits on the stove every Sunday morning.","say":"HAH-mih-nee","hook":"Nahuatl ends words in -tl and often in -atl: atlatl, axolotl, quetzal. Most others reached English through Spanish, which reshaped them."},{"w":"llama","def":"wild or domesticated South American cud-chewing animal related to camels but smaller and lacking a hump","ex":"The llama stared at the tourists with its long lashes, then calmly spit and walked away.","say":"LAH-muh","hook":"Nahuatl ends words in -tl and often in -atl: atlatl, axolotl, quetzal. Most others reached English through Spanish, which reshaped them.","o":"Spanish"},{"w":"alpaca","def":"a fluffy South American animal like a small llama, raised for its soft wool","ex":"She wrapped herself in a scarf made from soft alpaca wool to stay warm on the cold mountain trail.","say":"a-LPA-kuh","hook":"Nahuatl ends words in -tl and often in -atl: atlatl, axolotl, quetzal. Most others reached English through Spanish, which reshaped them.","o":"Spanish"},{"w":"quinoa","def":"A highly nutritious grain-like seed harvested from a South American plant, widely used as a food staple valued for its high protein content and gluten-free properties.","ex":"She topped her salad with fluffy quinoa, which provided a complete protein source without any meat.","say":"K IY2 N OW1 AH0","hook":"Nahuatl ends words in -tl and often in -atl: atlatl, axolotl, quetzal. Most others reached English through Spanish, which reshaped them.","o":"Spanish"},{"w":"guano","def":"the excrement of sea birds; used as fertilizer","ex":"Farmers in the 1800s imported tons of guano from island seabird colonies because it was one of the richest fertilizers available.","say":"GWAH-noh","hook":"Nahuatl ends words in -tl and often in -atl: atlatl, axolotl, quetzal. Most others reached English through Spanish, which reshaped them.","o":"Spanish"}]},{"title":"Assimilation — why prefixes change their last letter","category":"How Words Are Built","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"The general course teaches this for ad- alone, but the same machinery governs every Latin prefix, and it explains most of the doubled letters in English. When a prefix ending in a consonant meets a root starting with a different one, Latin reshaped the prefix's final letter to match — and the result is two identical letters. Once you can see the prefix, you know exactly which letter doubles and why, instead of memorising each word.","method":"<div class='trick'>IMMARCESCIBLE</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> im-ar-SESS-ih-bul — unfading\n<b>Parse.</b> in- (not) + marcescere (to wither) + -ible\n<b>Assimilation.</b> in- before m becomes im-, so the two m letters are prefix plus root\n<b>Spell.</b> I M M A R C E S C I B L E\n<b>Family.</b> marcescent — withering, from the same root","cards":[{"title":"The four families","body":"in- meaning not or into becomes im- before b, m, p (impossible, immature), il- before l (illegible, illuminate), ir- before r (irregular, irascible). sub- becomes suc- suf- sug- sup- sur- (succumb, sufficient, suggestion, suppress, surreptitious). ad- becomes ac- af- ag- al- an- ap- ar- as- at- (accommodate, affinity, aggregate, alliteration, annihilate, apparatus, arrogate, assiduous, attenuate). con- becomes col- cor- com- (collateral, corroborate, commiserate)."},{"title":"This is where doubled letters come from","body":"Almost every awkward double in a Latin-derived word is a prefix meeting a root. Accommodate is ad plus commodus, so the double c is the assimilated prefix and the double m belongs to the root — which is exactly why it has two of each. Annihilate is ad plus nihil, nothing. Aggregate is ad plus grex, a flock. Once you see the seam, the doubling is not arbitrary."},{"title":"Why some words do not double","body":"The prefix only assimilates when it has to. Before a vowel it stays whole: adapt, adopt, adequate, inept, inane. Before certain consonants it simply drops its final letter without doubling: aspire is ad plus spirare, not adspire or asspire. So the absence of a double is information too — it tells you the root began with a vowel or an awkward cluster."},{"title":"Using it at the microphone","body":"This is the single most useful structural insight in the course, because it turns spelling into arithmetic. Hear a long Latin word, find the prefix, find the root, and the join tells you the doubling. Our core library holds 1,356 words that begin with an assimilated prefix and the full library 1,898 — more than any other pattern here."}],"words":[{"w":"immarcescible","def":"Incapable of withering or fading; not subject to decay or deterioration; everlasting in freshness.","ex":"The poet called her love immarcescible, insisting it would never wither or fade.","say":"ih-mar-SES-ih-bul","hook":"A prefix reshapes its final consonant to match what follows. in- becomes im- il- ir-, sub- becomes suc- suf- sug-, ad- becomes ac- af- ag- al- ap- ar- as- at-. That is where doubled letters come from.","o":"French"},{"w":"surreptitious","def":"marked by quiet and caution and secrecy; taking pains to avoid being observed","ex":"The spy cast a surreptitious glance over his shoulder before slipping the coded note under the door.","say":"sur-uh-PTIH-shuhs","hook":"A prefix reshapes its final consonant to match what follows. in- becomes im- il- ir-, sub- becomes suc- suf- sug-, ad- becomes ac- af- ag- al- ap- ar- as- at-. That is where doubled letters come from.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"accommodate","def":"is to do a kindness or a favor to oblige","ex":"The hotel staff worked hard to accommodate every guest's special request, arranging extra pillows, dietary meals, and late checkout times to ensure that everyone left feeling comfortable and well cared for.","say":"uh-k-AH-m-uh-d-ay-t","hook":"A prefix reshapes its final consonant to match what follows. in- becomes im- il- ir-, sub- becomes suc- suf- sug-, ad- becomes ac- af- ag- al- ap- ar- as- at-. That is where doubled letters come from.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"alliteration","def":"use of the same consonant at the beginning of each stressed syllable in a line of verse","ex":"Poets love alliteration, like 'silly snakes slither swiftly' in a funny rhyme.","say":"uh-LIH-ter-ay-shuhn","hook":"A prefix reshapes its final consonant to match what follows. in- becomes im- il- ir-, sub- becomes suc- suf- sug-, ad- becomes ac- af- ag- al- ap- ar- as- at-. That is where doubled letters come from.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"corroborate","def":"establish or strengthen as with new evidence or facts","ex":"Two other campers stepped forward to corroborate her story about the shooting star.","say":"ker-AH-ber-ayt","hook":"A prefix reshapes its final consonant to match what follows. in- becomes im- il- ir-, sub- becomes suc- suf- sug-, ad- becomes ac- af- ag- al- ap- ar- as- at-. That is where doubled letters come from.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"commiserate","def":"to feel or express sympathy or compassion","ex":"After losing the championship game, the teammates sat together to commiserate over the tough defeat.","say":"kuh-MIH-ser-ayt","hook":"A prefix reshapes its final consonant to match what follows. in- becomes im- il- ir-, sub- becomes suc- suf- sug-, ad- becomes ac- af- ag- al- ap- ar- as- at-. That is where doubled letters come from.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"annihilate","def":"kill in large numbers","ex":"The invading army threatened to annihilate every village in its path, leaving nothing but ash and silence.","say":"uh-NEYE-uh-layt","hook":"A prefix reshapes its final consonant to match what follows. in- becomes im- il- ir-, sub- becomes suc- suf- sug-, ad- becomes ac- af- ag- al- ap- ar- as- at-. That is where doubled letters come from.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"irascible","def":"is easily provoked to anger","ex":"The irascible old parrot squawked angrily whenever anyone dared touch his cage.","say":"ih-r-A-s-ih-b-uh-l","hook":"A prefix reshapes its final consonant to match what follows. in- becomes im- il- ir-, sub- becomes suc- suf- sug-, ad- becomes ac- af- ag- al- ap- ar- as- at-. That is where doubled letters come from.","o":"French"},{"w":"illuminate","def":"make lighter or brighter","ex":"A single lantern was enough to illuminate the whole tent during our late-night ghost stories.","say":"ih-LOO-mih-niht","hook":"A prefix reshapes its final consonant to match what follows. in- becomes im- il- ir-, sub- becomes suc- suf- sug-, ad- becomes ac- af- ag- al- ap- ar- as- at-. That is where doubled letters come from.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"illegible","def":"is hard to read or decipher because of poor handwriting","ex":"His hurried signature was so illegible that no one could read it.","say":"ih-l-EH-j-uh-b-uh-l","hook":"A prefix reshapes its final consonant to match what follows. in- becomes im- il- ir-, sub- becomes suc- suf- sug-, ad- becomes ac- af- ag- al- ap- ar- as- at-. That is where doubled letters come from.","o":"English"},{"w":"collateral","def":"a security pledged for the repayment of a loan","ex":"Cousins are collateral relatives.","say":"kuh-LA-ter-uhl","hook":"A prefix reshapes its final consonant to match what follows. in- becomes im- il- ir-, sub- becomes suc- suf- sug-, ad- becomes ac- af- ag- al- ap- ar- as- at-. That is where doubled letters come from.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"corrigible","def":"capable of being corrected or set right","ex":"The school counselor believed every corrigible student could improve their behavior with the right guidance, patience, and consistent support from both teachers and caring family members at home.","say":"KAW-ruh-juh-buhl","hook":"A prefix reshapes its final consonant to match what follows. in- becomes im- il- ir-, sub- becomes suc- suf- sug-, ad- becomes ac- af- ag- al- ap- ar- as- at-. That is where doubled letters come from.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"colloquial","def":"characteristic of informal spoken language or conversation","ex":"Wrote her letters in a colloquial style.","say":"kuh-LOH-kwee-uhl","hook":"A prefix reshapes its final consonant to match what follows. in- becomes im- il- ir-, sub- becomes suc- suf- sug-, ad- becomes ac- af- ag- al- ap- ar- as- at-. That is where doubled letters come from.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"apparatus","def":"equipment designed to serve a specific function","ex":"The scientist carefully cleaned each piece of the laboratory apparatus before beginning her chemistry experiment.","say":"a-per-A-tuhs","hook":"A prefix reshapes its final consonant to match what follows. in- becomes im- il- ir-, sub- becomes suc- suf- sug-, ad- becomes ac- af- ag- al- ap- ar- as- at-. That is where doubled letters come from.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"assiduous","def":"marked by care and persistent effort","ex":"The assiduous student reviewed her notes every evening and never missed a homework assignment, which is why she earned the top grade.","say":"uh-SIH-dwuhs","hook":"A prefix reshapes its final consonant to match what follows. in- becomes im- il- ir-, sub- becomes suc- suf- sug-, ad- becomes ac- af- ag- al- ap- ar- as- at-. That is where doubled letters come from.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"attenuate","def":"weaken the consistency of (a chemical substance)","ex":"Adding more water will attenuate the juice until it tastes weak and watery.","say":"uh-TEH-nyoo-ayt","hook":"A prefix reshapes its final consonant to match what follows. in- becomes im- il- ir-, sub- becomes suc- suf- sug-, ad- becomes ac- af- ag- al- ap- ar- as- at-. That is where doubled letters come from.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"aggregate","def":"the whole amount","ex":"In the aggregate, the students raised over two thousand dollars for the school's new science lab.","say":"A-gruh-guht","hook":"A prefix reshapes its final consonant to match what follows. in- becomes im- il- ir-, sub- becomes suc- suf- sug-, ad- becomes ac- af- ag- al- ap- ar- as- at-. That is where doubled letters come from.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"suggestion","def":"an idea that is suggested","ex":"The picnic was her suggestion.","say":"suh-JEH-schuhn","hook":"A prefix reshapes its final consonant to match what follows. in- becomes im- il- ir-, sub- becomes suc- suf- sug-, ad- becomes ac- af- ag- al- ap- ar- as- at-. That is where doubled letters come from.","o":"Anglo-Norman"},{"w":"sufficient","def":"of a quantity that can fulfill a need or requirement but without being abundant","ex":"Before setting off on the three-day hiking trail, the experienced guide reminded everyone to pack sufficient water and high-energy snacks, warning that there would be no stores or streams along the remote route.","say":"suh-FIH-shuhnt","hook":"A prefix reshapes its final consonant to match what follows. in- becomes im- il- ir-, sub- becomes suc- suf- sug-, ad- becomes ac- af- ag- al- ap- ar- as- at-. That is where doubled letters come from.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"suppress","def":"to put down by force or authority","ex":"Suppress a nascent uprising.","say":"suh-PREHS","hook":"A prefix reshapes its final consonant to match what follows. in- becomes im- il- ir-, sub- becomes suc- suf- sug-, ad- becomes ac- af- ag- al- ap- ar- as- at-. That is where doubled letters come from.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"succumb","def":"consent reluctantly","ex":"Despite his best efforts to resist dessert, he finally began to succumb to the aroma of fresh-baked pie.","say":"suh-KUHM","hook":"A prefix reshapes its final consonant to match what follows. in- becomes im- il- ir-, sub- becomes suc- suf- sug-, ad- becomes ac- af- ag- al- ap- ar- as- at-. That is where doubled letters come from.","o":"French"},{"w":"affinity","def":"(immunology) the attraction between an antigen and an antibody","ex":"In anatomical structure prehistoric man shows close affinity with modern humans.","say":"uh-FIH-nuh-tee","hook":"A prefix reshapes its final consonant to match what follows. in- becomes im- il- ir-, sub- becomes suc- suf- sug-, ad- becomes ac- af- ag- al- ap- ar- as- at-. That is where doubled letters come from.","o":"French"},{"w":"arrogate","def":"demand as being one's due or property; assert one's right or title to","ex":"It is unfair to arrogate all the credit when the whole team helped.","say":"A-roh-gayt","hook":"A prefix reshapes its final consonant to match what follows. in- becomes im- il- ir-, sub- becomes suc- suf- sug-, ad- becomes ac- af- ag- al- ap- ar- as- at-. That is where doubled letters come from.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"opponent","def":"a contestant that you are matched against","ex":"Opponent (or opposing) armies.","say":"uh-POH-nuhnt","hook":"A prefix reshapes its final consonant to match what follows. in- becomes im- il- ir-, sub- becomes suc- suf- sug-, ad- becomes ac- af- ag- al- ap- ar- as- at-. That is where doubled letters come from.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"immanent","def":"of a mental act performed entirely within the mind","ex":"A cognition is an immanent act of mind.","say":"IH-muh-nuhnt","hook":"A prefix reshapes its final consonant to match what follows. in- becomes im- il- ir-, sub- becomes suc- suf- sug-, ad- becomes ac- af- ag- al- ap- ar- as- at-. That is where doubled letters come from.","o":"Latin"}]},{"title":"Stacking combining forms — how the longest words are built","category":"How Words Are Built","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"The longest words in the library are not harder than short ones; they are just longer. They are chains of combining forms, each with a fixed spelling, joined by a connecting vowel — o for Greek, i for Latin. The entire skill is finding the joints. Once a word is broken at its joints, you are spelling six familiar pieces in sequence rather than forty-five arbitrary letters, and the pieces recur across hundreds of other words.","method":"<div class='trick'>OTORHINOLARYNGOLOGY</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> oh-toh-rye-noh-lar-in-GOL-uh-jee\n<b>Joints.</b> ot / o / rhin / o / laryng / o / logy\n<b>Parts.</b> ous ear, rhis nose, larynx throat, logos study\n<b>Spell.</b> O T O R H I N O L A R Y N G O L O G Y\n<b>Family.</b> otology, rhinoplasty, laryngitis — every piece reappears","cards":[{"title":"Find the joints first","body":"Never spell a long word left to right. Break it at the connecting vowels first. Otorhinolaryngology becomes ot-o-rhin-o-laryng-o-logy: ear, nose, throat, study. Electroencephalogram becomes electr-o-en-cephal-o-gram. Psychopharmacology becomes psych-o-pharmac-o-logy. Six short familiar pieces beat one long unfamiliar string every time."},{"title":"Greek joins with o, Latin with i","body":"This is the rule that decides the connectors. Greek compounds use o: ochlocracy, myrmecophagy, thermometer, dendrochronology. Latin compounds use i: fossiliferous, cupriferous, insecticide, verisimilitude. So if the pieces are Greek, put an o at each joint; if Latin, put an i. A mixed word takes the vowel belonging to the piece before the joint."},{"title":"The showpieces come apart too","body":"Pneumonoultramicroscopicsilicovolcanoconiosis is pneumon-o-ultra-microscopic-silic-o-volcan-o-coni-osis: lung, beyond, tiny, silica, volcanic, dust, condition. Floccinaucinihilipilification is flocci-nauci-nihili-pili-fication, four Latin words for something worthless plus making. Hippopotomonstrosesquippedaliophobia stacks horse, river, monster, a foot and a half long, and fear. All buildable."},{"title":"The pieces pay you back","body":"The reason this is worth practising is reuse. Learn cephal, head, and you have cephalic, encephalogram, hydrocephalus, cephalopod, acephalous, cephalomancy. Learn laryng and you have laryngitis, laryngology, laryngoscope. Our core library holds 2,871 words whose syllable count is six or more, and nearly all of them are stacks. Every piece you learn appears in dozens."}],"words":[{"w":"pneumonoultramicroscopicsilicovolcanoconiosis","def":"a lung disease caused by breathing very fine volcanic silica dust","ex":"To win the class's long-word contest, Ava wrote pneumonoultramicroscopicsilicovolcanoconiosis across two whole lines of the whiteboard.","say":"noo-muh-noh-ul-truh-my-kruh-skop-ik-sil-uh-koh-vol-kay-noh-koh-nee-OH-sis","hook":"Long scientific words are chains of morphemes, not memorised strings. Greek joins with o, Latin with i. Find the joints and a 45-letter word becomes six short ones.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"hippopotomonstrosesquippedaliophobia","def":"a humorous name for the fear of very long words","ex":"Jamal laughed at the irony that hippopotomonstrosesquippedaliophobia, the fear of long words, is itself a monster to spell.","say":"hip-oh-pot-oh-mon-stroh-ses-kwip-uh-day-lee-oh-FOH-bee-uh","hook":"Long scientific words are chains of morphemes, not memorised strings. Greek joins with o, Latin with i. Find the joints and a 45-letter word becomes six short ones.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"floccinaucinihilipilification","def":"the act of judging something to be completely worthless","ex":"Priya wowed the judges by rattling off floccinaucinihilipilification, then joked that the word described her brother's opinion of her music.","say":"flok-suh-naw-suh-ny-hil-uh-pil-uh-fuh-KAY-shun","hook":"Long scientific words are chains of morphemes, not memorised strings. Greek joins with o, Latin with i. Find the joints and a 45-letter word becomes six short ones.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"honorificabilitudinitatibus","def":"the state of being able to receive honors, famed as Shakespeare's longest word","ex":"Showing off after rehearsal, the drama club dared each other to spell honorificabilitudinitatibus from Love's Labour's Lost.","say":"hon-uh-rif-ih-kab-ih-lih-too-dih-nih-TAY-tih-bus","hook":"Long scientific words are chains of morphemes, not memorised strings. Greek joins with o, Latin with i. Find the joints and a 45-letter word becomes six short ones.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"otorhinolaryngology","def":"the branch of medicine dealing with the ear, nose, and throat","ex":"After her third ear infection, Sana's pediatrician referred her to a specialist in otorhinolaryngology.","say":"oh-toh-ry-noh-lar-un-GOL-uh-jee","hook":"Long scientific words are chains of morphemes, not memorised strings. Greek joins with o, Latin with i. Find the joints and a 45-letter word becomes six short ones.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"abdominohysterectomy","def":"A surgical procedure in which the uterus is removed through an incision in the abdominal wall, as opposed to a vaginal hysterectomy. It is one of the most common major gynecological surgeries.","ex":"Following her diagnosis, the surgeon recommended an abdominohysterectomy to remove the uterus through an abdominal incision for the safest outcome.","say":"ab-DAH-mih-noh-his-tuh-REK-tuh-mee","hook":"Long scientific words are chains of morphemes, not memorised strings. Greek joins with o, Latin with i. Find the joints and a 45-letter word becomes six short ones.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"immunoelectrophoresis","def":"electrophoresis to separate antigens and antibodies","ex":"The lab used immunoelectrophoresis to separate and identify the abnormal antibodies found in the patient's blood sample.","say":"ih-myoo-noh-ih-lek-troh-fuh-REE-sis","hook":"Long scientific words are chains of morphemes, not memorised strings. Greek joins with o, Latin with i. Find the joints and a 45-letter word becomes six short ones.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"psychopharmacology","def":"the study of drugs that affect the mind","ex":"She decided to study psychopharmacology because she wanted to understand how medications could treat depression and anxiety.","say":"sy-koh-fahr-muh-KOL-uh-jee","hook":"Long scientific words are chains of morphemes, not memorised strings. Greek joins with o, Latin with i. Find the joints and a 45-letter word becomes six short ones.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"kakorrhaphiophobia","def":"an abnormal fear of failure","ex":"The counselor told the spellers that a little nervousness is normal, but kakorrhaphiophobia can freeze you at the microphone.","say":"kak-uh-raf-ee-uh-FOH-bee-uh","hook":"Long scientific words are chains of morphemes, not memorised strings. Greek joins with o, Latin with i. Find the joints and a 45-letter word becomes six short ones.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"arachibutyrophobia","def":"a humorous name for the fear of peanut butter sticking to the roof of your mouth","ex":"Dev claimed arachibutyrophobia was the only reason he traded his sandwich for pizza every single day.","say":"uh-rak-uh-byoo-tuh-roh-FOH-bee-uh","hook":"Long scientific words are chains of morphemes, not memorised strings. Greek joins with o, Latin with i. Find the joints and a 45-letter word becomes six short ones.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"thermoluminescence","def":"The emission of light from a material, such as a crystal or mineral, when it is heated, caused by the release of energy previously stored from exposure to radiation; used in archaeological dating.","ex":"Archaeologists used thermoluminescence to date the ancient pottery by measuring the light it released when heated.","say":"ther-moh-loo-mih-NES-unts","hook":"Long scientific words are chains of morphemes, not memorised strings. Greek joins with o, Latin with i. Find the joints and a 45-letter word becomes six short ones.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"sesquipedalianism","def":"the habit of using very long words","ex":"Mr. Ortiz accused the class of sesquipedalianism when every book report suddenly sprouted five-syllable vocabulary.","say":"ses-kwuh-puh-DAY-lee-uh-niz-um","hook":"Long scientific words are chains of morphemes, not memorised strings. Greek joins with o, Latin with i. Find the joints and a 45-letter word becomes six short ones.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"dendrochronology","def":"the science of dating events and variations in environment in former periods by study of the rings of growth in trees and aged wood.","ex":"Through dendrochronology, researchers determined that the ancient timber beam had been cut from a tree felled in 1347.","say":"deh-ndroh-kruh-NAH-luh-jee","hook":"Long scientific words are chains of morphemes, not memorised strings. Greek joins with o, Latin with i. Find the joints and a 45-letter word becomes six short ones.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"trichotillomania","def":"an irresistible urge to pull out your own hair","ex":"After being diagnosed with trichotillomania, the teenager began therapy to help manage the compulsive urge to pull out her own hair.","say":"trik-oh-til-oh-MAY-nee-uh","hook":"Long scientific words are chains of morphemes, not memorised strings. Greek joins with o, Latin with i. Find the joints and a 45-letter word becomes six short ones.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"radioimmunoassay","def":"immunoassay of a substance that has been radioactively labeled","ex":"The laboratory used radioimmunoassay to measure the exact level of hormones in the patient's blood sample.","say":"ray-dee-oh-ih-myoo-noh-AS-ay","hook":"Long scientific words are chains of morphemes, not memorised strings. Greek joins with o, Latin with i. Find the joints and a 45-letter word becomes six short ones.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"zenzizenzizenzic","def":"the eighth power of a number, in old arithmetic books","ex":"For math history day, Nia explained that Robert Recorde used zenzizenzizenzic in 1557 for a number multiplied by itself eight times over.","say":"zen-zee-zen-zee-ZEN-zik","hook":"Long scientific words are chains of morphemes, not memorised strings. Greek joins with o, Latin with i. Find the joints and a 45-letter word becomes six short ones.","o":"Italian"}]},{"title":"Hyphens, spaces and closed compounds","category":"How Words Are Built","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"On a bee stage a hyphen is a letter: you have to say it, and getting it wrong loses the word. The rules are conventional rather than logical, but they are learnable. Borrowed French and Italian phrases keep the hyphens they arrived with. The prefix self- always takes one. English compounds tend to start open, become hyphenated, then close up entirely, so the answer depends on how old the compound is — and the dictionary of record decides.","method":"<div class='trick'>MILLE-FEUILLE</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> meel-FOY — a layered pastry\n<b>Origin.</b> French mille feuilles, a thousand leaves\n<b>Rule.</b> a borrowed French phrase keeps its hyphen; you must say hyphen aloud\n<b>Spell.</b> M I L L E hyphen F E U I L L E\n<b>Family.</b> porte-monnaie, carton-pierre — same rule","cards":[{"title":"Say the hyphen","body":"This is the practical point that matters most. At the microphone you spell a hyphenated word by naming it: m-i-l-l-e-hyphen-f-e-u-i-l-l-e. If you run the letters together without saying hyphen, you have misspelled the word. Practise saying it aloud, because it feels unnatural and that is exactly when people forget."},{"title":"Borrowed phrases keep their hyphens","body":"French and Italian phrases that entered English as units keep the punctuation they came with. Porte-monnaie, mille-feuille, carton-pierre, loup-garou, cul-de-sac, avant-gardism, mezzo-soprano, privat-docent from German. If the word is a phrase in its own language and English took the whole phrase, expect a hyphen."},{"title":"self- always, and the life cycle","body":"The prefix self- is reliably hyphenated: self-conscious, self-portrait, self-sabotage, self-mastery, self-sterile, self-support. Beyond that, English compounds age. They start as two words, acquire a hyphen, and finally close up — to day became to-day became today. So the newer the compound, the more likely the hyphen; the older, the more likely it has closed."},{"title":"When you genuinely cannot tell","body":"Sometimes there is no derivable answer and the dictionary simply rules. In that case use what you do know: is it a foreign phrase? Then hyphen. Does it start with self-? Then hyphen. Is it an old, everyday English compound? Then probably closed. Is it a technical modifier before a noun? Then probably hyphenated. Our core library holds 111 hyphenated or open forms and the full library 752."}],"words":[{"w":"self-conscious","def":"aware of yourself as an individual or of your own being and actions and thoughts","ex":"Feeling self-conscious about her braces, she smiled shyly until her friends smiled back.","say":"S EH2 L F K AA1 N SH AH0 S","hook":"You must say the hyphen out loud. French phrase borrowings keep their hyphens; English compounds usually lose them over time; self- always keeps one.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"absent-minded","def":"Tending to forget things or not pay attention to what is happening around you because your thoughts are elsewhere; habitually inattentive or forgetful.","ex":"The absent-minded professor walked into class still wearing his pajama top.","say":"AB-snt-MYN-did","hook":"You must say the hyphen out loud. French phrase borrowings keep their hyphens; English compounds usually lose them over time; self- always keeps one.","o":"French"},{"w":"avant-gardism","def":"The philosophy or practice of supporting and creating avant-garde art, ideas, or movements that are experimental, innovative, and ahead of their time.","ex":"Her professor wrote his thesis on avant-gardism in 1920s Paris, where artists constantly broke every traditional rule.","say":"ah-vahn-GARD-iz-um","hook":"You must say the hyphen out loud. French phrase borrowings keep their hyphens; English compounds usually lose them over time; self- always keeps one.","o":"French"},{"w":"porte-monnaie","def":"A small purse or wallet designed specifically for carrying coins; a coin purse, borrowed directly from the French meaning 'carry money.'","ex":"The antique dealer displayed a delicate Victorian porte-monnaie made of beaded silk, explaining that wealthy ladies once carried such elegant little purses to pay for small purchases.","say":"port-moh-NAY","hook":"You must say the hyphen out loud. French phrase borrowings keep their hyphens; English compounds usually lose them over time; self- always keeps one.","o":"French"},{"w":"mezzo-soprano","def":"a soprano with a voice between soprano and contralto","ex":"The mezzo-soprano's rich, warm voice filled the opera house as she performed the role of the queen.","say":"MET-soh-suh-PRAH-noh","hook":"You must say the hyphen out loud. French phrase borrowings keep their hyphens; English compounds usually lose them over time; self- always keeps one.","o":"Italian"},{"w":"carton-pierre","def":"A type of papier-mâché material made from paper pulp, chalk, and other substances, molded to imitate stone, wood, or metal for decorative architectural purposes.","ex":"The ornate ceiling rosettes in the grand ballroom were crafted from carton-pierre, fooling every visitor into believing they were solid carved stone.","say":"kar-tohn-PYAIR","hook":"You must say the hyphen out loud. French phrase borrowings keep their hyphens; English compounds usually lose them over time; self- always keeps one.","o":"French"},{"w":"mille-feuille","def":"A classic French pastry made of many thin, flaky layers of puff pastry alternated with cream or custard filling, whose name means 'a thousand leaves' in French.","ex":"The French bakery's mille-feuille had hundreds of flaky layers stacked with cream and fresh strawberries.","say":"meel-FOY","hook":"You must say the hyphen out loud. French phrase borrowings keep their hyphens; English compounds usually lose them over time; self- always keeps one.","o":"French"},{"w":"privat-docent","def":"In European universities, especially in German-speaking countries, an unsalaried lecturer who is qualified to teach at a university level and earns fees directly from students attending lectures.","ex":"The privat-docent lectured at the university without receiving an official salary, relying solely on student fees.","say":"prih-VAHT-doh-sent","hook":"You must say the hyphen out loud. French phrase borrowings keep their hyphens; English compounds usually lose them over time; self- always keeps one.","o":"German"},{"w":"hsaing-waing","def":"A traditional Burmese percussion ensemble centered around a circle of tuned drums, used to accompany classical dance, theater, and ceremonial performances in Myanmar.","ex":"The ceremonial procession was led by musicians playing the hsaing-waing, Burma's traditional drum circle ensemble.","say":"SAING-waing","hook":"You must say the hyphen out loud. French phrase borrowings keep their hyphens; English compounds usually lose them over time; self- always keeps one."},{"w":"loup-garou","def":"a monster able to change appearance from human to wolf and back again","ex":"The villagers bolted their doors at night, terrified that the loup-garou prowling the forest would find its way into town.","say":"loo-gah-ROO","hook":"You must say the hyphen out loud. French phrase borrowings keep their hyphens; English compounds usually lose them over time; self- always keeps one.","o":"French"},{"w":"hunky-dory","def":"being satisfactory or in satisfactory condition","ex":"After the repairs were finished, the mechanic assured us that everything was hunky-dory with the engine.","say":"HUNK-ee-DOR-ee","hook":"You must say the hyphen out loud. French phrase borrowings keep their hyphens; English compounds usually lose them over time; self- always keeps one."},{"w":"cul-de-sac","def":"a passage with access only at one end","ex":"The children played safely in the cul-de-sac because no through traffic ever came down the dead-end street.","say":"K AH1 L D IH0 S AE2 K","hook":"You must say the hyphen out loud. French phrase borrowings keep their hyphens; English compounds usually lose them over time; self- always keeps one.","o":"French"}]},{"title":"Doublets — one root, two arrivals, two spellings","category":"How Words Are Built","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"English borrowed from French for six hundred years, and French itself changed during that time — so the same Latin root frequently arrived twice, at different dates, wearing different clothes. These pairs are called doublets, and they explain a great deal that otherwise looks random. Northern Norman French kept a w and a hard c; central Parisian French turned those into gu and ch. So one root produced guard and ward, guarantee and warranty, chief and chef.","method":"<div class='trick'>CHATTEL</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> CHAT-ul — a movable possession\n<b>Doublet.</b> cattle is the same Latin word, capitale, meaning property\n<b>Rule.</b> Parisian French turned Latin c into ch, so cattle and chattel split\n<b>Spell.</b> C H A T T E L\n<b>Family.</b> capital, cattle, chattel — all from capitale","cards":[{"title":"The w and gu pairs","body":"Norman French kept a Germanic w; Parisian French wrote gu instead. So English got both. Ward and guard. Warranty and guarantee. Wile and guile. Warden and guardian. Wallop and gallop. William and Guillaume. If you meet a word with an unexpected gu, look for a w-partner — and if it has a w, look for the gu one."},{"title":"The c and ch pairs","body":"Latin c before a became ch in Parisian French and stayed c in Norman. Cattle and chattel, from capitale. Catch and chase, from captiare. Cap and chief and chef, all from caput, a head. Castle kept its c; chateau took ch. Canal and channel. Cant and chant. Two spellings, one root, arriving a century apart."},{"title":"The learned versus popular pairs","body":"A third kind of doublet: one form worn down by centuries of speech, the other taken straight from written Latin much later. Frail and fragile, both from fragilis. Sure and secure, from securus. Poison and potion, from potio. Reason and ration. Blame and blaspheme. Spice and species. The short one is the old spoken route, the long one the later scholarly borrowing."},{"title":"Why this helps you spell","body":"Doublets are a memory device with a reason inside it. If you know chattel is cattle, the double t stops being arbitrary. If you know guarantee is warranty, the gu makes sense. And when a pronouncer gives you the rarer member of a pair, the common one is your route in — hostel and hotel, corps and corpse, shirt and skirt, gentle and genteel."}],"words":[{"w":"chattel","def":"a movable item of personal property","ex":"In the historical novel, the characters debate whether a person could ever rightfully be treated as chattel.","say":"ch-A-t-uh-l","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"French"},{"w":"guarantee","def":"a written assurance that some product or service will be provided or will meet certain specifications","ex":"There is no guarantee that they are not lying.","say":"geh-ruh-NTEE","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"French"},{"w":"warranty","def":"is assurance","ex":"When the new laptop stopped working after a week, Mom used the warranty to get it replaced for free.","say":"w-AW-r-uh-n-t-ee","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"Anglo-Norman"},{"w":"fragile","def":"easily broken or damaged or destroyed","ex":"The archaeologist handled the fragile pottery shard with two fingers, knowing that a single wrong move could destroy a 3,000-year-old artifact.","say":"FRA-juhl","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"genteel","def":"marked by refinement in taste and manners","ex":"Despite living in a tiny apartment, the elderly woman maintained a genteel lifestyle, always setting the table with her finest china for guests.","say":"jehn-TEEL","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"French"},{"w":"tradition","def":"an inherited pattern of thought or action","ex":"Lighting candles at dinner was a beloved family tradition passed down for four generations.","say":"truh-DIH-shuhn","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"treason","def":"a crime that undermines the offender's government","ex":"The general was arrested and charged with treason after he plotted against his own country.","say":"TREE-zuhn","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"French"},{"w":"regard","def":"To think of or consider someone or something in a certain way","ex":"Everyone in class regards Maya as the best artist in fourth grade.","say":"rih-GARD","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"French"},{"w":"guard","def":"a person who keeps watch over something or someone","ex":"The left guard was injured on the play.","say":"GAHRD","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"French"},{"w":"ward","def":"a person who is under the protection or in the custody of another","ex":"They put her in a 4-bed ward.","say":"WAWRD","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"chief","def":"a person who is in charge","ex":"","say":"CHEEF","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"French"},{"w":"chef","def":"a professional cook","ex":"The talented chef created a magnificent five-course dinner that left every guest at the table speechless.","say":"SHEHF","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"French"},{"w":"frail","def":"Weak, delicate, and easily broken or hurt.","ex":"An invalid's frail body.","say":"FRAYL","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"French"},{"w":"cattle","def":"domesticated bovine animals as a group regardless of sex or age","ex":"So many head of cattle.","say":"KA-tuhl","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"French"},{"w":"reward","def":"a recompense for worthy acts or retribution for wrongdoing","ex":"","say":"rih-WAWRD","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"Anglo-Norman"},{"w":"wile","def":"the use of tricks to deceive someone (usually to extract money from them)","ex":"The clever fox used every wile it knew to sneak past the farmer's locked henhouse.","say":"WEYEL","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"guile","def":"shrewdness as demonstrated by being skilled in deception","ex":"The fox in the fable used its guile to trick the crow into dropping its food.","say":"GEYEL","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"Norse"},{"w":"catch","def":"a drawback or difficulty that is not readily evident","ex":"It sounds good but what's the catch?","say":"KACH","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"Anglo-Norman"},{"w":"chase","def":"the act of pursuing in an effort to overtake or capture","ex":"The playful puppy loved to chase its own tail around the yard.","say":"CHAYS","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"French"},{"w":"royal","def":"belonging to or having to do with a king or queen","ex":"The royal family appeared on the palace balcony, waving to the enormous crowd gathered below to celebrate the coronation of the newly crowned king.","say":"ROY-uhl","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"French"},{"w":"regal","def":"belonging to or befitting a supreme ruler","ex":"","say":"REE-guhl","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"French"},{"w":"gentle","def":"cause to be more favorably inclined; gain the good will of","ex":"","say":"JEH-ntuhl","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"French"},{"w":"poison","def":"any substance that causes injury or illness or death of a living organism","ex":"The poison of fascism.","say":"POY-zuhn","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"French"},{"w":"potion","def":"a medicinal or magical or poisonous beverage","ex":"The wizard handed her a glowing blue potion that promised to cure any illness.","say":"POH-shuhn","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"secure","def":"get by special effort","ex":"","say":"sih-KYUUR","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"sure","def":"having or feeling no doubt or uncertainty; confident and assured","ex":"","say":"SHUUR","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"French"},{"w":"hostel","def":"a hotel providing overnight lodging for travelers","ex":"The backpackers saved money by sharing a room at a cozy hostel near the city center.","say":"HAH-stuhl","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"French"},{"w":"hotel","def":"a building where travelers can pay for lodging and meals and other services","ex":"The hotel had a rooftop pool with a breathtaking view of the glittering city skyline.","say":"hoh-TEHL","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"French"},{"w":"corps","def":"an army unit usually consisting of two or more divisions and their support","ex":"The Marine Corps trained intensively for months in the desert, preparing every soldier in the unit to work together as a disciplined team capable of handling the most challenging and dangerous missions.","say":"KAWR","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"French"},{"w":"shirt","def":"a garment worn on the upper half of the body","ex":"He ironed his best shirt carefully before the important job interview.","say":"SHURT","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"skirt","def":"cloth covering that forms the part of a garment below the waist","ex":"She wore a flowing floral skirt that swirled around her ankles as she danced at the festival.","say":"SKURT","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"Norse"},{"w":"shatter","def":"break into many pieces","ex":"The wine glass shattered.","say":"SHA-ter","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"scatter","def":"a haphazard distribution in all directions","ex":"She waved her hand and scattered the crowds.","say":"SKA-ter","hook":"The same Latin word often entered English twice: once through Norman French with a w or a hard c, once through Parisian French with a g or a ch. Guard and ward. Chief and chef.","o":"Old English"}]},{"title":"Heteronyms — same spelling, two pronunciations","category":"How Words Are Built","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"Heteronyms are the mirror image of homophones: same spelling, different sound and sense. They matter on a bee stage for one specific reason — the pronunciation you are given may not match the word you are thinking of, and the fix is to ask for the part of speech and the definition. English marks most of these pairs by stress, and there is a rule behind it: nouns take stress on the first syllable, verbs on the second.","method":"<div class='trick'>SEPARATE</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> SEP-uh-rut (adjective) or SEP-uh-rate (verb)\n<b>Same spelling.</b> both are s-e-p-a-r-a-t-e\n<b>Strategy.</b> the pronunciation differs but the spelling does not — so relax, and confirm the definition anyway\n<b>Spell.</b> S E P A R A T E\n<b>Family.</b> separation, separately","cards":[{"title":"The stress rule","body":"For dozens of two-syllable pairs, the noun stresses the first syllable and the verb the second. CON-duct the noun, con-DUCT the verb. RE-cord and re-CORD. PRO-ject and pro-JECT. SUB-ject and sub-JECT. PRE-sent and pre-SENT. RE-fuse the rubbish and re-FUSE to go. This is regular enough to predict, and it tells you the word is the same either way."},{"title":"The -ate words","body":"A large family of words ending -ate has a full vowel as a verb and a reduced one as an adjective or noun. SEP-uh-rate the verb against SEP-uh-rut the adjective. Also moderate, deliberate, intimate, laminate, alternate, appropriate, associate, estimate, graduate, predicate, syndicate, advocate, delegate. The spelling never changes, which is the reassuring part."},{"title":"The unrelated pairs","body":"Some heteronyms are two entirely different words that collided. Tear the eye-water and tear the rip. Wind the air and wind the clock. Bow the ribbon and bow the greeting. Sow the seed and sow the pig. Lead the metal and lead the way. Dove the bird and dove into the pool. Live the adjective and live the verb. Close the door and stand close."},{"title":"What to do at the microphone","body":"This is a question strategy, not a spelling rule. If the pronunciation sounds wrong for the word you have in mind, ask for the part of speech and then for the definition. For the -ate and stress pairs the spelling is identical anyway, so nothing is at risk. The danger is only when you assume a different word entirely and spell that instead."}],"words":[{"w":"separate","def":"a separately printed article that originally appeared in a larger publication","ex":"Our teacher passed around a separate, a single article reprinted from the big journal.","say":"SEH-per-ayt","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"moderate","def":"a person who takes a position in the political center","ex":"John moderated the discussion.","say":"MAH-der-uht","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"deliberate","def":"think about carefully; weigh","ex":"Take a moment to deliberate carefully before choosing which experiment to present at the fair.","say":"dih-LIH-ber-uht","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"intimate","def":"someone to whom private matters are confided","ex":"She shared her secret plan only with her most intimate friend.","say":"IH-ntuh-muht","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"laminate","def":"a sheet of material made by bonding two or more sheets or layers","ex":"The teacher laminated the colorful classroom posters so they would last through the entire school year without tearing.","say":"LA-muh-nuht","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"alternate","def":"someone who takes the place of another person","ex":"When the lead actress fell ill, her alternate stepped onto the stage and delivered a stunning performance.","say":"AW-lter-nuht","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"appropriate","def":"give or assign a resource to a particular person or cause","ex":"The mayor decided to appropriate part of the budget to build a new playground.","say":"uh-PROH-pree-uht","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"associate","def":"a person who joins with others in some activity or endeavor","ex":"He had to consult his associate before continuing.","say":"uh-SOH-see-uht","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"estimate","def":"an approximate calculation of quantity or degree or worth","ex":"The contractor gave the homeowners a detailed estimate showing that repairing the roof would cost approximately four thousand dollars.","say":"EH-stuh-muht","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"graduate","def":"one that has received an academic degree a diploma or a certificate","ex":"She graduated in 1990.","say":"g-r-A-j-uh-w-uh-t","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"predicate","def":"in logic, the part of a statement that tells something about the subject","ex":"`Socrates is a man' predicates manhood of Socrates.","say":"PREH-duh-kayt","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"French"},{"w":"syndicate","def":"a loose affiliation of gangsters in charge of organized criminal activities","ex":"In the mystery novel, brave detectives worked to expose the crime syndicate in the city.","say":"SIH-ndih-kuht","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"French"},{"w":"present","def":"A gift given to someone, often for a birthday, holiday, or special achievement.","ex":"That is enough for the present.","say":"PREH-zuhnt","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"French"},{"w":"minute","def":"Extremely small; tiny.","ex":"The biologist studied minute creatures swimming in a single water drop.","say":"my-NOOT","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"august","def":"the month following July and preceding September","ex":"","say":"AH-guhst","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"invalid","def":"someone who is incapacitated by a chronic illness or injury","ex":"","say":"IH-nvuh-luhd","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"conduct","def":"manner of acting or controlling yourself","ex":"You cannot conduct business like this.","say":"KAH-nduhkt","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"refuse","def":"food that is discarded (as from a kitchen)","ex":"","say":"ruh-FYOOZ","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"French"},{"w":"produce","def":"fresh fruits and vegetable grown for the market","ex":"The tree would not produce fruit.","say":"pruh-DOOS","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"content","def":"everything that is included in a collection and that is held or included in something","ex":"He emptied the contents of his pockets.","say":"KAH-ntehnt","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"French"},{"w":"object","def":"a tangible and visible entity; an entity that can cast a shadow","ex":"It was full of rackets, balls and other objects.","say":"AH-bjehkt","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"subject","def":"The topic or thing that people are talking, writing, or learning about.","ex":"He didn't want to discuss that subject.","say":"suh-BJEHKT","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"project","def":"any piece of work that is undertaken or attempted","ex":"","say":"PRAH-jehkt","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"record","def":"information kept or saved so people can remember what happened","ex":"The film provided a valuable record of stage techniques.","say":"ruh-KAWRD","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"French"},{"w":"polish","def":"the property of being smooth and shiny","ex":"They performed with great polish.","say":"PAH-lihsh","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"French"},{"w":"tear","def":"a drop of the clear salty saline solution secreted by the lacrimal glands","ex":"His story brought tears to her eyes.","say":"TEHR","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"wind","def":"air moving (sometimes with considerable force) from an area of high pressure to an area of low pressure","ex":"Trees bent under the fierce winds.","say":"WEYEND","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"bow","def":"a knot formed by doubling a string into two loops","ex":"He pointed the bow of the boat toward the finish line.","say":"b-OW","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"Welsh"},{"w":"sow","def":"an adult female hog","ex":"She sowed sunflower seeds.","say":"SOW","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"lead","def":"To guide people or show the way.","ex":"The team captain will lead the warm-up before the big game.","say":"LEED","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"live","def":"To be alive; not dead; having life or existence as opposed to being deceased or inanimate.","ex":"People lived in Africa millions of years ago.","say":"LEYEV","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"close","def":"the temporal end; the concluding time","ex":"","say":"KLOHS","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"French"},{"w":"dove","def":"any of numerous small pigeons","ex":"A white dove perched gracefully on the windowsill, cooing softly in the morning light.","say":"DUHV","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"Norse"},{"w":"wound","def":"an injury to living tissue (especially an injury involving a cut or break in the skin)","ex":"He feared that mentioning it might reopen the wound.","say":"WOWND","hook":"One spelling, two words. Stress usually marks the difference: a noun stresses the first syllable, a verb the second. Ask for the part of speech, not the spelling.","o":"Old English"}]},{"title":"Metathesis — the sounds that swapped places","category":"How Words Are Built","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"Metathesis is the swapping of two sounds within a word, and it is one of the commonest ways English spellings drift away from their origins. It happens most around r and l. Sometimes the swap won and became the standard spelling, which is why some words look scrambled compared to their relatives. Sometimes it is still in progress, which is why the pronunciations people are corrected for — nucular, comfterble — are the same process caught in the act.","method":"<div class='trick'>NOSTRIL</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> NOSS-trul\n<b>Origin.</b> Old English nosthyrl — nose plus thyrel, a hole\n<b>What happened.</b> the th and the r traded places and the word compressed\n<b>Spell.</b> N O S T R I L\n<b>Family.</b> nose, thrill — thrill is the same thyrel, meaning to pierce","cards":[{"title":"Swaps that won","body":"Old English bridd became bird. thridda became third. hros became horse. Old English wæps became wasp. hæps became hasp. And nosthyrl became nostril. In each case the r or s moved and the new order became correct. So a word can look nothing like its own history, and its relatives are your only clue."},{"title":"Doublets made by metathesis","body":"Sometimes both orders survived as separate words. Through and thorough are the same Old English word, thurh, with different vowel and stress development. Curd and crud are one word split by a swapped r. Tinsel and scintilla share a root. Wattle and waddle. When two words feel oddly related and their letters seem shuffled, metathesis is usually why."},{"title":"The swaps still in progress","body":"The pronunciations people get corrected for are metathesis happening now. Nuclear said as nucular. Comfortable as comfterble. Integral as intregal. Cavalry as calvary. Asterisk as asterix. Espresso as expresso. Sherbet as sherbert. Jewelry as jewelery. Prescription as perscription. Every one is two sounds trading places, exactly as bird and horse did."},{"title":"Why a speller must care","body":"Because the pronouncer will say the word correctly and your ear may still hear the swapped version. Nuclear is n-u-c-l-e-a-r, and if you have said nucular all your life you will want to write it that way. Cavalry is horsemen, Calvary is the hill — the swap changes the word entirely. Learn the ones you personally swap, because those are the ones that will cost you."}],"words":[{"w":"nostril","def":"either one of the two external openings to the nasal cavity in the nose","ex":"The horse flared its nostril and snorted loudly as the stranger approached.","say":"NAH-strihl","hook":"Two sounds trade places over time, usually around an r. That is why through and thorough differ, why nostril hides nose-thirl, and why people say nucular.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"thorough","def":"painstakingly careful and accurate","ex":"Our accountant is thorough.","say":"THUR-oh","hook":"Two sounds trade places over time, usually around an r. That is why through and thorough differ, why nostril hides nose-thirl, and why people say nucular.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"through","def":"having finished or arrived at completion","ex":"","say":"THROO","hook":"Two sounds trade places over time, usually around an r. That is why through and thorough differ, why nostril hides nose-thirl, and why people say nucular.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"comfortable","def":"providing or experiencing physical well-being or relief (`comfy' is informal)","ex":"After hiking through the muddy trail for six exhausting hours, the campers were finally comfortable sitting beside the warm fire, wrapped in dry blankets and sipping hot cocoa.","say":"KUH-mfer-tuh-buhl","hook":"Two sounds trade places over time, usually around an r. That is why through and thorough differ, why nostril hides nose-thirl, and why people say nucular.","o":"French"},{"w":"integral","def":"In math, the total found by adding up many tiny parts; the reverse of a derivative.","ex":"Practice is an integral part of becoming skilled at any instrument.","say":"IH-ntuh-gruhl","hook":"Two sounds trade places over time, usually around an r. That is why through and thorough differ, why nostril hides nose-thirl, and why people say nucular.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"nuclear","def":"(weapons) deriving destructive energy from the release of atomic energy","ex":"Scientists debated whether nuclear energy could serve as a safe and reliable alternative to fossil fuels, given both its enormous power output and the serious risks posed by radioactive waste.","say":"NOO-klee-er","hook":"Two sounds trade places over time, usually around an r. That is why through and thorough differ, why nostril hides nose-thirl, and why people say nucular.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"interpret","def":"make sense of; assign a meaning to","ex":"Can you interpret what the poet meant by 'the dancing leaves'?","say":"ih-NTUR-pruht","hook":"Two sounds trade places over time, usually around an r. That is why through and thorough differ, why nostril hides nose-thirl, and why people say nucular.","o":"French"},{"w":"asterisk","def":"a star-shaped character * used in printing","ex":"A small asterisk beside the word pointed me to the note at the bottom.","say":"A-ster-ihsk","hook":"Two sounds trade places over time, usually around an r. That is why through and thorough differ, why nostril hides nose-thirl, and why people say nucular.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"cavalry","def":"troops trained to fight on horseback","ex":"","say":"KA-vuh-lree","hook":"Two sounds trade places over time, usually around an r. That is why through and thorough differ, why nostril hides nose-thirl, and why people say nucular.","o":"French"},{"w":"espresso","def":"strong black coffee brewed by forcing hot water under pressure through finely ground coffee beans","ex":"The barista carefully pulled a perfect shot of espresso, the dark liquid flowing thick and rich into the tiny cup.","say":"eh-SPREH-soh","hook":"Two sounds trade places over time, usually around an r. That is why through and thorough differ, why nostril hides nose-thirl, and why people say nucular.","o":"Italian"},{"w":"sherbet","def":"a frozen dessert made primarily of fruit juice and sugar, but also containing milk or egg-white or gelatin","ex":"The bright orange sherbet tasted like a creamy blend of tangerines and vanilla on a sweltering July afternoon.","say":"SHUR-buht","hook":"Two sounds trade places over time, usually around an r. That is why through and thorough differ, why nostril hides nose-thirl, and why people say nucular.","o":"Turkish"},{"w":"jewelry","def":"an adornment (as a bracelet or ring or necklace) made of precious metals and set with gems (or imitation gems)","ex":"She kept her grandmother's jewelry in a velvet-lined box on her dresser.","say":"JOO-uh-lree","hook":"Two sounds trade places over time, usually around an r. That is why through and thorough differ, why nostril hides nose-thirl, and why people say nucular.","o":"French"},{"w":"spaghetti","def":"a white starchy pasta of Italian origin","ex":"She twirled the spaghetti around her fork and dipped it into the rich tomato sauce.","say":"s-p-uh-g-EH-t-ee","hook":"Two sounds trade places over time, usually around an r. That is why through and thorough differ, why nostril hides nose-thirl, and why people say nucular.","o":"English"},{"w":"tremble","def":"a reflex motion caused by cold or fear or excitement","ex":"","say":"TREH-mbuhl","hook":"Two sounds trade places over time, usually around an r. That is why through and thorough differ, why nostril hides nose-thirl, and why people say nucular.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"tinsel","def":"a showy decoration that is basically valueless","ex":"All the tinsel of self-promotion.","say":"TIH-nsuhl","hook":"Two sounds trade places over time, usually around an r. That is why through and thorough differ, why nostril hides nose-thirl, and why people say nucular.","o":"French"},{"w":"modern","def":"a contemporary person","ex":"The self-described modern believed that today's artists and thinkers were far more creative and innovative than any who had come before them in previous generations.","say":"MAH-dern","hook":"Two sounds trade places over time, usually around an r. That is why through and thorough differ, why nostril hides nose-thirl, and why people say nucular.","o":"French"},{"w":"hundred","def":"the number equal to ten times ten, written 100","ex":"The baker prepared over a hundred golden croissants for the morning rush at the downtown café.","say":"HUH-ndruhd","hook":"Two sounds trade places over time, usually around an r. That is why through and thorough differ, why nostril hides nose-thirl, and why people say nucular.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"fringe","def":"the outside boundary or surface of something","ex":"","say":"FRIHNJ","hook":"Two sounds trade places over time, usually around an r. That is why through and thorough differ, why nostril hides nose-thirl, and why people say nucular.","o":"French"},{"w":"wasp","def":"a white person of Anglo-Saxon ancestry who belongs to a Protestant denomination","ex":"The yellow jacket wasp built its papery nest under the porch railing over the summer.","say":"WAHSP","hook":"Two sounds trade places over time, usually around an r. That is why through and thorough differ, why nostril hides nose-thirl, and why people say nucular.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"hasp","def":"a fastener for a door or lid; a hinged metal plate is fitted over a staple and is locked with a pin or padlock","ex":"She snapped the hasp shut over the staple and clicked the padlock into place.","say":"HASP","hook":"Two sounds trade places over time, usually around an r. That is why through and thorough differ, why nostril hides nose-thirl, and why people say nucular.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"clasp","def":"a fastener (as a buckle or hook) that is used to hold two things together","ex":"He released his clasp on my arm.","say":"KLASP","hook":"Two sounds trade places over time, usually around an r. That is why through and thorough differ, why nostril hides nose-thirl, and why people say nucular.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"grasp","def":"understanding of the nature or meaning or quality or magnitude of something","ex":"He has a good grasp of accounting practices.","say":"GRASP","hook":"Two sounds trade places over time, usually around an r. That is why through and thorough differ, why nostril hides nose-thirl, and why people say nucular.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"curd","def":"the soft, white lumps that form when milk thickens or sours","ex":"When the chef added lemon juice to the warm milk, a soft white curd began to form, separating slowly from the thin, watery whey surrounding it.","say":"KURD","hook":"Two sounds trade places over time, usually around an r. That is why through and thorough differ, why nostril hides nose-thirl, and why people say nucular.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"crud","def":"heavy wet snow that is unsuitable for skiing","ex":"He said he had the crud and needed a doctor.","say":"KRUHD","hook":"Two sounds trade places over time, usually around an r. That is why through and thorough differ, why nostril hides nose-thirl, and why people say nucular."},{"w":"bird","def":"warm-blooded egg-laying vertebrates characterized by feathers and forelimbs modified as wings","ex":"A bright red cardinal perched on the snowy branch, the only colorful bird visible in the winter landscape.","say":"BURD","hook":"Two sounds trade places over time, usually around an r. That is why through and thorough differ, why nostril hides nose-thirl, and why people say nucular.","o":"Malay"},{"w":"third","def":"one of three equal parts of a divisible whole","ex":"She ate one third of the pizza and saved the rest for her brother and sister.","say":"THURD","hook":"Two sounds trade places over time, usually around an r. That is why through and thorough differ, why nostril hides nose-thirl, and why people say nucular.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"pretty","def":"pleasing by delicacy or grace; not imposing","ex":"The hillside meadow was pretty in late spring, blanketed with wildflowers in shades of violet, yellow, and white that swayed gently in the warm afternoon breeze.","say":"PRIH-tee","hook":"Two sounds trade places over time, usually around an r. That is why through and thorough differ, why nostril hides nose-thirl, and why people say nucular.","o":"Old English"}]},{"title":"Clipping, blending, back-formation, reduplication","category":"How Words Are Built","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"Not every word is inherited. English manufactures words, and it does so in four recognisable ways. Clipping cuts a long word short. Blending fuses two words into one. Back-formation invents a shorter word by removing an ending that was never a suffix at all. Reduplication repeats a syllable with a change. Each method leaves spelling fingerprints, and knowing which one produced a word tells you whether to keep the original letters or the new ones.","method":"<div class='trick'>CHORTLE</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> CHOR-tul — to laugh with glee\n<b>Origin.</b> a blend of chuckle and snort, invented by Lewis Carroll\n<b>Rule.</b> a blend keeps the start of the first word and the end of the second\n<b>Spell.</b> C H O R T L E\n<b>Family.</b> galumph — Carroll's other surviving blend","cards":[{"title":"Clipping","body":"Cut a long word short and keep the spelling of the part you kept. Flu from influenza, so it is f-l-u with no e. Fridge from refrigerator — and note that fridge gained a d the original never had, because English needed it to keep the g soft. Bus from omnibus, cab from cabriolet, gym from gymnasium, hippo, rhino, memo, zoo, exam, photo."},{"title":"Blending","body":"Fuse two words and keep the front of one and the back of the other. Brunch from breakfast and lunch. Smog from smoke and fog. Motel from motor and hotel. Chortle from chuckle and snort. Galumph from gallop and triumph. Workaholic, emoticon, infomercial, staycation, telethon, spork. The seam is invisible, which is why you must know both parents."},{"title":"Back-formation","body":"The strangest method. English sees a word, wrongly assumes part of it is a suffix, removes it, and creates a shorter word that never existed. Editor came first and edit was carved out of it. Pease was already singular and pea was cut from it. Cherise gave cherry the same way. Burgle came from burglar, and burger from hamburger — which was named for Hamburg and never contained ham."},{"title":"Reduplication","body":"Repeat a syllable, usually with one sound changed. Rhyming: hocus-pocus, razzle-dazzle, hunky-dory, namby-pamby. Vowel-changing: zigzag, flimflam, riffraff, knickknack, dillydally, wishy-washy, chitchat. English strongly prefers i in the first half and a or o in the second — never zagzig or flamflim. That preference is a real spelling rule."}],"words":[{"w":"infomercial","def":"a television commercial presented in the form of a short documentary","ex":"The late-night infomercial promised that the blender could turn almost anything into a smoothie.","say":"IH-nfoh-mer-shuh-l","hook":"Four ways English makes short words: cut one down, fuse two, invent a root by removing a suffix that was never there, or repeat with a twist.","o":"Anglo-Norman"},{"w":"workaholic","def":"person with a compulsive need to work","ex":"Such a workaholic was she that she brought her laptop on vacation and answered emails from the beach.","say":"wur-kuh-HAH-lihk","hook":"Four ways English makes short words: cut one down, fuse two, invent a root by removing a suffix that was never there, or repeat with a twist.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"staycation","def":"A vacation spent at home or in one's local area rather than traveling to a distant destination, often involving leisure activities close to home.","ex":"Since flights were too expensive, the family enjoyed a staycation exploring local parks and museums.","say":"stay-KAY-shun","hook":"Four ways English makes short words: cut one down, fuse two, invent a root by removing a suffix that was never there, or repeat with a twist.","o":"French"},{"w":"knickknack","def":"a small inexpensive mass-produced article","ex":"Grandma kept every souvenir magnet and knickknack from her travels on the living room shelf.","say":"NIH-knak","hook":"Four ways English makes short words: cut one down, fuse two, invent a root by removing a suffix that was never there, or repeat with a twist.","o":"Dutch"},{"w":"dillydally","def":"postpone doing what one should be doing","ex":"Don't dillydally on the way home, or you'll miss dinner with us.","say":"dih-lee-DA-lee","hook":"Four ways English makes short words: cut one down, fuse two, invent a root by removing a suffix that was never there, or repeat with a twist."},{"w":"flimflam","def":"a swindle in which you cheat at gambling or persuade a person to buy worthless property","ex":"The traveling salesman's flimflam fooled the entire town into buying bottles of colored water as miracle cures.","say":"FLIH-mflam","hook":"Four ways English makes short words: cut one down, fuse two, invent a root by removing a suffix that was never there, or repeat with a twist.","o":"Norse"},{"w":"riffraff","def":"disparaging terms for the common people","ex":"The snobbish hotel manager looked down his nose at the tourists, dismissing them as riffraff who didn't belong in his establishment.","say":"RIH-fraf","hook":"Four ways English makes short words: cut one down, fuse two, invent a root by removing a suffix that was never there, or repeat with a twist.","o":"French"},{"w":"emoticon","def":"a representation of a facial expression (as a smile or frown) created by typing a sequence of characters in sending email","ex":":-( and :-) are emoticons.","say":"uh-MOH-tuh-kah-n","hook":"Four ways English makes short words: cut one down, fuse two, invent a root by removing a suffix that was never there, or repeat with a twist.","o":"French"},{"w":"galumph","def":"move around heavily and clumsily","ex":"The giant tortoises galumphed around in their pen.","say":"guh-LUMF","hook":"Four ways English makes short words: cut one down, fuse two, invent a root by removing a suffix that was never there, or repeat with a twist."},{"w":"chortle","def":"a soft, partly suppressed laugh expressing pleasure or amusement; a blend of chuckle and snort","ex":"Marcus let out a chortle when his little sister tried to sneak a cookie and accidentally knocked the whole jar off the shelf.","say":"CHAW-rtuhl","hook":"Four ways English makes short words: cut one down, fuse two, invent a root by removing a suffix that was never there, or repeat with a twist."},{"w":"brunch","def":"combination breakfast and lunch; usually served in late morning","ex":"We brunch in Sundays.","say":"BRUHNCH","hook":"Four ways English makes short words: cut one down, fuse two, invent a root by removing a suffix that was never there, or repeat with a twist."},{"w":"zigzag","def":"an angular shape characterized by sharp turns in alternating directions","ex":"The river zigzags through the countryside.","say":"ZIH-gzag","hook":"Four ways English makes short words: cut one down, fuse two, invent a root by removing a suffix that was never there, or repeat with a twist.","o":"German"},{"w":"fridge","def":"a refrigerator in which the coolant is pumped around by an electric motor","ex":"After school, Marcus opened the fridge and grabbed a cold apple and some leftover pizza for a snack.","say":"FRIHJ","hook":"Four ways English makes short words: cut one down, fuse two, invent a root by removing a suffix that was never there, or repeat with a twist."},{"w":"motel","def":"a motor hotel","ex":"The family stopped at a roadside motel halfway through their cross-country road trip.","say":"moh-TEHL","hook":"Four ways English makes short words: cut one down, fuse two, invent a root by removing a suffix that was never there, or repeat with a twist.","o":"French"},{"w":"smog","def":"air pollution by a mixture of smoke and fog","ex":"A thick layer of smog hung over the city, making it hard to see the mountains in the distance.","say":"SMAHG","hook":"Four ways English makes short words: cut one down, fuse two, invent a root by removing a suffix that was never there, or repeat with a twist."},{"w":"burger","def":"United States jurist appointed chief justice of the United States Supreme Court by Richard Nixon (1907-1995)","ex":"Chief Justice Burger presided over the Supreme Court during a period of landmark legal decisions.","say":"BUR-ger","hook":"Four ways English makes short words: cut one down, fuse two, invent a root by removing a suffix that was never there, or repeat with a twist.","o":"German"},{"w":"cherry","def":"a tree that gives small red fruit and reddish wood used in furniture","ex":"The carpenter chose fine cherry for the bookcase because of its warm reddish tone and beautiful grain.","say":"CHEH-ree","hook":"Four ways English makes short words: cut one down, fuse two, invent a root by removing a suffix that was never there, or repeat with a twist.","o":"French"},{"w":"hippo","def":"an ancient Numidian town in northwestern Africa adjoining present-day Annaba in northeastern Algeria","ex":"Saint Augustine served as bishop in the ancient city of Hippo, located in North Africa.","say":"HIH-poh","hook":"Four ways English makes short words: cut one down, fuse two, invent a root by removing a suffix that was never there, or repeat with a twist.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"rhino","def":"massive powerful herbivorous odd-toed ungulate of southeast Asia and Africa having very thick skin and one or two horns on the snout","ex":"The endangered white rhino grazed slowly across the African savanna as tourists watched in amazement.","say":"REYE-noh","hook":"Four ways English makes short words: cut one down, fuse two, invent a root by removing a suffix that was never there, or repeat with a twist.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"memo","def":"a written proposal or reminder","ex":"The principal sent a memo to all teachers reminding them about the staff meeting on Friday.","say":"MEH-moh","hook":"Four ways English makes short words: cut one down, fuse two, invent a root by removing a suffix that was never there, or repeat with a twist.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"edit","def":"prepare for publication or presentation by correcting, revising, or adapting","ex":"Edit a book on lexical semantics.","say":"EH-duht","hook":"Four ways English makes short words: cut one down, fuse two, invent a root by removing a suffix that was never there, or repeat with a twist.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"flu","def":"an acute febrile highly contagious viral disease","ex":"Half the class was absent because the flu had spread rapidly through the school that week.","say":"FLOO","hook":"Four ways English makes short words: cut one down, fuse two, invent a root by removing a suffix that was never there, or repeat with a twist.","o":"Italian"},{"w":"gym","def":"athletic facility equipped for sports or physical training","ex":"After school, Marcus headed straight to the gym to practice his basketball free throws.","say":"JIHM","hook":"Four ways English makes short words: cut one down, fuse two, invent a root by removing a suffix that was never there, or repeat with a twist.","o":"Greek"},{"w":"cab","def":"a compartment at the front of a motor vehicle or locomotive where driver sits","ex":"The truck driver climbed into the cab and adjusted the mirrors before pulling onto the highway.","say":"KAB","hook":"Four ways English makes short words: cut one down, fuse two, invent a root by removing a suffix that was never there, or repeat with a twist.","o":"Hebrew"},{"w":"bus","def":"a vehicle carrying many passengers; used for public transport","ex":"He always rode the bus to work.","say":"BUHS","hook":"Four ways English makes short words: cut one down, fuse two, invent a root by removing a suffix that was never there, or repeat with a twist.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"pea","def":"A small, round, green seed eaten as a vegetable.","ex":"She carefully shelled each pea from the garden pod before dropping them into the pot of soup.","say":"PEE","hook":"Four ways English makes short words: cut one down, fuse two, invent a root by removing a suffix that was never there, or repeat with a twist."},{"w":"zoo","def":"the facility where wild animals are housed for exhibition","ex":"On a sunny Saturday, families lined up outside the zoo eager to see the newly arrived baby elephant.","say":"ZOO","hook":"Four ways English makes short words: cut one down, fuse two, invent a root by removing a suffix that was never there, or repeat with a twist.","o":"Greek"}]},{"title":"Folk etymology — wrong guesses that became correct","category":"How Words Are Built","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"When a borrowed or archaic word contained an element speakers no longer understood, they reshaped it to resemble something familiar — and often the reshaping stuck and became the dictionary spelling. This explains a whole class of words whose letters contradict their history: an island with an s it never had, a bridegroom with no groom in it, a hangnail that never hung. Knowing the real origin is what lets you remember the illogical spelling.","method":"<div class='trick'>ISLAND</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> EYE-lund\n<b>Origin.</b> Old English igland — ig meant island already, land was added\n<b>What happened.</b> the s was inserted by scribes copying French isle, from Latin insula\n<b>Verdict.</b> the s is a mistake that became compulsory\n<b>Spell.</b> I S L A N D","cards":[{"title":"Letters inserted by mistake","body":"Island had no s: Old English igland was respelled to match French isle. Could had no l: Old English cuthe gained one by analogy with should and would. Ptarmigan had no p: Gaelic tarmachan was given a Greek-looking p by a scholar who thought it looked more learned. In each case a wrong guess is now the only correct spelling."},{"title":"Words reshaped to look familiar","body":"Bridegroom was Old English brydguma, bride-man — guma meant man, and when that word died English replaced it with groom, which then meant a boy. Coleslaw is Dutch koolsalade, cabbage salad, respelled as though it were cold slaw. Hangnail was angnail, from ang meaning painful, and had nothing to do with hanging. Cockroach was Spanish cucaracha, reshaped into cock and roach."},{"title":"Belfry had no bell","body":"Belfry is the finest example. It was berfrey, a movable siege tower, from Old French berfrei. Since towers held bells, English heard bell in the first syllable and respelled it. Similarly penthouse was apentis, an attached lean-to, from the same root as append — nothing to do with houses. Titmouse ends in mose, an Old English word for a small bird, not mouse."},{"title":"How to use this","body":"These words cannot be derived, so the origin story is the memory aid. You will not deduce the s in island, but you will remember that scribes put it there to match isle. Posthumous gained its h because scholars linked it to humus, earth, when it is really postumus, last. Carat, rosemary, liquorice, asparagus and curmudgeon all carry similar scars."}],"words":[{"w":"bridegroom","def":"a man who has recently been married","ex":"The nervous bridegroom smiled with relief when he finally saw his partner walking down the aisle.","say":"BREYE-dgroom","hook":"When a word stopped making sense, English respelled it to look like words it did recognise. Those wrong guesses are now the official spellings.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"posthumous","def":"is arising occurring or continuing after one's death","ex":"The soldier's posthumous medal of honor was presented to his tearful daughter at a solemn ceremony, recognizing the extraordinary courage he had shown during his final mission overseas.","say":"p-AH-s-ch-uh-m-uh-s","hook":"When a word stopped making sense, English respelled it to look like words it did recognise. Those wrong guesses are now the official spellings.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"curmudgeon","def":"a crusty, irascible, cantankerous old person full of stubborn ideas","ex":"The old curmudgeon next door shouted at every child who dared to cut across his perfectly manicured lawn.","say":"k-ur-m-UH-j-ih-n","hook":"When a word stopped making sense, English respelled it to look like words it did recognise. Those wrong guesses are now the official spellings.","o":"English"},{"w":"liquorice","def":"deep-rooted coarse-textured plant native to the Mediterranean region having blue flowers and pinnately compound leaves; widely cultivated in Europe for its long thick sweet roots","ex":"The old-fashioned candy shop sold twisted ropes of black liquorice that made your tongue turn dark.","say":"LIK-er-ish","hook":"When a word stopped making sense, English respelled it to look like words it did recognise. Those wrong guesses are now the official spellings.","o":"French"},{"w":"asparagus","def":"plant whose succulent young shoots are cooked and eaten as a vegetable","ex":"Mom roasted the asparagus with olive oil and garlic until the tips were slightly crispy and the stalks were tender.","say":"uh-SPEH-ruh-guhs","hook":"When a word stopped making sense, English respelled it to look like words it did recognise. Those wrong guesses are now the official spellings.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"rosemary","def":"widely cultivated for its fragrant grey-green leaves used in cooking and in perfumery","ex":"She added a sprig of rosemary to the roasted chicken to give it a rich, herbal flavor.","say":"ROH-zmeh-ree","hook":"When a word stopped making sense, English respelled it to look like words it did recognise. Those wrong guesses are now the official spellings.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"cockroach","def":"any of numerous chiefly nocturnal insects; some are domestic pests","ex":"A single cockroach scurried across the kitchen floor and disappeared behind the refrigerator.","say":"KAH-krohch","hook":"When a word stopped making sense, English respelled it to look like words it did recognise. Those wrong guesses are now the official spellings.","o":"Spanish"},{"w":"penthouse","def":"an apartment located on the top floors of a building","ex":"The wealthy architect designed a stunning penthouse with floor-to-ceiling windows overlooking the entire city skyline.","say":"PEH-nthows","hook":"When a word stopped making sense, English respelled it to look like words it did recognise. Those wrong guesses are now the official spellings.","o":"Anglo-Norman"},{"w":"hangnail","def":"a loose narrow strip of skin near the base of a fingernail; tearing it produces a painful sore that is easily infected","ex":"She winced as she accidentally tore the hangnail on her thumb.","say":"HANG-nayl","hook":"When a word stopped making sense, English respelled it to look like words it did recognise. Those wrong guesses are now the official spellings.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"coleslaw","def":"basically shredded cabbage","ex":"He piled tangy coleslaw on top of his pulled pork sandwich at the summer cookout.","say":"KOH-lslah","hook":"When a word stopped making sense, English respelled it to look like words it did recognise. Those wrong guesses are now the official spellings.","o":"Dutch"},{"w":"muskrat","def":"a North American water rodent with webbed back feet, a flat scaly tail, and glossy brown fur","ex":"The trapper carefully stretched the muskrat pelt on a wooden frame to dry in the cool autumn air.","say":"MUH-skrat","hook":"When a word stopped making sense, English respelled it to look like words it did recognise. Those wrong guesses are now the official spellings."},{"w":"woodchuck","def":"reddish brown North American marmot","ex":"A plump woodchuck sat at the edge of the garden, eyeing the vegetable patch before waddling over to nibble on a carrot top.","say":"WUU-dchuhk","hook":"When a word stopped making sense, English respelled it to look like words it did recognise. Those wrong guesses are now the official spellings."},{"w":"mushroom","def":"common name for an edible agaric (contrasting with the inedible toadstool)","ex":"She carefully sliced a portobello mushroom and added it to the sizzling pan of vegetables.","say":"MUH-shroom","hook":"When a word stopped making sense, English respelled it to look like words it did recognise. Those wrong guesses are now the official spellings.","o":"French"},{"w":"primrose","def":"any of numerous short-stemmed plants of the genus Primula having tufted basal leaves and showy flowers clustered in umbels or heads","ex":"A cheerful yellow primrose bloomed in the garden, signaling that spring had finally arrived.","say":"PRIH-mrohz","hook":"When a word stopped making sense, English respelled it to look like words it did recognise. Those wrong guesses are now the official spellings.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"sirloin","def":"the portion of the loin (especially of beef) just in front of the rump","ex":"Dad grilled a thick sirloin steak for the family barbecue.","say":"SUR-loyn","hook":"When a word stopped making sense, English respelled it to look like words it did recognise. Those wrong guesses are now the official spellings.","o":"French"},{"w":"hiccough","def":"(usually plural) the state of having reflex spasms of the diaphragm accompanied by a rapid closure of the glottis producing an audible sound; sometimes a symptom of indigestion","ex":"","say":"HIH-kuhp","hook":"When a word stopped making sense, English respelled it to look like words it did recognise. Those wrong guesses are now the official spellings."},{"w":"belfry","def":"a bell tower; usually stands alone unattached to a building","ex":"Bats swooped out of the old church belfry at dusk, circling the stone tower before disappearing into the night sky.","say":"BEH-lfree","hook":"When a word stopped making sense, English respelled it to look like words it did recognise. Those wrong guesses are now the official spellings.","o":"French"},{"w":"island","def":"a land mass (smaller than a continent) that is surrounded by water","ex":"The tiny island in the middle of the lake was covered in pine trees and completely silent.","say":"EYE-luhnd","hook":"When a word stopped making sense, English respelled it to look like words it did recognise. Those wrong guesses are now the official spellings.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"carat","def":"a unit of weight for precious stones = 200 mg","ex":"The jeweler examined the diamond and confirmed it was a flawless two-carat stone.","say":"KEH-ruht","hook":"When a word stopped making sense, English respelled it to look like words it did recognise. Those wrong guesses are now the official spellings.","o":"French"},{"w":"chaise","def":"a long chair; for reclining","ex":"She curled up on the velvet chaise by the window and read her novel all afternoon.","say":"SHAYZ","hook":"When a word stopped making sense, English respelled it to look like words it did recognise. Those wrong guesses are now the official spellings.","o":"French"},{"w":"pickax","def":"a heavy iron tool with a wooden handle and a curved head that is pointed on both ends","ex":"","say":"PIK-aks","hook":"When a word stopped making sense, English respelled it to look like words it did recognise. Those wrong guesses are now the official spellings.","o":"Old English"},{"w":"helpmate","def":"a helpful partner","ex":"Throughout his long career, his wife was his most trusted helpmate and advisor.","say":"HELP-mayt","hook":"When a word stopped making sense, English respelled it to look like words it did recognise. Those wrong guesses are now the official spellings.","o":"Old English"}]},{"title":"Tautonyms — words that repeat themselves exactly","category":"How Words Are Built","difficulty":"expert","adv":true,"concept":"A tautonym is a word made of the same element twice with nothing altered — unlike reduplication, where a vowel changes. They are unusually easy competition words once you recognise the shape, because you only need to spell half of it and then repeat exactly. The reason they exist is that many languages use repetition as grammar, marking a plural or an intensity, and English borrowed the doubled form whole.","method":"<div class='trick'>BERIBERI</div>\n<b>Heard.</b> berry-BERRY — a disease caused by lack of thiamine\n<b>Origin.</b> Sinhalese beri, weakness, said twice for emphasis\n<b>Rule.</b> the halves are identical, so spell b-e-r-i once and repeat it\n<b>Spell.</b> B E R I B E R I\n<b>Family.</b> laulau, mahimahi — the same doubling","cards":[{"title":"Repetition as grammar","body":"In many languages, saying a word twice does real grammatical work: it makes a plural, an intensive, or a diminutive. Sinhalese beri, weakness, doubled becomes beriberi, great weakness. Malay and Hawaiian use doubling constantly. So the doubled form is the actual word in the source language, not an English decoration."},{"title":"The Pacific set","body":"Hawaiian and Polynesian languages give the largest group. Laulau, a wrapped dish. Mahimahi, the dolphinfish. Lavalava, a wrapped garment. Makomako and poroporo, New Zealand plants. Muumuu, the dress. All obey the Hawaiian rules from the origins chapter too — thirteen letters, every syllable ending in a vowel — so they are doubly predictable."},{"title":"The rest of the world","body":"Couscous is Berber. Tsetse is Tswana. Juju is West African. Dodo is Portuguese doudo, a simpleton. Chowchow, greegree, quiaquia, caracara, gang-gang, boubou. And the French pairs: bonbon, froufrou, cancan, tutu. Notice that these are food, animals, clothing and music — concrete things that got their names by repetition."},{"title":"Do not confuse them with near-doubles","body":"A true tautonym repeats exactly. Words like murmur, tartar, tartan and dumdum look similar but are not all the same thing — murmur is Latin murmurare, imitating a sound, and tartar has a complicated Greek and Arabic history. And zigzag, flimflam and riffraff change the vowel, which makes them reduplications, not tautonyms. Only three true ones sit in the core library, and 42 in the full library."}],"words":[{"w":"beriberi","def":"a disease caused by a vitamin B1 deficiency","ex":"Sailors on long voyages who ate only white rice sometimes developed beriberi due to the lack of thiamine in their diet.","say":"BEHR-ee-behr-ee","hook":"A tautonym repeats a syllable with no change at all: beriberi, laulau, couscous. Most come from languages where repetition is grammar, so the halves are always identical.","o":"English"},{"w":"mahimahi","def":"the lean flesh of a saltwater fish found in warm waters (especially in Hawaii)","ex":"The restaurant served grilled mahimahi with a mango salsa that tasted like a tropical vacation.","say":"mah-hee-MAH-hee","hook":"A tautonym repeats a syllable with no change at all: beriberi, laulau, couscous. Most come from languages where repetition is grammar, so the halves are always identical."},{"w":"caracara","def":"any of various long-legged carrion-eating hawks of South America and Central America","ex":"The caracara strutted boldly across the savanna, scavenging scraps left behind by larger predators.","say":"kah-ruh-KAH-ruh","hook":"A tautonym repeats a syllable with no change at all: beriberi, laulau, couscous. Most come from languages where repetition is grammar, so the halves are always identical.","o":"Spanish"},{"w":"chowchow","def":"chopped pickles in mustard sauce","ex":"She spread tangy chowchow relish across the hot dog before handing it to her brother.","say":"CHOW-chow","hook":"A tautonym repeats a syllable with no change at all: beriberi, laulau, couscous. Most come from languages where repetition is grammar, so the halves are always identical."},{"w":"couscous","def":"a spicy dish that originated in northern Africa; consists of pasta steamed with a meat and vegetable stew","ex":"She served a fragrant couscous topped with roasted vegetables and a drizzle of lemon-herb sauce.","say":"KOOS-koos","hook":"A tautonym repeats a syllable with no change at all: beriberi, laulau, couscous. Most come from languages where repetition is grammar, so the halves are always identical.","o":"Arabic"},{"w":"greegree","def":"an African amulet","ex":"The traveler bought a greegree from the market vendor, believing it would protect him from harm.","say":"GREE-gree","hook":"A tautonym repeats a syllable with no change at all: beriberi, laulau, couscous. Most come from languages where repetition is grammar, so the halves are always identical."},{"w":"lavalava","def":"a skirt consisting of a rectangle of calico or printed cotton; worn by Polynesians (especially Samoans)","ex":"He wore a brightly patterned lavalava to the island festival, moving gracefully as he danced.","say":"lah-vah-LAH-vah","hook":"A tautonym repeats a syllable with no change at all: beriberi, laulau, couscous. Most come from languages where repetition is grammar, so the halves are always identical."},{"w":"makomako","def":"graceful deciduous shrub or small tree having attractive foliage and small red berries that turn black at maturity and are used for making wine","ex":"The hikers admired the makomako tree's delicate red berries glowing against its bright green leaves.","say":"mah-koh-MAH-koh","hook":"A tautonym repeats a syllable with no change at all: beriberi, laulau, couscous. Most come from languages where repetition is grammar, so the halves are always identical."},{"w":"poroporo","def":"Australian annual sometimes cultivated for its racemes of purple flowers and edible yellow egg-shaped fruit","ex":"The gardener planted poroporo along the fence, admiring its clusters of vivid purple flowers each spring.","say":"por-oh-POR-oh","hook":"A tautonym repeats a syllable with no change at all: beriberi, laulau, couscous. Most come from languages where repetition is grammar, so the halves are always identical."},{"w":"quiaquia","def":"small fusiform fish of western Atlantic","ex":"The marine biologist spotted a quiaquia darting through the warm Atlantic shallows near the reef.","say":"kwee-AH-kwee-ah","hook":"A tautonym repeats a syllable with no change at all: beriberi, laulau, couscous. Most come from languages where repetition is grammar, so the halves are always identical."},{"w":"gang-gang","def":"A medium-sized Australian cockatoo with a distinctive rounded head, the male of which has a bright red head and crest, known for its unique creaking call that resembles a cork being pulled from a bottle.","ex":"The gang-gang cockatoo tapped its bright red crest as it chewed seeds from the eucalyptus branch.","say":"GANG-gang","hook":"A tautonym repeats a syllable with no change at all: beriberi, laulau, couscous. Most come from languages where repetition is grammar, so the halves are always identical.","o":"Wiradhuri"},{"w":"laulau","def":"A traditional Hawaiian dish consisting of pork, fish, or other ingredients wrapped in taro leaves and ti leaves, then steamed or baked.","ex":"At the luau, the steaming laulau bundles were unwrapped to reveal tender pork and buttery taro leaves that melted in your mouth.","say":"LOW-low","hook":"A tautonym repeats a syllable with no change at all: beriberi, laulau, couscous. Most come from languages where repetition is grammar, so the halves are always identical."},{"w":"muumuu","def":"a woman's loose unbelted dress","ex":"She wore a brightly patterned muumuu to the beach luau, swaying comfortably in the ocean breeze.","say":"MOO-moo","hook":"A tautonym repeats a syllable with no change at all: beriberi, laulau, couscous. Most come from languages where repetition is grammar, so the halves are always identical.","o":"Hawaiian"},{"w":"tsetse","def":"bloodsucking African fly; transmits sleeping sickness etc.","ex":"Travelers in sub-Saharan Africa are warned to wear protective clothing to avoid the bite of the deadly tsetse fly.","say":"TSEE-tsee","hook":"A tautonym repeats a syllable with no change at all: beriberi, laulau, couscous. Most come from languages where repetition is grammar, so the halves are always identical.","o":"Tswana"},{"w":"froufrou","def":"elaborate or excessive ornamentation, frills, or fussiness in clothing or decoration; also used to describe a rustling sound made by a silk or taffeta garment","ex":"The boutique was filled with froufrou decorations — lace, ribbons, and silk bows on every shelf.","say":"FROO-froo","hook":"A tautonym repeats a syllable with no change at all: beriberi, laulau, couscous. Most come from languages where repetition is grammar, so the halves are always identical.","o":"French"},{"w":"bonbon","def":"a candy that usually has a center of fondant or fruit or nuts coated in chocolate","ex":"She unwrapped a chocolate bonbon and savored its creamy fondant center slowly.","say":"BON-bon","hook":"A tautonym repeats a syllable with no change at all: beriberi, laulau, couscous. Most come from languages where repetition is grammar, so the halves are always identical.","o":"French"},{"w":"cancan","def":"a high-kicking dance of French origin performed by a female chorus line","ex":"The dancers' ruffled skirts flew into the air as they performed a lively cancan on the Parisian stage.","say":"KA-nkan","hook":"A tautonym repeats a syllable with no change at all: beriberi, laulau, couscous. Most come from languages where repetition is grammar, so the halves are always identical.","o":"French"},{"w":"dumdum","def":"a soft-nosed small-arms bullet that expands when it hits a target and causes a gaping wound","ex":"The use of the dumdum bullet was banned by international treaty because of the devastating wounds it caused.","say":"DUM-dum","hook":"A tautonym repeats a syllable with no change at all: beriberi, laulau, couscous. Most come from languages where repetition is grammar, so the halves are always identical."},{"w":"atlatl","def":"A device, typically a flat board or stick with a hooked end, used to hurl a spear or dart with greater force and distance than by hand alone.","ex":"The museum volunteer demonstrated how an atlatl could launch a dart nearly twice as far as an arm throw, astonishing the crowd of students.","say":"AT-latl","hook":"A tautonym repeats a syllable with no change at all: beriberi, laulau, couscous. Most come from languages where repetition is grammar, so the halves are always identical.","o":"Classical Nahuatl"},{"w":"murmur","def":"a low continuous indistinct sound; often accompanied by movement of the lips without the production of articulate speech","ex":"A soft murmur of conversation drifted from the café into the quiet street outside.","say":"MUR-mer","hook":"A tautonym repeats a syllable with no change at all: beriberi, laulau, couscous. Most come from languages where repetition is grammar, so the halves are always identical.","o":"Latin"},{"w":"tartar","def":"a salt used especially in baking powder","ex":"The dentist warned that a buildup of tartar on the teeth can lead to serious gum problems.","say":"TAH-rter","hook":"A tautonym repeats a syllable with no change at all: beriberi, laulau, couscous. Most come from languages where repetition is grammar, so the halves are always identical.","o":"French"},{"w":"juju","def":"A magic charm or power in some West African beliefs.","ex":"The elder warned the travelers not to disturb the ancient shrine, as it carried powerful juju.","say":"JOO-joo","hook":"A tautonym repeats a syllable with no change at all: beriberi, laulau, couscous. Most come from languages where repetition is grammar, so the halves are always identical."},{"w":"dodo","def":"someone whose style is out of fashion","ex":"My uncle still uses a flip phone and refuses to text, making him a real dodo in the eyes of his teenage nephews.","say":"DOH-doh","hook":"A tautonym repeats a syllable with no change at all: beriberi, laulau, couscous. Most come from languages where repetition is grammar, so the halves are always identical.","o":"Portuguese"},{"w":"tutu","def":"South African prelate and leader of the antiapartheid struggle (born in 1931)","ex":"The young dancer twirled across the stage, her pink tutu fanning out like the petals of a flower.","say":"TOO-too","hook":"A tautonym repeats a syllable with no change at all: beriberi, laulau, couscous. Most come from languages where repetition is grammar, so the halves are always identical.","o":"French"}]}]};
window.SB_ADV_CSCRIPT={"0":{"label":"schwa rescue","scenes":[{"mood":"think","cap":"The schwa gives you nothing","say":"Every other sound in English narrows your options. Hear an eff sound and you are choosing between three spellings. Hear a schwa, and you are choosing between every vowel in the alphabet, plus the letter y. It is the only sound that tells you nothing at all.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"ə","sub":"any vowel, and y"}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"DEF-uh-nit — which vowel is that?","say":"Take the word definite. The second syllable is unstressed, so it reduces. Def, uh, nit. That uh could be an a, an e, an i, an o or a u. Listening harder will not help you, because the information is not in the sound.","show":{"t":"rescue","unclear":{"word":"definite","at":3}}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"Find a relative that stresses it","say":"So do not listen harder. Find a relative. Definition. Now the syllable takes the stress. Def, i, NI, tion. The vowel is an i, and it was an i in definite all along.","show":{"t":"rescue","unclear":{"word":"definite","at":3},"clear":{"word":"definition","syl":["def","i","ni","tion"],"on":2}}},{"mood":"think","cap":"It works across the whole language","say":"This is not a trick for one word. English keeps a vowel spelled the same across a word family even when the sound changes. Custody is unclear; custodian is not. Major is unclear; majority is not. Local is unclear; locality is not.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"custodian","hi":[4,1]},{"word":"majority","hi":[3,1]},{"word":"locality","hi":[3,1]}]}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"Four suffixes do the work","say":"The suffixes that help are the ones that pull stress toward themselves. Ity. Ic. Ian. Ation. When you hear a schwa, reach for one of those forms of the same word, because those are the endings that relocate the stress onto the syllable you could not hear.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"-ity -ic -ian -ation","sub":"these pull the stress"}},{"mood":"think","cap":"No relative? Go to the root","say":"Some words have no stressed relative. For those, the root is your route. Definite comes from the Latin finis, meaning boundary, and that carries an i as well. And if the root is unknown, ask for the language of origin and spell to that language default. Guessing a is not a strategy.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"definite","parts":[{"txt":"de-","gloss":"completely"},{"txt":"fin","gloss":"boundary"},{"txt":"-ite","gloss":"made so"}]}}]},"1":{"label":"stress shift","scenes":[{"mood":"think","cap":"Stress is not fixed","say":"Stress in English moves. Attach a suffix and the loud beat can jump to a different syllable, and whichever syllable loses the stress reduces to a schwa. So stress shift is not a curiosity. It is the mechanism that decides which vowel is about to fail you.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"stress moves","sub":"and vowels reduce"}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"PHO-to-graph → pho-TOG-ra-phy","say":"Photograph. The stress is on the first syllable. Now add a y. Photography. The stress has moved to the second. Add ic instead, and you get photographic, with the stress on the third. One family, three stress patterns.","show":{"t":"shift","rows":[{"label":"photograph","syl":["pho","to","graph"],"on":0},{"label":"photography","syl":["pho","tog","ra","phy"],"on":1},{"label":"photographic","syl":["pho","to","graph","ic"],"on":2}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"The consequence","say":"Here is why that matters. In photograph, the second o is unstressed and mumbled. In photography, the same o carries the stress and rings out clearly. Same letter, same family, two different degrees of certainty. Borrow from the certain one.","show":{"t":"shift","rows":[{"label":"photograph","syl":["pho","to","graph"],"on":0,"dim":1},{"label":"photography","syl":["pho","tog","ra","phy"],"on":1}]}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"Which suffixes move stress","say":"Ity, ic, ian, ify, ation and ial pull the stress toward themselves, usually onto the syllable right before them. Ness, less, ment, ful, ly and er leave the stress exactly where it was. The first group is the Latin layer of English. The second is the Germanic layer, and it behaves politely.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"monarchic","hi":[3,3]},{"word":"patriarchal","hi":[6,3]},{"word":"lithography","hi":[4,3]}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Learn families, not words","say":"Strong competitors do not memorise words. They memorise families, because one family shares one set of vowels across three or four stress patterns. Monarch, monarchy, monarchic. Lithograph, lithography, lithographic. Learn the family and every member becomes spellable.","show":{"t":"shift","rows":[{"label":"monarch","syl":["mon","arch"],"on":0},{"label":"monarchy","syl":["mon","ar","chy"],"on":0},{"label":"monarchic","syl":["mo","nar","chic"],"on":1}]}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"The base form is the hard one","say":"One warning. The base form is usually the hardest member of a family to spell, because base forms carry the most unstressed syllables. Derived forms are easier. So when a base form is called, reach for the derivative in your head first, and work back from it.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"base → derived → back","sub":"spell the easy one first"}}]},"2":{"label":"the origin tree","scenes":[{"mood":"think","cap":"Knowing origins is not using them","say":"You can know that Greek uses p h for an eff sound and still freeze at the microphone. Knowing the origins is not the same as being able to use them. The usable form runs the other way: you hear a sound, and you need the candidate spellings, ranked.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"sound → spellings","sub":"the inverse lookup"}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"One sound, five spellings","say":"Take the sh sound. In English it is s h, as in shell. In French it is c h, as in chef. In German it is s c h, as in schadenfreude. In Italian it is s c i, as in prosciutto. In Latin words it can be t i or c i, as in nation. Five branches from one sound.","show":{"t":"tree","sound":"/sh/","branches":[{"sp":"sh","org":"English","ex":"shell"},{"sp":"ch","org":"French","ex":"chef"},{"sp":"sch","org":"German","ex":"schnitzel"},{"sp":"sci","org":"Italian","ex":"prosciutto"},{"sp":"ti","org":"Latin","ex":"nation"}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Ask, and the tree collapses","say":"Now ask one question. What is the language of origin? The answer does not give you a letter. It gives you an entire orthography, and the five branches collapse to one. That is why origin is the highest value question you are allowed to ask.","show":{"t":"ask","q":"Language of origin?","rules":["Greek → ph, y, ch, rh","French → silent finals, ou, eau","Italian → every vowel sounded","German → sch, compounds"]}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"Five fans carry most of the risk","say":"A handful of sounds carry nearly all the danger. The eff sound splits into f, p h and g h. A hard k before a front vowel splits into k, c, c h and q u. The sh sound splits five ways. A long ee at the end of a word splits into i, y, i e, i s and e e. Learn those fans cold.","show":{"t":"tree","sound":"/f/","branches":[{"sp":"f","org":"English / Latin","ex":"fable"},{"sp":"ph","org":"Greek","ex":"phoneme"},{"sp":"gh","org":"Old English","ex":"laugh"}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Origin defaults","say":"Each origin has a default that is right far more often than not. Greek: p h for eff, y for a short i, c h for a hard k, r h at the start. French: a silent final consonant, o u for oo, e a u for oh. Italian: every vowel sounded, doubled consonants, a final vowel.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"phoneme","hi":[0,2]},{"word":"rhetoric","hi":[0,2]},{"word":"tableau","hi":[5,3]}]}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"When two branches survive","say":"Sometimes two branches survive the origin question. That is when alternate pronunciations earn their keep. A second pronunciation often stresses a different syllable and exposes a vowel the first one hid. Ask for it. It is the least used of the permitted questions, and one of the most informative.","show":{"t":"ask","q":"Are there alternate pronunciations?","rules":["a second stress pattern","exposes a hidden vowel"]}}]},"3":{"label":"at the microphone","scenes":[{"mood":"think","cap":"Five questions, five different jobs","say":"You are allowed five questions. Language of origin. Definition. Part of speech. Alternate pronunciations. Use in a sentence. Each one eliminates a different class of spelling, so the order you ask in should depend on the ambiguity in front of you, not on habit.","show":{"t":"ask","q":"The five permitted questions","rules":["origin — picks the orthography","definition — separates homophones","part of speech — settles the ending","alternate pronunciation — moves the stress","sentence — confirms the sense"]}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"Origin first, almost always","say":"Origin is usually first, because it is the only question that selects a whole spelling system rather than a single letter. Every other question then operates inside the system that origin picked. There is one exception, and it matters.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"origin","sub":"picks the whole system"}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"Unless it might be a homophone","say":"If the word could be a homophone, the definition has to come first. Otherwise you will spell the wrong word perfectly, and a perfect spelling of the wrong word is still an elimination. Hear anything that could be two words, and ask what it means before you ask anything else.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"principal","right":"principle","root":"both from Latin princeps","note":"the definition is the only thing that separates them"}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Part of speech is underrated","say":"English distinguishes noun and adjective endings that sound identical. Ance against ence. Ant against ent. Ise against ice. Knowing whether you are spelling a thing or a description of a thing often settles the final letters, and it is a question almost nobody asks.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"dependant","right":"dependent","root":"noun / adjective","note":"part of speech decides the ending"}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"Ask with a purpose","say":"Asking questions is not stalling. It is the mechanism the rules give you for gathering evidence. But ask with a purpose. Decide what you do not know, ask the question that resolves it, then spell. Asking all five in order signals that you have no hypothesis, and it burns the clock you may need later.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"hypothesis first","sub":"then the question that tests it"}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Say it, spell it, say it","say":"Then spell. Say the word, spell at a steady rhythm, say it again. The rhythm matters more at this level than at a school bee, because it stops you compressing a doubled letter, and it gives you a beat in which to catch an error while a correction is still allowed. A rushed string of letters cannot be audited.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"say · spell · say","sub":"the rhythm is the audit"}}]},"4":{"label":"Greek openings","scenes":[{"mood":"think","cap":"Greek allowed clusters English does not say","say":"Ancient Greek let words begin with consonant clusters that English cannot pronounce. When English borrowed those words it kept the spelling and quietly dropped the first sound. So an unexplained silent consonant at the start of a word is almost always a Greek fingerprint.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"ps pn mn rh","sub":"written, not spoken"}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"The set is closed — memorise it","say":"The good news is that the set is closed. P s, as in psalm. P n, as in pneumonia. M n, as in mnemonic. C h t h, as in chthonic. P h t h, as in phthisis. R h, as in rhetoric. B d, as in bdelloid. Add g n and p t and you have almost the whole inventory.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"pseudonym","hi":[0,2]},{"word":"mnemonic","hi":[0,2]},{"word":"phthisis","hi":[0,3]}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Which silent letter, though?","say":"Hearing an n at the start leaves you choosing between k n, g n, p n and a plain n. Origin decides it. K n and g n are Old English, as in knife and gnaw. P n is Greek, as in pneumonia. The silent letter is a label telling you which language you are standing in.","show":{"t":"tree","sound":"/n/ at the start","branches":[{"sp":"n","org":"most words","ex":"nature"},{"sp":"kn","org":"Old English","ex":"knife"},{"sp":"gn","org":"Old English","ex":"gnaw"},{"sp":"pn","org":"Greek","ex":"pneumatic"}]}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"Worked: pneumonia","say":"So, worked through. You hear noo moh nyuh. It starts with an n sound. You ask the origin and you are told Greek. Greek default for an unexplained initial n is p n, from pneuma, meaning breath. P, n, e, u, m, o, n, i, a.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"pneumonia","hi":[0,2],"parts":[{"txt":"pneum-","gloss":"breath, lung"},{"txt":"-ia","gloss":"condition"}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"rh, and the doubled rrh","say":"One more. Greek rho takes an h after it at the start of a word. And when a prefix pushes that rho inside a compound, it doubles. Rhythm, but arrhythmia. Rhoea, but diarrhoea. Rhage, but haemorrhage. If you hear a Greek word with an r after a prefix, suspect r r h.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"arrhythmia","hi":[1,3]},{"word":"haemorrhage","hi":[5,3]}]}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"This is worth an hour of drill","say":"This is not a curiosity. The core competition library carries a hundred and seventy words with one of these openings, and the full library carries four hundred and twenty seven. Rhinorrhagia, pneumatocyst, bdelloid and phthisis are already in the pool you are studying.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"170 / 427","sub":"words in your own library"}}]},"5":{"label":"classical plurals","scenes":[{"mood":"think","cap":"Borrowed whole, plural and all","say":"Some words came into English whole, and brought their original plural with them. That plural is not decoration. The ending tells you which Latin or Greek declension the singular belonged to, and that tells you how the singular is spelled.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"-ae  -i  -a  -ices","sub":"the plural is evidence"}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"The five main pairs","say":"Latin a becomes a e. Vertebra, vertebrae. Latin us becomes i. Fungus, fungi. Latin um becomes a. Datum, data. Latin ex or ix becomes i c e s. Vortex, vortices. Greek ma becomes m a t a. Stigma, stigmata. Learn the pair, never the single form.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"vertebrae","hi":[7,2]},{"word":"vortices","hi":[4,4]},{"word":"stigmata","hi":[5,3]}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"The plural is the informative form","say":"At the microphone you are often given the plural, and that is the more useful form. A e tells you the singular ended in a. I c e s tells you it ended in e x or i x, and warns you that a c appears where you might have written an x. M a t a tells you Greek.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"vortices","hi":[4,4],"parts":[{"txt":"vortex","gloss":"the singular"},{"txt":"-ices","gloss":"Latin -ex / -ix plural"}]}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"English did not always agree","say":"Be careful, because English overrode some of these. A few words now take both plurals, and a few classical plurals became English singulars in the move. Agenda and data both began life as Latin plurals. When both forms are valid, the bee accepts the one it asked you for, so listen to the ending you were given.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"datum","right":"data","root":"Latin singular / plural","note":"data is now often treated as singular"}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"Beyond Latin and Greek","say":"Other languages contribute their own. French gives e a u x for singulars in e a u. Bureau, bureaux. Italian gives i for o. Virtuoso, virtuosi. Hebrew gives i m. Cherub, cherubim. Seraph, seraphim. Each is a small closed set, and each is worth learning as a set.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"bureaux","hi":[4,3]},{"word":"virtuosi","hi":[7,1]},{"word":"cherubim","hi":[6,2]}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Two hundred and fifty four in your pool","say":"The core library carries two hundred and fifty four words with a classical plural ending, and the full library carries four hundred and sixty eight. Maxillae, auspices, glossopetrae and abietineae are all sitting in the pool already. Drill them as pairs.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"254 / 468","sub":"words in your own library"}}]},"6":{"label":"ize or ise","scenes":[{"mood":"think","cap":"One sound, two national habits","say":"This is the biggest spelling split in English. Hundreds of verbs end in the same eyes sound, and American spelling writes it with a z while British publishing writes it with an s. Neither is a mistake. But on a bee stage only one of them is the dictionary's first choice, and you need to know which.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"ize · ise","sub":"same sound, different coasts"}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"The Greek suffix behind it","say":"Nearly all of these words are built the same way. Take a noun or adjective, add the Greek suffix izein, meaning to make or to treat as, and you get a verb. Canon becomes canonize. Item becomes itemize. Immune becomes immunize. When that suffix is present, American spelling always writes z.","show":{"t":"build","root":"canon","affix":"-ize","side":"suf"}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"The dozen that are never z","say":"Now the trap. In a small group of words the s is not a suffix at all — it came through French and it belongs to the root. Advertise, surprise, compromise, exercise, supervise, improvise, despise, disguise, revise. There are only about a dozen, and for these an s is correct in every country. Memorise the dozen and the rest of the pattern is safe.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"advertise","hi":[7,3]},{"word":"surprise","hi":[5,3]},{"word":"compromise","hi":[7,3]},{"word":"exercise","hi":[5,3]}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"The test that separates them","say":"Here is the test. Strip the ending and ask whether a real root is left behind that means something on its own. Canon, item, immune — real roots, so the ending is a suffix and takes z. Advert, surpr, comprom — not roots, so nothing was suffixed and the s stays.","show":{"t":"ask","q":"Is a real root left when you strip the ending?","rules":["Yes → the ending is a suffix → -ize","No → the s belongs to the root → -ise","Root is Greek lusis → -yse / -yze pair","Variant offered → either is accepted"]}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"-yse and -yze","say":"Analyse, paralyse, catalyse and hydrolyse are the same story shifted one letter. Their root is the Greek lusis, a loosening, so there is no root s to protect. British spelling writes y s e, American writes y z e, and the four of them behave identically. Learn one, own all four.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"analyse","right":"analyze","root":"Greek lusis = a loosening","note":"British and American, both correct in their own country"}},{"mood":"love","cap":"The noun always follows the verb","say":"One last piece. The noun copies whatever the verb did. Civilize gives civilization with a z, civilise gives civilisation with an s. You never mix them. So you only have to make the decision once per word, and then the whole family follows. Seven hundred and fifty five of these sit in your core word list.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"civilize","hi":[5,1]},{"word":"civilization","hi":[5,1]},{"word":"civilise","hi":[5,1]},{"word":"civilisation","hi":[5,1]}]}}]},"7":{"label":"shus endings","scenes":[{"mood":"oops","cap":"Five spellings, one sound","say":"Listen to the end of these words. Malicious. Ambitious. Sebaceous. Conscious. Obnoxious. Every one of them ends in the same shus sound, and every one of them spells it differently. Five spellings for one ending is the worst kind of trap, because your ear gives you nothing.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"ceous cious tious scious xious","sub":"all pronounced shus"}},{"mood":"think","cap":"The family noun holds the answer","say":"Here is the move that turns a guess into a decision. Do not think about the adjective. Think about the noun in its family. Malice gives malicious. Space gives spacious. Avarice gives avaricious. When the noun ends in c e, the adjective ends c i o u s.","show":{"t":"shift","rows":[{"label":"malice","syl":["mal","ice"],"on":1},{"label":"malicious","syl":["ma","li","cious"],"on":2}]}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"And the other way round","say":"Now the mirror image. Ambition gives ambitious. Caution gives cautious. Nutrition gives nutritious. Superstition gives superstitious. When the family noun ends in t i o n, the adjective ends t i o u s. Two tests, and you have just covered most of the pattern.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"ambition","hi":[7,1]},{"word":"ambitious","hi":[6,3]},{"word":"nutrition","hi":[8,1]},{"word":"nutritious","hi":[7,3]}]}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"-ceous means made of","say":"The c e o u s spelling comes from Latin aceus, meaning made of or having the nature of. English kept it for substances and for whole plant and animal families. Sebaceous, of tallow. Cetaceous, of whales. Saponaceous, of soap. Gallinaceous, of the chicken family. If the word tells you what something is made of, start with c e o u s.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"saponaceous","parts":[{"txt":"sapon","gloss":"soap"},{"txt":"-aceous","gloss":"having the nature of"}]}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"Two closed sets you can memorise","say":"Two of the five are tiny. S c i o u s appears only in words built on Latin scire, to know — conscious, unconscious, subconscious. X i o u s is smaller still — anxious, noxious, obnoxious. Both lists are closed. Once you know them, they can never surprise you again.","show":{"t":"tree","sound":"/shəs/","branches":[{"sp":"cious","org":"noun in -ce","ex":"malice → malicious"},{"sp":"tious","org":"noun in -tion","ex":"ambition → ambitious"},{"sp":"ceous","org":"made of","ex":"saponaceous"},{"sp":"scious","org":"scire = know","ex":"conscious"},{"sp":"xious","org":"root has x","ex":"obnoxious"}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"When nothing rhymes with it","say":"Some of these words have no helpful relative at all. Meretricious. Adscititious. Tralatitious. Obreptitious. For those, fall back on origin. A Latin verb whose participle ends t u s pushes you toward t i o u s, and a Latin noun in a x pushes you toward a c i o u s. A hundred and forty of these live in your core list.","show":{"t":"ask","q":"No relative to test?","rules":["Latin participle in -tus → -tious","Latin noun in -ax, -acis → -acious","Names a substance or family → -ceous","Root means to know → -scious"]}}]},"8":{"label":"oid endings","scenes":[{"mood":"happy","cap":"Greek for having the shape of","say":"Greek eidos meant form or shape. Put it on the end of a noun and you get oeides, having the form of. English took that and turned it into one of the busiest endings in science. An asteroid is star shaped. A humanoid is human shaped. A spheroid is nearly a sphere but not quite.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"asteroid","parts":[{"txt":"aster","gloss":"star"},{"txt":"-oid","gloss":"having the form of"}]}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"The oy trap","say":"Here is where spellers lose it. The ending sounds like oyd, and English really does spell that sound o y in ordinary words — boy, joy, employ, destroy. But this suffix is Greek, and Greek never wrote it that way. In every single one of these words it is o i d. Not one exception.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"employ","right":"solenoid","root":"same sound, different origin","note":"English oy versus Greek -oid — the meaning tells you which"}},{"mood":"think","cap":"The adjective adds only -al","say":"Now the mistake that costs more words than the oy trap. To make the adjective you add a l and nothing else. Toroidal. Cuboidal. Adenoidal. Sinusoidal. Spheroidal. There is no i before the a l. Sinusoidial is not a word, and neither is spheroidial.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"toroidal","hi":[4,4]},{"word":"cuboidal","hi":[4,4]},{"word":"sinusoidal","hi":[6,4]},{"word":"colloidal","hi":[5,4]}]}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"The root does all the work","say":"Because the suffix never changes, the root is the whole puzzle, and Greek shape nouns are wonderfully concrete. Solen, a pipe, gives solenoid. Rhabdos, a rod, gives rhabdoid. Botrys, a bunch of grapes, gives botryoid. Kotyle, a cup, gives cotyloid. Xiphos, a sword, gives xiphoid. Learn the shape word and the spelling is a gift.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"rhabdoid","hi":[0,6]},{"word":"botryoid","hi":[0,5]},{"word":"cotyloid","hi":[0,5]},{"word":"solenoid","hi":[0,5]}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Two imposters","say":"Be careful with words that end in these letters for other reasons. Avoid and devoid come from French vuide, meaning empty, and have nothing to do with shape. Sangfroid is French sang froid, cold blood, and the d at the end is silent. None of these take an oidal adjective, because none of them is this suffix.","show":{"t":"tree","sound":"/oid/ at the end","branches":[{"sp":"-oid","org":"Greek eidos","ex":"asteroid = star shaped"},{"sp":"-oid","org":"French vuide","ex":"avoid, devoid"},{"sp":"-oid","org":"French froid","ex":"sangfroid, silent d"},{"sp":"-oyd","org":"never Greek","ex":"not a spelling of this suffix"}]}},{"mood":"love","cap":"A hundred and twelve waiting for you","say":"This pattern is unusually kind. One fixed suffix, one fixed adjective form, and a root that tells you what the word means. Our core competition list holds a hundred and twelve of them and the full library three hundred and eighty nine. That is a large block of words you can now spell on sight.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"-oid → -oidal","sub":"112 in core, 389 in full"}}]},"9":{"label":"becoming words","scenes":[{"mood":"think","cap":"Latin had a tense for starting","say":"Latin had a whole class of verbs for beginning to be something. It marked them with escere, and grammarians call them inchoative — the verbs of setting out. Rubescere was not to be red, it was to start reddening. Senescere was not to be old, it was to start ageing. English kept the machinery.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"-escere","sub":"beginning to become"}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"The trio","say":"The suffix comes in three shapes and they always travel together. The verb ends e s c e. The adjective ends e s c e n t. The noun ends e s c e n c e. Coalesce, coalescent, coalescence. Effervesce, effervescent, effervescence. One word learned is three words spelled.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"coalesce","hi":[5,3]},{"word":"coalescent","hi":[5,5]},{"word":"coalescence","hi":[5,6]}]}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"The c is the whole point","say":"Spellers drop that c constantly, because you barely hear it. But it is the visible piece of the Latin suffix and it carries the meaning. Senescent means growing old. Senile means already old. Liquescent means becoming liquid. Liquid just is. Drop the c and you have deleted the idea of becoming.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"senescent","right":"senile","root":"both from Latin senex = old","note":"the -esc- says the process is under way, not finished"}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"The colour cluster","say":"Watch how many of these describe something changing shade. Albescent, turning white. Canescent, turning grey. Nigrescent, turning black. Flavescent, turning yellow. Virescent, turning green. Erubescent, turning red. Iridescent, taking on the colours of the rainbow. If the definition says turning some colour, you are almost certainly in this pattern.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"albescent","hi":[3,5]},{"word":"nigrescent","hi":[4,5]},{"word":"flavescent","hi":[4,5]},{"word":"iridescent","hi":[4,5]}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Not the word essence","say":"Here is the noun trap. The ending sounds exactly like the ordinary word essence, and spellers write obsolessence and effervessence. But essence is a different word from a different root entirely. This ending is e s c e n c e, with the c before the e, because it inherited the Latin marker. Never two s letters.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"effervescence","parts":[{"txt":"ef-","gloss":"out"},{"txt":"ferv","gloss":"boil"},{"txt":"-escence","gloss":"the process of beginning"}]}},{"mood":"love","cap":"Sixty-seven in your list","say":"Once you hear becoming in a definition, this pattern is nearly automatic. Sixty-seven of these words are in the core competition list and a hundred and fifteen in the full library, and because each root gives you a verb, an adjective and a noun, the real reach is larger than the count suggests.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"-esce · -escent · -escence","sub":"one root, three words"}}]},"10":{"label":"efy or ify","scenes":[{"mood":"happy","cap":"One Latin verb, two English endings","say":"Latin facere meant to make, and English turned it into a verb ending. Classify is to make into classes. Magnify is to make large. Terrify is to make terrified. The trouble is that this ending comes in two spellings that sound identical, i f y and e f y, and only one of them is right for a given word.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"-ify · -efy","sub":"both from facere = to make"}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"The exception list is four words long","say":"Here is the good news, and it is very good news. Only four English verbs take e f y. Liquefy. Rarefy. Putrefy. Stupefy. That is the complete list. Everything else in the language — hundreds of words — takes i f y. This is one of the few spelling patterns you can finish learning today.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"liquefy","hi":[4,3]},{"word":"rarefy","hi":[3,3]},{"word":"putrefy","hi":[4,3]},{"word":"stupefy","hi":[4,3]}]}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"Why people get them wrong","say":"Every one of the four has a tempting relative that pulls you toward an i. Liquid pulls liquefy toward liquify. Rare pulls rarefy toward rarify. Stupid pulls stupefy toward stupify. The pull is real and it is wrong every time. When you hear one of the four, ignore the relative and write the e.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"liquid","right":"liquefy","root":"the relative tempts an i","note":"the verb keeps the Latin e regardless"}},{"mood":"think","cap":"The noun gives the game away","say":"Now the test that makes it automatic. The four e f y verbs form nouns ending e f a c t i o n. Liquefaction. Rarefaction. Putrefaction. Stupefaction. Every i f y verb forms a noun in i f i c a t i o n. So if you can hear the noun in your head, the verb spelling is decided for you.","show":{"t":"ask","q":"Which noun does the word make?","rules":["Noun in -efaction → verb in -efy","Noun in -ification → verb in -ify","Only four verbs are in the -efy group","The f-a-c is Latin facere, to make"]}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"The fac root is everywhere","say":"That f a c in the middle of liquefaction is Latin facere showing itself. You have met it before. Factory, a place of making. Manufacture, making by hand. Benefaction, a good deed. Malefaction, a bad one. Once you can see the root, the a in the noun stops looking arbitrary.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"liquefaction","parts":[{"txt":"lique","gloss":"liquid"},{"txt":"fac","gloss":"make"},{"txt":"-tion","gloss":"the act of"}]}},{"mood":"love","cap":"Four words, a whole pattern","say":"Three hundred and sixty four words in the full library end in this family, and a hundred and forty two in the core competition list. But you only ever have to remember four of them, plus one rule about nouns. That is the best return on memory anywhere in this course.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"liquefy rarefy putrefy stupefy","sub":"the entire exception list"}}]},"11":{"label":"bearing and eating","scenes":[{"mood":"happy","cap":"Five Latin verbs turned into endings","say":"Latin took five very ordinary verbs and turned them into word endings that English still uses to build new scientific vocabulary. Ferre, to bear. Vorare, to devour. Parere, to bring forth. Fugere, to flee. Fluere, to flow. Each one became a suffix, and together they cover a large block of hard words.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"ferre vorare parere fugere fluere","sub":"bear · devour · bring forth · flee · flow"}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"What each one builds","say":"A coniferous tree bears cones. A carnivorous animal devours flesh. An oviparous animal brings forth eggs. A centrifugal force flees the centre. A mellifluous voice flows like honey. Once you can hear which of the five verbs is inside the word, the ending spells itself.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"coniferous","hi":[4,6]},{"word":"carnivorous","hi":[5,6]},{"word":"oviparous","hi":[2,7]},{"word":"mellifluous","hi":[5,6]}]}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"The connecting vowel is where marks go","say":"Here is the real difficulty, and it is not the ending. Latin joined the root to the ending with a small connecting vowel, and spellers guess it. Is it cupraferous or cupriferous? Plumbeferous or plumbiferous? In this family the answer is almost always i. Assume i, then look for a reason to change your mind.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"cupriferous","parts":[{"txt":"cupr","gloss":"copper"},{"txt":"i","gloss":"connecting vowel"},{"txt":"-ferous","gloss":"bearing"}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"The metals are a free set","say":"Mineralogy is full of these, and the roots are the Latin metal names. Aurum, gold, gives auriferous. Argentum, silver, gives argentiferous. Cuprum, copper, gives cupriferous. Plumbum, lead, gives plumbiferous. Ferrum, iron, gives ferriferous. Stannum, tin, gives stanniferous. Six metals, six long words, no new spelling rules.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"auriferous","hi":[0,3]},{"word":"cupriferous","hi":[0,4]},{"word":"plumbiferous","hi":[0,5]},{"word":"stanniferous","hi":[0,6]}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"When the vowel is not i","say":"A few members break the pattern because the root already carries its own vowel. Omnivorous keeps the i that belongs to omni, all. Septemfluous keeps the e of septem, seven. Odiferous exists beside odoriferous because the root odor can be trimmed. When the root ends in a vowel already, it keeps it rather than adding another.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"odiferous","right":"odoriferous","root":"both from Latin odor","note":"the root can keep or drop its own vowel — both spellings are recorded"}},{"mood":"love","cap":"Fifty-five in core, and they are long","say":"Fifty-five of these are in the core competition list and a hundred and eleven in the full library. They are long words, which frightens people, but they are built from parts you now recognise, and a long word made of known parts is easier than a short word made of nothing.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"root + i + ending","sub":"55 in core, 111 in full"}}]},"12":{"label":"ance or ence","scenes":[{"mood":"think","cap":"A rule hiding in plain sight","say":"Most spellers treat a n c e against e n c e as pure memory. For a lot of words it is. But when the letter just before the ending is a c or a g, it is not memory at all. It is forced, and the thing that forces it is something you already know about English.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"-ance · -ence","sub":"after c or g this is not a guess"}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"Soft and hard, and what controls them","say":"In English a c before a, o or u is hard. Cat. Cot. Cut. Before e, i or y it is soft. Cent. City. Cycle. Exactly the same is true of g. Gate and got are hard, gem and gin are soft. The vowel after the letter is an instruction about how to say the letter before it.","show":{"t":"tree","sound":"the letter c","branches":[{"sp":"ca, co, cu","org":"hard","ex":"cat, cot, cut"},{"sp":"ce, ci, cy","org":"soft","ex":"cent, city, cycle"},{"sp":"ga, go, gu","org":"hard","ex":"gate, got, gum"},{"sp":"ge, gi, gy","org":"soft","ex":"gem, gin, gym"}]}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"Now point it at the ending","say":"So say the word and listen to the consonant right before the ending. If it is soft — a j sound or an s sound — the ending has to start with e, or the softness would vanish. Negligence. Indulgence. Divergence. Reticence. Innocence. The e is not a choice, it is load bearing.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"negligence","hi":[5,1]},{"word":"indulgence","hi":[5,1]},{"word":"divergence","hi":[5,1]},{"word":"reticence","hi":[4,1]}]}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"Test it by breaking it","say":"Try the test in reverse and you will feel it. Say insurgance out loud. The a forces the g hard, so you get in sur GANCE, which is not the word. That mismatch is your proof. Any time an a would harden a letter that should be soft, the a is wrong.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"insurgence","right":"insurgance","root":"the a would harden the g","note":"say the wrong one aloud and the rule announces itself"}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"When a really is allowed","say":"The reverse case matters too. If the sound is hard, an a is fine. Arrogance, with a hard g. Elegance, hard g. Extravagance, hard g. Significance and vacancy, where the c is a k sound. And the shorter noun forms follow the same logic — emergency and exigency keep e, vacancy and mendicancy take a.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"arrogance","hi":[4,1]},{"word":"elegance","hi":[3,1]},{"word":"significance","hi":[8,1]},{"word":"vacancy","hi":[3,1]}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Know what it does not cover","say":"Be honest about the limits. This only works when the letter before the ending is c or g. Resistance against existence, assistance against persistence — those still need the Latin verb or plain memory. But the c and g cases are sixty five words in your core list, and they are the ones that look impossible until you see the phonics.","show":{"t":"ask","q":"Is the letter before the ending c or g?","rules":["Yes and it sounds soft → -ence","Yes and it sounds hard → -ance","No → the rule is silent, use the Latin verb","65 core words, 119 in the full library"]}}]},"13":{"label":"rule words","scenes":[{"mood":"happy","cap":"Two Greek roots for power","say":"Almost every English word for a form of government comes from one of two Greek roots. Arkhein meant to rule, or to be first. Kratos meant strength, or power. Between them they build monarchy, anarchy, democracy, theocracy and dozens of rarer words that turn up on bee stages.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"arkhein · kratos","sub":"to rule · power"}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Person or system","say":"First decision, and it is easy once you notice it. Does the word name a person or a system? A monarch is a person, a monarchy is the system. A patriarch is a person, a patriarchy is the system. A democrat is a person, a democracy is the system. Person or system chooses your ending before you write anything.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"monarch","right":"monarchy","root":"the ruler and the rule","note":"-arch and -crat name people; -archy and -cracy name systems"}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"The ch is never a k","say":"Now the spelling trap. That k sound in monarch is written c h, because the Greek letter behind it was chi. Monarch. Anarchy. Hierarchy. Architect. Archive. Archaic. Archaeology. Not one of them uses a plain k. When you hear a k sound in a word about ruling, being first, or being ancient, write c h.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"monarch","hi":[5,2]},{"word":"hierarchy","hi":[6,2]},{"word":"architect","hi":[3,2]},{"word":"archaeology","hi":[3,2]}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"The connecting o","say":"Greek joined a root to kratos with an o, and that little o is the letter spellers lose. Theocracy, from theos, a god. Ochlocracy, from ochlos, a crowd. Plutocracy, from ploutos, wealth. Gerontocracy, from geron, an old man. When you build one of these, assume the joint is an o.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"ochlocracy","parts":[{"txt":"ochlo","gloss":"crowd, mob"},{"txt":"cracy","gloss":"power"}]}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"Still making new words","say":"The best evidence that you can build these rather than memorise them is that English is still inventing them. Squattocracy, rule by landholders. Punditocracy, rule by commentators. Kindergarchy, a household run by its children. Kakistocracy, government by the very worst people. All in your word library, all built the same way.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"squattocracy","hi":[6,6]},{"word":"punditocracy","hi":[6,6]},{"word":"kindergarchy","hi":[6,6]},{"word":"kakistocracy","hi":[6,6]}]}},{"mood":"love","cap":"Sixty-one in core","say":"Sixty one of these words are in your core competition list and a hundred and fifty two in the full library. And because the pattern is productive, a definition that names a group and says they hold the power is nearly always spellable on the spot. Root, then o, then c r a c y.","show":{"t":"ask","q":"Building one from a definition?","rules":["Names a person → -arch or -crat","Names a system → -archy or -cracy","k sound in the root → write ch","Joint between root and ending → o"]}}]},"14":{"label":"itude nouns","scenes":[{"mood":"happy","cap":"Latin naming a quality","say":"Latin had a neat trick. Take an adjective and add itudo, and you get a noun naming the quality itself. Altus meant high, and altitudo meant height. Latus meant wide, and latitudo meant width. English borrowed the whole machine and still runs it.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"altitude","parts":[{"txt":"alt","gloss":"high"},{"txt":"-itude","gloss":"the quality of being"}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"The vowel is always i","say":"First rule, and it has no exceptions. The connecting vowel is i. Aptitude, gratitude, certitude, servitude, fortitude, magnitude, rectitude. There is no such thing as an a t u d e ending in English. If you hear this suffix, the letter before the t is an i.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"-itude","sub":"never -atude"}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"The roots are worth knowing","say":"The competition words here are all transparent once you have the Latin adjective. Pulcher, beautiful, gives pulchritude. Turpis, shameful, gives turpitude. Lassus, weary, gives lassitude. Vicis, a turn or change, gives vicissitude. Decrepitus, broken down, gives decrepitude. Learn the adjective and the long word assembles itself.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"pulchritude","hi":[0,6]},{"word":"turpitude","hi":[0,4]},{"word":"lassitude","hi":[0,4]},{"word":"vicissitude","hi":[0,6]}]}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"Where the adjectives come from","say":"Now the piece that catches almost everyone. Why is it longitudinal and not longitudal? Because in Latin the suffix declined, and its stem was i t u d i n. English builds all further words on that stem. Longitudinal. Latitudinal. Attitudinal. Multitudinous. Pulchritudinous.","show":{"t":"shift","rows":[{"label":"longitude","syl":["lon","gi","tude"],"on":2},{"label":"longitudinal","syl":["lon","gi","tu","di","nal"],"on":3}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"The longest one in the family","say":"Verisimilitude is the showpiece. Verus meant true and similis meant like, so verisimilitude is the quality of seeming true. Four parts, all of them Latin you have met: verus, similis, itudo. Fourteen letters that you can build rather than memorise.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"verisimilitude","parts":[{"txt":"veri","gloss":"true"},{"txt":"simil","gloss":"like"},{"txt":"-itude","gloss":"the quality of"}]}},{"mood":"love","cap":"A small family that punches up","say":"Only thirty five of these are in the core library and sixty one in the full one, so this is a small family. But they appear at the hard end of word lists far more often than their number suggests, because the roots are rare Latin adjectives. A short list, and worth owning completely.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"-itude → -itudinous","sub":"35 in core, 61 in full"}}]},"15":{"label":"divination","scenes":[{"mood":"excited","cap":"Prophecy by anything at all","say":"Greek manteia meant prophecy. English attached it to the name of whatever you were reading the future in, and the result is one of the most entertaining word families in the language. Divination by fire, by water, by dreams, by barley, by mirrors, by the behaviour of cats. All of them real words, all of them in your library.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"manteia","sub":"prophecy, divination"}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"Three forms, one spelling","say":"The family has three shapes and none of them varies. The method ends m a n c y. The practitioner ends m a n c e r. The adjective ends m a n t i c. Necromancy, necromancer, necromantic. The a is always there, because Greek manteia had an a. There is no such ending as m e n c y.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"necromancy","hi":[5,5]},{"word":"necromancer","hi":[5,6]},{"word":"necromantic","hi":[5,6]}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"The joint is o","say":"Greek joined the root to the suffix with an o, and that o is the only real decision in the word. Geomancy. Pyromancy. Selenomancy. Oneiromancy. Bibliomancy. Once you have written the root, put an o, then m a n c y, and you are done.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"selenomancy","parts":[{"txt":"selen","gloss":"moon"},{"txt":"o","gloss":"Greek joint"},{"txt":"mancy","gloss":"divination by"}]}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"Build one you have never seen","say":"This is the family where building beats memorising. Cheir means hand, so palm reading is chiromancy. Ichthys means fish, so ichthyomancy. Aleuron means flour, so aleuromancy. Daktylos means finger, so dactylomancy. Ailouros means cat, so ailuromancy. You can spell a word you have never met if you know the Greek noun.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"chiromancy","hi":[0,5]},{"word":"ichthyomancy","hi":[0,6]},{"word":"aleuromancy","hi":[0,5]},{"word":"dactylomancy","hi":[0,6]}]}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"Three imposters","say":"Watch for words that end in these letters by accident. Romantic came from Rome through the Romance languages. Semantic came from Greek sema, a sign. Dormancy came from Latin dormire, to sleep. None of them is about prophecy, and the test is simple — none of them has a m a n c e r form.","show":{"t":"tree","sound":"/mansi/ at the end","branches":[{"sp":"-mancy","org":"Greek manteia","ex":"pyromancy — has a -mancer"},{"sp":"-mantic","org":"Greek manteia","ex":"necromantic"},{"sp":"-mantic","org":"Latin Roma","ex":"romantic — no -mancer"},{"sp":"-mancy","org":"Latin dormire","ex":"dormancy — no -mancer"}]}},{"mood":"love","cap":"Thirty in core, and they are showpieces","say":"Thirty of these are in the core competition list and forty six in the full library. They are long, they look impossible on a card, and they are among the easiest hard words you will ever meet — because the ending is fixed, the joint is o, and the root is just the Greek word for a thing.","show":{"t":"ask","q":"Heard a divination word?","rules":["Ending is -mancy, never -mency","Practitioner is -mancer","Adjective is -mantic","Joint between root and ending is o"]}}]},"16":{"label":"gm and gn","scenes":[{"mood":"think","cap":"A letter you cannot hear","say":"Some words end in a g that makes no sound at all. Paradigm. Phlegm. Diaphragm. Benign. Campaign. Impugn. If you spell only what you hear, every one of these comes out wrong. But the g is not random, and there is a reliable way to prove it is there.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"-gm  -gn","sub":"the g is written, not spoken"}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"Add a suffix and the g speaks","say":"Here is the test, and it is the whole chapter. Say the word with a suffix on it. Paradigm becomes paradigmatic, and the g comes back. Phlegm becomes phlegmatic, and there it is again. Malign becomes malignant. Benign becomes benignity. Sign becomes signature. The relative pronounces what the base word hides.","show":{"t":"shift","rows":[{"label":"paradigm","syl":["pa","ra","digm"],"on":2},{"label":"paradigmatic","syl":["pa","ra","dig","ma","tic"],"on":2}]}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"The Greek -gm words","say":"The g m words are Greek, from roots where gamma and mu sat side by side. Paradeigma, an example, gives paradigm. Phlegma, inflammation, gives phlegm. Diaphragma, a partition, gives diaphragm. Apophthegma, a terse saying, gives apothegm. Borborygmos, a rumbling stomach, gives borborygm. Every one of them takes a t i c adjective that says the g.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"paradigm","hi":[6,2]},{"word":"phlegm","hi":[4,2]},{"word":"apothegm","hi":[6,2]},{"word":"borborygm","hi":[7,2]}]}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"The Latin -gn words","say":"The g n words are mostly Latin. Benignus and malignus give benign and malign. Dignare, to think worthy, gives deign. Fingere, to shape, gives feign. Pugnare, to fight, gives impugn and oppugn. Ad rationem, to call to account, gives arraign. Campus, a field, gives campaign.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"impugn","parts":[{"txt":"im-","gloss":"against"},{"txt":"pugn","gloss":"fight"}]}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"Two words where the test fails","say":"Now the honest exception. Sovereign and foreign have a g that never belonged to them. Sovereign came from Latin super, foreign from foras, meaning outdoors, and neither had a g. Medieval scribes added it by false comparison with reign. So no relative will ever pronounce it, and the test cannot help you. Those two you simply learn.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"sovereign","right":"foreign","root":"the g was inserted by scribes","note":"no derivative pronounces it — memorise these two"}},{"mood":"love","cap":"Small set, big payoff","say":"Twenty four of these are in the core competition list and sixty six in the full library. It is a small set, and the derivative test cracks nearly all of it. When you hear a word that seems to end in a bare m or n sound, ask yourself whether a longer relative would put a g in front of it.","show":{"t":"ask","q":"Does the word seem to end in -m or -n?","rules":["Find a relative with a suffix","Relative says the g → write the g","Greek root → -gm, adjective in -matic","Sovereign and foreign are memorised, not derived"]}}]},"17":{"label":"cide words","scenes":[{"mood":"think","cap":"A Latin verb about cutting down","say":"Latin caedere meant to cut down, and from cutting down it came to mean killing. English turned it into an ending that does two jobs at once — it names the act of killing, and it names the substance that does the killing. Regicide is a deed. Pesticide is a bottle on a shelf.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"caedere","sub":"to cut down, to kill"}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"The victim is in the root","say":"The root tells you who or what dies. Rex, a king, gives regicide. Mater, a mother, gives matricide. Frater, a brother, gives fratricide. Vermis, a worm, gives vermicide. Acarus, a mite, gives acaricide. Fungus gives fungicide. Read the root and you have read the definition.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"regicide","hi":[0,3]},{"word":"matricide","hi":[0,4]},{"word":"vermicide","hi":[0,4]},{"word":"acaricide","hi":[0,4]}]}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"The joint is i, not a","say":"Here is where marks go. Latin joined the root to the ending with an i, and spellers write an a. Herbicide, not herbacide. Fungicide, not fungacide. Larvicide, not larvacide. Every time you build one of these, the letter before the c is an i.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"larvicide","parts":[{"txt":"larv","gloss":"larva"},{"txt":"i","gloss":"Latin joint"},{"txt":"cide","gloss":"killer of"}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"The long one","say":"Molluscicide is the showpiece, because the root keeps its own c. Mollusc ends in c, then comes the connecting i, then c i d e. So you get two c letters with an i between them. M O L L U S C I C I D E. If you can see the parts, the pile of consonants stops being frightening.","show":{"t":"word","word":"molluscicide","hi":[6,3]}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"The adjective just adds al","say":"To make the adjective, drop the final e and add a l. Homicidal. Suicidal. Genocidal. Fungicidal. Bactericidal. Molluscicidal. Barmecidal. There is no i and no e before the a l. The c stays soft because the i in front of it is still doing its job.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"homicidal","hi":[6,3]},{"word":"fungicidal","hi":[7,3]},{"word":"genocidal","hi":[6,3]},{"word":"barmecidal","hi":[7,3]}]}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"A different verb, same letters","say":"Last thing, and it is the real trap. A completely different Latin verb, cadere, meaning to fall, gives coincide, to fall together, and decide, to cut off deliberation. Nothing is killed in either. And here is your test — neither one has a c i d a l form. No victim, no killing verb.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"insecticide","right":"coincide","root":"caedere to kill versus cadere to fall","note":"only the killing verb makes a -cidal adjective"}}]},"18":{"label":"eating words","scenes":[{"mood":"happy","cap":"One Greek verb, four endings","say":"Greek phagein meant to eat, and English built four different endings out of it. The adjective for a diet is p h a g o u s. The name of the habit is p h a g y. A medical swallowing disorder is p h a g i a. And a thing that eats is a p h a g e. Same root, four jobs.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"-phagous · -phagy · -phagia · -phage","sub":"diet · habit · disorder · eater"}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"Greek phi is always ph","say":"The first mistake is the easiest to fix. That f sound is a Greek phi, and English writes phi as p h. Never f. Phone, photo, physics, philosophy, sphere, graph — and phagein. So the eating words are p h a g o u s and p h a g y. There is no fagous.","show":{"t":"word","word":"saprophagous","hi":[5,2]}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"The root is the menu","say":"The root tells you what is being eaten, and the roots are ordinary Greek nouns. Sapros, rotten, gives saprophagous. Entomon, insect, gives entomophagy. Mykes, fungus, gives mycophagy. Xylon, wood, gives xylophagy. Ophis, snake, gives ophiophagy. Myrmex, ant, gives myrmecophagy. Read the root and you have the definition.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"entomophagy","hi":[0,6]},{"word":"mycophagy","hi":[0,4]},{"word":"ophiophagy","hi":[0,4]},{"word":"myrmecophagy","hi":[0,6]}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Choosing between the four","say":"So which ending? If the word describes a creature and its diet, use p h a g o u s. If it names the practice as a thing, use p h a g y. If it is clinical and about swallowing, use p h a g i a. If it names the eater itself, use p h a g e. Entomophagous insects, entomophagy the practice, dysphagia the disorder, macrophage the cell.","show":{"t":"ask","q":"Which of the four endings?","rules":["Describes a diet → -phagous","Names the habit → -phagy","Clinical, about swallowing → -phagia","Names the eater → -phage"]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"The clinical group","say":"Medicine uses the i a ending, and it is worth knowing separately. Dysphagia, difficulty swallowing. Aphagia, inability to swallow. Odynophagia, painful swallowing. Hyperphagia, eating far too much. Aerophagia, swallowing air. When the definition sounds like something a doctor would write down, the ending is i a.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"dysphagia","hi":[3,6]},{"word":"aphagia","hi":[1,6]},{"word":"hyperphagia","hi":[5,6]},{"word":"geophagia","hi":[3,6]}]}},{"mood":"love","cap":"Autophagy and its Nobel Prize","say":"One of these is worth knowing for its own sake. Autophagy, from autos, self, is how a cell breaks down and recycles its own parts, and the biologist who worked it out won a Nobel Prize for it. Seventeen of these eating words are in your core list and thirty six in the full library.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"autophagy","parts":[{"txt":"auto","gloss":"self"},{"txt":"phagy","gloss":"eating"}]}}]},"19":{"label":"worship words","scenes":[{"mood":"happy","cap":"Greek for service and worship","say":"Greek latreia meant service, and especially the service you give to a god. English attached it to the name of whatever was being worshipped, and got a compact, very regular family of words. Idolatry, worship of images. Zoolatry, worship of animals. Pyrolatry, worship of fire.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"latreia","sub":"service, worship"}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Three forms, one vowel change","say":"The practice ends l a t r y. The adjective ends l a t r o u s. And the person ends l a t e r, with an e. Idolatry, idolatrous, idolater. That vowel change catches people, because you might expect idolatrer. It is idolater.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"idolatry","hi":[4,4]},{"word":"idolatrous","hi":[4,6]},{"word":"idolater","hi":[4,4]}]}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"What people have worshipped","say":"The root names the object of worship, and the list is a small history of human devotion. Helios, sun, gives heliolatry. Lithos, stone, gives litholatry. Nekros, the dead, gives necrolatry. Kyon, dog, gives cynolatry. Ichthys, fish, gives ichthyolatry. And bardolatry, coined in English, means the worship of Shakespeare.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"heliolatry","hi":[0,4]},{"word":"litholatry","hi":[0,4]},{"word":"cynolatry","hi":[0,3]},{"word":"bardolatry","hi":[0,4]}]}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"Why idolatry has one l","say":"Idolatry looks like it should be idololatry — eidolon plus latreia — and that longer form really does exist in old writing. English squeezed it, dropping one o l. So the modern word has a single l. The commonest error is idollatry, doubling the l because you can see the word idol inside it.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"idolatry","parts":[{"txt":"idol","gloss":"image"},{"txt":"atry","gloss":"contracted from -olatry"}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"The joint is o, as usual","say":"Like most Greek compounds, the joint is an o. Zoolatry, from zoon. Pyrolatry, from pyr. Monolatry, from monos, one — the worship of a single god without denying others exist. Autolatry, from autos, self. Write the root, then o, then l a t r y.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"monolatry","parts":[{"txt":"mon","gloss":"one"},{"txt":"o","gloss":"Greek joint"},{"txt":"latry","gloss":"worship of"}]}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"Words that only look like worship","say":"Finally, the imposters. Electroplater, desolater, inflater and dilater all end in l a t e r, but they are just agent nouns from Latin verbs — one who plates, one who desolates. Nothing is worshipped and none of them has a l a t r o u s adjective. That missing adjective is your test.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"idolater","right":"electroplater","root":"worship versus a plain agent noun","note":"only the worship word has a -latrous adjective"}}]},"20":{"label":"trade endings","scenes":[{"mood":"happy","cap":"A native family for once","say":"Almost everything in this course is Latin or Greek. This chapter is not. Old English mangere meant a trader, wryhta meant a maker, and smitha meant someone who works metal. All three still build new words, and they behave completely differently from classical endings.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"mangere · wryhta · smitha","sub":"trader · maker · metalworker"}},{"mood":"think","cap":"No joint at all","say":"This is the tell, and it is the most useful thing in the chapter. A Latin or Greek compound needs a connecting vowel — cupriferous, ochlocracy, myrmecophagy. Old English just shoves the two words together. Wheelwright. Blacksmith. Fishmonger. If you feel the urge to insert an o or an i, stop — you are treating a Germanic word as classical.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"ochlocracy","right":"wheelwright","root":"classical joint versus Germanic none","note":"Greek needs the o; Old English needs nothing"}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"A wright makes, it does not write","say":"The commonest error in this family. A wright is a maker, from wryhta, and it is spelled w r i g h t. A playwright makes plays. It has nothing to do with writing them, so playwrite is always wrong. Cartwright. Wheelwright. Shipwright. Wainwright. Millwright. That g h is a fossil of a sound English stopped making centuries ago.","show":{"t":"word","word":"playwright","hi":[4,6]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Monger turned sour","say":"Mangere just meant trader, and you can still hear the neutral sense in fishmonger and ironmonger. But English slowly made the ending disapproving, so nearly every modern coinage is an insult. Scandalmonger. Warmonger. Rumourmonger. Phrasemonger. Meritmonger. Powermonger. The spelling never changes — m o n g e r, with the e.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"scandalmonger","hi":[7,6]},{"word":"phrasemonger","hi":[6,6]},{"word":"meritmonger","hi":[5,6]},{"word":"fishmonger","hi":[4,6]}]}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"Smith left the forge","say":"A smith worked metal. Blacksmith with iron, goldsmith, silversmith, coppersmith, whitesmith with tin. Then the ending broke away from metal altogether and came to mean any skilled maker. Wordsmith. Tunesmith. Jokesmith. Gunsmith. Locksmith. The trade disappeared and the suffix kept working.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"coppersmith","hi":[7,5]},{"word":"wordsmith","hi":[4,5]},{"word":"tunesmith","hi":[4,5]},{"word":"jokesmith","hi":[4,5]}]}},{"mood":"love","cap":"Why this chapter matters","say":"Only eleven of these are in the core competition list and thirty two in the full library, so the count is small. But the lesson is large — when a word is Germanic, every habit you have built up for Latin and Greek is wrong. No joint, no hyphen, and endings that are spelled nothing like they sound.","show":{"t":"ask","q":"Is the compound Germanic?","rules":["Both parts are plain English words → Germanic","Germanic → no connecting vowel, no hyphen","A maker is -wright, never -write","A trader is -monger, always with the e"]}}]},"21":{"label":"leading words","scenes":[{"mood":"happy","cap":"Greek for leading","say":"Greek agein meant to lead, and agogos meant leading. English kept it in a handful of words about people who lead others, and the practices they lead. A pedagogue leads children. A demagogue leads the mob. A synagogue is a leading together, which is why it came to mean a place where people assemble.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"agogos","sub":"leading"}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Three forms you need all of","say":"The person ends g o g u e. The practice ends g o g y. The adjective ends g o g i c. Pedagogue, pedagogy, pedagogic. Demagogue, demagogy, demagogic. A bee will ask for whichever of the three you have thought about least, so learn all of them together.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"pedagogue","hi":[5,4]},{"word":"pedagogy","hi":[5,3]},{"word":"pedagogic","hi":[5,4]}]}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"The silent ue came from French","say":"Greek had no u e on the end of these words. It arrived when they passed through French, which wrote g u e to keep the g hard before a final e. Without the u, the g would go soft — you would say pedagoje. The same French habit gives you catalogue, dialogue, monologue, epilogue, fatigue and vogue.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"catalogue","hi":[6,3]},{"word":"dialogue","hi":[5,3]},{"word":"fatigue","hi":[4,3]},{"word":"pedagogue","hi":[6,3]}]}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"Read the root, get the meaning","say":"Every one of these tells you what is being led. Pais, a child, gives pedagogue. Demos, the people, gives demagogue. Ana, upward, gives anagogic, leading the mind up to a spiritual meaning. Hypnos, sleep, gives hypnagogic, the state that leads you into sleep. Sialon, saliva, gives sialagogue, something that brings on saliva.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"hypnagogic","parts":[{"txt":"hypn","gloss":"sleep"},{"txt":"agogic","gloss":"leading into"}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Synagogue has no e after the n","say":"One specific warning. Synagogue starts with the prefix syn, meaning together, so there is no e after the n. It is s y n a g o g u e. People write senagogue or synogogue because they are spelling by ear. Recognise the prefix and the first half is settled.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"synagogue","parts":[{"txt":"syn","gloss":"together"},{"txt":"agogue","gloss":"a leading"}]}},{"mood":"love","cap":"The smallest pattern in the course","say":"Only eight of these are in the core competition list and twelve in the full library. That makes this the smallest family you will study — and that is exactly why it earns a chapter. A set this small can be learned completely, and once it is, it can never cost you a word again.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"-gogue · -gogy · -gogic","sub":"8 in core, 12 in full — learnable in full"}}]},"22":{"label":"cede ceed sede","scenes":[{"mood":"think","cap":"The most quoted spelling problem","say":"Every list of English spelling traps includes this one, and for once the trap is completely solvable. Precede or preceed? Supersede or supercede? There are only five words you need to store, and after that the whole family is decided.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"-cede · -ceed · -sede","sub":"one sound, three spellings"}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"The default is cede","say":"Almost all of these come from Latin cedere, meaning to go or to yield, and almost all of them are spelled c e d e. Precede. Recede. Secede. Concede. Accede. Intercede. Antecede. Retrocede. If you have no particular reason to think otherwise, c e d e is your answer.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"precede","hi":[3,4]},{"word":"concede","hi":[3,4]},{"word":"intercede","hi":[5,4]},{"word":"antecede","hi":[4,4]}]}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"Exactly three take ceed","say":"Now the first exception, and it is only three words long. Succeed. Proceed. Exceed. That is the entire list. There is no clever rule underneath it — they arrived in English through French forms with a doubled e and the spelling stuck. Say them together as a trio until they are automatic. Succeed, proceed, exceed.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"succeed","hi":[4,3]},{"word":"proceed","hi":[4,3]},{"word":"exceed","hi":[3,3]}]}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"One word takes sede, and there is a reason","say":"Supersede is the famous one, and its s is not an accident. It does not come from cedere at all. It comes from sedere, to sit — super plus sedere, to sit above, and so to take the place of. Different Latin verb, different letter. That is why supercede feels right and is wrong.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"supersede","right":"precede","root":"sedere to sit versus cedere to yield","note":"only supersede is built on sitting — hence the s"}},{"mood":"think","cap":"The nouns give you a back door","say":"The nouns can rescue you. Precede gives precession and precedence. Succeed gives succession. Exceed gives excess. Recede gives recession. Supersede gives supersession, keeping its s. If the verb spelling deserts you at the microphone, ask what the noun looks like and work backwards.","show":{"t":"shift","rows":[{"label":"supersede","syl":["su","per","sede"],"on":2},{"label":"supersession","syl":["su","per","ses","sion"],"on":2}]}},{"mood":"love","cap":"Four words, done forever","say":"So the whole family reduces to four things to remember. Succeed, proceed and exceed take a doubled e. Supersede takes an s because it is built on sitting, not yielding. Everything else is c e d e. One more warning — procedure drops the second e, so it is not proceedure.","show":{"t":"ask","q":"Which spelling?","rules":["succeed, proceed, exceed → -ceed","supersede → -sede, from sedere to sit","everything else → -cede","procedure has one e, not two"]}}]},"23":{"label":"Dutch markers","scenes":[{"mood":"think","cap":"The biggest gap in the course","say":"Your general course covers eight loanword languages. It does not cover Dutch, and Dutch is the single largest untaught origin in your library — fifteen hundred and fifty words in the full library, more than any language outside Latin, Greek, French and German. That is a serious hole, and this chapter closes it.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"1,550","sub":"Dutch words in the full library"}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"Double o is the signature","say":"Start with the easiest marker. Dutch writes a long oo sound as a doubled o, and English kept the spelling. Caboose, from kabuis, a ship's galley. Schooner. Sloop. Cruiser. Snoop. Spook. Stoop, from stoep, a doorstep. If a plain, concrete word has a doubled o in it, Dutch is a good first guess.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"caboose","hi":[3,2]},{"word":"schooner","hi":[3,2]},{"word":"sloop","hi":[2,2]},{"word":"cruiser","hi":[2,2]}]}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"They gave us the sea","say":"In the sixteen hundreds the Dutch ran the world's shipping, and English took their vocabulary whole. Yacht. Sloop. Schooner. Cruiser. Keelhaul. Maelstrom. Hoist. Smuggle. Deck. Skipper. Dock. Buoy. Walrus. If a word is about ships or the sea and is not Latin, try Dutch.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"yacht ← jacht","sub":"a ch English does not pronounce"}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"Yacht is the showpiece","say":"Yacht deserves its own moment. Dutch jacht meant a hunting ship. English swapped the j for a y and then kept the c h, a Dutch throat sound that English simply does not make. So you get y a c h t, four consonants for one syllable, and no way to hear three of them.","show":{"t":"word","word":"yacht","hi":[2,2]}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"And the kitchen","say":"The other half of Dutch borrowing is domestic. Cookie, from koekje, a little cake — that k j e is a Dutch diminutive that English flattened to i e. Coleslaw, from koolsalade, cabbage salad. Waffle from wafel. Gherkin from gurkijn. Brandy from brandewijn, meaning burnt wine. Plain words for plain things.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"cookie","parts":[{"txt":"koek","gloss":"cake"},{"txt":"-je","gloss":"Dutch diminutive → -ie"}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Telling Dutch from German","say":"Both languages use s c h and both build compounds, so spellers mix them up. Use subject and length instead. Dutch loans are short, old, and about ships, food or money — sloop, cookie, guilder, polder. German loans are longer, newer, and about ideas — zeitgeist, schadenfreude, gestalt, doppelganger. That distinction is far more reliable than any letter.","show":{"t":"tree","sound":"a word beginning sch-","branches":[{"sp":"Dutch","org":"short, old, concrete","ex":"schooner, school"},{"sp":"German","org":"longer, abstract","ex":"schadenfreude"},{"sp":"Yiddish","org":"often sch- + -l or -k","ex":"schlep, schmooze"},{"sp":"Greek","org":"sch- from skhole","ex":"scheme, schism"}]}}]},"24":{"label":"Yiddish & Hebrew","scenes":[{"mood":"think","cap":"Two layers, not one","say":"These look like one source and they are two, separated by a thousand years. Hebrew came into English early, through scripture, so its words are formal — jubilee, sabbath, leviathan. Yiddish came late, through daily speech, so its words are informal — schlep, klutz, nosh. Deciding which layer a word belongs to decides how you spell it.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"leviathan","right":"klutz","root":"Hebrew through scripture versus Yiddish through speech","note":"the formal layer looks Latin; the informal layer looks German"}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"Yiddish loves a cluster","say":"Yiddish begins words with clusters English normally refuses. S c h — schlep, schmooze, schmaltz, schtick. K n with the k actually pronounced — knish. And it writes a ts sound as t z — chutzpah, kibitz, klutz, matzo. When you hear one of those clusters, you are in Yiddish.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"schlep","hi":[0,3]},{"word":"knish","hi":[0,2]},{"word":"klutz","hi":[3,2]},{"word":"kibitz","hi":[4,2]}]}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"The endings -el and -ke","say":"A large number of Yiddish words end in e l or k e. Bagel. Dreidel. Kugel. Strudel. Yarmulke. Tchotchke. Latke. If a homely, food-or-object word ends in one of those, and it is not Dutch, try Yiddish. Tchotchke is the hardest of them — t c h o t c h k e, two identical clusters.","show":{"t":"word","word":"tchotchke","hi":[0,4]}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"The ch is a throat sound","say":"Both languages have a sound English does not, and both write it c h. Chutzpah. Challah. Chanukah. Chazan. It is not the c h of church and not the c h of chorus. So when a Hebrew or Yiddish word starts with what sounds like a plain h, stop and ask whether it should be spelled c h.","show":{"t":"tree","sound":"written ch","branches":[{"sp":"church","org":"English","ex":"chin, chair"},{"sp":"chorus","org":"Greek chi","ex":"chaos, chronic"},{"sp":"chef","org":"French","ex":"machine"},{"sp":"chutzpah","org":"Hebrew / Yiddish","ex":"challah, chazan"}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Hebrew brought its plurals","say":"Here is the cleanest marker in the chapter. Hebrew brought its own plural endings into English. The masculine plural is i m — seraphim, cherubim, kibbutzim, goyim. The feminine is o t h — sephiroth, mitzvoth. A scholarly or religious word ending i m or o t h is almost certainly Hebrew, and its singular will end a h or a bare consonant.","show":{"t":"shift","rows":[{"label":"seraph","syl":["ser","aph"],"on":1},{"label":"seraphim","syl":["ser","a","phim"],"on":2}]}},{"mood":"love","cap":"Eight hundred words","say":"Between them these two layers put more than eight hundred words into your full library and fifty four into the core competition list. The rule of thumb is simple. Formal and biblical means Hebrew, so look for i m, o t h and a h. Informal and everyday means Yiddish, so look for s c h, k n and t z.","show":{"t":"ask","q":"Which layer is this word from?","rules":["Formal, biblical → Hebrew: -im, -oth, -ah","Everyday, informal → Yiddish: sch-, kn-, tz","Throat h sound → written ch in both","808 in the full library, 54 in core"]}}]},"25":{"label":"Russian markers","scenes":[{"mood":"happy","cap":"Suffixes you can rely on","say":"Slavic languages build meaning by stacking endings, and those endings survive the trip into English almost untouched. That makes these words far more spellable than they look. N i k names a person. K a makes a noun. O v and e v end surnames that became words. Learn four suffixes and a whole language group opens up.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"-nik · -ka · -ov · -ism","sub":"person · thing · name · doctrine"}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"What -nik and -ka build","say":"Sputnik means fellow traveller — s p u t plus n i k, the person suffix. Babushka is a grandmother, with the k a noun ending. Matryoshka, the nesting doll. Balalaika, the triangular guitar. Troika, a three horse team, and by extension any group of three. Once you hear the ending, the last three letters are settled.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"sputnik","parts":[{"txt":"sput","gloss":"travelling with"},{"txt":"-nik","gloss":"one who"}]}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"The oy sound is written o-i","say":"Here is a small rule that decides several words. Russian transliteration writes the oy sound as o i, never o y. Perestroika, not perestroyka. Troika, not troyka. Your English instinct says o y, because that is what boy and toy do. In Slavic words it is always o i.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"perestroika","right":"perestroyka","root":"transliteration uses -oi-","note":"English oy never appears in Russian loanwords"}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Clusters English would break up","say":"Slavic languages happily begin and end words with consonant piles English refuses. Borscht ends in three consonants in a row. Chastushka has a shch cluster in the middle. Glasnost, gulag and sputnik stack sounds that English would separate with a vowel. If a word looks unpronounceable at either end, Slavic is a fair guess.","show":{"t":"word","word":"chastushka","hi":[4,4]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Three waves, three subjects","say":"The words came in three distinct waves, and knowing which one helps. The oldest is rural and courtly — steppe, tundra, samovar, sable, czar, vodka. The Soviet wave is political — commissar, gulag, apparatchik, glasnost, perestroika. The newest is technical — sputnik, cosmonaut, kalashnikov, molotov. Subject matter dates the word.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"samovar","hi":[0,4]},{"word":"apparatchik","hi":[7,4]},{"word":"cosmonaut","hi":[0,4]},{"word":"kalashnikov","hi":[8,3]}]}},{"mood":"love","cap":"A surname that became a word","say":"One last delight. Stroganoff was a count, molotov a foreign minister, kalashnikov a weapons designer, pavlova a ballerina. Their names ended in o v, e v or a, and English kept the ending exactly. Twenty three of these words are in your core list and four hundred and thirty eight in the full library.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"-ov · -ev · -ova","sub":"a surname turned into a common noun"}}]},"26":{"label":"Portuguese markers","scenes":[{"mood":"excited","cap":"The first global sailors","say":"Portuguese ships reached West Africa, Brazil, India, China and Japan before anyone else in Europe. So when English wanted words for the wider world, it borrowed them from Portuguese. Which means a Portuguese loanword is often not Portuguese at all — it is a Tupi or Malay or Bantu word wearing Portuguese clothes.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"Tupi → Portuguese → English","sub":"you spell the middle step"}},{"mood":"think","cap":"The nasal ending became -oon","say":"Portuguese has a nasal ending written a with a tilde and an o, and English had no way to write that sound. It came out as o o n. Galleon from galeão. Monsoon by way of monção. Festoon and dragoon arrived along the same route. When a word ends o o n and concerns ships, weather or the tropics, suspect Portuguese.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"monsoon","hi":[4,3]},{"word":"flamingo","hi":[0,4]},{"word":"commando","hi":[0,6]},{"word":"albatross","hi":[0,3]}]}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"nh and lh","say":"Here is the cleanest Portuguese marker. Where Spanish writes n with a tilde, Portuguese writes n h. Where Spanish writes double l, Portuguese writes l h. Piranha has that n h, giving the n y sound. If you see n h in a loanword, you are looking at Portuguese, not Spanish.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"piranha","right":"señor","root":"Portuguese nh versus Spanish ñ","note":"same sound, two different spelling traditions"}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Words that were only passing through","say":"Watch how many of these are couriers. Jacaranda and piranha are Tupi, from Brazil. Cobra is Portuguese for hooded snake. Mango is Tamil mankay. Teak is Malayalam tekka. Molasses is melaço. Pagoda came through pagode. English copied the Portuguese spelling, so the Portuguese spelling is the one you learn.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"jacaranda","parts":[{"txt":"jacarandá","gloss":"Tupi, via Portuguese"}]}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"Albatross was rebuilt twice","say":"Albatross has the strangest history in the chapter. Arabic al qadus gave Portuguese alcatraz, a pelican. English sailors heard it, then reshaped the start toward Latin albus, meaning white, because the bird is white. So the word is Arabic, then Portuguese, then folk-corrected by Latin. And Alcatraz is the same word, untouched.","show":{"t":"shift","rows":[{"label":"alcatraz","syl":["al","ca","traz"],"on":0},{"label":"albatross","syl":["al","ba","tross"],"on":1}]}},{"mood":"love","cap":"Telling it from Spanish","say":"So, Portuguese or Spanish? Portuguese uses n h and l h and the c with a cedilla. Spanish uses n with a tilde and double l. And the subjects differ — Portuguese words cluster around Brazil, Africa and the sea route to Asia, Spanish ones around Mexico and the Caribbean. Twenty two in core, two hundred and seventy one in the full library.","show":{"t":"ask","q":"Portuguese or Spanish?","rules":["nh or lh → Portuguese","ñ or ll → Spanish","Brazil, Africa, Asia route → Portuguese","Mexico, Caribbean, Southwest → Spanish"]}}]},"27":{"label":"Celtic markers","scenes":[{"mood":"oops","cap":"Letters that are not sounds","say":"Celtic words look impossible, and the reason is honest. Irish and Welsh use the Roman alphabet for sounds it was never built for, and they solved that with pairs of letters rather than new letters. So in these words, some letters are instructions about other letters, not sounds of their own.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"bh mh dh gh ll dd","sub":"pairs that do a single job"}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Irish adds an h to soften","say":"Irish softens a consonant by writing an h after it, and the result often sounds nothing like the letters. B h and m h sound like v or w. D h and g h frequently go silent altogether. T h sounds like a plain h. So bodhran, with a d h, is said bow rawn. You see more than you hear.","show":{"t":"word","word":"bodhran","hi":[2,2]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Irish vowels can be grammar","say":"Irish also marks whether a consonant is slender or broad using the vowel written next to it. That is why Irish words carry vowels you never pronounce. Taoiseach has a, o and i side by side. Those three letters are grammar, not sound. Do not try to hear them — learn a o i as a single unit.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"taoiseach","parts":[{"txt":"ta","gloss":"broad"},{"txt":"oi","gloss":"slender marker"},{"txt":"seach","gloss":"chief"}]}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"Welsh uses w and y as vowels","say":"Welsh looks vowel-less to English eyes and it is not — its vowels are w and y. Cwm is a valley. Crwth is a stringed instrument. And Welsh double l is a single sound made by blowing air past the side of the tongue, which English simply does not have. Llan, Llewellyn, Llanfair. Welsh d d is the t h of this.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"eisteddfod","hi":[5,2]},{"word":"cromlech","hi":[6,2]},{"word":"penguin","hi":[0,3]}]}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"The words English actually kept","say":"Most Celtic loans in English are informal and vivid. Hooligan. Banshee, from bean sídhe, a woman of the fairy mound. Smithereens. Shillelagh. Galore, from go leor, meaning enough. Whiskey, from uisce beatha, the water of life. Slogan, from sluagh ghairm, a battle cry. Words with a bit of noise in them.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"whiskey","parts":[{"txt":"uisce","gloss":"water"},{"txt":"beatha","gloss":"of life"}]}},{"mood":"love","cap":"The longest place name in Britain","say":"And if you want proof that Welsh spelling is systematic rather than random, the village of Llanfairpwllgwyngyll and its full fifty eight letter name is in your library. Every one of those letters follows Welsh rules — double l, w as a vowel, y as a vowel. Thirteen Celtic words sit in core and two hundred and ninety two in the full library.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"ll · dd · w · y","sub":"Welsh is systematic, not arbitrary"}}]},"28":{"label":"Turkish & Persian","scenes":[{"mood":"happy","cap":"Words that travelled a long road","say":"The Silk Road and the Ottoman court sent a long list of words west, and almost none of them came into English directly. Persian gave us gardens, poetry, textiles and chess. Turkish gave us the court, the army and the kitchen. And nearly every one passed through Arabic, Italian or French on the way.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"Persian → Arabic → French → English","sub":"the route decides the spelling"}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"Chess is entirely Persian","say":"Here is a lovely one. Checkmate comes from Persian shah mat, meaning the king is helpless. Rook comes from rukh. Check itself is shah, the king. So the whole vocabulary of the game is Persian, worn down by centuries of English mouths until shah mat became checkmate.","show":{"t":"shift","rows":[{"label":"shah mat","syl":["shah","mat"],"on":0},{"label":"checkmate","syl":["check","mate"],"on":0}]}},{"mood":"love","cap":"And so is paradise","say":"Paradise is Persian too — pairidaeza, meaning a wall built around a garden. The Greeks borrowed it as paradeisos, the Bible translators used it for the garden of Eden, and now it means heaven. A word for a garden fence became a word for heaven. Jasmine, lilac, tulip, azure and spinach came along the same road.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"paradise","parts":[{"txt":"pairi","gloss":"around"},{"txt":"daeza","gloss":"wall"}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Turkish gave us the court","say":"Turkish supplied the vocabulary of the Ottoman state. Pasha. Vizier. Janissary. Seraglio. Khan. Horde, from ordu, an army camp. Kiosk, from kösk, a garden pavilion. Fez, named for the Moroccan city. Turban. If the word is about rank, court or camp east of the Mediterranean, try Turkish.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"janissary","hi":[0,4]},{"word":"vizier","hi":[0,3]},{"word":"horde","hi":[0,5]},{"word":"kiosk","hi":[0,5]}]}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"One root, three spellings","say":"Now the reason these spellings look so inconsistent. Arabic sharba meant a drink. It reached English three separate times by three routes and became sherbet through Turkish, syrup through Latin, and shrub through a different Arabic form. Same root, three words, three spellings. The route is what differs.","show":{"t":"tree","sound":"Arabic sharba = a drink","branches":[{"sp":"sherbet","org":"via Turkish şerbet","ex":"the frozen dessert"},{"sp":"syrup","org":"via Latin siropus","ex":"the sweet liquid"},{"sp":"shrub","org":"via Arabic sharāb","ex":"the fruit cordial"},{"sp":"sorbet","org":"via French","ex":"the same Turkish word again"}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Tulip was a turban","say":"One more to finish. Turkish tülbend meant a turban. The Turks thought the flower looked like one, French took the word as tulipe, and English made it tulip. So tulip and turban are the same word twice. Twelve of these are in your core list and two hundred and ninety four in the full library.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"tulip","right":"turban","root":"both from Turkish tülbend","note":"the flower was named for the headwear"}}]},"29":{"label":"Malay markers","scenes":[{"mood":"happy","cap":"The trade language of the islands","say":"Malay was the common language of the whole East Indies, so when English wanted words for Southeast Asia, it took them through Malay — often with Portuguese or Dutch ships doing the carrying. And these words are unusually kind to a speller, for a reason worth understanding.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"Malay","sub":"the trade language of the East Indies"}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"Simple syllables, simple spelling","say":"Malay builds words from consonant vowel syllables and allows almost no consonant clusters. So the words alternate neatly all the way through. Rat tan. Ba tik. Sa rong. Bin tu rong. Read one aloud slowly and you can very often just write it. That is the opposite of French or Irish.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"binturong","parts":[{"txt":"bin","gloss":"syllable 1"},{"txt":"tu","gloss":"syllable 2"},{"txt":"rong","gloss":"syllable 3"}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"The -ng ending","say":"Malay ends a great many words in n g and English kept it. Sarong. Gong. Binturong. Linsang. Parang. That n g is one nasal sound, not an n followed by a g. When a tropical word or an animal name ends in n g, Malay is a strong first guess.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"sarong","hi":[4,2]},{"word":"binturong","hi":[7,2]},{"word":"linsang","hi":[5,2]},{"word":"parang","hi":[4,2]}]}},{"mood":"love","cap":"Forest person","say":"Orangutan is the one everybody should know. Malay orang hutan means person of the forest. English squeezed the two words together and lost the h, so the phrase became a single word. If you can hear orang and hutan inside it, you will never misspell it.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"orangutan","parts":[{"txt":"orang","gloss":"person"},{"txt":"hutan","gloss":"forest"}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Ketchup went the furthest","say":"Ketchup is the great traveller of this chapter. It began as a Hokkien Chinese fish sauce, passed into Malay as kicap, was picked up by English traders in the East Indies, and eventually landed on a bottle of tomato sauce. Four languages and a complete change of recipe.","show":{"t":"tree","sound":"ketchup","branches":[{"sp":"kê-tsiap","org":"Hokkien Chinese","ex":"a fermented fish sauce"},{"sp":"kicap","org":"Malay","ex":"the trade form"},{"sp":"catchup","org":"early English","ex":"a mushroom sauce"},{"sp":"ketchup","org":"modern English","ex":"tomato, finally"}]}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"The animals are the exam words","say":"The bee level vocabulary here is mostly natural history, and it is all simply spelled and completely unfamiliar, which is the perfect combination for a competition. Binturong. Linsang. Babirusa. Dugong. Cassowary. Mangosteen. Rambutan. Twelve in your core list, two hundred and fifty nine in the full library.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"babirusa","hi":[0,4]},{"word":"dugong","hi":[0,3]},{"word":"rambutan","hi":[0,4]},{"word":"mangosteen","hi":[0,5]}]}}]},"30":{"label":"Chinese & Korean","scenes":[{"mood":"oops","cap":"One language, two spelling systems","say":"Here is the difficulty, and it is not really about Chinese. It is about English. English has used two rival systems for writing Chinese sounds, so the same syllable gets two completely different spellings depending on when the word arrived. Knowing which system produced a word is the entire spelling decision.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"Wade-Giles · Pinyin","sub":"two ways to write the same sound"}},{"mood":"think","cap":"The four swaps","say":"Learn four correspondences and most of it resolves. Wade Giles wrote c h where Pinyin writes z h or j. Wade Giles t s is Pinyin z or c. Wade Giles h s is Pinyin x. And Wade Giles k is often Pinyin g. That is why Peking became Beijing and Taoism became Daoism.","show":{"t":"tree","sound":"the same Chinese syllable","branches":[{"sp":"ch","org":"Wade-Giles","ex":"Chungking"},{"sp":"zh / j","org":"Pinyin","ex":"Chongqing"},{"sp":"hs","org":"Wade-Giles","ex":"Hsinhua"},{"sp":"x","org":"Pinyin","ex":"Xinhua"}]}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"Old words kept old spellings","say":"The reason the vocabulary looks so mixed is that old borrowings never updated. Tea, typhoon, kowtow, tycoon, sampan, oolong, souchong, pekoe, litchi, kumquat, ginseng and kaolin all arrived long before Pinyin existed, so they keep their older forms. Renminbi, pinyin and Beijing came in with the new system.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"souchong","hi":[3,5]},{"word":"oolong","hi":[0,2]},{"word":"kowtow","hi":[0,3]},{"word":"renminbi","hi":[0,3]}]}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"The southern ports","say":"A lot of the early borrowing happened through Canton and the southern ports, so many of these words are Cantonese rather than Mandarin. Cheongsam. Wok. Dim sum. Bok choy. Kumquat. Loquat. Souchong. If a food or clothing word feels southern, expect Cantonese vowels — the e o of cheongsam, the o y of choy.","show":{"t":"word","word":"cheongsam","hi":[2,3]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Korean writes vowels in pairs","say":"Korean romanisation has its own conventions, and the important one is that it writes several single vowels with two letters. E o. E u. A e. So bibimbap, naengmyeon, taekwondo and hangul all carry vowel pairs that are one sound each. Naengmyeon looks alarming until you know that a e and y e o are each a single vowel.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"bibimbap","parts":[{"txt":"bibim","gloss":"mixed"},{"txt":"bap","gloss":"rice"}]}},{"mood":"love","cap":"What to do at the microphone","say":"So when you get one of these, ask two questions. Does the word feel old and homely, like tea or typhoon? Then it uses the old system. Does it feel recent and official, like renminbi? Then it is Pinyin. And if it is a food or a garment, suspect Cantonese. Four are in your core list and a hundred and seventy five in the full library.","show":{"t":"ask","q":"Chinese word at the mic?","rules":["Old and homely → Wade-Giles or postal spelling","Recent and official → Pinyin: zh, x, q, g","Food or clothing → likely Cantonese","Vowel pairs eo, eu, ae → Korean"]}}]},"31":{"label":"Hawaiian markers","scenes":[{"mood":"excited","cap":"The smallest alphabet you will meet","say":"Hawaiian uses thirteen letters. Five vowels — a, e, i, o, u. Seven consonants — h, k, l, m, n, p, w. And a glottal stop. That is the entire alphabet. There is no b, no c, no d, no f, no g, no s, no t, no v. Three quarters of the English alphabet is simply unavailable.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"a e i o u  h k l m n p w","sub":"the entire Hawaiian alphabet"}},{"mood":"love","cap":"Which makes these the easiest hard words","say":"Think about what that means for you. The moment you know a word is Hawaiian, most letters are eliminated before you start. No other language group in this course gives you a constraint that strong. These look like the hardest words on the list and they are among the most predictable.","show":{"t":"ask","q":"Told the word is Hawaiian?","rules":["Only a e i o u and h k l m n p w are possible","Every syllable ends in a vowel","No two consonants ever touch","Doubled vowels are normal, not errors"]}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"Consonant, vowel, consonant, vowel","say":"No Hawaiian syllable ends in a consonant, and no two consonants ever sit next to each other. So the shape of every word is consonant vowel, consonant vowel, all the way through. Pa ho e ho e. Ki pu ka. Wa hi ne. Hau pi a. Say it slowly and write what you hear — there is no other legal spelling.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"pahoehoe","parts":[{"txt":"pa","gloss":"CV"},{"txt":"ho","gloss":"CV"},{"txt":"e","gloss":"V"},{"txt":"ho","gloss":"CV"},{"txt":"e","gloss":"V"}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Doubled vowels are correct","say":"Because there are only five vowels doing all the work, Hawaiian doubles and triples them freely, and that is not an error. Aa is the rough lava. Aalii is a shrub. Muumuu is the dress. Laulau and lavalava and mahimahi double whole syllables. If your instinct says that looks like a typo, your instinct is wrong here.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"aalii","hi":[0,2]},{"word":"muumuu","hi":[1,2]},{"word":"laulau","hi":[0,3]},{"word":"mahimahi","hi":[0,4]}]}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"Twenty-one letters, one pattern","say":"Humuhumunukunukuapuaa is the Hawaiian triggerfish. Twenty one letters, and every one of them predictable. Humu, humu, nuku, nuku, a, puaa. Two doubled words, a connector, and puaa, meaning pig, because the fish grunts like one. It is genuinely easier than a twenty one letter Latin word.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"humuhumunukunukuapuaa","parts":[{"txt":"humuhumu","gloss":"triggerfish"},{"txt":"nukunuku","gloss":"snout"},{"txt":"a","gloss":"like"},{"txt":"puaa","gloss":"pig"}]}},{"mood":"love","cap":"Across the Pacific","say":"The related languages behave the same way with slightly bigger alphabets. Maori allows t and r and gives us tuatara, kakapo and moa. Taboo is Tongan tabu and tattoo is Tahitian tatau — both brought back by Captain Cook's crews. Eight of these are in your core list and eighty two in the full library.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"tabu","right":"taboo","root":"Tongan, via Captain Cook","note":"tattoo came from Tahitian tatau the same way"}}]},"32":{"label":"African markers","scenes":[{"mood":"think","cap":"Two sources, one heading","say":"This chapter covers two very different things. Afrikaans is a daughter language of Dutch, so it compounds the same way and its words come apart neatly. The Bantu languages work completely differently — they mark a noun's class with a prefix at the front. Confusing the two is where mistakes come from.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"Afrikaans · Bantu","sub":"compounding versus prefixing"}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"Afrikaans compounds","say":"Learn a short list of Afrikaans elements and the compounds open right up. Aard means earth, so aardvark is earth pig and aardwolf is earth wolf. Veld is a field. Kop is a head or a hill. Bok is a buck, giving springbok and gemsbok. Slang is a snake, so boomslang is tree snake.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"aardwolf","parts":[{"txt":"aard","gloss":"earth"},{"txt":"wolf","gloss":"wolf"}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"The doubled a is Dutch inheritance","say":"Afrikaans marks a long vowel by doubling the letter, exactly as Dutch does. That is why you get aardvark, aardwolf and aasvogel with two a letters at the front. Aas means carrion, so an aasvogel is a carrion bird, a vulture. If a South African word starts with a long ah sound, expect two a letters.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"aardvark","hi":[0,2]},{"word":"aardwolf","hi":[0,2]},{"word":"aasvogel","hi":[0,2]},{"word":"braai","hi":[2,2]}]}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"Bantu prefixes are not roots","say":"Now the Bantu half, and this is the key idea. Bantu languages mark what class a noun belongs to using a prefix. So the m, n, k i or v u you hear at the start is grammar, not part of the root. Vuvuzela. Impala. Kalimba. Marimba. Do not go looking for meaning in those first letters.","show":{"t":"word","word":"vuvuzela","hi":[0,2]}},{"mood":"love","cap":"Words that crossed the Atlantic twice","say":"There is a third route worth knowing. West African words travelled to the Americas with enslaved people and came back into English through the Caribbean and the American South. Banjo. Banana. Jazz. Jumbo. Goober, meaning peanut. Gumbo. Jambalaya. Tote. Okra. Yam. They arrived without their original spellings, which is why they look completely English.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"jambalaya","hi":[0,4]},{"word":"gumbo","hi":[0,5]},{"word":"banjo","hi":[0,5]},{"word":"jumbo","hi":[0,5]}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Apart-hood","say":"One word deserves its own note. Apartheid is Afrikaans apart plus heid, and heid is the same ending as English hood in childhood or brotherhood. So the word literally means apart hood, the state of being kept apart. Spell the h e i d, not h o o d. Twenty eight of these are in core and a hundred and eleven in the full library.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"apartheid","right":"brotherhood","root":"Afrikaans -heid is English -hood","note":"the ending is heid, never hood"}}]},"33":{"label":"Native American","scenes":[{"mood":"excited","cap":"The cluster English cannot say","say":"Nahuatl, the language of the Aztecs, allows a t l cluster at the end of a word, and English very nearly cannot pronounce it. But English kept the letters anyway. Atlatl. Axolotl. Nahuatl itself. That t l ending is the single clearest fingerprint anywhere in this chapter.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"-tl","sub":"the Nahuatl signature"}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"Read the parts","say":"Axolotl comes apart neatly. Atl means water, and xolotl means a servant or a monster. So an axolotl is a water monster, which is a fair description of a salamander that never grows up. That x is not a k s — in Nahuatl it stands for a sound like s h.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"axolotl","parts":[{"txt":"a","gloss":"water (atl)"},{"txt":"xolotl","gloss":"monster, servant"}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Spanish trimmed the endings","say":"Most Nahuatl words reached English through Spanish, and Spanish trimmed those endings to fit its own habits. Tomatl became tomate, then tomato. Ahuacatl became aguacate, then avocado. Chocolatl became chocolate. Coyotl became coyote. So the English spelling is usually the Spanish reshaping, not the original.","show":{"t":"shift","rows":[{"label":"ahuacatl","syl":["a","hua","catl"],"on":2},{"label":"aguacate","syl":["a","gua","ca","te"],"on":2},{"label":"avocado","syl":["a","vo","ca","do"],"on":2}]}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"The Atlantic coast words hide completely","say":"Now a warning. Along the Atlantic coast, English colonists borrowed Algonquian words and respelled them thoroughly. Raccoon. Skunk. Moose. Opossum. Squash. Hickory. Pecan. Persimmon. Succotash. Terrapin. Moccasin. Powwow. Toboggan. Because English speakers did the respelling, these carry no foreign marker at all.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"succotash","hi":[0,4]},{"word":"persimmon","hi":[0,3]},{"word":"toboggan","hi":[0,2]},{"word":"moccasin","hi":[0,3]}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"The Andes came through Spanish too","say":"A third group is Quechua, from the Andes, and it also arrived through Spanish. Llama, with a Spanish double l. Alpaca. Vicuna. Condor. Guano. Pampa. Quinine, from quina. Quinoa. Jerky, from charqui. Puma. Coca. If the word names a South American animal or plant, try Quechua.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"llama","hi":[0,2]},{"word":"alpaca","hi":[0,6]},{"word":"quinoa","hi":[0,6]},{"word":"guano","hi":[0,5]}]}},{"mood":"love","cap":"So what do you do at the mic","say":"Put it together. A t l ending, or an x for a sh sound, means Nahuatl. An ordinary looking English word for a North American animal or plant may well be Algonquian, and you spell it as English. A South American animal or plant is probably Quechua through Spanish. Two of these sit in your core list and forty six in the full library.","show":{"t":"ask","q":"Which American language?","rules":["-tl ending, x for sh → Nahuatl","Looks fully English, North American → Algonquian","Andes, ll or qu → Quechua via Spanish","Spanish trimmed most Nahuatl endings"]}}]},"34":{"label":"assimilation","scenes":[{"mood":"happy","cap":"A rule you already half know","say":"Your general course taught you that a d changes shape before different letters. What it did not tell you is that every Latin prefix does the same thing, and that this one piece of machinery explains most of the doubled letters in English. This is the most useful structural idea in the whole advanced course.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"in- → im- il- ir-","sub":"the prefix matches what follows"}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Why a prefix would change at all","say":"Latin speakers did what all speakers do — they took the easy path. Saying in possible is awkward, so the n slid forward to become m, giving impossible. In legible became illegible. In regular became irregular. The prefix reshapes its last consonant to match the first consonant of the root.","show":{"t":"shift","rows":[{"label":"in + possible","syl":["in","pos","si","ble"],"on":0},{"label":"impossible","syl":["im","pos","si","ble"],"on":0}]}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"Now the doubled letters make sense","say":"Here is the payoff. Accommodate is a d plus commodus. The prefix a d becomes a c before the c, giving two c letters, and the root already had two m letters. That is why the word has two of each — and why it is the most misspelled word in English until you see the seam.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"accommodate","parts":[{"txt":"ac-","gloss":"ad- assimilated"},{"txt":"commod","gloss":"fitting"},{"txt":"-ate","gloss":"make"}]}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"Four families to know","say":"Four prefixes do most of the work. I n becomes i m, i l or i r. S u b becomes s u c, s u f, s u g, s u p or s u r. A d becomes a c, a f, a g, a l, a n, a p, a r, a s or a t. C o n becomes c o l, c o r or c o m. Learn those four and you can take apart hundreds of words.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"succumb","hi":[0,3]},{"word":"suggestion","hi":[0,3]},{"word":"collateral","hi":[0,3]},{"word":"corroborate","hi":[0,3]}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"When it does not double","say":"The absence of a double is information too. Before a vowel the prefix stays whole — adapt, adopt, adequate, inept, inane. And before some awkward clusters it just drops its last letter without doubling — aspire is a d plus spirare, not adspire and not asspire. So no double means the root started with a vowel or a cluster.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"accommodate","right":"aspire","root":"both from ad-","note":"one assimilates and doubles, the other simply drops the d"}},{"mood":"love","cap":"Spelling as arithmetic","say":"So this is how you use it. Hear a long Latin word, find the prefix, find the root, and the seam between them tells you which letter doubles. One thousand three hundred and fifty six words in your core library begin with an assimilated prefix — more than any other pattern in this course.","show":{"t":"ask","q":"Long Latin word, unsure about doubling?","rules":["Find the prefix and the root","Prefix consonant matched the root → double","Root begins with a vowel → no double","1,356 words in core follow this"]}}]},"35":{"label":"stacking","scenes":[{"mood":"excited","cap":"Long is not the same as hard","say":"The longest words in your library are not harder than the short ones. They are just longer. Every one of them is a chain of small pieces with fixed spellings, joined by a single connecting vowel. The entire skill is finding where the joints are.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"ot · o · rhin · o · laryng · o · logy","sub":"ear · nose · throat · study"}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Never spell left to right","say":"Here is the habit to build. Do not start writing at the first letter. Break the word at its joints first, in your head, and only then spell the pieces in order. Otorhinolaryngology is ear, nose, throat, study. Four ideas. Nineteen letters that you now assemble rather than recall.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"otorhinolaryngology","parts":[{"txt":"ot","gloss":"ear"},{"txt":"rhin","gloss":"nose"},{"txt":"laryng","gloss":"throat"},{"txt":"logy","gloss":"study of"}]}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"Greek takes o, Latin takes i","say":"The connecting vowel is decided by the language of the pieces. Greek compounds join with o — ochlocracy, myrmecophagy, thermometer, dendrochronology. Latin compounds join with i — fossiliferous, cupriferous, insecticide, verisimilitude. Identify the language, and every joint in the word is settled at once.","show":{"t":"tree","sound":"the connecting vowel","branches":[{"sp":"o","org":"Greek pieces","ex":"dendrochronology"},{"sp":"i","org":"Latin pieces","ex":"fossiliferous"},{"sp":"o","org":"Greek even when long","ex":"psychopharmacology"},{"sp":"i","org":"Latin even when long","ex":"verisimilitude"}]}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"The forty-five letter one","say":"Take the longest word in your library. Pneumonoultramicroscopicsilicovolcanoconiosis. Break it. Pneumon, lung. Ultra, beyond. Microscopic, tiny. Silico, silica. Volcano. Coni, dust. Osis, a condition. It is a lung disease from breathing fine volcanic silica dust. Forty five letters and not one of them is a surprise.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"pneumonoultramicroscopicsilicovolcanoconiosis","parts":[{"txt":"pneumon","gloss":"lung"},{"txt":"ultramicroscopic","gloss":"too small to see"},{"txt":"silico","gloss":"silica"},{"txt":"volcano","gloss":"volcanic"},{"txt":"coni","gloss":"dust"},{"txt":"osis","gloss":"condition"}]}},{"mood":"love","cap":"Four words for worthless","say":"Floccinaucinihilipilification is even better. Flocci, a tuft of wool. Nauci, a trifle. Nihili, nothing. Pili, a hair. Fication, making. Four Latin words that all mean something worthless, stacked up, and then made into a noun. It means the habit of judging things to be worthless. Somebody built that word as a joke and it stuck.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"floccinaucinihilipilification","parts":[{"txt":"flocci","gloss":"a tuft of wool"},{"txt":"nauci","gloss":"a trifle"},{"txt":"nihili","gloss":"nothing"},{"txt":"pili","gloss":"a hair"},{"txt":"fication","gloss":"making"}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Every piece pays you back","say":"The reason to practise this is reuse. Learn cephal, meaning head, and you get cephalic, encephalogram, hydrocephalus, cephalopod, acephalous and cephalomancy. Learn laryng and you get laryngitis, laryngology, laryngoscope. Two thousand eight hundred and seventy one words in your core list have six syllables or more, and almost all of them are stacks.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"cephalopod","hi":[0,6]},{"word":"encephalogram","hi":[2,6]},{"word":"hydrocephalus","hi":[5,6]},{"word":"cephalomancy","hi":[0,6]}]}}]},"36":{"label":"hyphens","scenes":[{"mood":"oops","cap":"A hyphen is a letter","say":"Start with the thing that actually loses words on stage. A hyphen is not punctuation you can skip. You have to say it out loud. M i l l e, hyphen, f e u i l l e. If you run the letters together and never say the word hyphen, you have misspelled it, and the judges will rule against you.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"say \"hyphen\"","sub":"silence is a misspelling"}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Borrowed phrases keep their punctuation","say":"The most reliable rule. When English borrows a whole phrase from another language, it keeps the punctuation that came with it. Porte monnaie. Mille feuille. Carton pierre. Loup garou. Cul de sac. Mezzo soprano. Privat docent, from German. If it was a phrase over there, it takes hyphens over here.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"porte-monnaie","hi":[5,1]},{"word":"loup-garou","hi":[4,1]},{"word":"cul-de-sac","hi":[3,1]},{"word":"mezzo-soprano","hi":[5,1]}]}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"self- is dependable","say":"One prefix you can count on completely. Self always takes a hyphen. Self conscious. Self portrait. Self sabotage. Self mastery. Self sterile. Self support. If you hear self at the front of a word, put in a hyphen and say it.","show":{"t":"word","word":"self-conscious","hi":[4,1]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Compounds have a life cycle","say":"Beyond that, English compounds age through three stages. They start as two separate words. Then they acquire a hyphen. Then they close up entirely. To day became to day with a hyphen and finally today. So the newer the compound, the more likely a hyphen. The older and more everyday, the more likely it has closed.","show":{"t":"shift","rows":[{"label":"to day","syl":["to","day"],"on":0},{"label":"to-day","syl":["to","day"],"on":0},{"label":"today","syl":["to","day"],"on":1}]}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"The strangest one in your library","say":"Hsaing waing is a Burmese orchestra, and it is in your word list. H s a i n g, hyphen, w a i n g. An initial h s, which almost no English word has, and a doubled structure with a hyphen in the middle. If you can spell that one aloud, hyphen included, you can spell any of them.","show":{"t":"word","word":"hsaing-waing","hi":[6,1]}},{"mood":"love","cap":"When you truly cannot tell","say":"Sometimes there is no rule and the dictionary simply decides. When that happens, use what you do know. Foreign phrase? Hyphen. Starts with self? Hyphen. Old everyday English compound? Probably closed up. A hundred and eleven of these are in your core list and seven hundred and fifty two in the full library.","show":{"t":"ask","q":"Hyphen, space or closed?","rules":["Borrowed foreign phrase → hyphen","Begins with self- → hyphen","Old, everyday compound → closed","And say the hyphen out loud"]}}]},"37":{"label":"doublets","scenes":[{"mood":"think","cap":"The same word, borrowed twice","say":"English borrowed from French for six hundred years, and French itself changed during that time. So the same Latin word often arrived twice, at different dates, looking different. Those pairs are called doublets, and they explain an enormous amount that otherwise looks completely random.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"one root → two words","sub":"borrowed twice, centuries apart"}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"w in the north, gu in Paris","say":"Northern Norman French kept a Germanic w. Central Parisian French wrote g u instead. English took words from both, so we ended up with both. Ward and guard. Warranty and guarantee. Wile and guile. Warden and guardian. Same root, two spellings, and now two slightly different meanings.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"warranty","right":"guarantee","root":"the same Frankish word, two French routes","note":"Norman kept the w, Parisian wrote gu"}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"c in the north, ch in Paris","say":"The same split happened with c. Latin c before an a stayed c in Norman French and became c h in Parisian. So capitale, meaning property, gave English both cattle and chattel. Captiare gave catch and chase. Caput, a head, gave cap, chief and chef. Three words from one head.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"cattle","hi":[0,1]},{"word":"chattel","hi":[0,2]},{"word":"chief","hi":[0,2]},{"word":"chef","hi":[0,2]}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Worn down or taken fresh","say":"There is a third kind. One form was worn smooth by centuries of ordinary speech, the other was lifted straight out of written Latin much later by scholars. Fragilis gave both frail and fragile. Securus gave sure and secure. Potio gave poison and potion. Ratio gave reason and ration. Short and homely means the old spoken route.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"frail","right":"fragile","root":"both from Latin fragilis","note":"one worn down by speech, one taken from the page"}},{"mood":"love","cap":"Poison and potion","say":"My favourite pair. Latin potio just meant a drink. Taken directly from Latin it gave English potion, a magical drink. Taken through French speech it gave poison, a drink that kills you. Exactly the same word, and the difference between them is which route it walked.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"poison","parts":[{"txt":"potio","gloss":"a drink"},{"txt":"→ poison","gloss":"through French speech"}]}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"How to use a doublet on stage","say":"Here is the practical value. When a pronouncer hands you the rarer member of a pair, the common one is your way in. Chattel? Think cattle, and the double t is explained. Genteel? Think gentle. Corps? Think corpse. Hostel? Think hotel. The familiar twin tells you what the strange one is made of.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"genteel","hi":[0,4]},{"word":"gentle","hi":[0,4]},{"word":"hostel","hi":[0,4]},{"word":"hotel","hi":[0,3]}]}}]},"38":{"label":"heteronyms","scenes":[{"mood":"think","cap":"The mirror image of a homophone","say":"A homophone sounds the same and is spelled differently. A heteronym is the opposite — spelled the same, sounds different, means different things. And it matters on a bee stage for one specific reason, which has nothing to do with spelling and everything to do with what you assume.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"one spelling, two words","sub":"the opposite of a homophone"}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"The stress rule","say":"For dozens of two syllable pairs there is a real rule. The noun stresses the first syllable and the verb stresses the second. CON duct the noun, con DUCT the verb. RE cord and re CORD. PRO ject and pro JECT. PRE sent and pre SENT. Regular enough that you can predict it.","show":{"t":"shift","rows":[{"label":"CONduct (noun)","syl":["CON","duct"],"on":0},{"label":"conDUCT (verb)","syl":["con","DUCT"],"on":1}]}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"The -ate family","say":"A big group ends in a t e, with a full vowel as a verb and a reduced one as an adjective. SEP uh rate the verb, SEP uh rut the adjective. The same is true of moderate, deliberate, intimate, alternate, appropriate, associate, estimate, graduate, predicate and syndicate. And here is the good news — the spelling is identical either way.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"separate","hi":[5,3]},{"word":"moderate","hi":[5,3]},{"word":"deliberate","hi":[7,3]},{"word":"syndicate","hi":[6,3]}]}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"The genuinely different words","say":"Some heteronyms are two unrelated words that happen to collide. Tear, the water in your eye, and tear, to rip. Wind, the air, and wind, to turn a key. Bow, the ribbon, and bow, the greeting. Lead, the metal, and lead, to go first. Dove, the bird, and dove, jumped in. These are where real danger lives.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"tear (eye)","right":"tear (rip)","root":"two unrelated words, one spelling","note":"the definition is the only thing that separates them"}},{"mood":"think","cap":"The strategy at the mic","say":"So here is what you do. If the pronunciation sounds wrong for the word you had in mind, do not adjust your spelling — ask for the part of speech, then ask for the definition. For the stress pairs and the a t e words the spelling is the same either way, so nothing is at risk.","show":{"t":"ask","q":"Pronunciation sounds wrong?","rules":["Ask for the part of speech first","Then ask for the definition","Stress pairs and -ate words spell identically","Only unrelated pairs can actually cost you"]}},{"mood":"love","cap":"Minute and august","say":"Two nice ones to finish. Minute is sixty seconds, and minute is tiny — same seven letters. August is the month and august means dignified and imposing. In both cases the spelling never moves. Knowing that means you can hear an unfamiliar pronunciation and stay completely calm.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"minute","hi":[0,6]},{"word":"august","hi":[0,6]},{"word":"invalid","hi":[0,7]},{"word":"polish","hi":[0,6]}]}}]},"39":{"label":"metathesis","scenes":[{"mood":"think","cap":"Sounds that trade places","say":"Metathesis is when two sounds inside a word swap positions. It happens in every language, it happens most around r and l, and in English it has quietly rewritten a lot of spellings. Some of those swaps finished centuries ago and became correct. Others are happening right now, in your own mouth.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"br → rb","sub":"two sounds trading places"}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"The swaps that won","say":"Old English bridd became bird. Thridda became third. Hros became horse. Waeps became wasp. Haeps became hasp. In every case the letters changed order and the new order became the standard. So a word can look nothing like its own history, and its relatives are the only clue you have.","show":{"t":"shift","rows":[{"label":"bridd","syl":["bridd"],"on":0},{"label":"bird","syl":["bird"],"on":0}]}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"Through and thorough","say":"Here is a pair worth knowing. Through and thorough are the same Old English word, thurh. One kept the compressed form, one developed an extra syllable, and English decided they were different words with different meanings. Same letters, rearranged, and now two words you must not confuse.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"through","right":"thorough","root":"both from Old English thurh","note":"one word that split into two spellings and two meanings"}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Nostril is nose plus hole","say":"Nostril is a lovely piece of hidden history. Old English nosthyrl meant nose hole — nose plus thyrel, a hole. The t h and the r traded places and the whole thing compressed into nostril. And thyrel is also the source of thrill, which originally meant to pierce.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"nostril","parts":[{"txt":"nos","gloss":"nose"},{"txt":"tril","gloss":"thyrel, a hole"}]}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"The swaps happening now","say":"Now the ones that matter to you. Every pronunciation people get corrected for is metathesis in progress. Nuclear said as nucular. Comfortable as comfterble. Integral as intregal. Asterisk as asterix. Espresso as expresso. Sherbet as sherbert. All of them two sounds trading places, exactly as bird and horse did.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"nuclear","hi":[3,4]},{"word":"asterisk","hi":[5,3]},{"word":"espresso","hi":[1,2]},{"word":"sherbet","hi":[4,3]}]}},{"mood":"love","cap":"Learn your own swaps","say":"So here is your homework, and it is personal. The pronouncer will say the word correctly, but your ear will hear whatever you have said all your life. If you say nucular, you will want to write nucular. Cavalry is horsemen and Calvary is a hill — the swap changes the word completely. Find the ones you swap, because those are the ones that will cost you.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"cavalry","right":"Calvary","root":"horsemen versus the hill","note":"a swapped l and v — two entirely different words"}}]},"40":{"label":"new words","scenes":[{"mood":"excited","cap":"English manufactures words","say":"Not every word is inherited from somewhere. English builds words on purpose, and it does it four recognisable ways. It cuts long words short. It fuses two words together. It invents a shorter word by removing an ending that was never a suffix. And it repeats a syllable with a twist.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"clip · blend · back-form · repeat","sub":"four ways to make a new word"}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"Clipping keeps what is left","say":"Clipping just cuts. Influenza became flu, so it is f l u with no e on the end. Omnibus became bus. Cabriolet became cab. Gymnasium became gym. But watch refrigerator becoming fridge — it gained a d that the original never had, because English needed the d to keep that g soft.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"refrigerator","right":"fridge","root":"a clipping that gained a letter","note":"the d was added to keep the g soft"}},{"mood":"love","cap":"Blends keep two halves","say":"A blend takes the front of one word and the back of another. Breakfast and lunch give brunch. Smoke and fog give smog. Motor and hotel give motel. Chuckle and snort give chortle — Lewis Carroll invented that one, and galumph, from gallop and triumph. The seam is invisible, so you have to know both parents.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"chortle","parts":[{"txt":"chu","gloss":"chuckle"},{"txt":"ortle","gloss":"snort"}]}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"Back-formation invents a root","say":"This is the strangest one. English looks at a word, wrongly decides part of it is a suffix, chops it off, and creates a shorter word that never existed. Editor came first, and edit was carved out of it. Pease was already singular, and pea was cut from it. Burglar gave burgle. Hamburger gave burger — and hamburger was named for Hamburg and never had any ham in it.","show":{"t":"shift","rows":[{"label":"editor","syl":["e","di","tor"],"on":2},{"label":"edit","syl":["e","dit"],"on":1}]}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"Reduplication has a vowel rule","say":"Now a real spelling rule hiding in a silly place. When English repeats a syllable and changes the vowel, it puts i first and a or o second. Zigzag, not zagzig. Flimflam, not flamflim. Riffraff. Knickknack. Dillydally. Wishy washy. Chitchat. The order is fixed, and you can rely on it.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"zigzag","hi":[1,1]},{"word":"flimflam","hi":[2,1]},{"word":"riffraff","hi":[1,1]},{"word":"knickknack","hi":[2,1]}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Why the method matters","say":"Knowing which method made a word tells you which letters to keep. A clipping keeps the original spelling of the part that survived. A blend keeps two fragments, so you need both parents. A back formation keeps the shape of the longer word it was carved from. And a reduplication follows the i then a rule. Method first, letters second.","show":{"t":"ask","q":"Which method built this word?","rules":["Short form of a longer word → clipping, keep those letters","Two words fused → blend, know both parents","Feels carved out of a longer word → back-formation","Repeated syllable → i first, then a or o"]}}]},"41":{"label":"folk etymology","scenes":[{"mood":"think","cap":"Wrong guesses that became law","say":"When English met a word containing a piece nobody understood any more, speakers reshaped it to look like something they did recognise. And very often the wrong guess stuck and became the official spelling. That is why some words have letters that flatly contradict their own history.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"igland → island","sub":"a scribe added the s"}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"The s in island is a mistake","say":"Island is the classic case. Old English igland already meant island — ig meant island and land was added for emphasis. There was never an s. Scribes inserted it because French isle, from Latin insula, had one and they thought the words matched. They did not. The s is an error you are now required to write.","show":{"t":"shift","rows":[{"label":"igland","syl":["ig","land"],"on":0},{"label":"iland","syl":["i","land"],"on":0},{"label":"island","syl":["is","land"],"on":0}]}},{"mood":"excited","cap":"The bridegroom has no groom","say":"Bridegroom is better still. Old English brydguma meant bride man — guma was simply the word for a man. Then guma died out of the language, so English swapped in the nearest surviving word that sounded right, which was groom, meaning a boy. So the second half of the word is a substitution, not a survival.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"bridegroom","parts":[{"txt":"bride","gloss":"bride"},{"txt":"groom","gloss":"replaced guma, a man"}]}},{"mood":"love","cap":"The belfry had no bell","say":"My favourite. A belfry was a berfrey, a movable siege tower, from Old French berfrei. Nothing to do with bells at all. But towers had bells in them, so English heard bell in the first syllable and respelled it. Penthouse is the same story — it was apentis, an attached lean to, related to append.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"berfrey","right":"belfry","root":"a siege tower, not a bell tower","note":"English heard \"bell\" and rewrote the word"}},{"mood":"think","cap":"Coleslaw and hangnail","say":"Two more you have met. Coleslaw is Dutch koolsalade, cabbage salad, and English respelled it as though it were cold slaw. Hangnail was angnail, where ang meant painful — nothing hangs. Cockroach was Spanish cucaracha, chopped into cock and roach. Titmouse ends in mose, an old word for a small bird, not mouse.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"coleslaw","hi":[0,4]},{"word":"hangnail","hi":[0,4]},{"word":"cockroach","hi":[4,5]},{"word":"penthouse","hi":[4,5]}]}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"The story is the memory aid","say":"You cannot derive these words, so the origin story is the tool. You will never deduce the s in island, but you will remember the scribes who put it there. Posthumous gained its h because scholars wrongly linked it to humus, earth, when it is really postumus, meaning last. Learn the story, and the illogical spelling sticks.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"posthumous","parts":[{"txt":"post","gloss":"after"},{"txt":"h","gloss":"wrongly added for humus"},{"txt":"umous","gloss":"from postumus, last"}]}}]},"42":{"label":"tautonyms","scenes":[{"mood":"excited","cap":"A word that says itself twice","say":"A tautonym is a word made of the same piece twice, with nothing changed at all. Beriberi. Laulau. Couscous. Mahimahi. And they are wonderful competition words, because you only ever have to spell half of one and then repeat it exactly.","show":{"t":"glyph","text":"beri + beri","sub":"spell half, then repeat"}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"Repetition is grammar","say":"Why do these exist? Because in many languages saying a word twice does real work. It makes a plural, or an intensive, or a small version. Sinhalese beri meant weakness, and doubling it made beriberi, meaning great weakness. Malay and Hawaiian do this constantly. The doubled form is the real word.","show":{"t":"breakdown","word":"beriberi","parts":[{"txt":"beri","gloss":"weakness"},{"txt":"beri","gloss":"said twice = great weakness"}]}},{"mood":"love","cap":"The Pacific gives the most","say":"Hawaiian and Polynesian languages supply the biggest group, and they are doubly predictable — they follow the tautonym rule and the Hawaiian alphabet rule at the same time. Laulau. Mahimahi. Lavalava. Muumuu. Makomako. Poroporo. Only thirteen letters available, every syllable ending in a vowel, and both halves identical.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"laulau","hi":[0,3]},{"word":"mahimahi","hi":[0,4]},{"word":"lavalava","hi":[0,4]},{"word":"poroporo","hi":[0,4]}]}},{"mood":"think","cap":"And the rest of the world","say":"Elsewhere they turn up as food, animals, clothes and music. Couscous is Berber. Tsetse is Tswana. Juju is West African. Dodo is Portuguese doudo, a simpleton. Caracara and quiaquia are birds. And French gives bonbon, froufrou, cancan and tutu.","show":{"t":"list","items":[{"word":"couscous","hi":[0,4]},{"word":"tsetse","hi":[0,3]},{"word":"froufrou","hi":[0,4]},{"word":"bonbon","hi":[0,3]}]}},{"mood":"oops","cap":"Not everything that looks doubled is","say":"Be careful. A true tautonym repeats exactly. Murmur looks like one but is Latin murmurare, imitating a sound. Tartar has a tangled Greek and Arabic history. And zigzag, flimflam and riffraff change the vowel between halves, which makes them reduplications from the previous chapter, not tautonyms.","show":{"t":"pair","left":"mahimahi","right":"zigzag","root":"identical halves versus a changed vowel","note":"a tautonym repeats exactly; a reduplication alters a sound"}},{"mood":"happy","cap":"The easiest hard words on the list","say":"So when a pronouncer gives you a word that repeats, relax. Spell the first half carefully, then write it again exactly. Three true tautonyms sit in your core competition list and forty two in the full library, and they are among the least frightening long words you will ever be handed.","show":{"t":"ask","q":"Heard a repeating word?","rules":["Spell the first half, then repeat exactly","Pacific ones also follow Hawaiian letter rules","Vowel changes between halves → not a tautonym","Check for a hyphen: gang-gang has one"]}}]}};
